= Electromagnetism
{wiki}

Electromagnetism describes how <electric charge> and <electric current> interact through the <electric field> $\mathbf E$ and <magnetic field> $\mathbf B$. The <Maxwell equations> describe the fields and their sources; the <Lorentz force> describes the response of a particle of <electric charge> $q$ and <velocity> $\mathbf v$:
$$
\mathbf F=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B),\qquad
\frac{d\mathcal E_{\mathrm{particle}}}{dt}=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v.
$$
The <electric field> can change the particle's <energy>. The magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> is perpendicular to <velocity>, so <magnetic forces do no work>; they can bend a trajectory. These are local interactions: a particle responds to the <electromagnetic field> at its position. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_01.html[Feynman lectures, II.1: fields and forces]{ref}.

The remarkable unification is that the same <Maxwell equations> connect <Coulomb's law>, <Faraday's law of induction>, and freely propagating <electromagnetic waves>. A changing <electric field> contributes to magnetic circulation through <displacement current>; together with <Faraday's law>, this permits waves in vacuum without conducting matter. The <Poynting theorem> then explains how the fields store and transport <energy>. This connects the force on a charge to the energy carried by light.

In his 1865 paper, <James Clerk Maxwell> derived transverse disturbances and compared the propagation speed inferred from electrical measurements with independently measured light speeds. Part VI, especially §§95–97, argues that their agreement supports identifying light as an <electromagnetic wave>. This was a comparison with finite experimental accuracy, rather than a numerical identity between the historical measurements. https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rstl.1865.0008[Maxwell, A Dynamical Theory of the Electromagnetic Field, Philosophical Transactions 155 (1865), 459–512]{ref}; https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Dynamical_Theory_of_the_Electromagnetic_Field/Part_VI[transcription of Maxwell's original Part VI]{ref}.

= Thermoelectric effect
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A <thermoelectric effect> couples <heat transfer> and the transport of <electric charge>: a <temperature gradient> can generate an <electromotive force>, while an <electric current> can carry <heat> and cause heating or cooling at a junction of different materials. This coupling makes electrical measurement of <temperature> possible through a <thermocouple>, and permits conversion of a sustained flow of <heat> into electrical <work>. A finite open-circuit voltage does not itself deliver <power>: electrical <power> requires an <electric current> through a load. Thermoelectric conversion therefore involves both <electromagnetism> and <thermodynamics>. https://thermoelectrics.matsci.northwestern.edu/thermoelectrics/history.html[Northwestern thermoelectrics research group: thermoelectric effects and applications]{ref}.

= Seebeck effect
{c}
{parent=Thermoelectric effect}

The <Seebeck effect> is the generation of a thermoelectric <electromotive force> by a <temperature> difference. In a <thermocouple>, two different homogeneous <electrical conductors> A and B connect a measuring junction at $T_h$ to two reference connections at a common $T_c$. With negligible <electric current>, the measured terminal voltage is determined by the difference of their <Seebeck coefficients>. A hot junction alone does not specify the reading: the reference <temperature>, the second material, and the terminal order matter.

\Image[seebeck-thermocouple-experiment.png]
{title=Two-material <thermocouple>: junction <temperatures> and the voltage sign}
{description=An idealized open-circuit <Seebeck effect> experiment. The A and B wires meet at the measuring junction; each reference connection meets an identical meter lead at the same $T_c$. The positive meter input connects to A. The shown $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, $70\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and $+2\,\mathrm{mV}$ belong to the explicitly toy constant-<Seebeck-coefficient> example below, not measured data.}

For a motivating experiment, join one end of A to one end of B, insulating the wires elsewhere. Connect their free ends to identical meter leads at reference <temperature> $T_c$, for example in an ice-water <heat bath>. A meter with input <electrical resistance> much larger than the <thermocouple>'s <electrical resistance> approximates an open circuit, meaning $I\approx0$. Keep the measuring junction in a separately controlled <heat bath> at $T_h$ and wait for stable junction <temperatures>. Both reference connections must share $T_c$; identical leads with identical endpoint <temperatures> have cancelling thermoelectric contributions, allowing the meter to be elsewhere. https://www.nist.gov/system/files/documents/director/planning/report97-1.pdf[Marx, Link and Scott, Economic Assessment of the NIST Thermocouple Calibration Program (1997), §2.1]{ref}.

Define the reading as $V_{AB}=V_A-V_B$, with the positive input on the A reference terminal and the negative input on B. Use the <Seebeck coefficient> convention in which the voltage change along a homogeneous wire at zero <electric current> is $dV=-S(T)\,dT$. Following the path from A at $T_c$ to the common junction at $T_h$, then back along B to $T_c$, gives
$$
V_{AB}(T_h,T_c)=\int_{T_c}^{T_h}\bigl[S_A(T)-S_B(T)\bigr]\,dT.
$$
This formula assumes homogeneous materials whose <Seebeck coefficients> depend only on the local <temperature>, and no additional uncompensated lead contributions. Here voltage is the electrical transport voltage associated with <electrochemical potential>, rather than a direct measurement of an isolated equilibrium contact <electric potential>.

Start with $T_h=T_c$, then increase $T_h$ while holding $T_c$ fixed and record $V_{AB}$ at each stable setting. In the ideal model the equal-<temperature> reading is zero. If $S_A-S_B>0$ throughout the interval, A reads positive when $T_h>T_c$. Exchanging meter inputs reverses the reading; exchanging the two junction <temperatures> reverses the oriented <integral>. Repeating the run with identical homogeneous materials gives zero because $S_A=S_B$. These controls test the stated model; a nonzero experimental baseline should be measured rather than silently removed by assuming it is zero.

For an explicitly toy linear example, stipulate constant <Seebeck coefficients> $S_A=25\,\mu\mathrm{V/K}$ and $S_B=-15\,\mu\mathrm{V/K}$. Choose reference <temperature> $T_c=20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ and measuring-junction <temperature> $T_h=70\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. A <temperature> difference has the same numerical size in <kelvins> and on the <degree Celsius> scale, so
$$
S_A-S_B=40\,\mu\mathrm{V/K},\qquad \Delta T=50\,\mathrm K,
\qquad V_{AB}=(40\,\mu\mathrm{V/K})(50\,\mathrm K)=+2.00\,\mathrm{mV}.
$$
Swapping the inputs gives $-2.00\,\mathrm{mV}$. Bringing both junction regions to the same <temperature> gives zero. With <temperature>-dependent <Seebeck coefficients>, shifting both junction <temperatures> equally while keeping their difference fixed can change the reading. No actual material pair is claimed to have the stipulated coefficients over this interval.

\Image[seebeck-toy-voltage-slope.png]
{title=Toy <Seebeck effect> calibration: voltage slope and reversed inputs}
{description=Calculated lines, not measured data. The solid line is $V_{AB}=(40\,\mu\mathrm{V/K})\Delta T$; the dashed line shows the same device with reversed meter inputs. The <slope> is the difference of the two <Seebeck coefficients>, not either coefficient separately. The reference <temperature> is fixed at $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$; negative $\Delta T$ means the measuring junction is colder than the reference connections.}

Real <thermocouples> need a calibrated voltage-versus-<temperature> relation: the constant-<Seebeck-coefficient> approximation is useful only where the coefficient difference changes little. Wire inhomogeneity can make the result depend on where the <temperature gradient> lies, and reference connections at unequal or unknown <temperatures> introduce errors. An electronic reference correction uses a measured reference <temperature> in place of an ice bath. https://www.nist.gov/system/files/documents/director/planning/report97-1.pdf[Marx, Link and Scott (1997), §2.1: reference correction and wire homogeneity]{ref}. Burns and colleagues provide the standardized reference functions for common letter-designated <thermocouple> types; the toy line above is not a replacement for them. https://www.nist.gov/publications/temperature-electromotive-force-reference-functions-and-tables-letter-designated[Burns et al., Temperature-electromotive force reference functions and tables (1993), NIST Monograph 175]{ref}.

The historical experiment used a different detector. In his 1821–1823 investigations, Seebeck found that a closed <electrical circuit> of two dissimilar materials with junctions at different <temperatures> deflected a compass needle. The modern explanation is that thermoelectric <electromotive force> drives an <electric current>, whose <magnetic field> deflects the needle. The open-circuit experiment above detects voltage while drawing negligible <electric current>. The striking connection is that a thermal imbalance can become an electrical signal and then a magnetic response. https://thermoelectrics.matsci.northwestern.edu/thermoelectrics/history.html[Northwestern thermoelectrics research group: Seebeck's compass experiment]{ref}.

= Seebeck coefficient
{c}
{parent=Seebeck effect}
{title2=$S$}

= Thermopower
{synonym}

The <Seebeck coefficient> $S(T)$ quantifies the thermoelectric voltage response per small <temperature> difference at zero <electric current>. Use $S=-dV/dT$ along a homogeneous material, with voltage understood through <electrochemical potential>. Its units are <volts> per <kelvin>, commonly $\mu\mathrm{V/K}$. The word <thermopower> is also used, despite this being a voltage response rather than <power>; some authors use that word for $|S|$, so check their convention. https://thermoelectrics.matsci.northwestern.edu/thermoelectrics/[Northwestern thermoelectrics research group: Seebeck coefficient and thermopower convention]{ref}.

For the A-minus-B terminal order in the <Seebeck effect> experiment,
$$
\frac{\partial V_{AB}}{\partial T_h}=S_A(T_h)-S_B(T_h),\qquad
\frac{\partial V_{AB}}{\partial T_c}=-\bigl[S_A(T_c)-S_B(T_c)\bigr].
$$
Thus a small <temperature> difference around $T_0$ gives $V_{AB}\approx[S_A(T_0)-S_B(T_0)]\Delta T$. The finite-interval ratio $V_{AB}/\Delta T$ is an average of the coefficient difference, not necessarily its value at either endpoint. Ordinary two-material measurements determine a relative <Seebeck coefficient>: to recover $S_A$ from the measured difference, add a separately known $S_B$. Neither the choice of a zero for voltage nor the use of a familiar reference material makes $S_B$ identically zero.

A concrete research example is Martin and colleagues' 2021 certification experiment. They measured small <temperature> differences and voltages on six <silicon>–germanium specimens at 13 base <temperatures> between $295$ and $900\,\mathrm K$. At each setting they acquired 40 voltage-and-<temperature>-difference pairs and used <linear regression> with a free intercept, so a constant voltage offset did not become part of the <slope>. Platinum voltage leads required a reference-material correction; their terminal order gave $S_{\rm measured}=S_{\rm Pt}-S_{\rm specimen}$, hence $S_{\rm specimen}=S_{\rm Pt}-S_{\rm measured}$. This is an actual measurement protocol, unlike the toy plot above. https://tsapps.nist.gov/publication/get_pdf.cfm?pub_id=932592[Martin et al., Development of a high-temperature (295–900 K) Seebeck coefficient Standard Reference Material (2021), certification measurements and Eq. (1)]{ref}.

Microscopically, a <temperature gradient> changes <charge carrier> transport until an opposing electrical response balances it at zero net <electric current>. In the usual simple <semiconductor> picture, <electrons> and <electron holes> give opposite <Seebeck-coefficient> signs, and their contributions are weighted by their <electrical conductivities>. The <Seebeck coefficient> is a transport property: <density of states> alone does not determine it, since carrier motion and scattering also matter. Its usefulness extends beyond <temperature> sensing to diagnosing which carriers transport <electric charge>. https://thermoelectrics.matsci.northwestern.edu/thermoelectrics/[Northwestern thermoelectrics research group: Seebeck coefficient, scattering contributions, and bipolar transport]{ref}.

= Thermocouple
{parent=Seebeck effect}
{wiki}

A <thermocouple> uses two different <electrical conductors> and the <Seebeck effect> to infer a measuring-junction <temperature> from a voltage and a known reference <temperature>. The reference material and the reference <temperature> are distinct choices: B specifies the comparison <Seebeck coefficient>, while $T_c$ specifies the lower limit of the <integral>. The two-material experiment in <Seebeck effect> gives the geometry, sign convention, and calibration limitations.

= Lorenz-gauge reduction of the electromagnetic action
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}

The <electromagnetic field tensor> action differs from the component-gradient potential action by $(2\mu_0)^{-1}\int \partial^bA^a\partial_aA_b\,d^4x$. Integration by parts expresses this term as a boundary integral plus $(2\mu_0)^{-1}\int(\partial_aA^a)^2\,d^4x$. With the <Lorenz gauge> and fixed or vanishing boundary data, the two actions therefore differ only by a boundary term. Varying the component-gradient action without restriction gives the potential wave equations; the Lorenz condition must be supplied to recover all <Maxwell equations>.

= Bioelectromagnetism
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

= Volume conductor
{parent=Bioelectromagnetism}

A <volume conductor> is a conducting three-dimensional medium through which <electric currents> flow. In the quasistatic bioelectric model, the total <current density> is $\mathbf J=\mathbf J^p-\sigma\nabla u$, comprising an impressed source current and its Ohmic return current. <Electric charge conservation> gives $\nabla\cdot(\sigma\nabla u)=\nabla\cdot\mathbf J^p$. An insulating boundary has zero normal total current.

= Geselowitz formula
{c}
{parent=Volume conductor}
{title2=$\mathbf B=\mathbf B^p-\frac{\mu_0\sigma}{4\pi}\int_S u\,\mathbf n'\times\frac{\mathbf r-\mathbf r'}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|^3}\,dS'$}

For a homogeneous <volume conductor> with an insulating boundary $S$, the <Geselowitz formula> expresses the exterior <magnetic field> as the primary <Biot-Savart law> field minus a surface contribution from the induced <electric potential>. For a <current dipole>, $\mathbf B^p=\mu_0\mathbf Q\times(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)/(4\pi|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|^3)$. On a spherical boundary, $\mathbf n'$ is parallel to $\mathbf r'$, so the surface term has zero radial component at the observation point.

= Spherical volume conductor model
{parent=Volume conductor}
{title2=$|\mathbf r|<a$}

A <spherical volume conductor model> approximates a conducting body by a sphere, usually with uniform or radially stratified conductivity. It provides explicit forward models for <electroencephalography> and <magnetoencephalography>. Its symmetry makes a <radial current dipole> magnetically silent outside the sphere; departures from spherical symmetry need not retain that property.

= Sarvas formula
{c}
{parent=Spherical volume conductor model}
{title2=$\mathbf B=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\nabla\frac{(\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0)\cdot\mathbf r}{F}$}

For the exterior of a <spherical volume conductor model>, put $P=|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|$, $r=|\mathbf r|$ and $F=rP^2+P\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)$. The displayed <Sarvas formula> follows by taking the radial component of the <Geselowitz formula> and integrating the <magnetic scalar potential> from infinity. It is independent of a spherically symmetric conductivity profile. The sign shown uses $\mathbf B=+\mu_0\nabla U/(4\pi)$; conventions defining a magnetic potential with a minus sign reverse $U$. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3823129/[Sarvas's original paper] derives the spherical-conductor field and its consequences for the inverse problem.

= Spherical current-dipole logarithmic kernel
{parent=Sarvas formula}
{title2=$\phi=\frac1b\log\frac{r+P+b}{r+P-b},\quad b=|\mathbf r_0|$}

For $r>b$ and $P=|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|$, the displayed kernel satisfies $\phi=\int_r^\infty ds/[s|s\widehat{\mathbf r}-\mathbf r_0|]$. The limit at $b=0$ is $1/r$. Differentiating along $\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0$, which preserves $b$, gives $(\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0)\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf r_0}\phi=(\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0)\cdot\mathbf r/F$. Thus it generates the normalized <magnetic scalar potential> in the <Sarvas formula>.

= Maxwell–Chern–Simons one-form equation
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$d{*F}+kF=0$}

For a <differential one-form> $A$ on an oriented three-dimensional spacetime, varying $\frac12\int(F\wedge*F+kA\wedge F)$, with $F=dA$, gives $d*F+kF=0$. With signature $(-,+,+)$, ${*}^2=-1$ on one- and two-forms; writing $B=*F$ gives $dB-k*B=0$ and the identity $d*B=0$. An Abelian <gauge transformation> changes the action by $\frac k2\int_{\partial M}\Lambda F$. Thus the bulk field equation is invariant, while invariance of the integrated action depends on boundary conditions.

= Electromagnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$\mathbf E,\mathbf B$}
{wiki}

= Maxwell field
{synonym}
{c}

The combined <electric field> and <magnetic field> that determine the <Lorentz force> on a charged particle.

= Electrical circuit
{parent=Electromagnetism}

An electrical circuit connects components that carry <electric current> and sustain <electric potential difference> differences. Its dynamics may couple <electrical resistance>, <inductance> and <capacitance>.

= Passive sign convention
{parent=Electrical circuit}
{title2=$p_{\rm absorbed}=VI$}

The <passive sign convention> defines terminal <electric potential difference> $V=\phi(A)-\phi(B)$ and positive <electric current> entering at $A$. Then $VI$ is absorbed electrical <power>: positive means the component receives <energy>, negative means it supplies <energy>. Reversing both reference directions leaves the product unchanged. For a positive <electrical resistance> obeying <Ohm's law>, both $V$ and $I$ reverse together and <Joule heating> remains $I^2R\geq0$.

= Electrical impedance
{parent=Electrical circuit}
{title2=$Z(\omega)=\widehat V/\widehat I$}
{wiki}

Electrical impedance is the ratio of voltage and current <complex amplitudes> for sinusoidal steady response at a specified <angular frequency>. With time dependence $e^{\mathrm i\omega t}$, an ideal <resistor>, <inductor> and <capacitor> have $Z_R=R$, $Z_L=\mathrm i\omega L$ and $Z_C=1/(\mathrm i\omega C)$. Their <electrical impedances> add in a <series RLC circuit>, giving $Z=R+\mathrm i(\omega L-1/(\omega C))$. The current <amplitude> is $V_0/|Z|$, so cancellation of the imaginary terms explains the <resonant series RLC response>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-3-rlc-series-circuits-with-ac[OpenStax University Physics 2, §15.3: impedance and series resonance]{ref}.

= Electrical reactance
{parent=Electrical impedance}
{title2=$X=\operatorname{Im}Z$}

Electrical reactance $X$ is the signed <imaginary part> of <electrical impedance> $Z=R+\mathrm iX$, measured in <ohms>. It describes the part of the terminal <electric potential difference> in <phase quadrature> with the <electric current> in a sinusoidal <steady state>. In the $e^{\mathrm i\omega t}$ convention, an ideal <inductor> has $X_L=\omega L>0$ and an ideal <capacitor> has $X_C=-1/(\omega C)<0$ for $\omega>0$. A large $|X|$ reduces <electric current> at fixed applied voltage, but an ideal purely reactive component returns the <energy> it receives over each cycle. This is why <electrical reactance> and <electrical resistance> have the same units yet describe different physical behavior. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_22.html#Ch22-S5[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, The Feynman Lectures on Physics, II.22.5: resistance, reactance and energy loss]{ref}.

\Image[reactance-frequency-cancellation.png]
{title=Signed <electrical reactances> cancel in a toy <series RLC circuit>}
{description=Calculated ideal curves, not measured data: <inductance> $L=10\,\mathrm{mH}$, <capacitance> $C=1\,\mu\mathrm F$, total <electrical resistance> $R_\Sigma=100+10=110\,\Omega$, and applied <root mean square> voltage $1.00\,\mathrm V$. The $10\,\Omega$ is the sensing <resistor> in <electrical reactance bench measurement>. Top: $X_L$, signed $X_C$, and their sum. Bottom: <electric current> and voltage-minus-current <phase difference>. At $f_0=1.592\,\mathrm{kHz}$ the sum vanishes, the <electric current> peaks, and terminal voltage and <electric current> are in phase.}

In a <series RLC circuit>, <electrical impedances> add because the same <electric current> passes through every component. For ideal positive <inductance> $L$, <capacitance> $C$, and total <electrical resistance> $R_\Sigma>0$,
$$
X(\omega)=\omega L-\frac1{\omega C},\qquad
|Z|=\sqrt{R_\Sigma^2+X^2},\qquad
I_{\rm rms}=\frac{V_{\rm rms}}{|Z|},\qquad
\phi=\arctan\frac{X}{R_\Sigma}.
$$
Here $\phi$ is voltage <wave phase> minus current <wave phase>. Below $\omega_0=1/\sqrt{LC}$, $X<0$ and <electric current> leads terminal voltage; above it, $X>0$ and <electric current> lags. At $\omega_0$, the two signed <electrical reactances> cancel, leaving $Z=R_\Sigma$ and the maximum <electric current> at fixed terminal voltage. This is the <resonant series RLC response>. <Electrical resistance> does not cancel: the toy circuit gives $f_0=\omega_0/(2\pi)=1591.55\,\mathrm{Hz}$ and $I_{\rm rms}=9.091\,\mathrm{mA}$ there. These numbers follow directly from the stipulated component values. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-3-rlc-series-circuits-with-ac[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics 2, §15.3: series impedance and resonance]{ref}.

Cancellation concerns terminal voltage, not the disappearance of stored <energy>. In the toy circuit at <resonance>, the individual <inductor> and <capacitor> voltages are each $0.9091\,\mathrm V$ in <root mean square> magnitude, with opposite <wave phases>, while their sum is zero. Their peak stored <magnetic energy> and <capacitor energy> are each $0.8264\,\mu\mathrm J$, attained at different times. With smaller positive $R_\Sigma$, either component voltage can exceed the applied voltage. This ability to select a <frequency> while sustaining internal <energy> exchange connects <electrical reactance> to tuning and <frequency response>.

For an ideal <inductor> or <capacitor>, the absorbed instantaneous <power> equals the rate of change of stored <energy>:
$$
p_L=v_Li=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac12Li^2\right),\qquad
p_C=v_Ci=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac12Cv_C^2\right).
$$
Positive <power> stores <energy>; negative <power> returns it. Over a periodic <steady state>, each stored <energy> returns to its initial value, so its cycle-averaged absorbed <power> is zero. An ideal <resistor> instead has $p_R=Ri^2\geq0$, transferring <energy> into <Joule heating>. More generally, the average terminal <power> of this <series RLC circuit> is
$$
\overline p=V_{\rm rms}I_{\rm rms}\cos\phi=I_{\rm rms}^2R_\Sigma.
$$
Zero average <power> in an ideal reactive component does not imply zero instantaneous <power>, zero <electric current>, or zero stored <energy>. Real coils and <capacitors> have losses, so zero ideal reactive loss is a model assumption rather than a claim about every component. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-4-power-in-an-ac-circuit[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics 2, §15.4: instantaneous and average AC power]{ref}.

= Reactance phase convention
{parent=Electrical reactance}
{title2=$v(t)=\operatorname{Re}(\widehat V e^{\mathrm i\omega t})$}

Use <complex amplitudes> only for signals at one common positive <angular frequency> $\omega$. Define the real terminal voltage and <electric current> by
$$
v(t)=\operatorname{Re}\left(\widehat V e^{\mathrm i\omega t}\right),\qquad
i(t)=\operatorname{Re}\left(\widehat I e^{\mathrm i\omega t}\right),\qquad
Z=\frac{\widehat V}{\widehat I}.
$$
The <complex amplitudes> here use peak magnitudes; both can instead use <root mean square> magnitudes without changing their ratio. Choose positive <electric current> entering the terminal assigned positive voltage, so $p=vi$ is absorbed <power>. For an ideal linear <inductor> and <capacitor>, their terminal laws $v_L=L\,di/dt$ and $i_C=C\,dv_C/dt$ give
$$
\widehat V_L=\mathrm i\omega L\widehat I,\qquad
\widehat I_C=\mathrm i\omega C\widehat V_C,\qquad
Z_L=\mathrm i\omega L,\qquad
Z_C=\frac1{\mathrm i\omega C}=-\frac{\mathrm i}{\omega C}.
$$
The <derivative> multiplies a <complex amplitude> by $\mathrm i\omega$: this single algebraic step explains both signs. Taking $i(t)=I_0\cos\omega t$ as a reference gives $v_L(t)=-\omega LI_0\sin\omega t$ and $v_C(t)=I_0\sin\omega t/(\omega C)$ for the sinusoidal response. Thus <inductor> voltage leads <electric current> by $\pi/2$, while <capacitor> voltage lags <electric current> by $\pi/2$. A constant offset or a free transient is outside this single-<frequency> representation. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_22.html#Ch22-S1[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.22.1: complex sinusoidal representation and component impedances]{ref}.

With the alternative $e^{-\mathrm i\omega t}$ convention, the same real signals use conjugated <complex amplitudes> and $Z_L=-\mathrm i\omega L$, $Z_C=+\mathrm i/(\omega C)$. Their physical leads and lags stay the same; the sign of $\operatorname{Im}Z$ changes. Also, some textbooks call the positive magnitude $1/(\omega C)$ the capacitive reactance and write the series expression as $X_L-|X_C|$. This article consistently uses signed $X_C=-1/(\omega C)$ and hence $X=X_L+X_C$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-2-simple-ac-circuits[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics 2, §15.2: positive capacitive-reactance magnitude and quarter-cycle phase relations]{ref}.

= Electrical reactance bench measurement
{parent=Electrical reactance}
{title2=$X=|Z|\sin\phi$}

The motivating observation is a pair of sinusoids on an oscilloscope: changing <frequency> changes both their relative <amplitudes> and their <phase difference>. Gary Tuttle's EE 201 laboratory uses resistor voltage as the current-phase reference, compares predictions with readings at several <frequencies>, and explicitly asks students to measure component values and account for coil <electrical resistance>. The following bench arrangement applies that method to a <series RLC circuit>; its component values and illustrated traces are our own toy example. https://gtuttle.net/circuits/lab/ac_circuits_1.pdf[Gary Tuttle, EE 201 Lab 9: AC circuits, especially the notes and RC/RL measurements]{ref}.

\Image[reactance-bench-phase-sensing.png]
{title=Toy AC bench arrangement and predicted voltage/current timing}
{description=Top: a sine generator drives ideal $L=10\,\mathrm{mH}$, $C=1\,\mu\mathrm F$, $R=100\,\Omega$, and current-sensing $R_s=10\,\Omega$ in series. Channel A measures loaded terminal voltage; channel B measures $v_s=R_si$ relative to the common return. Bottom: calculated separate voltage traces at $f=500\,\mathrm{Hz}$ and terminal $V_{\rm rms}=1.00\,\mathrm V$, not an oscilloscope recording or measured data. The rising zero of channel B occurs $0.3835\,\mathrm{ms}$ earlier: $\phi=-69.02^\circ$ and predicted $I_{\rm rms}=3.255\,\mathrm{mA}$. The two trace axes have different voltage scales.}

Connect a low-voltage sine generator to a series chain ending in a known sensing <resistor> $R_s$ at the common return. Use a <resistor> whose unwanted <inductance> is negligible over the chosen <frequency> range. An oscilloscope displays voltage against time: channel A reads the loaded voltage across the whole chain, and channel B reads the voltage $v_s$ across $R_s$. Both channel ground connections and the generator return meet at that same node. Ordinary bench oscilloscope grounds are electrically common; attaching one to an intermediate circuit node would change the circuit. A separate floating component voltage needs a suitable differential measurement, rather than moving a ground clip. https://www.tek.com/en/documents/technical-brief/floating-oscilloscope-measurements-and-operator-protection[Tektronix, Floating Oscilloscope Measurements and Operator Protection: ground-referenced inputs]{ref}.

After the free transient decays, record <frequency> $f$, period $T=1/f$, both voltage <amplitudes>, and matched rising zero-crossing times $t_A,t_B$. Check that the traces are sinusoidal. Define $\Delta t=t_B-t_A$ on the branch $|\Delta t|<T/2$. With negligible probe loading and negligible sensing-<resistor> <electrical reactance>, $v_s=R_si$, so channel B is the <electric current> reference, and in the <reactance phase convention>
$$
I_{\rm rms}=\frac{V_{s,\rm rms}}{R_s},\qquad
|Z_{\rm chain}|=\frac{V_{A,\rm rms}}{I_{\rm rms}},\qquad
\phi=\frac{2\pi\Delta t}{T},\qquad
X_{\rm chain}=|Z_{\rm chain}|\sin\phi,\qquad
R_{\rm chain}=|Z_{\rm chain}|\cos\phi.
$$
A later current crossing gives $\Delta t>0$, hence positive <electrical reactance>; an earlier crossing gives negative <electrical reactance>. Use the same <amplitude> convention for both channels. An amplitude-only ratio recovers $|Z|$, so it cannot distinguish the two signs: timing supplies the missing information. The formulas follow from $Z=|Z|e^{\mathrm i\phi}$ and the chosen terminal orientation.

Begin with a known <resistor> replacing the reactive chain: its expected <phase difference> is approximately zero, testing channel polarity and relative delay. Measure the same signal with both channels to establish the instrumental delay baseline, then exchange probes to check that a phase offset does not belong to one channel. Next test an <inductor> and a <capacitor> separately with $R_s$: after subtracting $R_s$ from the inferred real part, expect approximately $X/f=2\pi L$ and $fX=-1/(2\pi C)$, respectively. Finally combine $L,C,R,R_s$ and sweep through the predicted <resonance>. These are proposed controls and consequences of the model, not reported observations.

For the toy <series RLC circuit>, the prediction at $500\,\mathrm{Hz}$ is $X=-286.894\,\Omega$, $|Z|=307.259\,\Omega$, $\phi=-69.02^\circ$, and $V_{s,\rm rms}=32.55\,\mathrm{mV}$. At $5000\,\mathrm{Hz}$, $X=+282.328\,\Omega$ and $\phi=+68.71^\circ$. Near $1591.55\,\mathrm{Hz}$ the model predicts zero <phase difference> and $V_{s,\rm rms}=90.91\,\mathrm{mV}$. Keep actual loaded $V_{A,\rm rms}$ fixed or normalize by it at each <frequency>; the generator's display alone does not establish that voltage. The $10\,\Omega$ sensing <resistor> is part of the $110\,\Omega$ total and therefore changes the predicted peak <electric current>.

Real components add unintended <electrical impedance>: coil wire has <electrical resistance>, nearby turns add <capacitance>, and <capacitor> leads add <inductance>. These effects can produce a component's own <resonance>, beyond which the ideal $\omega L$ or $-1/(\omega C)$ approximation fails. Work in a range where that approximation is adequate, measure coil wire <electrical resistance>, and compare the inferred $R_{\rm chain}$ with the sensing <resistor>, intended <resistor>, and component losses. A measured nonzero real part is evidence of loss in the assembly, not negative or positive <electrical reactance> becoming heat. https://www.keysight.com.cn/cn/zh/assets/7018-06840/application-notes/5950-3000.pdf[Keysight, Impedance Measurement Handbook, §§1.5.1 and 5.2.1: component frequency response and parasitic elements]{ref}; https://gtuttle.net/circuits/lab/ac_circuits_1.pdf[Tuttle, EE 201 Lab 9: parasitic coil series resistance]{ref}.

The probes also add <electrical resistance> and <capacitance> to the measured nodes; their loading becomes more important as <frequency> increases. Use compensated probes with known input <electrical impedance> and short ground connections, and repeat with a smaller drive voltage to test whether the inferred $X$ depends on <amplitude>. https://www.tek.com/en/documents/application-note/how-oscilloscope-probes-affect-your-measurement[Tektronix, How Oscilloscope Probes Affect Your Measurement: resistive and capacitive loading]{ref}. Record actual voltage and phase readings separately from calculated predictions, with component tolerances and timing uncertainty. A phase error $\delta\phi$ alone produces approximately $\delta X=|Z|\cos\phi\,\delta\phi$, so near zero <electrical reactance>, a small delay can imitate a small residual $X$. Agreement within stated uncertainties tests the model; the smooth toy curves establish no experimental accuracy.

= Electric battery
{parent=Electrical circuit}
{wiki}

= Battery
{synonym}

An <electric battery> supplies <energy> to an <electrical circuit> by moving <electric charge> through a terminal <electric potential difference>. In the ideal fixed-voltage model used in <fixed-voltage capacitor energy balance>, it maintains a prescribed terminal <electric potential difference> while <electric charge> flows to or from the <capacitor>.

= RL circuit
{c}
{parent=Electrical circuit}
{title2=$L\,dI/dt+RI=V$}
{wiki}

An <RL circuit> combines <electrical resistance> $R$ and <self-inductance> $L$. In the series model, the same <electric current> $I$ flows through both components. An ideal <battery> maintains terminal <electric potential difference> $V$; the drops in the direction of positive <electric current> obey <Ohm's law> and the <inductor> relation:
$$
v_R=RI,\qquad v_L=L\frac{dI}{dt},\qquad
V=v_R+v_L.
$$
This is <Kirchhoff's voltage law> with the <inductor>'s terminal drop, rather than its oppositely signed induced <electromotive force>. For constant $R>0$, $L>0$, and $V$, the <first-order linear differential equation> has solution
$$
I(t)=I_\infty+(I(0)-I_\infty)e^{-t/\tau},\qquad
I_\infty=\frac VR,\qquad \tau=\frac LR.
$$
The <time constant> $\tau$ measures <exponential decay> of the difference from the final <electric current>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-4-rl-circuits[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.4: RL circuits]{ref}.

For a stipulated example, take <electrical resistance> $R=2\,\Omega$, <self-inductance> $L=0.5\,\mathrm H$, terminal <electric potential difference> $V=6\,\mathrm V$, and initial <electric current> $I(0)=0$. Then
$$
\tau=0.25\,\mathrm s,\qquad I_\infty=3\,\mathrm A,
\qquad I(t)=(3\,\mathrm A)(1-e^{-t/(0.25\,\mathrm s)}).
$$
With $t$ measured numerically in seconds, this is $I(t)=3(1-e^{-4t})$ in amperes. At one <time constant>,
$$
I(\tau)=3(1-e^{-1})\,\mathrm A\approx1.89636\,\mathrm A,
$$
and the terminal <electric potential difference> drops are
$$
v_R(\tau)=6(1-e^{-1})\,\mathrm V\approx3.79272\,\mathrm V,
\qquad v_L(\tau)=6e^{-1}\,\mathrm V\approx2.20728\,\mathrm V.
$$
Their sum is exactly $6\,\mathrm V$. At $t=0^+$, the <inductor> takes the entire terminal <electric potential difference> while the <electric current> is still zero. At steady <electric current>, the <inductor>'s terminal drop is zero and the entire drop is across the <electrical resistance>.

\Image[rl-current-and-voltage.png]
{title=<Electric current> and terminal <electric potential difference> drops during an <RL circuit> response}
{description=The <battery> maintains $6\,\mathrm V$ across <electrical resistance> $2\,\Omega$ and <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$ in series. The dashed vertical line marks <time constant> $0.25\,\mathrm s$; $v_L$ uses the positive terminal-drop convention.}

The final <magnetic energy> stored by the <inductor> is $U_\infty=LI_\infty^2/2=2.25\,\mathrm J$. At one <time constant>, the <electric current> has reached about $63.21\%$ of its final value, but its <magnetic energy> has reached only about $39.96\%$:
$$
U(\tau)=\frac12L I(\tau)^2
=2.25(1-e^{-1})^2\,\mathrm J\approx0.899047\,\mathrm J.
$$
This difference follows from the square of <electric current> in the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy>.

For release, take $t=0$ at the start of a separate experiment with initial <electric current> $3\,\mathrm A$. Remove the <battery> from the model while maintaining a closed path through the same <electrical resistance> and <inductor>. Then
$$
L\frac{dI}{dt}+RI=0,\qquad
I(t)=(3\,\mathrm A)e^{-t/(0.25\,\mathrm s)}.
$$
Now $v_L=-v_R$: the induced <electromotive force> supports the continuing positive <electric current> as its magnitude decreases. This closed-loop model describes <exponential decay>, not opening the sole path and imposing an instantaneous jump to zero <electric current>. The <inductor>'s <magnetic energy> and the <power> of <Joule heating> are
$$
U(t)=(2.25\,\mathrm J)e^{-2t/\tau},\qquad
P_R(t)=RI(t)^2=(18\,\mathrm W)e^{-2t/\tau},\qquad
\frac{dU}{dt}=-P_R.
$$
Their <time constant> is $\tau/2=0.125\,\mathrm s$. The total <energy> dissipated by <Joule heating> is exactly the initial <magnetic energy>:
$$
\int_0^\infty P_R(t)\,dt
=(18\,\mathrm W)\frac{\tau}{2}=2.25\,\mathrm J.
$$
The second diagram in <inductor> shows this <energy> transfer. A finite closed path lets us predict both how long the <electric current> persists and how much <energy> becomes heat. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-4-rl-circuits[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.4: decay in a closed RL loop]{ref}.

= RLC circuit
{c}
{parent=Electrical circuit}
{wiki}

An <RLC circuit> combines <electrical resistance> $R$, <inductance> $L$, and <capacitance> $C$. The <capacitor> stores <energy> in its <electric field>; the <inductor> stores <energy> in its <magnetic field>. Their exchange can produce an <oscillation>, while <Joule heating> removes <energy>. A <series RLC circuit> gives a particularly clear example: changing $R$ turns ringing into a direct return to equilibrium, and changing $L$ or $C$ tunes a <resonance>.

These circuits connect two useful viewpoints. In time, they describe how stored <energy> is released after switching. In <frequency>, they select a range of signals: the <resonant series RLC response> explains radio tuning and simple filters. The same mathematics describes a <damped harmonic oscillator>, so insight about mechanical ringing transfers directly to an <electrical circuit>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_23.html#Ch23-S3[Feynman lectures, I.23.3: electrical and mechanical resonance]{ref}; https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_22.html#Ch22-S7[Feynman lectures, II.22.7: frequency filters]{ref}.

= Series RLC circuit
{parent=RLC circuit}
{title2=$Lq''+Rq'+q/C=V(t)$}

In a <series RLC circuit>, the same <electric current> $i=q'$ flows through the <resistor> with <electrical resistance> $R$, the <inductor>, and the <capacitor>. Choose $q$ as the signed <electric charge> on the <capacitor> plate into which positive $i$ flows. With terminal drops measured in that direction, <Kirchhoff's voltage law> gives
$$
v_R=Ri,\qquad v_L=Li',\qquad v_C=q/C,\qquad
Lq''+Rq'+q/C=V(t).
$$
Here $V(t)$ is the applied <electric potential difference>; assume constant $L>0$, $C>0$, and $R>0$. With the applied source set to zero and the loop kept closed, the free response satisfies
$$
q''+2\alpha q'+\omega_0^2q=0,\qquad
\alpha=\frac{R}{2L},\qquad \omega_0=\frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}}.
$$
The <angular frequency> $\omega_0$ is the value for lossless free <oscillation>; $\alpha$ is the decay parameter. The three possibilities are <underdamped RLC response> for $\alpha<\omega_0$, <critically damped RLC response> for $\alpha=\omega_0$, and <overdamped RLC response> for $\alpha>\omega_0$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-6-rlc-series-circuits[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.6: free RLC response]{ref}.

For a fair comparison, start each circuit with the same <electric charge> $q(0)=q_0$ and zero <electric current> $q'(0)=0$. Set $\tau=\omega_0t$ and $a=\alpha/\omega_0$; then $x=q/q_0$ obeys $d^2x/d\tau^2+2a\,dx/d\tau+x=0$. The plot compares $a=0.5,1,2$. These dimensionless curves apply to any $L,C$ after rescaling time and <electric charge>.

\Image[rlc-free-damping.png]
{title=Free <series RLC circuit> responses with identical <initial conditions>}
{description=Left: <electric charge> for <underdamped RLC response>, <critically damped RLC response>, and <overdamped RLC response>. Right: total stored <energy>, which decreases in all three cases even when <electric charge> oscillates. The normalization is $\omega_0=1$, $q_0=1$, $q'(0)=0$.}

The stored <energy> is the sum of <magnetic energy> and <capacitor energy>:
$$
E=\frac12Li^2+\frac{q^2}{2C}.
$$
Differentiating and using the circuit equation gives the complete <power> balance,
$$
E'=i\left(Li'+\frac qC\right)=V(t)i-Ri^2.
$$
In the free response, $E'=-Ri^2\leq0$: <Joule heating> accounts exactly for the lost <energy>. For the plotted <initial conditions>, $E(0)=q_0^2/(2C)$ and $E/E(0)=x^2+(dx/d\tau)^2$. Since every free solution tends to zero for $R>0$,
$$
\int_0^\infty Ri(t)^2\,dt=E(0).
$$
In the lossless limit $R=0$, <energy> stays constant and shuttles between the <capacitor> and <inductor>. Thus an <oscillation> of <electric charge> and a decrease of total <energy> are compatible statements.

The analogy with a <damped harmonic oscillator> is exact within these ideal models: <electric charge> corresponds to displacement, <electric current> to <velocity>, <inductance> to <mass>, $1/C$ to <spring constant>, and $R$ to the coefficient of a resisting force proportional to <velocity>. The two stored <energy> terms play the roles of <kinetic energy> and elastic <potential energy>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_23.html#Ch23-S3[Feynman lectures, I.23.3: the oscillator analogy]{ref}.

Differentiating the driven equation also gives $Li''+Ri'+i/C=V'$. When using this equation to solve for <electric current>, retain the original constraint $Li'(0)+Ri(0)+q(0)/C=V(0)$: the initial <electric charge> fixes the initial rate of change of <electric current>.

= Resonant series RLC response
{parent=Series RLC circuit}
{title2=$I_{\mathrm{amp}}(\omega_0)=V_0/R$}

Drive a <series RLC circuit> with <electric potential difference> $V(t)=V_0\cos(\omega t)$, where $V_0>0$ and $\omega>0$. After the free transient decays, the <electric current> has the same <angular frequency> as the source:
$$
i_{\mathrm{ss}}(t)=I_{\mathrm{amp}}\cos(\omega t-\phi),\qquad
I_{\mathrm{amp}}(\omega)=\frac{V_0}{\sqrt{R^2+(\omega L-1/(\omega C))^2}},\qquad
\tan\phi=\frac{\omega L-1/(\omega C)}{R},\quad -\frac\pi2<\phi<\frac\pi2.
$$
For fixed positive $R,L,C,V_0$, the <electric current> <amplitude> is largest exactly at $\omega=\omega_0=1/\sqrt{LC}$. The two <electrical reactance> contributions cancel there, leaving only the drop from <electrical resistance>. In particular,
$$
i_{\mathrm{ss}}(t)=\frac{V_0}{R}\cos(\omega_0t),\qquad
q_{\mathrm{ss}}(t)=\frac{V_0}{R\omega_0}\sin(\omega_0t).
$$
Every solution approaches this periodic response because its difference from the displayed solution is a decaying free response. The peak remains at $\omega_0$ even for an <overdamped RLC response>; free ringing is not required for a maximum of driven <electric current>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-3-rlc-series-circuits-with-ac[OpenStax University Physics 2, §15.3: current amplitude, phase, and resonance]{ref}.

For example, stipulate $L=10\,\mathrm{mH}$, $C=1\,\mu\mathrm F$, $R=20\,\Omega$, and $V_0=1\,\mathrm V$. Then $\omega_0=10^4\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, $f_0=\omega_0/(2\pi)\approx1591.55\,\mathrm{Hz}$, and the peak <electric current> <amplitude> is $50\,\mathrm{mA}$. Holding the source <amplitude> fixed is essential when comparing responses at different <frequencies>.

\Image[rlc-current-resonance.png]
{title=Driven <electric current> <resonance> and the distinct <electric charge> peak}
{description=Left: fixed $L=1\,\mathrm H$, $C=1\,\mathrm F$, $V_0=1\,\mathrm V$, with positive $R=0.5,1,2,4\,\Omega$. Right: at $R=1\,\Omega$, the charge-amplitude peak, free ringing frequency, and current-amplitude peak occur at $\omega/\omega_0\approx0.707,0.866,1$, respectively. Both driven curves are divided by their own peak amplitude.}

Three <frequencies> must be distinguished. The free <underdamped RLC response> rings at $\omega_d=\sqrt{\omega_0^2-\alpha^2}$. Driven <electric current> peaks at $\omega_0$. Driven <electric charge> has <amplitude>
$$
q_{\mathrm{amp}}(\omega)=\frac{I_{\mathrm{amp}}(\omega)}{\omega}
=\frac{V_0/L}{\sqrt{(\omega_0^2-\omega^2)^2+4\alpha^2\omega^2}},
$$
whose peak occurs at $\sqrt{\omega_0^2-2\alpha^2}$ only if $\alpha<\omega_0/\sqrt2$. Otherwise it decreases from its zero-frequency limit $CV_0$, with no positive-frequency peak. The different peak locations follow because <electric current> is the time <derivative> of <electric charge>, multiplying a sinusoidal <amplitude> by $\omega$. The limiting responses are $I_{\mathrm{amp}}\sim V_0C\omega$ as $\omega\to0^+$ and $I_{\mathrm{amp}}\sim V_0/(L\omega)$ as $\omega\to\infty$.

The <Q factor> measures selectivity:
$$
Q=\frac{L\omega_0}{R}=\frac{\omega_0}{2\alpha}.
$$
The average <Joule heating> <power> is $RI_{\mathrm{amp}}^2/2$. Its half-maximum <angular frequencies> and their separation are exactly
$$
\omega_\pm=\sqrt{\omega_0^2+\alpha^2}\pm\alpha,\qquad
\Delta\omega=\omega_+-\omega_-=2\alpha=\frac RL=\frac{\omega_0}{Q}.
$$
These follow by setting $|\omega L-1/(\omega C)|=R$. In the numerical example, $Q=5$ and $\Delta\omega=2000\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$. Reducing $R$ makes a taller, narrower <electric current> peak; varying $C$ tunes the selected <frequency>. Measuring the drop $v_R=Ri$ gives a <band-pass filter> that suppresses very low and very high <frequencies> while passing a range near <resonance>. This is useful for selecting a radio signal, though the range must be broad enough to pass its modulation. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_22.html#Ch22-S7[Feynman lectures, II.22.7: resonant filters and radio sidebands]{ref}.

= Overdamped RLC response
{parent=Series RLC circuit}
{title2=$\alpha>\omega_0$}

An <overdamped RLC response> is a free <series RLC circuit> response with $R>2\sqrt{L/C}$, equivalently $\alpha>\omega_0$. Put $\beta=\sqrt{\alpha^2-\omega_0^2}$. The distinct negative <characteristic roots> are $r_\pm=-\alpha\pm\beta$, so the general <electric charge> is
$$
q(t)=Ae^{r_+t}+Be^{r_-t},\qquad i(t)=q'(t).
$$
For <initial conditions> $q(0)=q_0$, $i(0)=0$,
$$
q(t)=q_0e^{-\alpha t}\left(\cosh(\beta t)+\frac\alpha\beta\sinh(\beta t)\right),\qquad
i(t)=-\frac{q_0\omega_0^2}{\beta}e^{-\alpha t}\sinh(\beta t).
$$
For $q_0>0$, this <electric charge> decreases to zero without reversing sign. In the dimensionless example $\omega_0=1$, $\alpha=2$, the <characteristic roots> are $-2\pm\sqrt3\approx-0.268,-3.732$. The slow term dominates late times. Stronger <damping> can therefore make the final return slower: for $\alpha\gg\omega_0$, $r_+\sim-\omega_0^2/(2\alpha)=-1/(RC)$. Nonoscillation alone does not imply a fast response, and monotonicity here uses the specified <initial conditions>.

= Critically damped RLC response
{parent=Series RLC circuit}
{title2=$\alpha=\omega_0,\quad R=2\sqrt{L/C}$}

A <critically damped RLC response> is the boundary between <underdamped RLC response> and <overdamped RLC response>. The free <series RLC circuit> has a repeated <characteristic root> $-\omega_0$, with general solution
$$
q(t)=(A+Bt)e^{-\omega_0t}.
$$
For <initial conditions> $q(0)=q_0$, $i(0)=0$,
$$
q(t)=q_0(1+\omega_0t)e^{-\omega_0t},\qquad
i(t)=-q_0\omega_0^2t e^{-\omega_0t}.
$$
In the plotted normalization, $q(1)=2/e\approx0.735759$ and $i(1)=-1/e\approx-0.367879$. The <electric charge> returns without ringing; the <polynomial> factor $1+\omega_0t$ matters, so a single <exponential function> does not give the whole response.

For fixed $L,C$ and these <initial conditions>, <critical damping> avoids ringing while giving a faster asymptotic return than any larger $R$: the <overdamped RLC response> has a root closer to zero. This makes <critical damping> a useful tuning target for settling after switching. An MIT laboratory exercise makes the distinction observable by asking students to compare all three cases with $L=47\,\mathrm{mH}$ and $C=47\,\mathrm{nF}$. Those values give $R_{\mathrm{crit}}=2\sqrt{L/C}=2000\,\Omega$ and $\omega_0\approx21276.6\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-071j-introduction-to-electronics-signals-and-measurement-spring-2006/491e7e50ee65521c601ee51d3c6f7e5f_lab15_transients.pdf[MIT 6.071, Spring 2006, Laboratory 15, exercise 2]{ref}.

= Underdamped RLC response
{parent=Series RLC circuit}
{title2=$\omega_d=\sqrt{\omega_0^2-\alpha^2}$}

An <underdamped RLC response> is a free <series RLC circuit> response with $0<R<2\sqrt{L/C}$, equivalently $0<\alpha<\omega_0$. Its <characteristic roots> are $-\alpha\pm\mathrm{i}\omega_d$, and its general <electric charge> is
$$
q(t)=e^{-\alpha t}\left(A\cos(\omega_dt)+B\sin(\omega_dt)\right).
$$
For <initial conditions> $q(0)=q_0$, $i(0)=0$,
$$
q(t)=q_0e^{-\alpha t}\left(\cos(\omega_dt)+\frac\alpha{\omega_d}\sin(\omega_dt)\right),\qquad
i(t)=-\frac{q_0\omega_0^2}{\omega_d}e^{-\alpha t}\sin(\omega_dt).
$$
In the dimensionless example $\omega_0=1$, $\alpha=0.5$, the ringing <angular frequency> is $\omega_d=\sqrt3/2\approx0.866025$, below the driven <electric current> <resonance> at $\omega_0=1$. The <electric charge> first crosses zero at $t=4\pi/(3\sqrt3)\approx2.41840$ and reaches its first negative extremum at $t=2\pi/\sqrt3\approx3.62760$, where $q/q_0=-e^{-\pi/\sqrt3}\approx-0.163034$.

The sinusoidal factor changes sign while its envelope decays as $e^{-\alpha t}$. The total <energy> follows the exact balance $E'=-Ri^2$, rather than a pure <exponential decay> at every instant; in particular, its rate of loss is zero whenever $i=0$. As $\alpha\to0$, the displayed solution tends to the lossless <harmonic oscillator> $q=q_0\cos(\omega_0t)$. As $\alpha\to\omega_0$ from below, $\sin(\omega_dt)/\omega_d\to t$, recovering the <critically damped RLC response> continuously. The <overdamped RLC response> reaches the same limit through $\sinh(\beta t)/\beta\to t$.

= Gaussian units
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

= Capacitor
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A <capacitor> stores <electrostatic energy> by separating <electric charge> between two <electrical conductors>. In the ideal two-terminal model their <electric charges> are $+Q$ and $-Q$, with a vacuum or insulating gap preventing direct transfer between them. Write $V=\phi_+-\phi_-$ for the terminal <electric potential difference>, where $\phi_+$ and $\phi_-$ are the <electric potentials> of the two <electrical conductors>. A linear <capacitor> obeys
$$
Q=CV,
$$
where its <capacitance> $C$ depends on the geometry and <permittivity>, and is independent of the applied <electric potential difference> while these stay fixed. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: capacitors and capacitance]{ref}.

For example, the <parallel-plate capacitor> below has <capacitance> $C=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$. At terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$, the <electric charge> magnitude on either plate is
$$
Q=CV=(8.854\times10^{-11})(10)\,\mathrm C
=8.854\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm C=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}.
$$
The total <electric charge> of the device is $Q+(-Q)=0$: <charge neutrality> does not imply zero <capacitor energy>. This <capacitor> stores $4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}$ in its <electric field>. The schematic in <parallel-plate capacitor> shows the separation of <electric charge>; the plots in <capacitor energy> show how storage grows with terminal <electric potential difference>.

The useful distinction is between storing net <electric charge> and storing separated <electric charge>. A <capacitor> can release <electrostatic energy> into an <electrical circuit>; in an ideal <RLC circuit>, <energy> moves between the <capacitor>'s <electric field> and the <magnetic field> associated with <inductance>. An ideal disconnected <capacitor> retains its separated <electric charges> and <capacitor energy> because there is no path for discharge.

= Parallel-plate capacitor
{parent=Capacitor}
{title2=$C=\epsilon A/d$}

A <parallel-plate capacitor> consists of two parallel <electrical conductors> with overlapping <area> $A$ and separation $d$. If the gap is small compared with both plate widths and is filled by a uniform material of positive <permittivity> $\epsilon$, its <capacitance> is approximately
$$
C\simeq\frac{\epsilon A}{d}.
$$
This neglects the <fringing field of a capacitor> near the plate edges. The interior <electric field> points from the plate of positive <electric charge> to the plate of negative <electric charge>, and is approximately uniform. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: the parallel-plate approximation]{ref}.

For a worked vacuum example, stipulate $A=0.01\,\mathrm{m^2}$, $d=0.001\,\mathrm m$, and <vacuum permittivity> $\epsilon_0\approx8.854\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{F/m}$. This rounds the value in <Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>. Within the approximation,
$$
C\simeq\frac{(8.854\times10^{-12})(0.01)}{0.001}\,\mathrm F
=8.854\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm F=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}.
$$
At terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$, the <electric field> magnitude is $E\simeq V/d=10^4\,\mathrm{V/m}$, the plate <electric charge> magnitude is $Q=CV=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}$, and the <capacitor energy> is $U=CV^2/2=4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}$. These numbers use the stipulated rounded <vacuum permittivity>; finite plates also have corrections from the <fringing field of a capacitor>.

\Image[parallel-plate-capacitor-field.png]
{title=Uniform <electric field> in a <parallel-plate capacitor>}

The formula makes design changes easy to predict: doubling the overlapping <area> doubles the <capacitance>, and halving the gap doubles the <capacitance>, with <permittivity> fixed and the <fringing field of a capacitor> neglected. Doubling <permittivity> also doubles <capacitance> if the material uniformly fills the gap and remains linear. This lets a compact device store more separated <electric charge> at the same terminal <electric potential difference>.

The short derivation uses <surface charge density> $\sigma=Q/A$. In the interior, <Gauss's law> gives <electric field> magnitude $E\simeq\sigma/\epsilon=Q/(\epsilon A)$. The <electric potential difference> is $V\simeq Ed=Qd/(\epsilon A)$, so dividing $Q$ by $V$ gives $C\simeq\epsilon A/d$. A narrow curved <capacitor> gap has the same leading local approximation when its width is much less than the curvature radius.

= Fringing field of a capacitor
{parent=Parallel-plate capacitor}

= Capacitor fringing field
{synonym}

The <fringing field of a capacitor> is the nonuniform <electric field> near the edges of its <electrical conductors>, extending outside the gap. For a <parallel-plate capacitor>, neglecting it is useful when the gap is much smaller than both plate widths; the approximation $C\simeq\epsilon A/d$ is less reliable when these lengths become comparable.

Finite plates break the translational <symmetry> of the infinite-plate model. Each <conductor in electrostatic equilibrium> is an <equipotential>, so the <electric field> must meet a smooth conducting surface normally. Near an edge, the <equipotentials> curve into the surrounding space and the <electric field> changes both direction and magnitude. The <surface charge density> adjusts to enforce the conducting <boundary conditions>; it is not uniform all the way to a sharp edge. The <electrostatic boundary conditions at a conductor> explain why zero <electric field> inside the metal does not imply zero <electric field> just outside it.

The practical issue is where the nearly uniform region ends. A probe near the centre can support $E\simeq V/d$ while a probe close to an edge detects a lateral component and a different magnitude. A total <capacitance> reading also includes <electric flux> outside the simple footprint. The <fringing field of a capacitor> can therefore matter in <permittivity> measurements even when a central <electric field> measurement looks nearly ideal. Keysight's three-<electrode> fixture diverts the fringe contribution away from the measured response.<Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>[Solution: fringe capacitance and the three-electrode 16451B fixture]{ref}

\Image[capacitor-fringing-field-and-guard-electrode.png]
{title=Finite-plate <electric field> and a separate <guard electrode of a capacitor>}
{description=Left: original <bounded two-dimensional capacitor field model> with $d/W=0.25$, showing computed <equipotentials> and <electric field> direction. The visible window is a crop of the stated bounded domain. Right: a conceptual cross-section through a surrounding guard ring, with an insulated central sensing <electrode>; the arrows are schematic, not a computed guarded solution. Neither panel is measured data.}

= Capacitor gap aspect ratio
{parent=Fringing field of a capacitor}
{title2=$\eta=d/W$}

The <capacitor gap aspect ratio> compares the gap $d$ with a lateral plate width $W$. For rectangular plates of widths $W_x,W_z$, require both $d/W_x\ll1$ and $d/W_z\ll1$ for the leading <parallel-plate capacitor> approximation. Large <area> alone is insufficient: a long narrow strip can have large <area> and still have $d$ comparable with its smaller width. In addition, the observation point must be many gap widths from an edge. Reducing $d/W$ improves the interior approximation without making the edge itself disappear.

In a two-dimensional straight gap, write the <electric potential> as the infinite-plate solution plus a perturbation:
$$
\phi(x,y)=\frac{V}{d}y+\delta\phi(x,y),\qquad -\frac d2<y<\frac d2.
$$
The perturbation satisfies the <Laplace equation> and vanishes on the two ideal plates. <Separation of variables> in a semi-infinite strip, with $s$ the distance inward from its entrance, gives transverse modes
$$
\delta\phi_n(s,y)=a_n e^{-n\pi s/d}\sin\!\left(\frac{n\pi(y+d/2)}{d}\right),\qquad n=1,2,\ldots.
$$
Thus edge disturbances have a natural penetration scale set by $d$. Modes forbidden by a particular <symmetry> have zero coefficients; this expression is an explanation of localization in an ideal strip, not a universal numerical correction to finite-plate <capacitance>. The outside <boundary conditions> determine the coefficients.

\Image[capacitor-fringing-small-gap-aspect-ratio.png]
{title=<Capacitor gap aspect ratio> and the width of the nearly uniform <electric field> region}
{description=Three original solutions of the same <bounded two-dimensional capacitor field model> at $d/W=0.10,0.25,0.50$. The lower curves show $-E_y d/V$ on the midplane $y=0$; the dashed reference is the infinite-plate value inside the gap. The dashed value is not the infinite-plate prediction outside the plate footprint. All curves are calculated, with the same mesh and outer <Dirichlet boundary conditions>; they are neither measurements nor an exact open-domain three-dimensional <capacitance> calculation.}

There is no single aspect-ratio cutoff guaranteeing an assigned accuracy: the requested observable, plate shape and thickness, surroundings, <dielectric> distribution, and guard geometry all matter. The small-gap approximation is local and is not uniform across an edge. It also does not justify extrapolating $C\simeq\epsilon A/d$ to arbitrarily large $d$, where finite <electrical conductors> and their external <electric field> dominate. At fixed $V$, formally shrinking an ideal gap makes the interior <electric field> grow like $1/d$; real <electric currents> across an imperfectly insulating gap, surface roughness and contact eventually invalidate that ideal limit.

= Bounded two-dimensional capacitor field model
{parent=Fringing field of a capacitor}
{title2=$\partial_x^2\phi+\partial_y^2\phi=0$}

The diagrams solve an explicitly bounded <Dirichlet problem> in a uniform charge-free medium of constant <permittivity>. Take a width scale $W=1$ and terminal <electric potential difference> $V=1$. Two zero-thickness ideal plate segments occupy $|x|\leq W/2$ at $y=\pm d/2$, with <electric potentials> $\pm V/2$. Fix $\phi=0$ on the entire rectangle $x=\pm2W$, $y=\pm1.5W$. This boundary is an additional enclosing <equipotential>, rather than a condition at infinity. The model is invariant along the omitted third direction. The connection between the <electric potential>, its <gradient>, and the charge-free <Laplace equation> follows from <electrostatics>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[II.6–1, equations (6.3)–(6.6)]{ref}

On a square mesh of spacing $h=W/160$, the <finite difference method> imposes
$$
\phi_{i,j}=\frac14\left(\phi_{i+1,j}+\phi_{i-1,j}+\phi_{i,j+1}+\phi_{i,j-1}\right)
$$
at free nodes, while plate and outer nodes retain their prescribed <electric potentials>. There are $641\times481$ nodes. Each plotted gap aligns exactly with the mesh; the smallest gap has 16 mesh intervals. The generators solve the resulting <linear system> by the <conjugate gradient method>, stopping when the maximum dimensionless five-point residual is below $2\times10^{-10}$, and use <central finite differences> to approximate $\mathbf E=-\nabla\phi$ away from plate nodes.

The <discrete maximum principle> keeps the computed <electric potential> between $-V/2$ and $+V/2$. Reflection checks require evenness in $x$ and oddness in $y$; halving $h$ tests discretization effects, while enlarging the outer rectangle tests sensitivity to its artificial <boundary conditions>. These are different checks: a small residual only establishes that the discrete equations were solved. Differentiating near zero-thickness endpoints is especially sensitive to the mesh, so the plots do not report a converged edge peak. No total <electric charge>, exact open-domain three-dimensional <capacitance>, or measured material property is extracted from this model.

For a bounded numerical check, compare $h=W/80$ with the plotted $h=W/160$ at midplane positions $x/W=0,0.25,0.40,0.50,0.60,0.80$. At $d/W=0.10$, the largest change in $-E_y d/V$ is approximately $0.0265$, at $x/W=0.50$; the other five sampled changes are below $0.00472$. At $d/W=0.25$, moving the outer rectangle from $\pm2W\times\pm1.5W$ to $\pm3W\times\pm2W$ while retaining $h=W/80$ changes these samples by at most approximately $0.00622$. These calculated sensitivities support the qualitative comparison and expose its limits; they are not experimental uncertainties or evidence that the artificial boundary has converged to infinity.

= Mapping a capacitor fringing field
{parent=Fringing field of a capacitor}

The <Field Mapper Kit (PK-9023), Product Manual 012-04346D by PASCO scientific> (p. 3, Parallel plate capacitor) proposes a direct investigation of the plate-length-to-gap ratio using <conductive-paper electric potential mapping>. Its finite straight <electrodes> represent a cross-section of the plates. The apparatus is a gridded resistive sheet on a corkboard, silver-ink <electrodes>, pushpin electrical contacts, a $5$–$20\,\mathrm V$ direct supply capable of $25\,\mathrm{mA}$, and a <voltmeter> with input <electrical resistance> at least $10\,\mathrm{M\Omega}$. The manual specifies waiting 20 minutes for the ink to reach its maximum <electrical conductivity>, then checking each <electrode>: its internal voltage drop should be no more than $1\%$ of the applied <electric potential difference>.<Field Mapper Kit (PK-9023), Product Manual 012-04346D by PASCO scientific>[pp. 1–2: required equipment, drying, contacts, electrode-conductivity check]{ref}

For the readout, keep one <voltmeter> lead on the reference <electrode> and scan the other across the sheet. Mark points having the same reading to trace an <equipotential>. A second method holds the two probe tips at a fixed separation and pivots them to find the largest <electric potential difference>; a suggested separation is one centimetre. Avoid printed grid marks when making contact.<Charge, Equipotential, and Field Mapper (ES-9060), Instruction Manual by PASCO scientific>[pp. 8–10, Parts III–IV: equipotential tracing and paired-probe field-gradient mapping]{ref}

The inference follows from the measured <electric potential>, rather than assuming straight <electric field> lines. With probe separation $\Delta s$ and direction $\widehat{\mathbf t}$, a local <finite difference> gives
$$
\mathbf E\cdot\widehat{\mathbf t}\simeq-\frac{\phi(P+\Delta s\widehat{\mathbf t})-\phi(P)}{\Delta s}.
$$
An arrow toward rising <electric potential> follows $+\nabla\phi$, so it must be reversed to show $\mathbf E$. To investigate the <fringing field of a capacitor>, compare contours well inside the gap with contours near and beyond the ends, then repeat at a changed gap while keeping the sheet boundary and plate length fixed. Curving contours and an outside <gradient> establish nonuniformity in this planar setup. Compare $-E_y d/V$ at recorded positions to distinguish simple voltage rescaling from geometric changes; no measured values are supplied here.

Additional useful controls are repeat reference readings to detect drift, reversal of supply polarity, and repeating selected readings with reduced probe spacing. Keep contact quality and sheet edges fixed during the aspect-ratio comparison. Finite probe separation averages the <gradient>; reducing it improves spatial resolution but magnifies the effect of voltage uncertainty. <Voltmeter loading>, imperfect ink <electrodes>, and inhomogeneous sheet <electrical conductivity> can mimic distortions. These proposed checks supplement the manufacturer's electrode check, rather than claiming a completed experiment.

The sheet carries a steady <electric current>. For uniform <electrical conductivity>, <Ohm's law> and <electric charge conservation> give the two-dimensional <Laplace equation> away from <electrodes>, as explained in <conductive-paper electric potential mapping>. Uncontacted sheet edges have zero normal <electric current>, whereas the diagram model fixes zero outer <electric potential>. These distinct <boundary conditions> must be matched before a quantitative comparison. The apparatus reveals a planar <electric potential> map; it does not directly measure the free-space three-dimensional <capacitance> of metal plates.

= Guard electrode of a capacitor
{parent=Fringing field of a capacitor}

A <guard electrode of a capacitor> surrounds an electrically isolated sensing <electrode> and is held at the same <electric potential>. The small insulating slot separates their readout paths. The guard extends the nearly planar <equipotential>, moving the main outer edge away from the sensing region; it does not abolish the <fringing field of a capacitor> everywhere. In a capacitance fixture, fringe <electric current> is routed through the guard instead of the sensing readout. Keysight describes this three-<electrode> method in <Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials> (Solution, 16451B fixture).<Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>[Solution: guard absorbs current through fringe capacitance]{ref}

Measuring the sensing and guard <electric currents> together would include the diverted response again. Grounding an arbitrary surrounding <electrode> also does not reproduce a guard unless its <electric potential> matches the sensing <electrode>. Finite guard width, the insulating slot, plate misalignment and specimen air gaps limit the approximation. Use <capacitance measurement calibration> and the geometry appropriate to the sensing region when inferring <permittivity>. The source also warns that guarding changes the <electromagnetic field> unfavourably at high <frequency>, so the low-<frequency> electrostatic picture has a restricted range.<Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>[Solution: air gaps, high-frequency guard limitations, fixture compensation]{ref}

= Spherical capacitor
{parent=Capacitor}
{title2=$C=4\pi\epsilon_0ab/(b-a)$}

Two concentric conducting spherical shells of radii $a<b$ with charges $Q,-Q$ have an <electric field> only in the intervening vacuum: $E_r=Q/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2)$ by <Gauss's law>. Integrating gives a potential difference $Q(1/a-1/b)/(4\pi\epsilon_0)$, and hence the displayed <capacitance>. The <electrostatic energy> is $Q^2/(2C)$, either by charging work or by integrating $\epsilon_0|E|^2/2$ over the gap.

= Capacitor energy
{title2=$U=Q^2/(2C)$}
{parent=Capacitor}

The <capacitor energy> is the <electrostatic energy> stored when <electric charge> is separated. For a linear <capacitor> of fixed positive <capacitance> $C$, plate <electric charge> magnitude $Q$, and terminal <electric potential difference> $V=Q/C$, it is
$$
U=\frac{Q^2}{2C}=\frac12CV^2=\frac12QV.
$$
The zero is the uncharged state. Thus the <capacitor energy> is positive for nonzero $Q$, even though the total <electric charge> of the two plates is zero. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-3-energy-stored-in-a-capacitor[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.3: energy stored in a capacitor]{ref}.

For the <parallel-plate capacitor> example, <capacitance> $C=8.854\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm F$ and terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$ give
$$
U=\frac12(8.854\times10^{-11})(10)^2\,\mathrm J
=4.427\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm J=4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}.
$$
The plate <electric charge> magnitude is $Q=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}$. Doubling terminal <electric potential difference> with <capacitance> unchanged doubles the plate <electric charge> magnitude and quadruples the <capacitor energy>.

\Image[capacitor-charge-and-energy.png]
{title=Linear <electric charge> and quadratic <capacitor energy> versus <electric potential difference>}

The <electric charge> is a <linear function> of terminal <electric potential difference>, while <capacitor energy> is a <quadratic function>. The second curve in each plot uses twice the <capacitance>: at a given terminal <electric potential difference>, both plate <electric charge> magnitude and <capacitor energy> double. These relations let us calculate how much <energy> an <electrical circuit> can receive when a <capacitor> discharges.

A brief charging argument explains the factor $1/2$. If the plate <electric charge> magnitude has reached $q$, the terminal <electric potential difference> is $q/C$. Transferring a further amount $dq$ of <electric charge> quasistatically requires <work> $dW=(q/C)\,dq$. With the geometry and <permittivity> fixed, the <integral> is
$$
U=\int_0^Q\frac qC\,dq=\frac{Q^2}{2C}.
$$
Using $Q=CV$ gives the other two forms. This <integral> accounts for the gradually rising terminal <electric potential difference>; it is the <work> stored in the <capacitor>'s <electric field>, rather than a claim that all <energy> drawn from a fixed-voltage <battery> during charging ends up stored.

The field picture gives the same result. In the vacuum gap of a <parallel-plate capacitor>, the <electric field> has <energy density> $u=\epsilon_0 E^2/2$. Multiplying by the gap <volume> $Ad$ gives the <electrostatic energy>
$$
U\simeq\frac12\epsilon_0E^2Ad
=\frac12\frac{\epsilon_0 A}{d}V^2
=\frac12CV^2.
$$
For the worked example, $u=4.427\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{J/m^3}$ and $Ad=10^{-5}\,\mathrm{m^3}$ reproduce $U=4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}$. The <electrostatic energy> resides in the <electric field> throughout the gap, despite overall <charge neutrality>.

When <capacitance> changes, specify the constraint. At fixed plate <electric charge> magnitude $Q$, increasing <capacitance> decreases <capacitor energy> $Q^2/(2C)$; doubling <capacitance> halves it. At fixed terminal <electric potential difference> $V$, increasing <capacitance> increases <capacitor energy> $CV^2/2$; doubling <capacitance> doubles it. In the latter case a connected <battery> supplies additional <electric charge> and <energy>, and there may also be mechanical <work>. Use the <fixed-voltage capacitor energy balance> to account for all transfers before inferring a <force> from changing <capacitor energy>.

= Fixed-voltage capacitor energy balance
{parent=Capacitor energy}
{title2=$dU_{\rm field}=dW_{\rm mech}+V\,dQ$}

For a quasistatic change of <capacitance> at fixed terminal <electric potential difference>, the <capacitor energy> is $U=CV^2/2$ and plate <electric charge> is $Q=CV$. Electrical <work> supplied to the <electric field> by the <battery> is $V\,dQ$, so mechanical <work> done on the <capacitor> is $dW_{\rm mech}=dU-V\,dQ=-V^2dC/2$. Increasing the separation of a <parallel-plate capacitor> makes $dC<0$: mechanical <work> is positive even though <electrostatic energy> decreases, because electrical <energy> returns to the source. Conversely, when $dC>0$, the <battery> supplies $V^2dC$: half increases <capacitor energy> and half is available as mechanical <work> done by the <electric field>. Differentiating <capacitor energy> alone at fixed terminal <electric potential difference> omits this exchange with the <battery>.

= Electromagnetic interaction
{parent=Electromagnetism}

The electromagnetic interaction is the long-range interaction of electrically charged matter with the electromagnetic field. Its massless gauge boson is the <photon>.

= Electromagnetic radiation
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

<Electromagnetic radiation> consists of propagating oscillations of the <electric field> and <magnetic field> that carry <energy> and <momentum>.

= X-ray
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}

X-rays are high-frequency <electromagnetic radiation>, conventionally with wavelengths of roughly $0.01$ to $10\,\mathrm{nm}$. The precise wavelength convention varies with context. Hot accreting gas and energetic electronic processes can emit X-rays, whose short wavelengths make them useful probes of compact objects and atomic structure.

= Larmor formula
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{c}
{title2=$P=\frac{q^2\mu_0a^2}{6\pi c}$}
{wiki}

The nonrelativistic radiated power of an accelerated point charge is $P=q^2\mu_0a^2/(6\pi c)$ in <SI> units. Integrating its dipole radiation pattern over solid angle gives this result; relativistic motion requires the Lienard generalization.

= Liénard radiated-power formula
{c}
{parent=Larmor formula}
{title2=$P=\mu_0q^2\gamma^6(|a|^2-|v\times a|^2)/(6\pi)$, $c=1$}

= Lienard formula
{c}
{synonym}

The relativistic generalization of the <Larmor formula> uses the invariant proper-acceleration magnitude: $P=-\mu_0q^2 A^aA_a/(6\pi)$ in signature $(+---)$. In coordinate time this is the displayed expression. For perpendicular magnetic circular motion, $\gamma m|a|=|q|vB$, giving $P=\mu_0q^4(\gamma^2-1)B^2/(6\pi m^2)$. The statement concerns total instantaneous emitted power; directional radiation flux is not itself invariant.

= Mie scattering
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{c}
{wiki}

The electromagnetic scattering solution for a sphere describes the transition between <Rayleigh scattering> and <geometrical optics>. Grain-size-dependent efficiencies can produce structure in the <radiation-pressure coefficient>.

= Bremsstrahlung
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{wiki}

<Bremsstrahlung> is <electromagnetic radiation> emitted when a charged particle accelerates during interaction with another charged particle or field. The microscopic process $e^-+Z\to e^-+Z+\gamma$ creates a <photon>, while its inverse absorbs one. Thus it contributes to a photon-number collision source in the <photon number current>, unlike ordinary <Compton scattering>. <Thermal bremsstrahlung> averages this emission over a thermal distribution of charged particles.

= Thermal radiation
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{wiki}

Thermal radiation is <electromagnetic radiation> emitted by matter because of its temperature. Matter in <thermal equilibrium> emits a spectrum governed by its absorptivity, approaching the <Planck law> for a <blackbody>.

= Scattering efficiency
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{title2=$Q_{\rm sca}=\sigma/(\pi a^2)$}

For a <sphere> of <radius> $a$, the <scattering efficiency> is its effective scattering <area> divided by the projected <geometric cross-section> $\pi a^2$. It is a <dimensionless quantity>. For <Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere> with relative <refractive index> $m$ and incident <wavenumber> $k$,
$$
Q_{\rm sca}=\frac{\sigma}{\pi a^2}=\frac83(ka)^4\left(\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}\right)^2.
$$
At fixed <wavelength> and fixed <refractive index>, the <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> scales as $a^6$, whereas the <scattering efficiency> scales as $a^4$. Doubling <radius> multiplies the former by $64$ and the latter by $16$. An efficiency here is an effective scattering <area> relative to a reference <area>, rather than a fraction of all light in an arbitrary beam.

For a worked toy example, take $a=10\,\mathrm{nm}$, $m=1.5$, and $\lambda=550\,\mathrm{nm}$. Then $ka=2\pi(10/550)\simeq0.1142$ and $(m^2-1)/(m^2+2)=5/17$. Substitution gives <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> $\sigma\simeq1.234\times10^{-20}\,\mathrm{m}^2$ and <scattering efficiency> $Q_{\rm sca}\simeq3.929\times10^{-5}$. At incident <light intensity> $1\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$, the scattered <power> is approximately $1.234\times10^{-20}\,\mathrm W$. These are leading small-particle estimates, not a measured air-<molecule> cross-section.

= Rayleigh scattering
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{wiki}

<Rayleigh scattering> is <elastic scattering> of <electromagnetic radiation> by particles much smaller than the incident <wavelength>. In the stationary particle approximation, the scattered <electromagnetic radiation> keeps the incident <frequency>: its direction changes, rather than its color. The incident <electric field> induces an oscillating <electric dipole moment>, which produces <electric dipole radiation>. This is a useful example of how a microscopic response can explain a familiar large-scale sight, <why the sky is blue>. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node108.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Rayleigh Scattering: bound-charge response and wavelength dependence]{ref}.

The smallness condition for <Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere> of <radius> $a$ is $ka\ll1$, where the <wavenumber> is $k=2\pi/\lambda$ and $\lambda$ is the surrounding medium's <wavelength>. For ordinary materials with modest <refractive index>, the incident <electric field> then varies little across the particle, making the <electric-dipole approximation for radiation> useful. For very large <refractive index>, also check the internal size $|m|ka\ll1$ before applying the simple <electric dipole> model. Once these conditions fail, use <Mie scattering> for a <sphere> instead of extrapolating the <Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence>.

Historically, the 1871 paper https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Scientific_Papers/Volume_1/On_the_Light_from_the_Sky,_Its_Polarization_and_Colour[Rayleigh, On the Light from the Sky, Its Polarization and Colour: original-paper transcription]{ref} connected the color and <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> of scattered skylight with particles smaller than the <wavelength>. Its <dimensional analysis> anticipates scattered <light intensity> proportional to particle <volume> squared and to $\lambda^{-4}$. The original mechanical picture is historical; the modern explanation uses induced <electric dipole radiation>.

= Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere
{c}
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}
{title2=$\sigma=\frac{8\pi}{3}k^4a^6\left(\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}\right)^2$}

= Rayleigh scattering cross-section
{c}
{synonym}

Consider a fixed, homogeneous, nonabsorbing, nonmagnetic <sphere> of <radius> $a$ in <vacuum>. Let $m$ be its relative <refractive index>, a fixed <real number>; in <vacuum> this equals the <sphere>'s <refractive index>. Treat $m$ as independent of <wavelength> over the range being compared, neglecting <optical dispersion>. Air can be approximated by <vacuum> here because its <refractive index> is close to one. These assumptions describe a toy particle, rather than a literal model of an individual air <molecule>.

The <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> $\sigma$ is an effective <area>: the total scattered <power> is $P_{\rm sca}=I_{\rm inc}\sigma$, where $I_{\rm inc}$ is the incident <light intensity>. For $ka\ll1$ its leading approximation is
$$
\sigma=\frac{8\pi}{3}k^4a^6\left(\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}\right)^2,\qquad k=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}.
$$
The units are <area>, since $k^4a^6$ has units $\mathrm{m}^{-4}\mathrm{m}^6=\mathrm{m}^2$. This <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> measures scattering, not conversion of the incident <electromagnetic radiation> into <heat>. The definition follows https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node107.html[Fitzpatrick, Thomson Scattering: total and differential scattering cross-sections]{ref}.

To see the mechanism, the nearly uniform applied <electric field> produces an <electric dipole moment> with peak magnitude
$$
p_0=4\pi\epsilon_0a^3\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}E_0,
$$
where $E_0$ is the incident <electric field> peak <wave amplitude> and $\epsilon_0$ is <vacuum permittivity>. This is the <sphere>'s response in <electrostatics>, with its <permittivity> divided by <vacuum permittivity> equal to $m^2$ under the nonmagnetic assumption. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node42.html[Fitzpatrick, Boundary Value Problems with Dielectrics, equation 554: induced sphere dipole]{ref}. The averaged <electric dipole radiation> <power> is $P_{\rm sca}=\omega^4p_0^2/(12\pi\epsilon_0c^3)$, with <angular frequency> $\omega=ck$ and <speed of light> $c$. Dividing by $I_{\rm inc}=\epsilon_0cE_0^2/2$ gives the displayed <Rayleigh scattering cross-section>. Thus doubling <radius> multiplies the <electric dipole moment> by eight and the scattered <power> by $64$, provided the larger <sphere> still satisfies $ka\ll1$. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node95.html[Fitzpatrick, Electric dipole radiation: angular power and its integral]{ref}.

= Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence
{c}
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}
{title2=$\sigma\propto\lambda^{-4}$}

For a fixed <sphere> and fixed <refractive index>, both the <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> and <scattering efficiency> are proportional to $\lambda^{-4}$. Halving <wavelength> multiplies either by $16$, as long as the shorter <wavelength> still satisfies $ka\ll1$. If <optical dispersion> changes $m$, the material factor changes too; the pure fourth-power comparison then needs correction.

For equal incident <light intensities> at blue <wavelength> $450\,\mathrm{nm}$ and red <wavelength> $650\,\mathrm{nm}$, the same toy <sphere> gives
$$
\frac{\sigma(450\,\mathrm{nm})}{\sigma(650\,\mathrm{nm})}=\left(\frac{650}{450}\right)^4=\frac{28561}{6561}\simeq4.35315.
$$
Blue <light> therefore produces about $4.35$ times the scattered <power>. This compares the particle's response at two <wavelengths>; it does not assert that an actual sky has this ratio of observed blue and red <light intensities>.

\Image[rayleigh-wavelength-scaling.png]
{title=<Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence> for a fixed small <sphere>, normalized at $650\,\mathrm{nm}$}
{description=The curve is $(650\,\mathrm{nm}/\lambda)^4$ over $350$ to $750\,\mathrm{nm}$. Blue and red comparison points are marked at $450$ and $650\,\mathrm{nm}$. Fixed <radius> and nondispersive <refractive index> are assumed, with $ka\ll1$ throughout. This is a particle response curve, not a simulated sky <optical spectrum>.}

= Rayleigh scattering angular pattern
{c}
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}
{title2=$F(\theta)=(1+\cos^2\theta)/2$}

For incident <unpolarized light> and the isotropic <electric dipole> response of <Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere>, let $\theta$ be the angle between the incident beam's propagation direction and the outgoing direction. The <differential scattering cross-section> and its relative angular pattern are
$$
\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}=\frac{3\sigma}{16\pi}(1+\cos^2\theta),\qquad F(\theta)=\frac{1+\cos^2\theta}{2},
$$
where $d\Omega$ is an element of <solid angle>. Averaging over the two transverse directions of incident <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> gives this pattern. Forward scattering at $0^\circ$ and backward scattering at $180^\circ$ are equally strong: $F=1$. Sideways scattering at $90^\circ$ is weakest, but nonzero: $F=1/2$. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node107.html[Fitzpatrick, Thomson Scattering, equations 1283–1287: averaging the dipole angular pattern]{ref}.

\Image[rayleigh-angular-scattering.png]
{title=<Rayleigh scattering angular pattern> for incident <unpolarized light>, measured from the incident beam}
{description=The plotted radial coordinate is $F(\theta)$, a relative <differential scattering cross-section>, rather than the distance travelled by <light>. The incident propagation axis points right: $0^\circ$ is forward, $180^\circ$ backward, and $90^\circ$ sideways. Rotating this plane about the incident axis gives the three-dimensional pattern.}

The normalization is consistent because
$$
\int(1+\cos^2\theta)\,d\Omega=2\pi\int_0^\pi(1+\cos^2\theta)\sin\theta\,d\theta=\frac{16\pi}{3}.
$$
Thus integrating $d\sigma/d\Omega$ gives the total <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> $\sigma$. The plotted $F$ has maximum one; it is not itself normalized to unit <solid angle> integral.

For a single <electric dipole> driven by incident <linear polarization>, the <angular distribution of electric dipole radiation> instead has a $\sin^2\alpha$ factor, where $\alpha$ is measured from the <electric dipole moment> direction, parallel to the incident <electric field>. That direction is perpendicular to the incident beam. Confusing $\alpha$ with the beam angle $\theta$ would incorrectly predict zero forward scattering. Averaging the dipole patterns over incident <unpolarized light> yields the displayed $F(\theta)$.

= Why the sky is blue
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}

In a clear <terrestrial atmosphere>, <Rayleigh scattering> by air <molecules> redirects some sunlight toward an observer looking away from the <Sun>. Shorter <wavelengths> are preferentially scattered, enriching the scattered <light> in blue compared with the incoming sunlight. Along the longer atmospheric path to the low <Sun>, more short-<wavelength> <light> is removed from the direct beam, helping produce red sunsets. https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/blue-sky/en/[NASA Space Place, Why Is the Sky Blue?: scattered sunlight and red sunsets]{ref}.

Violet <light> has an even shorter <wavelength> than blue <light>, so the <Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence> alone cannot predict the perceived sky color. The incoming solar <optical spectrum> and the eye's differing response to <wavelengths> both matter; human vision is less sensitive to violet than to blue. https://www.weather.gov/fgz/SkyBlue[NOAA National Weather Service, Why Is The Sky Blue?: solar spectrum and visual response]{ref}. <Multiple scattering> and other atmospheric particles also affect the observed sky. Cloud droplets are generally too large for this simple <Rayleigh scattering> model: <Mie scattering> describes spherical droplets, with much weaker visible <wavelength> selectivity, while repeated scattering helps make clouds appear white. https://www.giss.nasa.gov/edu/icp/research/ppa/1997/reyes/[NASA GISS, Aerosols in the Atmosphere: particle size, Mie scattering and white clouds]{ref}.

= Right-hand rule
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

The right-hand rule fixes the orientation of a <cross product> and relates the circulation direction of an <electric current> to the direction of its <magnetic field>.

= Permittivity
{title2=$\epsilon$}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

<Permittivity> describes how an <electric field> and a material's <electric polarization> combine into the <electric displacement field>. In a static, local response with <linearity> and <isotropy>, and no <electric polarization> at zero <electric field>, the <constitutive equation> is
$$
\mathbf D=\epsilon\mathbf E,\qquad \epsilon=\epsilon_0\epsilon_r.
$$
Here $\epsilon_0$ is <vacuum permittivity>, while $\epsilon_r$ is the <relative permittivity>, a <dimensionless quantity>. The <SI> unit of <permittivity> is <farad> per metre, $\mathrm{F/m}$: multiplying it by an <electric field> in $\mathrm{V/m}$ gives an <electric displacement field> in $\mathrm{C/m^2}$. A larger positive <permittivity> lets the same <parallel-plate capacitor> hold more plate <electric charge> at the same <electric potential difference>.

The material contribution is <electric polarization> $\mathbf P$, the <electric dipole moment> per unit <volume>. The <electric displacement field> and <electric susceptibility> $\chi_e$ satisfy
$$
\mathbf D=\epsilon_0\mathbf E+\mathbf P,\qquad
\mathbf P=\epsilon_0\chi_e\mathbf E,\qquad
\epsilon_r=1+\chi_e.
$$
Substitution gives the first <constitutive equation>. The <electric field> here is the averaged, total field inside the material, not just the field of the external plate <electric charges>. The <continuum approximation> averages over many <chemical atoms> or <molecules>; locality means that the <electric polarization> at a position depends on the <electric field> there. Static equilibrium, fixed material conditions and sufficiently small <electric fields> are needed for this scalar proportionality. <Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course by Richard Fitzpatrick/Polarization>{ref}.

For an ideal <parallel-plate capacitor> completely filled by a uniform <dielectric>, hold the free plate <electric charge> magnitude $Q$ fixed and neglect the <fringing field of a capacitor>. Writing $A$ for the plate <area>, <Gauss's law> gives $D=Q/A$. Thus
$$
E=\frac{Q}{\epsilon_0\epsilon_r A},\qquad
P=\frac{Q}{A}\left(1-\frac1{\epsilon_r}\right).
$$
The <electric dipoles> point along the <electric field>. Their surface <electric charges>, described by <polarization density>, oppose the field from the free plate <electric charges>. For a hypothetical <dielectric> with $\epsilon_r=4$, the internal <electric field> is one quarter of its <vacuum> value, while $P=3D/4$. At fixed <electric potential difference> instead, the internal <electric field> stays approximately $V/d$ and the plate <electric charge> grows fourfold. These are different experimental constraints. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_10.html#Ch10-S3[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.10–3, equations (10.5)–(10.11): polarization surface charge, field reduction and capacitance]{ref}.

\Image[dielectric-polarization-fixed-charge-fields.png]
{title=<Electric polarization> and <electric field> reduction at fixed plate <electric charge>}
{description=An original schematic and normalized model comparison for a uniform <dielectric> with <relative permittivity> four. The arrows for <electric dipole moments> run from negative to positive <electric charge>; the two field contributions oppose each other. The bar values compare <electric displacement field> $D$, $\epsilon_0E$, and <electric polarization> $P$, which all have units $\mathrm{C/m^2}$. The schematic is not to microscopic scale.}

<Permittivity> is a useful material summary, not a universal constant of the material under every condition. <Temperature>, <frequency> and <electric field> strength can change the response. In a <linear anisotropic dielectric>, <permittivity> is a <tensor>, $D_i=\sum_j\epsilon_{ij}E_j$, so the <electric displacement field> need not be parallel to the <electric field>. At nonzero <frequency>, delayed <electric polarization> requires <complex permittivity> and may produce <dielectric loss>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_32.html#Ch32-S2[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.32–2, equations (32.5)–(32.8) and following paragraph: frequency dependence and phase lag]{ref}.

= Dielectric
{parent=Permittivity}
{wiki}

A <dielectric> is a material in which an <electric field> produces <electric polarization> while transport of <electric charge> is sufficiently small for the intended use. An insulating solid in a <capacitor> is an example; real <dielectrics> can still have <dielectric loss> and nonzero <electrical conductivity>.

= Electric susceptibility
{parent=Dielectric}
{title2=$\chi_e$}
{wiki}

The <dimensionless quantity> relating induced <electric polarization> to the internal <electric field> through $\mathbf P=\epsilon_0\chi_e\mathbf E$ in the static scalar model. It describes the material contribution; <relative permittivity> includes the <vacuum> term as well, $\epsilon_r=1+\chi_e$. Zero <electric susceptibility> gives $\epsilon_r=1$, not zero <permittivity>.

= Complex permittivity
{parent=Permittivity}
{title2=$\epsilon^*(\omega)=\epsilon'(\omega)-i\epsilon''(\omega)$}

For harmonic <electric fields> with time convention $e^{i\omega t}$, write $\mathbf D=\epsilon^*(\omega)\mathbf E$ for their complex amplitudes, where $\omega$ is <angular frequency>. The <real part> $\epsilon'$ describes the in-phase <electric displacement field>; the minus <imaginary part> $\epsilon''$ describes the loss component. The sign reverses with the opposite time convention. <Complex permittivity> can also be a <tensor> in a <linear anisotropic dielectric>.

= Dielectric loss
{parent=Complex permittivity}
{wiki}

Conversion of electrical <energy> into heat in a <dielectric> driven by an <electric field>. In the stated harmonic convention, a passive scalar response has $\epsilon''\geq0$ for positive <angular frequency>. A <dielectric capacitance measurement> measures a dissipative component as well as <capacitance>; leakage from <electrical conductivity> can contribute to the inferred loss.

= Dielectric relaxation
{parent=Complex permittivity}

Delayed adjustment of <electric polarization> after a change in <electric field>. A simple illustrative model lets the slowly adjusting part $\mathbf P_s$ obey $\tau\,d\mathbf P_s/dt+\mathbf P_s=\epsilon_0\Delta\epsilon_r\mathbf E$, with a positive response time $\tau$. A harmonic <electric field> then gives a contribution $\Delta\epsilon_r/(1+i\omega\tau)$ to <complex permittivity> divided by <vacuum permittivity>. This model is an assumption, not a claim that all <dielectrics> have one response time.

= Dielectric capacitance measurement
{parent=Permittivity}

The motivating observation is that inserting an insulating material between fixed <capacitor> plates increases its <capacitance>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_10.html#Ch10-S1[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.10–1, opening paragraphs: observed increase in capacitance with an inserted insulator]{ref}. To investigate this response, use two flat parallel metal electrodes with measured overlapping <area> $A$ and separation $d$, a uniform specimen filling the gap, and an instrument that measures <electrical impedance>. Record the <capacitance> with and without the specimen at the same small applied <electric potential difference>, <frequency> and <temperature>, after <capacitance measurement calibration>. An increase in corrected <capacitance> indicates a larger <permittivity>, provided the geometry is unchanged. A guard electrode reduces the contribution of the <fringing field of a capacitor>. <Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>{ref}.

For negligible <dielectric loss> in the <parallel-plate capacitor> approximation,
$$
C=\frac{\epsilon A}{d},\qquad C_0=\frac{\epsilon_0A}{d},\qquad
\epsilon_r=\frac{C}{C_0}=\frac{Cd}{\epsilon_0A}.
$$
An air reference approximates $C_0$; it is not an exact <vacuum> reference. For the following synthetic example, stipulate $A=0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$, $d=1.00\,\mathrm{mm}$ and the rounded <vacuum permittivity> $\epsilon_0\approx8.854\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{F/m}$. Then $C_0=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$ in this model, and <relative permittivity> four gives $C=354.16\,\mathrm{pF}$. A residual parallel <capacitance> of $20\,\mathrm{pF}$ would make an uncorrected sample/reference ratio $374.16/108.54\approx3.447$, rather than four. This additive error does not cancel in a ratio.

\Image[synthetic-dielectric-capacitance-frequency-response.png]
{title=Synthetic <capacitance> response to <relative permittivity> and <frequency>}
{description=Left: the ideal scalar <capacitance> and a hypothetical additive stray <capacitance> of $20\,\mathrm{pF}$. Right: a hypothetical <dielectric relaxation> model, $\epsilon_r^*=2+2/(1+if/f_0)$ with $f_0=1\,\mathrm{kHz}$, for the same geometry. The solid curve is the parallel <capacitance> $C_p=C_0\epsilon_r'$; the dashed curve is the loss component $C_0\epsilon_r''$, not an independently measured storage <capacitance>. Every plotted value is synthetic.}

At nonzero <frequency> $f$, measure both magnitude and <phase> of the <electric current> relative to the applied <electric potential difference>. Define $Y=I/V=1/Z$, where $Z$ is <electrical impedance>. For the stated <complex permittivity> convention, the ideal uniform specimen gives
$$
Y=i\omega C_0\epsilon_r^*=\omega C_0\epsilon_r''+i\omega C_0\epsilon_r',\qquad
C_p=\frac{\operatorname{Im}Y}{\omega},\qquad \omega=2\pi f.
$$
Thus a parallel <capacitor> and <electrical conductance> describe the readout at that <frequency>; a single scalar <capacitance> cannot describe both components. The diagram's assumed <dielectric relaxation> gives $C_p=265.62\,\mathrm{pF}$ and $C_0\epsilon_r''=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$ at $1\,\mathrm{kHz}$. These are model predictions, not observations.

Repeat the reference measurement after removing the specimen and vary the small applied <electric potential difference> to test drift and <linearity>. Record specimen thickness, electrode <area>, contact conditions, <frequency> and <temperature>. Air gaps act as additional layers in series and can lower inferred <relative permittivity>; roughness, uncertain thickness and the <fringing field of a capacitor> limit the geometric formula. At sufficiently high <frequency>, the fixture itself changes the <electromagnetic field>, so low-<frequency> guarding and a lumped <capacitor> model require reassessment. <Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>{ref}.

= Capacitance measurement calibration
{parent=Dielectric capacitance measurement}

Characterize the instrument and fixture before inferring <permittivity>: use the prescribed open and short measurements to compensate residual parallel response and series <electrical impedance>, and check a known reference <capacitor> or the required load standard. Keep cables and electrode geometry reproducible. An open fixture measurement is distinct from the empty-gap reference <capacitor>; blindly subtracting its entire <capacitance> would remove the desired reference signal. Compensation cannot determine an incorrectly measured specimen thickness or eliminate all specimen-dependent <fringing field of a capacitor> errors. <Open and Short Compensation and Z Port Extension>{ref}.

= Vacuum permittivity
{title2=$\epsilon_0$}
{parent=Permittivity}
{wiki}

<Vacuum permittivity> is the electromagnetic constant $\epsilon_0$ in <Coulomb's law> and the <vacuum Maxwell equations>. In classical <vacuum>, $\mathbf P=0$ and the <electric displacement field> is $\mathbf D=\epsilon_0\mathbf E$. A material's <permittivity> includes its <electric polarization> response; <vacuum permittivity> supplies the common reference, not a separate adjustable material property. Its <SI> unit is <farad> per metre.

For elementary calculations, <vacuum permittivity> is approximately $8.854\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{F/m}$. This is a deliberately rounded value, not an exact decimal or a statement of current measurement precision.<Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>[2022 CODATA adjustment, vacuum electric permittivity row: value and nonzero standard uncertainty]{ref} It sets the scale in <Coulomb's law>, $F=|q_1q_2|/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2)$ for two stationary point <electric charges> with separation <length> $r$ in <vacuum>, and therefore enters the reference <capacitance> $C_0=\epsilon_0A/d$ of a <parallel-plate capacitor>.

Since the revised <SI> took effect on 20 May 2019, the numerical value of <vacuum permittivity> in <SI> units is determined experimentally, rather than fixed exactly by the unit definitions. The numerical values of the <elementary charge> $e$, <Planck constant> $h$ and <speed of light> $c$ are fixed exactly. The <fine-structure constant> $\alpha$ is measured, and
$$
\alpha=\frac{e^2}{2\epsilon_0hc},\qquad
\epsilon_0=\frac{e^2}{2\alpha hc},\qquad
\epsilon_0\mu_0=\frac1{c^2},
$$
where $\mu_0$ is <vacuum permeability>. Consequently <vacuum permittivity> and <vacuum permeability> have experimental uncertainty, while their product is exact in <SI> units because $c$ is exact. This is a change in metrological status, not a new material dependence of <vacuum permittivity>.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>[pp. 123–125 (defining constants and alpha relation); section 2.3.1, printed p. 128 (ampere, epsilon0 and mu0); 26th CGPM Resolution 1, printed p. 194 (20 May 2019 effective date)]{ref}

= Electric charge
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

<Electric charge> is an additive, signed property of matter that determines its coupling to the <electromagnetic field>. Write a particle's <electric charge> as $q$, or a collection's total <electric charge> as $Q$; the <SI> unit is the <coulomb>, $\mathrm C$. A stationary test particle in an <electric field> experiences <electric force> $\mathbf F=q\mathbf E$, while motion adds the magnetic part of the <Lorentz force>. <Electric charge> both produces an <electric field> and determines the response to one. For two stationary point particles in <vacuum>, <Coulomb's law> makes like signs repel and opposite signs attract. The choice of which sign to call positive is a convention, used consistently: <protons> carry $+e$ and <electrons> carry $-e$, where $e$ is the <elementary charge>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_01.html[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.1: electrical forces, fields and the Lorentz force]{ref}.

Additivity means adding signed <electric charges>, rather than their magnitudes:
$$
Q=\sum_i q_i,\qquad Q=e(N_p-N_e)
$$
for ordinary matter containing $N_p$ <protons>, $N_e$ <electrons>, and electrically neutral <neutrons>. A toy collection with seven <protons> and ten <electrons> has $Q=-3e$, however much neutral material accompanies it. <Charge neutrality> means $Q=0$; it does not mean an absence of charged constituents. Nor does it guarantee an absence of <electric force>: the spatial arrangement of positive and negative <electric charges> matters, as illustrated by an <electric dipole>. This makes a tiny imbalance in an otherwise nearly neutral object physically important. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_01.html#Ch1-S1[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.1.1: neutrality, imbalance and residual electrical forces]{ref}.

<Charge conservation> states that a collection closed to transport of <electric charge> keeps its total signed $Q$. If $n$ <electrons> move from object A to object B without other exchange, then $\Delta Q_A=+ne$ and $\Delta Q_B=-ne$; the sum is unchanged. The amount of positive <electric charge> alone need not be conserved: creation of an <electron> and a <positron> adds opposite <electric charges> with zero sum. These bookkeeping rules let us distinguish separation or transport of <electric charge> from creation of net <electric charge>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_04.html#Ch4-S4[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, I.4.4: conservation of net charge]{ref}.

A simple motivating experiment uses two charge-producing paddles, a conducting pail on an insulating support, a grounded outer shield and a high-input-impedance <electrometer>. Zero the pail, then isolate it. Rub the initially neutral paddles together, keeping their charged surfaces away from other objects. Lower each deeply into the pail without touching: the <electrometer> gives opposite signs. Inserting both together tests cancellation. For a separate contact test, insert one charged paddle without touching and withdraw it: the reading returns to zero. Repeat after zeroing, this time touching the inner wall before withdrawal: a residual reading shows transfer of <electric charge>. Zero the pail and reinsert the paddle to test how much <electric charge> remains on it. PASCO describes these procedures in https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Basic-Electrostatics-Sys-Manual-ES-9080B1.pdf#page=13[Basic Electrostatics System manual, demonstrations 1A and 1B, printed pp. 13–16].

\Image[electric-charge-transfer-schematic.png]
{title=<Electric charge> redistribution versus transfer in a conducting pail}
{description=Original qualitative schematic for a positively charged inserted object. Without contact, <electrostatic induction> separates negative inner-surface and positive outer-surface <electric charges>, while the pail's total remains zero. Contact can leave net positive <electric charge> after withdrawal. Symbols are not particle counts; no measured readings are plotted.}
{source=electric-charge-transfer-schematic.py}

The crucial control is withdrawal without contact: a response during insertion can come from <electrostatic induction>, which redistributes the pail's existing <electric charge>. In the fixed-geometry approximation, the <electrometer> measures <electric potential difference> proportional to enclosed <electric charge>, with conversion set by effective <capacitance>; a voltage reading is not already a measurement in <coulombs>. Opposite readings and their cancellation test additivity and <charge conservation> within resolution. Leakage or accidental contact spoils that interpretation, and contact need not transfer all the paddle's <electric charge>. This macroscopic experiment cannot resolve <elementary-charge> steps: that requires the much smaller particles of the <oil drop experiment>.

= Elementary charge
{parent=Electric charge}
{wiki}
{title2=$e=1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$}

The <elementary charge> is the positive magnitude $e$ of the <electron>'s <electric charge>, not the signed <electric charge> of the <electron> itself:
$$
q_{\mathrm{electron}}=-e,\qquad q_{\mathrm{proton}}=+e,\qquad e=1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C\quad\text{exactly in SI}.
$$
Since the 2019 revision of the <SI>, this numerical value defines the <ampere> together with the definition of the second; $1\,\mathrm C=1\,\mathrm{A\,s}$. Thus today's numerical exactness is a choice of units, whereas measured <electric charges> still have experimental uncertainty. An <oil drop experiment> can test <charge quantization> and the apparatus calibration; it does not supply a new uncertain defining value of $e$ in today's <SI>. https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/41483022/SI-Brochure-9-EN.pdf[BIPM, The International System of Units, ninth edition, sections 2.2 and 2.3.1, the ampere]{ref}.

For ordinary isolated <chemical atoms>, <ions> and charged objects whose net <electric charge> is counted through <protons> and <electrons>, <charge quantization> gives $Q=ne$ with signed <integer> $n$. A neutral <chemical atom> that loses two <electrons> becomes an <ion> of <electric charge> $+2e$. A toy object with $10^9$ excess <electrons> has $Q=-0.1602176634\,\mathrm{nC}$. Conversely, the scale $1\,\mathrm{nC}/e\simeq6.24\times10^9$ explains why laboratory <electric charge> often looks continuous: billions of microscopic increments fit into a small macroscopic amount. Averaged <charge density> is a continuum description, not a claim that each particle has an arbitrarily divisible <electric charge>.

The ordinary-matter qualifier matters. In the <Standard Model>, <quarks> have <electric charges> $+2e/3$ or $-e/3$, and <antiquarks> have the opposite signs. <Confinement> means these are constituents of <hadrons>, not isolated fractionally charged particles collected by an <oil drop experiment>. The usual valence <quark> sums give $2(2e/3)-e/3=e$ for a <proton> and $2e/3-2(e/3)=0$ for a <neutron>. Thus <elementary charge> should not be defined as the smallest nonzero <electric charge> of every constituent in particle theory. https://pdg.lbl.gov/2022/reviews/rpp2022-rev-quark-model.pdf[Amsler, DeGrand and Krusche, Particle Data Group, Quark Model, sections 15.1–15.2 and table 15.1]{ref}.

The beautiful experimental idea behind the <oil drop experiment> is to determine a microscopic <electric charge> by watching a visible carrier move. In his 1913 paper, Millikan timed the descent and electrically driven ascent of individual oil drops, repeatedly changing a drop's <electric charge> through capture of air <ions>. His apparatus used parallel plates separated by $16\,\mathrm{mm}$, a roughly $5300\,\mathrm V$ source, and an optical scale to measure travel distances. He reported multiple relationships among a given drop's inferred <electric charges>. He also found that uncorrected <Stokes's law> gave a size-dependent inferred elementary unit, and introduced a pressure-dependent slip correction. These were measured relationships with calibration and model uncertainties, not exact readings of today's defined $e$. https://grundpraktikum.physik.uni-saarland.de/gpalt/Anleitungen/Ergaenzungen/Versuche/Millikan/Millikan%20-%20On%20the%20Elementary%20Electrical%20Charge%20and%20the%20Avogadro%20Constant.pdf[R. A. Millikan, On the Elementary Electrical Charge and the Avogadro Constant, Physical Review 2 (1913), 109–143, especially pp. 109–112 and 115–116]{ref}.

\Image[electric-charge-oil-drop-forces.png]
{title=<Oil drop experiment>: apparatus and terminal <force> balances}
{description=Original schematic for a negative drop between a positive upper plate and negative lower plate. The <electric field> points downward and the <electric force> upward. <Drag> opposes each direction of motion; <buoyancy> remains upward. Geometry and arrow lengths are not to scale, and no historical measurements are recreated.}
{source=electric-charge-oil-drop-forces.py}

A modern instructional implementation sprays low-volatility oil of known <density> through a plate aperture, illuminates the drops, and follows one through a calibrated optical scale. Measure the plate spacing $d$, <electric potential difference> $V$, air <temperature> and <pressure>. Time a fixed distance $L$ with the <electric field> off to get a terminal falling speed $v_f=L/t_f$. Then choose the field polarity to drive the same drop upward and measure $v_r=L/t_r$. Repeat timings at a fixed <electric charge>, and compare before and after an ion-capture event. The same drop is especially useful because its <radius> and <mass> need not be inferred anew for each charge state. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Millikan-Oil-Drop-Manual-AP-8210B.pdf[PASCO, Millikan Oil Drop Apparatus AP-8210B manual, introduction, theory and experimental procedure]{ref}.

Here is the leading continuum model, retaining <buoyancy>. Assume a drop shaped as a <sphere> of <radius> $a$, oil and air <densities> $\rho_o$ and $\rho_a$, air <dynamic viscosity> $\eta$, and <gravitational acceleration> $g$. Define the downward effective weight $W$ and positive <linear-drag> coefficient $k$ by
$$
W=\frac{4\pi a^3}{3}(\rho_o-\rho_a)g,\qquad k=6\pi\eta a,\qquad E\simeq\frac{|V|}{d}.
$$
At <terminal velocity>, the net <force> vanishes. Falling with no <electric field>, the upward <drag force> balances weight minus <buoyancy>. Rising with the <electric force> upward, the <drag force> reverses and joins the downward effective weight:
$$
kv_f=W,\qquad |q|E=W+kv_r=k(v_f+v_r).
$$
Consequently the uncorrected estimates are
$$
a=\sqrt{\frac{9\eta v_f}{2(\rho_o-\rho_a)g}},\qquad |q|=\frac{6\pi\eta a(v_f+v_r)d}{|V|}.
$$
The observed direction of response, with known field polarity, supplies the sign of $q$. A suspended drop gives the alternative balance $|q|E=W$, but it still requires a determination of $W$: balancing a drop alone does not determine its <electric charge>.

For an explicitly invented continuum example, stipulate $a=1\,\mu\mathrm m$, $\rho_o=900\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, $\rho_a=1.2\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, $\eta=1.8\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$, $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, $q=-5e$ and a downward $E=10^5\,\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}$. The balances predict $W\simeq3.69\times10^{-14}\,\mathrm N$, $v_f\simeq0.109\,\mathrm{mm\,s^{-1}}$ and $v_r\simeq0.127\,\mathrm{mm\,s^{-1}}$. Substituting those speeds recovers $|q|=5e$ within rounding. This checks the algebra; it is not a precision prediction for real micrometre drops, which need a slip correction.

The inference has two distinct parts. Compare inferred <electric charges> or charge changes with a common unit, seeking $q_i\simeq n_i e$ within uncertainty, rather than demanding exact ratios of rounded measurements. Then establish the absolute scale through <electric field>, <density>, <dynamic viscosity> and distance/time calibration. If the observed steps are all even multiples, their common spacing alone cannot exclude an unobserved smaller unit. Air motion, evaporation and mistimed crossings can alter inferred <electric charge> without an ion-capture event. Small <Reynolds number> supports <Stokes's law>, but does not by itself justify its no-slip continuum coefficient: a significant ratio of air <mean free path> to drop <radius> requires a slip correction. Millikan's comparison of different drop sizes and pressures addressed that systematic limitation; the modern PASCO manual also includes the correction. Agreement supports <charge quantization> for the observed carriers within experimental resolution, rather than proving a universal statement about every possible particle.

Multiplying $e$ by the <Avogadro constant> gives the <Faraday constant>, $F=N_Ae$, the <electric charge> per <mole> of singly charged carriers. This connects counting individual <electric charges> with bulk <electrochemistry>. https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/wall_2022.pdf[NIST, CODATA 2022 defining constants and Faraday constant]{ref}.

= Oil drop experiment
{parent=Elementary charge}

The <oil drop experiment> infers an oil drop's <electric charge> from its motion under <gravity>, <buoyancy>, <drag> and a calibrated <electric field>. Repeated changes of a single drop's <electric charge> reveal discrete steps; see the model, experimental procedure and limitations in <elementary charge>.

= Electrometer
{parent=Electric charge}

An <electrometer> measures small <electric potential differences> or <electric charges> while drawing very little <electric current>. The conducting-pail experiment in <electric charge> uses a voltage response to infer <electric charge> with a fixed effective <capacitance>.

= Electrostatic induction
{parent=Electric charge}

<Electrostatic induction> is redistribution of <electric charge> in response to an external charged object. An isolated <electrical conductor> can acquire positive and negative surface regions while its total <electric charge> stays fixed; see the contact control in <electric charge>.

= Charge neutrality
{parent=Electric charge}

A system is charge neutral when its total positive and negative <electric charge> cancel.

= Charge conservation
{parent=Electric charge}
{wiki}
{title2=$\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0$}

= Conservation of electric charge
{synonym}

= Electric charge conservation
{synonym}

<Charge conservation> says that the total signed <electric charge> of an isolated collection stays constant. More generally, a collection closed to <electric charge> exchange has constant total <electric charge>, even if <energy> crosses its boundary. This is the charge bookkeeping relevant to a <closed system> or an <isolated system>; isolation from <energy> exchange is not required. Positive and negative <electric charge> count with opposite signs. Creating or removing an <electron> and a <positron> together is compatible with <charge conservation>, because their combined <electric charge> is zero. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_04.html#Ch4-S4[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, The Feynman Lectures on Physics, I.4.4: net charge conservation]{ref}.

Imagine a fixed box initially containing <electric charge> $Q(0)=10\,\mu\mathrm C$. Stipulate incoming <conventional current> $I_{\mathrm{in}}=3\,\mu\mathrm A$ and outgoing <conventional current> $I_{\mathrm{out}}=1\,\mu\mathrm A$ throughout $0\le t\le2\,\mathrm s$, with no other boundary transport. Since one <ampere> is one <coulomb> per second, the stored <electric charge> grows at $2\,\mu\mathrm{C/s}$:
$$
\frac{dQ}{dt}=I_{\mathrm{in}}-I_{\mathrm{out}}=2\,\mu\mathrm{C/s},\qquad Q(t)=10\,\mu\mathrm C+(2\,\mu\mathrm{C/s})t,\qquad Q(2\,\mathrm s)=14\,\mu\mathrm C.
$$
If instead the outgoing <electric current> also equals $3\,\mu\mathrm A$, the stored <electric charge> remains $10\,\mu\mathrm C$ while <electric charge> passes through. These are stipulated transport examples; constant terminal <electric currents> alone do not guarantee a time-independent interior <charge density>.

\Image[charge-conservation-control-volume.png]
{title=Signed <electric current> through a fixed <control volume>}
{description=The dashed box is an accounting boundary. Outward <unit normals> point left at the inlet and right at the outlet: the respective contributions to outward <electric current> are $-I_{\mathrm{in}}$ and $+I_{\mathrm{out}}$. Arrow lengths are schematic.}
{source=charge-conservation-control-volume.py}

\Image[charge-storage-versus-steady-current.png]
{title=Stored <electric charge> with unequal and balanced terminal <electric currents>}
{description=Synthetic curves from the stipulated rates over $0\le t\le2\,\mathrm s$, with the same initial <electric charge>; these are not measurements. Balanced transport keeps total <electric charge> fixed, and is compatible with steady transport.}
{source=charge-storage-versus-steady-current.py}

Locally, <charge conservation> is the <charge continuity equation>, an instance of the general <continuity equation>:
$$
\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0.
$$
Here $\rho(\mathbf r,t)$ is signed volume <charge density> and $\mathbf J(\mathbf r,t)$ is <current density>. A positive <divergence> of <current density> means net outward transport from a small neighborhood, so its <charge density> decreases. For a fixed <control volume> $V$ with <closed surface> $\partial V$ and outward <unit normal> $\mathbf n$, the equivalent integrated balance is
$$
Q_V(t)=\int_V\rho\,dV,\qquad \frac{dQ_V}{dt}=-\oint_{\partial V}\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf n\,dA=-I_{\mathrm{out,net}}.
$$
The <surface integral> is signed: incoming positive <electric charge> contributes negatively to outward <electric current>, and outgoing positive <electric charge> contributes positively. Integrate the <charge continuity equation> over fixed $V$ and apply the <divergence theorem> to obtain this balance. Zero net boundary transport gives constant $Q_V$; the global and local statements describe the same <conservation law> at different scales. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_13.html#Ch13-S2[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.13.2: local and integrated charge conservation]{ref}.

In a steady transport state, <charge density> and <current density> are time-independent, so the <charge continuity equation> reduces to $\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0$. At an <electrical circuit> junction, choose each terminal <electric current> $I_k$ positive outward and assume the terminals account for all boundary transport. Then
$$
\sum_k I_k=-\frac{dQ_{\mathrm{junction}}}{dt}.
$$
<Kirchhoff's current law>, $\sum_k I_k=0$, follows when accumulation of <electric charge> at the junction is negligible. During storage, retain the same balance with its nonzero <derivative>; <charge conservation> continues to hold. This makes <charge conservation> useful for checking <electrical circuit> models and for tracking the charging of a <capacitor>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-3-kirchhoffs-rules[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §10.3: Kirchhoff's junction rule]{ref}.

The beautiful consistency with <Maxwell equations> is that the <electric field> and <magnetic field> already enforce this transport balance. For sufficiently smooth fields in vacuum <SI>, take the <divergence> of the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>, use that the <divergence of a curl is zero>, and substitute <Gauss's law>, $\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=\rho/\epsilon_0$. Constant <vacuum permittivity> $\epsilon_0$ gives
$$
0=\nabla\cdot\mathbf J+\epsilon_0\partial_t(\nabla\cdot\mathbf E)=\nabla\cdot\mathbf J+\partial_t\rho.
$$
The <displacement current> term supplies the part needed when <electric charge> accumulates. Historically, Maxwell added that term to extend the steady-current equation; see the existing <charge conservation from Maxwell equations> for this connection. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_18.html#Ch18-S1[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.18.1: Maxwell's added term and charge conservation]{ref}.

= Charge density
{parent=Electric charge}
{wiki}

Charge density is electric charge per unit volume, area, or length according to the dimension of its support.

= Linear charge density
{parent=Charge density}
{title2=$\lambda=dQ/d\ell$}

= Charge per unit length
{synonym}

Linear charge density is <electric charge> divided by length along a charged line, or locally $dQ/d\ell$. Its SI unit is coulombs per metre. For a uniformly charged long cylindrical conductor, it equals the total <surface charge density> integrated around the cylinder's circumference; it need not represent a charge literally supported on a one-dimensional line.

= Line charge
{parent=Charge density}
{title2=$\mu=dQ/d\ell$}

A line charge is a charge distribution idealized as lying on a curve, described by charge per unit length. An infinite straight line with constant density $\mu$ produces the radial <electric field> $E_r=\mu/(2\pi\varepsilon_0r)$ by <Gauss's law>, and a logarithmic <electrostatic potential>.

= Electric current
{parent=Electric charge}
{wiki}
{title2=$I=dQ_{\mathrm{cross}}/dt$}

An <electric current> measures how quickly signed <electric charge> crosses a chosen fixed <oriented surface> $S$. Choose its <unit normal> $\mathbf n$; positive <electric charge> crossing along $\mathbf n$ counts positively, while crossing against it counts negatively. If $Q_{\mathrm{cross}}(t)$ is the accumulated signed <electric charge> transported through $S$, then
$$
I_S(t)=\frac{dQ_{\mathrm{cross}}}{dt}=\int_S\mathbf J(\mathbf r,t)\cdot\mathbf n\,dA,\qquad Q_{\mathrm{cross}}(t_2)-Q_{\mathrm{cross}}(t_1)=\int_{t_1}^{t_2}I_S(t)\,dt.
$$
Here $\mathbf J$ is the <current density>; the <surface integral> collects its local normal contributions. Reversing the <unit normal> reverses $I_S$. The <electric current> is a signed <scalar> associated with the whole chosen <oriented surface>, whereas <current density> is a <vector> at each point. The <SI> unit is the <ampere>, one <coulomb> per second. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-1-electrical-current[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §9.1: transported charge and current]{ref}; https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-2-model-of-conduction-in-metals[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.2: current density and its surface integral]{ref}.

For a constant <electric current> of $2\,\mathrm A$ over a <time> interval of $3\,\mathrm s$, the transported <electric charge> is $6\,\mathrm C$. This need not be <electric charge> accumulating in the wire: in steady transport, <electric charge> enters and leaves. For a closed <oriented surface> with outward <unit normal>, <electric charge conservation> instead gives $I_{\mathrm{out}}=-dQ_{\mathrm{inside}}/dt$. Keeping transported and enclosed <electric charge> distinct connects <electrical circuit> bookkeeping to the local <continuity equation>.

= Ammeter
{parent=Electric current}

An <ammeter> measures <electric current> through a branch of an <electrical circuit>. Connect it in series; its own finite <electrical resistance> changes the measured branch's <electric current> unless that effect is negligible or corrected.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/10.4 Electrical Measuring Instruments>[Measuring Current with an Ammeter, Figure 10.35 and following paragraph: connection and loading]{ref}

= Galvanometer
{parent=Electric current}

A <galvanometer> detects <electric current> through a mechanical response, such as the deflection of a pointer.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/10.4 Electrical Measuring Instruments>[Analog and Digital Meters: current-driven pointer response]{ref}

= Conventional current
{parent=Electric current}

<Conventional current> uses the direction in which positive <electric charge> flows. A moving negative <electric charge> contributes <electric current> in the opposite direction to its <velocity>. Thus <electrons> drifting left produce <conventional current> to the right; the convention describes signed <electric charge> transport, without requiring positive <charge carriers>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-1-electrical-current[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.1: conventional current direction]{ref}.

= Ampere
{parent=Electric current}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm A=\mathrm{C/s}$}

The <ampere> is the <SI> unit of <electric current>. Since 20 May 2019 its definition fixes the <elementary charge> exactly at $e=1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$, with $\mathrm C=\mathrm{A\,s}$. This gives electrical measurements a reference based on a fixed <elementary charge>. https://www.nist.gov/si-redefinition/ampere-introduction[NIST, Ampere: introduction to the 2019 definition]{ref}; https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Constants/Value/e.html[NIST, CODATA value of the elementary charge]{ref}.

= Current density
{parent=Electric current}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathbf J$}

= Electric current density
{synonym}

The <current density> $\mathbf J(\mathbf r,t)$ describes the amount and direction of local <electric charge> transport per <area> per <time>. Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{A/m^2}$. Through a small <area element> with <unit normal> $\mathbf n$, the contribution to <electric current> is $dI=\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf n\,dA$: the <dot product> selects transport through the surface. A <current density> tangent to the surface contributes zero. If <current density> is uniform and perpendicular to a wire's transverse slice, its magnitude $j=|\mathbf J|$ satisfies
$$
I=jA,\qquad j=\frac IA\quad\text{with the normal chosen along conventional current}.
$$
Here $A$ is the <wire cross-sectional area>. For nonuniform <current density>, $I/A$ is only the average normal component, and the <surface integral> is needed. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-2-model-of-conduction-in-metals[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.2: local current density]{ref}.

Consider two separate uniform-flow cases, each carrying <electric current> $I=2\,\mathrm A$. A <wire cross-sectional area> of $1\,\mathrm{mm^2}=10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m^2}$ gives $j=2\times10^6\,\mathrm{A/m^2}$; doubling the <wire cross-sectional area> to $2\,\mathrm{mm^2}$ gives $j=10^6\,\mathrm{A/m^2}$. Equal <electric current> can therefore mean different local transport intensity. This distinction matters for <Joule heating>, whose local <power> per volume depends on <current density> and <electric field>.

\Image[current-density-and-wire-area.png]
{title=Equal <electric current> through different <wire cross-sectional areas> gives different <current densities>}
{description=Both cases carry $2\,\mathrm A$ with uniform normal <current density>. The transverse slices use the same spatial scale; the bars compare $j=I/A$.}
{source=current-density-and-wire-area.py}

For a toy <electron> transport model, stipulate mobile <electron number density> $n=8\times10^{28}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$ and <drift velocity> $\mathbf v_d=-10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m/s}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$, with the positive <unit normal> $\widehat{\mathbf x}$. Each <electron> has <electric charge> $-e$, where the <elementary charge> $e=1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$ is exact in <SI>. These are defined model inputs, rather than measured parameters of a particular material. https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Constants/Value/e.html[NIST, exact elementary charge]{ref} The <current density> and its magnitude are
$$
\mathbf J=n(-e)\mathbf v_d=j\widehat{\mathbf x},\qquad j=ne|\mathbf v_d|=1.2817413072\times10^6\,\mathrm{A/m^2}.
$$
For <wire cross-sectional area> $A=1\,\mathrm{mm^2}$ and constant transport over <time> $10\,\mathrm s$, this gives
$$
I=jA=1.2817413072\,\mathrm A,\qquad \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cross}}=I\Delta t=12.817413072\,\mathrm C.
$$
The two minus signs make <conventional current> point opposite to the <electron> <drift velocity>. A small <drift velocity> can carry an appreciable <electric current> because many <charge carriers> contribute; it does not specify the propagation speed of an electrical disturbance.

= Wire cross-sectional area
{parent=Current density}
{title2=$A$}

The <wire cross-sectional area> is the <area> of a slice perpendicular to the wire's axis. For a round wire of radius $a$, it is $A=\pi a^2$. It enters both the uniform-flow formula $I=jA$ for <current density> and the formula $R=L/(\sigma A)$ for the <electrical resistance> of a uniform <Ohmic conductor>.

= Charge carrier
{parent=Current density}
{wiki}

A <charge carrier> transports <electric charge> by moving: <electrons> are one example, and <ions> are another. For species $s$ with <number density> $n_s$, <electric charge> $q_s$ and mean <velocity> $\mathbf v_s$, the transport contributions add as $\mathbf J=\sum_s n_sq_s\mathbf v_s$. A stationary charged background contributes to <charge density> but not to this <current density>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-1-electrical-current[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.1: positive and negative carriers]{ref}.

= Drift velocity
{parent=Charge carrier}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathbf v_d$}

The <drift velocity> is a <charge carrier> population's mean <velocity> relative to its material's rest frame. It describes the net transport superposed on microscopic motion. For a single species, <current density> is $\mathbf J=nq\mathbf v_d$; for <electrons>, $q=-e$ reverses the direction. It is distinct from the propagation speed of an electrical disturbance. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-2-model-of-conduction-in-metals[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.2: drift and transport]{ref}.

= Charge carrier mobility
{parent=Charge carrier}
{title2=$\mu=|q|\tau/m$}

<Charge carrier mobility> is the magnitude of steady <drift velocity> per unit weak <electric field> in a scalar transport model. In the <Drude model>, $\mathbf v_d=\operatorname{sgn}(q)\mu\mathbf E$ and $\sigma=n|q|\mu$. The positive <charge carrier mobility> separates ease of motion from the sign of <electric charge>; its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{m^2/(V\,s)}$.

= Current dipole
{parent=Current density}
{title2=$\mathbf J^p(\mathbf r)=\mathbf Q\delta(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)$}

A <current dipole> idealizes a localized impressed <electric current density> by the displayed <Dirac delta function> distribution. Its moment $\mathbf Q$ has units of current times length. In a homogeneous <volume conductor> of conductivity $\sigma$, its primary <electric potential> is $\Psi(\mathbf r;\mathbf r_0)=\mathbf Q\cdot(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)/(4\pi\sigma|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|^3)$.

= Radial current dipole
{parent=Current dipole}
{title2=$\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0=0$}

A <radial current dipole> has moment parallel to its displacement from the sphere centre. The <Sarvas formula> gives identically zero exterior <magnetic field> for such a source in a <spherical volume conductor model>, although its <electric potential> need not vanish. This is a nonuniqueness mechanism for the inverse problem in <magnetoencephalography>.

= Four-current
{parent=Current density}
{title2=$J^\mu=(c\rho,\boldsymbol J)$}

The four-current combines charge density and <current density> into the <four-vector> $J^\mu=(c\rho,\boldsymbol J)$. With $x^\mu=(ct,\boldsymbol x)$, its <four-divergence> vanishes by the charge <continuity equation>: $\partial_\mu J^\mu=\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot\boldsymbol J=0$. It is the source in the covariant <Maxwell equations>.

= Surface current density
{title2=$\mathbf K$}
{parent=Current density}
{wiki}

A surface current density is electric current per unit transverse length confined to a surface. Integrating a thin layer's <current density> through its thickness gives $\mathbf K$.

= Current sheet
{parent=Surface current density}
{wiki}

A current sheet idealizes electric current as supported on a surface. It produces a jump in the tangential <magnetic field>.

= Force on a current sheet from the mean magnetic field
{parent=Current sheet}
{title2=$\mathbf f_S=\mathbf K\times(\mathbf B_++\mathbf B_-)/2$}

The <Lorentz force> per unit area on an ideal <current sheet> is computed using the mean of its limiting <magnetic fields>. The sheet's own local <magnetic field> is equal and opposite on its two sides and cancels in this mean. Equivalently, integrate the <Lorentz force density> through a thin regularized layer: its self-field contribution averages to zero, leaving the external field acting on the <surface current density>.

= Magnetic-field jump across a surface current
{parent=Current sheet}

For a surface current density $\mathbf K$ on an interface with unit normal $\mathbf n$, <Ampère's law> gives
$$
\mathbf n\times(\mathbf B_+-\mathbf B_-)=\frac{4\pi}{c}\mathbf K
$$
in Gaussian units, or $\mu_0\mathbf K$ in <SI> units.

= Surface conductivity
{title2=$\Sigma$}
{parent=Surface current density}

Surface conductivity is the conductivity of a thin layer integrated through its thickness. The sheet form of <Ohm's law> is $\mathbf K=\Sigma\mathbf E_\parallel$.

= Line current
{parent=Electric current}

A line current idealizes an <electric current> as flowing along a curve; its current-density volume integral reduces to $I\,d\ell$ along that curve.

= Electromotive force
{parent=Electric current}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathcal E$}

<Electromotive force> is the driving <work> per unit <electric charge> around an <electrical circuit>, measured in <volts>, despite its name containing "force". For a stationary loop its electromagnetic contribution is $\mathcal E=\oint_C\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l$; for a moving <electrical conductor>, include the <motional electromotive force>. A nonzero closed-loop <line integral> of the <electric field> cannot be represented by a single-valued <electric potential> alone. An <electromotive force> specifies the drive; the resulting <electric current> also depends on the <electrical resistance> and <self-inductance>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_17.html#Ch17-S1[Feynman lectures, II.17.1: emf as a circuit integral]{ref}.

= Motional electromotive force
{parent=Electromotive force}
{title2=$\mathcal E_{\rm mot}=\oint_C(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf l$}

<Motional electromotive force> is the contribution $\oint_C(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf l$ from an <electrical conductor> moving with local <velocity> $\mathbf v$ through a <magnetic field>. The <cross product> comes from the magnetic part of the <Lorentz force>. For a straight rod of length $\ell$, perpendicular to both its <velocity> and a uniform <magnetic field>, its magnitude is $B\ell v$. The sign depends on the direction of integration along the rod. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-3-motional-emf[OpenStax University Physics 2, §13.3: motional emf]{ref}.

For a computed example, take $\ell=0.50\,\mathrm m$, <velocity> $\mathbf v=2.0\widehat{\mathbf x}\,\mathrm{m/s}$ to the right, and <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=0.40\widehat{\mathbf z}\,\mathrm T$ out of the page. The rod lies along $y$. Then
$$
\mathbf v\times\mathbf B=-0.80\widehat{\mathbf y}\,\mathrm{V/m},\qquad
\int_{\mathrm{top}}^{\mathrm{bottom}}(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf l
=B\ell v=0.40\,\mathrm V.
$$
Positive <electric charge> is driven downward. For an isolated rod after charge separation has equilibrated, $\mathbf E=-\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$ inside it, and the <electric potential difference> is $V_{\mathrm{bottom}}-V_{\mathrm{top}}=+0.40\,\mathrm V$; the bottom is positive. This open-circuit statement does not imply a sustained <electric current>.

Close the rod's path with stationary U-shaped rails and an <electrical resistance> $R>0$ at the left. With out-of-page <unit normal>, the positive loop direction is counterclockwise, hence upward along the rod. If the rod's horizontal position is $x(t)$, the enclosed <area> is $A=\ell x$ and the external <magnetic flux> is $\Phi_{\mathrm{ext}}=B\ell x$. Thus
$$
\mathcal E_{\mathrm{CCW}}=-B\ell\dot x=-0.40\,\mathrm V,\qquad
I_{\mathrm{CCW}}=-\frac{0.40\,\mathrm V}{R}.
$$
Here the resistive approximation neglects <self-inductance>. The negative <electric current> is clockwise, downward along the rod, consistent with the charge-separation calculation and <Lenz's law>.

\Image[motional-emf-sliding-rod.png]
{title=<Motional electromotive force> from a rod sliding right through an out-of-page <magnetic field>}
{description=The U-shaped return path closes the <electrical circuit> to the left of the rod. Increasing <area> increases positive <magnetic flux>; orange arrows show the clockwise induced <electric current>. The rod's bottom end is positive in the isolated-rod limit. The <electromotive force> has magnitude $0.40\,\mathrm V$, or signed value $-0.40\,\mathrm V$ for counterclockwise orientation.}

The closed-loop result illustrates why the moving-circuit form of <Faraday's law of induction> includes <motional electromotive force>: the <magnetic field> is constant in time, so the <electric field> circulation alone would miss this drive. Its mechanical consequence is <electromagnetic braking of a sliding rod>.

= Electromagnetic braking of a sliding rod
{parent=Motional electromotive force}
{title2=$\tau=mR/(B^2\ell^2)$}

A conducting rod sliding on rails separated by $\ell$ in a transverse magnetic field has induced current magnitude $B\ell v/R$ when self-inductance is neglected and resistance is $R$. The <Lorentz force> opposes motion with magnitude $B^2\ell^2v/R$, so free motion decays exponentially on the displayed time constant. <Joule heating> integrates to the rod's lost kinetic energy. Current direction follows the <Lenz's law>.

= Magnetic flux transport for a moving circuit
{parent=Motional electromotive force}
{title2=$\frac{d\Phi}{dt}=\int_S\partial_t\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S-\oint_C(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf r$}

For a smoothly moving circuit and a divergence-free <magnetic field>, the <magnetic flux> derivative is $d\Phi/dt=\int_S\partial_t\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S-\oint_C(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf r$. The minus sign follows by orienting the swept side surface compatibly with the two spanning surfaces. Combining this transport identity with <Faraday's law> yields the moving-loop electromotive-force law.

= Electrical resistance and conductance
{parent=Electric current}
{wiki}

For an ohmic element, electrical resistance is $R=V/I$ and conductance is its reciprocal $G=I/V$. They describe the same voltage-current response using reciprocal units. The relation $V=IR$ is <Ohm's law>.

= Electrical conductance
{parent=Electrical resistance and conductance}
{title2=$G=1/R$}

For an ohmic element with finite nonzero <electrical resistance> $R$, its <electrical conductance> is $G=1/R$: an <electric potential difference> $\Delta V$ drives <electric current> $I=G\Delta V$. Greater positive <electrical conductance> means a greater <electric current> for the same positive <electric potential difference>. <Electrical conductivity> is instead a bulk material coefficient relating <current density> to <electric field>.

= Electrical resistance
{parent=Electrical resistance and conductance}
{title2=$R$}

<Electrical resistance> measures the <electric potential difference> needed to drive a given <electric current> through a component. For an <Ohmic conductor> under fixed material conditions, $R=V/I$ is constant and <Ohm's law> gives $V=IR$. The <SI> unit is the <ohm>, $1\,\Omega=1\,\mathrm{V/A}$. At a fixed positive <electric potential difference>, increasing <electrical resistance> reduces <electric current>. <Electrical conductance> $G=1/R$ expresses the same response reciprocally. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-3-resistivity-and-resistance[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.3: resistance and its unit]{ref}.

For a straight uniform wire at rest, with constant <electrical conductivity> $\sigma>0$, <length> $L$ and <wire cross-sectional area> $A$, the uniform <Ohmic conductor> model gives
$$
R=\frac{L}{\sigma A}=\rho_{\mathrm{el}}\frac LA,\qquad \rho_{\mathrm{el}}=\frac1\sigma.
$$
The <electrical resistivity> $\rho_{\mathrm{el}}$ characterizes the material; <electrical resistance> also includes geometry. Doubling <length> doubles $R$, while doubling <wire cross-sectional area> halves $R$, with material and <temperature> fixed. The uniform-field assumption includes insulated sides and equipotential end faces, as explained in <uniform current in a straight homogeneous wire>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-3-resistivity-and-resistance[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.3: wire geometry and resistivity]{ref}.

As a defined wire example, take $L=1\,\mathrm m$, $A=1\,\mathrm{mm^2}$ and $\sigma=5\times10^5\,(\Omega\,\mathrm m)^{-1}$. Then $R=2\,\Omega$; applying <electric potential difference> $5\,\mathrm V$ drives <electric current> $2.5\,\mathrm A$. With the same <length> and material, a second wire of <wire cross-sectional area> $2\,\mathrm{mm^2}$ has $R=1\,\Omega$ and carries $5\,\mathrm A$ at that same <electric potential difference>. Fixing <electric potential difference> here differs from fixing <electric current> in the <current density> comparison: stating what is held fixed is essential.

= Ohm
{parent=Electrical resistance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Omega=\mathrm{V/A}$}

The <ohm> is the <SI> unit of <electrical resistance>, one <volt> per <ampere>. A component of <electrical resistance> $2\,\Omega$ needs an <electric potential difference> of $2\,\mathrm V$ to carry <electric current> $1\,\mathrm A$ under <Ohm's law>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-3-resistivity-and-resistance[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.3: the ohm]{ref}.

= Resistor
{parent=Electrical resistance}
{wiki}

A resistor is an <electrical circuit> component whose intended behavior is <electrical resistance>. An ideal ohmic resistor obeys <Ohm's law>, $V=IR$, and converts electrical <energy> into heat at <power> $P=I^2R$ through <Joule heating>. It provides the dissipative term in a <series RLC circuit>.

= Ohm's law
{title2=$V=IR$}
{parent=Electrical resistance}
{c}
{wiki}

<Ohm's law> states that an <Ohmic conductor> has <electric current> proportional to its applied <electric potential difference>, with constant <electrical resistance> under fixed material conditions:
$$
V=IR,\qquad I=\frac VR.
$$
Here $V=\phi(A)-\phi(B)$ is the terminal <electric potential difference>, and positive $I$ enters at $A$ and exits at $B$. This sign convention makes $VI$ the electrical <power> absorbed. The straight-line relation through the origin lets one constant predict the response at many applied <electric potential differences>. Merely defining $R=V/I$ at one nonzero operating point does not establish <Ohm's law>: a component may have a ratio that varies with applied <electric potential difference>. Material and <temperature> must be specified and held fixed; many components have a nonlinear response. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-4-ohms-law[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.4: Ohmic and non-Ohmic responses]{ref}.

For the ideal <resistor> model $R=2\,\Omega$ at <electric potential difference> $V=5\,\mathrm V$,
$$
I=\frac{5\,\mathrm V}{2\,\Omega}=2.5\,\mathrm A,\qquad P=VI=I^2R=\frac{V^2}{R}=12.5\,\mathrm W.
$$
This <power> is converted to <internal energy> by <Joule heating>; a <watt> is a <joule> per second. If the model holds for <time> $10\,\mathrm s$, the transferred <energy> is $125\,\mathrm J$. Doubling <electric potential difference> doubles <electric current> and quadruples <power> for the same fixed $R$. The model keeps material <temperature> fixed even while calculating <Joule heating>; appreciable changes in <temperature> may require a changing <electrical resistance>. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/9.5 Electrical Energy and Power>[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.5: electrical power and heating]{ref}.

\Image[ohmic-wire-voltage-and-current.png]
{title=<Ohm's law> gives linear <electric current> and quadratic <Joule heating> <power> at fixed <electrical resistance>}
{description=The ideal model holds $R=2\,\Omega$ fixed. At $V=5\,\mathrm V$, the marked values are $I=2.5\,\mathrm A$ and $P=12.5\,\mathrm W$.}
{source=ohmic-wire-voltage-and-current.py}

In 1827, <Georg Ohm> published his mathematical account of electrical transport in \i[Die galvanische Kette, mathematisch bearbeitet]. On printed page 36, his relation $S=A/L$ expresses <electric current> in terms of total driving tension and a reduced <length> incorporating <electrical conductivity> and geometry. This is historical notation: his $A$ is not the <wire cross-sectional area> used here, and his $L$ is not simply the wire's physical <length>. The striking idea is that material response and geometry can be combined into one quantity governing an <electrical circuit>. https://archive.org/details/diegalvanischek00ohmgoog[Ohm, Die galvanische Kette, mathematisch bearbeitet (1827), p. 36, original edition]{ref}.

= Georg Ohm
{parent=Ohm's law}
{c}
{wiki}

Author of the 1827 treatise developing the electrical transport relation now called <Ohm's law>. https://archive.org/details/diegalvanischek00ohmgoog[Ohm, original 1827 treatise]{ref}.

= Electrical conductivity
{parent=Ohm's law}
{title2=$\sigma$}

<Electrical conductivity> $\sigma$ measures the local response of <current density> to <electric field>. In the rest frame of an <isotropic> <Ohmic conductor>, the local form of <Ohm's law> is
$$
\mathbf J=\sigma\mathbf E,\qquad [\sigma]=\frac{\mathrm{A/m^2}}{\mathrm{V/m}}=(\Omega\,\mathrm m)^{-1}.
$$
Larger positive <electrical conductivity> gives larger <current density> at the same <electric field>. It is a bulk material coefficient, distinct from the geometry-dependent <electrical conductance> of a whole component and from a sheet's <surface conductivity>. <Electrical resistivity> is $\rho_{\mathrm{el}}=1/\sigma$ in this scalar model. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-3-resistivity-and-resistance[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.3: conductivity and resistivity]{ref}.

The local and whole-wire forms fit together particularly neatly. For <uniform current in a straight homogeneous wire>, with <length> $L$, <wire cross-sectional area> $A$, constant <electrical conductivity> and terminal <electric potential difference> $V$, the axial <electric field> has magnitude $E=V/L$. Thus
$$
I=jA=\sigma EA=\frac{\sigma A}{L}V,\qquad R=\frac{L}{\sigma A}.
$$
For the defined <electrical resistance> example $\sigma=5\times10^5\,(\Omega\,\mathrm m)^{-1}$, $L=1\,\mathrm m$, $A=10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and $V=5\,\mathrm V$, the local values are $E=5\,\mathrm{V/m}$ and $j=2.5\times10^6\,\mathrm{A/m^2}$; integrating gives $I=2.5\,\mathrm A$. One material law therefore supplies both a spatial description and a terminal relation.

The scalar form requires <isotropy>. In an <anisotropic> material the response can instead be a <tensor>, $J_i=\sum_j\sigma_{ij}E_j$, and <current density> need not be parallel to <electric field>; <uniaxial conductivity tensor> is one existing example. These statements concern the material's rest frame. A moving <electrical conductor> instead uses the <moving-conductor Ohm law>.

= Complex conductivity
{parent=Electrical conductivity}
{title2=$\widehat{\mathbf J}(\omega)=\sigma(\omega)\widehat{\mathbf E}(\omega)$}

<Complex conductivity> relates the <complex amplitudes> of <current density> and <electric field> at an <angular frequency> $\omega$ in sinusoidal response. Its <modulus> gives the amplitude ratio, while its <argument> encodes their relative <phase>. Both the exponential time convention and whether amplitudes are peak or <root mean square> values must be stated when interpreting <phase> or average <power>.

= Electrical resistivity
{parent=Electrical conductivity}
{title2=$\rho_{\rm el}=1/\sigma$}

Electrical resistivity is the inverse of scalar <electrical conductivity> in an isotropic Ohmic medium. In its rest frame, $\mathbf E=\rho_{\rm el}\mathbf j$, so the <electric field> does work per unit volume $\mathbf j\cdot\mathbf E=\rho_{\rm el}|\mathbf j|^2$. In <Gaussian units>, finite conductivity gives <magnetic diffusivity> $\eta=c^2/(4\pi\sigma)$; neglecting this diffusion is a distinct approximation from neglecting electron inertia in <Hall magnetohydrodynamics>.

= Uniaxial conductivity tensor
{parent=Electrical conductivity}
{title2=$\sigma=\alpha I+\gamma nn^{\mathsf T},\quad|n|=1$}

A uniaxial <electrical conductivity> has one distinguished direction $n$ and equal response in its orthogonal plane. Its eigenvalues are $\alpha$ in $n^\perp$ and $\alpha+\gamma$ along $n$, so it is invertible precisely when $\alpha(\alpha+\gamma)\ne0$. For an <electric field> perpendicular to $n$, the <electric current density> is $J=\alpha E$. Positive dissipation requires both eigenvalues to be nonnegative, a physical constraint separate from algebraic invertibility. For $D_{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}n_k$, one has $D^2=nn^{\mathsf T}-I$, and $\sigma D^2=-\sigma$ is equivalent to $\gamma=-\alpha$; the conductivity then vanishes along $n$.

= Electrical conductor
{parent=Electrical conductivity}
{wiki}

An electrical conductor supports mobile charge carriers and hence an electric current under a field. In a band description, a partially filled band or available overlapping bands supply nearby accessible states. The contrast with a <band insulator> depends on band filling as well as spectral gaps; <semiconductors> provide carriers through thermal excitation or doping.

= Ohmic conductor
{c}
{parent=Electrical conductivity}
{title2=$\mathbf J=\sigma\mathbf E$}

A material whose <current density> is proportional to its <electric field>, with proportionality given by its <electrical conductivity>.

= Uniform current in a straight homogeneous wire
{parent=Ohmic conductor}
{title2=$\mathbf j=I\mathbf e_z/(\pi a^2)$}

In a homogeneous <Ohmic conductor>, a steady <electric current density> obeys $\nabla\cdot\mathbf j=0$ and $\nabla\times\mathbf j=0$. Uniform axial current in a straight cylinder follows if its side is insulated and its flat end faces are equipotentials. The <electric potential> then solves <Laplace's equation> with those boundary conditions, and uniqueness makes it linear in the axial coordinate. Steadiness alone does not impose these contact conditions: nonuniform end potentials can produce nonuniform current even with an insulating side. Bending also generally redistributes current; in an annular conductor the azimuthal current density is proportional to the inverse radius.

= Charge relaxation
{parent=Ohmic conductor}
{title2=$\tau=\epsilon/\sigma$}

In a homogeneous <Ohmic conductor>, the <charge continuity equation> and <Gauss's law> imply exponential decay of the bulk <charge density>, $\rho(t)=\rho(0)e^{-\sigma t/\epsilon}$. For an isolated finite body, bulk charge moves to its boundary; the accumulating <surface charge density> preserves total <electric charge>. The formula uses a constant permittivity $\epsilon$ and the corresponding free-charge convention, or $\epsilon_0$ for total charge in the vacuum form of <Gauss's law>.

= Charge relaxation in an isolated conducting cylinder
{parent=Charge relaxation}
{title2=$\rho(t)=\rho_0e^{-\sigma t/\epsilon_0}$}

A uniform cylindrical volume charge relaxes exponentially under <Ohm's law> and the <continuity equation for electric charge>. In a cylinder of radius $R$ initially carrying no surface charge, the radial conduction current deposits $\rho_s(t)=R\rho_0(1-e^{-\sigma t/\epsilon_0})/2$ on its surface. Total charge per unit length remains $\pi R^2\rho_0$, so the exterior radial <electric field> is time independent even though the interior field decays. Neglecting the deposited surface charge violates charge conservation.

= Drude model
{c}
{parent=Electrical conductivity}

The <Drude model> explains finite <electrical conductivity> by balancing <charge carrier> <acceleration> in an <electric field> against loss of directed <momentum> through <collisions>. Its useful surprise is that continuous microscopic motion can produce a constant macroscopic <current density>: the applied <electric field> continually restores the directed <momentum> lost to the material. The simplest version has one mobile species, constant <number density> $n$, <electric charge> $q$, <mass> $m>0$, and <momentum relaxation time> $\tau>0$, in a stationary, homogeneous, <isotropic> material. At zero <magnetic field>, its mean <drift velocity> and <current density> obey
$$
m\frac{d\mathbf v_d}{dt}=q\mathbf E(t)-\frac{m\mathbf v_d}{\tau},\qquad
\mathbf J=nq\mathbf v_d,\qquad
\tau\frac{d\mathbf J}{dt}+\mathbf J=\sigma_0\mathbf E(t),\qquad
\sigma_0=\frac{nq^2\tau}{m}.
$$
For <electrons>, $q=-e$ with positive <elementary charge> $e$, so $\sigma_0=ne^2\tau/m$. The <electron> <drift velocity> points against a constant <electric field>, whereas <conventional current> points along it. Random microscopic <velocities> can have zero mean without being zero individually; <drift velocity> is this population mean, rather than each particle's speed.<Lecture notes in Solid State 3: Classical free electron model for metals: The Drude model by Eytan Grosfeld>[pp. 2–3, Eqs. (1.2)–(1.11): collision averaging, electron force, drift and conductivity]{ref}

The same <first-order linear differential equation> predicts both <Drude current relaxation> after switching an <electric field> and the <Drude frequency response> to a sinusoidal <electric field>. Together they let a measured <electrical conductivity> be interpreted in terms of a density, a <mass>, and a time scale; a single constant-field measurement determines only their combination $nq^2\tau/m$.

The original 1900 paper <Zur Elektronentheorie der Metalle by Paul Drude> sought a common account of electrical transport and <heat transfer> using mobile charged particles and ideas from the <kinetic theory> of gases. Its introduction, especially printed pp. 568–569, explains that comparing different transport phenomena could constrain the populations of free and bound charged particles. The modern <momentum relaxation time> formulation above is a useful presentation of the model, not a verbatim translation of the original notation or its historical assumptions about <mass>.<Zur Elektronentheorie der Metalle by Paul Drude>[pp. 568–569: motivation for combining transport phenomena; OCR has spelling and formula errors]{ref}

= Momentum relaxation time
{parent=Drude model}
{title2=$\tau$}

The <momentum relaxation time> is the <exponential relaxation time> of mean directed <momentum> after the driving <electric field> is removed. In the simplest <Drude model>, independent <collisions> occur with probability $dt/\tau$ during a short <time> interval $dt$ and reset the mean <velocity> of the collided population to zero. The remaining fraction preserves its previous mean <momentum>. To first order in $dt$, averaging gives $d\langle\mathbf p\rangle/dt=q\mathbf E-\langle\mathbf p\rangle/\tau$. This is a population equation: it does not assert that every particle stops at equally spaced instants.<Lecture notes in Solid State 3: Classical free electron model for metals: The Drude model by Eytan Grosfeld>[pp. 2–3, Eqs. (1.2)–(1.4): constant collision probability and vanishing post-collision mean momentum]{ref}

For real materials, a <collision> that changes a particle's direction only slightly need not erase its directed <momentum>, so the <momentum relaxation time> need not equal the time between all scattering events. This transport time is also distinct from the bulk <charge relaxation> time $\epsilon/\sigma$: one describes loss of directed motion, the other redistribution of <charge density>.

= Drude current relaxation
{c}
{parent=Drude model}
{title2=$\mathbf J(t)=\sigma_0\mathbf E_0+[\mathbf J(0)-\sigma_0\mathbf E_0]e^{-t/\tau}$}

Switch on a constant <electric field> $\mathbf E_0$ at $t=0$. For prescribed initial <current density> $\mathbf J(0)$, the exact <Drude model> solution is the displayed formula. For <electrons>, the corresponding <drift velocity> is
$$
\mathbf v_d(t)=-\frac{e\tau}{m}\mathbf E_0+
\left[\mathbf v_d(0)+\frac{e\tau}{m}\mathbf E_0\right]e^{-t/\tau}.
$$
Starting from zero <drift velocity>, the <current density> reaches $1-e^{-1}\approx0.632$ of its limiting value after one <momentum relaxation time>. Switching the <electric field> off at $T$ instead gives $\mathbf J(t)=\mathbf J(T)e^{-(t-T)/\tau}$ for $t\geq T$. A finite step of <electric field> changes the acceleration immediately but leaves <drift velocity> and <current density> continuous.

\Image[drude-electron-drift-current-transient.png]
{title=Exact <Drude current relaxation> during and after an <electric field> pulse}
{description=The applied <electric field> is $E_0\widehat{\mathbf x}$ for $0<t<3\tau$ and zero otherwise, with initially zero <current density>. The blue curve is $J_x/(\sigma_0E_0)$; the orange curve is $v_{d,x}/v_*$ for <electrons>, where $v_*=eE_0\tau/m>0$. The opposite signs implement <conventional current>. These are calculated model curves.}
{source=drude-electron-drift-current-transient.py}

More generally, multiplying the <current density> equation by an <integrating factor> gives
$$
\mathbf J(t)=e^{-(t-t_0)/\tau}\mathbf J(t_0)+\frac{nq^2}{m}\int_{t_0}^{t}e^{-(t-s)/\tau}\mathbf E(s)\,ds.
$$
This is the <causal response of a first-order relaxation equation>: only earlier <electric fields> enter the <integral>. It also shows why instantaneous <Ohm's law> is an approximation when the <electric field> changes appreciably during a <momentum relaxation time>.

= Drude frequency response
{c}
{parent=Drude model}
{title2=$\sigma(\omega)=\sigma_0/(1-i\omega\tau)$}

Use physical <electric fields> $\mathbf E(t)=\operatorname{Re}[\widehat{\mathbf E}e^{-i\omega t}]$ and <current densities> $\mathbf J(t)=\operatorname{Re}[\widehat{\mathbf J}e^{-i\omega t}]$, with peak <complex amplitudes>. After the initial transient has decayed, substitution into the <Drude model> equation gives the <complex conductivity>
$$
\sigma(\omega)=\frac{ne^2\tau/m}{1-i\omega\tau},\qquad
\operatorname{Re}\sigma=\frac{\sigma_0}{1+\omega^2\tau^2},\qquad
\operatorname{Im}\sigma=\frac{\sigma_0\omega\tau}{1+\omega^2\tau^2}.
$$
Here the mobile species is <electrons>. With the alternative $e^{+i\omega t}$ convention, the denominator is $1+i\omega\tau$ and the <imaginary part> changes sign; the physical response is identical.<Lecture notes on the electrodynamics of metals and superconductors>[p. 2, Eqs. (1)–(11), and pp. 5–6, Eqs. (23)–(27)]{ref}

For $E_x(t)=E_0\cos\omega t$ with $\omega>0$, the real-time <current density> is
$$
J_x(t)=\frac{\sigma_0E_0}{\sqrt{1+\omega^2\tau^2}}\cos(\omega t-\phi),\qquad
\phi=\arctan(\omega\tau).
$$
Thus a positive <argument> of <complex conductivity> in the $e^{-i\omega t}$ convention means that <current density> lags the <electric field>. At $\omega\tau=1$, its <real part> and <imaginary part> are both $\sigma_0/2$, its <modulus> is $\sigma_0/\sqrt2$, and the lag is $\pi/4$.

\Image[drude-complex-conductivity-frequency-response.png]
{title=<Drude frequency response>: dissipative component, amplitude, and <phase> lag}
{description=Exact dimensionless <Drude model> curves with $e^{-i\omega t}$ time dependence. The <real part> controls average electrical <power> absorption; the positive <imaginary part> describes delayed <current density>. The dotted line marks $\omega\tau=1$.}
{source=drude-complex-conductivity-frequency-response.py}

For $\omega\tau\ll1$, <complex conductivity> tends to the positive constant $\sigma_0$: <current density> nearly follows the <electric field>. For $\omega\tau\gg1$ within this model,
$$
\sigma(\omega)\sim\frac{i ne^2}{m\omega},\qquad
\operatorname{Re}\sigma\sim\frac{ne^2}{m\omega^2\tau},\qquad
\phi\longrightarrow\frac{\pi}{2}.
$$
The <current density> amplitude decreases and its response becomes predominantly inertial. Average <power> absorbed per volume is $\overline p=\tfrac12\operatorname{Re}\sigma\,|\widehat{\mathbf E}|^2\geq0$. These limits describe the material response to a specified local <electric field>, not the complete electromagnetic response of a finite specimen with contacts. The <Drude response> has its pole at $\omega=-i/\tau$, consistent with the decaying <causal response of a first-order relaxation equation>.

The simple <Drude model> does not predict the microscopic <momentum relaxation time>. It neglects <electronic band structure> and <Fermi-Dirac statistics>; a useful semiclassical extension replaces $m$ by an appropriate <band effective mass>, but neither a single scalar <mass> nor one constant <momentum relaxation time> describes every material. Several <charge carriers>, energy-dependent scattering, and transitions between electronic bands can spoil the simple <Drude frequency response>. A successful low-<angular-frequency> fit does not justify extrapolation to arbitrarily large <angular frequencies>.

= Drude conductivity and Hall experiment
{c}
{parent=Drude model}

A motivating laboratory realization is <The Hall Effect and Conductivity in Germanium by Rex Tayloe>. The handout specifies a germanium sample holder with <temperature> and <magnetic field> control, a low-voltage supply, digital meters, and a current-reversing switch. Measure the sample dimensions, map the contacts, and establish the directions of <electric current>, <magnetic field>, and transverse voltage. At fixed <temperature> and zero <magnetic field>, sweep positive and negative <electric currents> and record longitudinal <electric potential differences>. A straight voltage-versus-<electric-current> relation tests <Ohm's law>; sample geometry converts its <slope> to <electrical resistivity>. For the <Hall effect>, minimize the zero-field transverse offset and measure transverse voltage at all four combinations of $\pm I$ and $\pm B$, repeating for several <electric currents>. The readouts test longitudinal proportionality, transverse polarity, and transverse proportionality to <electric current>. These are protocol instructions, not reported measurement outcomes.<The Hall Effect and Conductivity in Germanium by Rex Tayloe>[pp. 1–3, Equipment, Fig. 1, and At room temperature: conductivity sweep and four polarity combinations]{ref}

For interpreting such measurements, an ideal uniform bar of <wire cross-sectional area> $A$ with longitudinal sensing contacts separated by $L$ gives $\sigma=L I/(A V_L)$. <Four-terminal sensing> suppresses voltage drops in current contacts when the voltage meter draws negligible <electric current>; it does not fix uncertain dimensions or a nonuniform <current density>. Reverse <electric current> and use $[V_L(+I)-V_L(-I)]/2$ to reject a constant voltage offset. Keep <temperature> stable, allow the <current density> to settle, and reduce <electric current> to test for <Joule heating>. Those are controls for interpreting the material coefficient, rather than reasons to assume every measured curve is linear.

For the transverse measurement define $\mathbf J=J_x\widehat{\mathbf x}$, $\mathbf B=B_z\widehat{\mathbf z}$, width $w$ along $y$, and thickness $d$ along $z$. Define $V_H=V(y=-w/2)-V(y=+w/2)=E_yw$, so $J_x=I/(wd)$ gives
$$
R_H=\frac{E_y}{J_xB_z}=\frac{dV_H}{IB_z}.
$$
Extract the part odd in both <electric current> and <magnetic field> using
$$
V_H^{\mathrm{odd}}=\frac{V(+I,+B)-V(-I,+B)-V(+I,-B)+V(-I,-B)}{4}.
$$
This combination rejects a constant offset and longitudinal pickup even in <magnetic field>. It cannot reject another signal with the same two reversal symmetries. Check field polarity and magnitude independently; a sign error in the contact map changes the inferred <Hall coefficient>.

For one <Drude model> species in transverse open circuit, the steady <Lorentz force> balance is $0=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v_d\times\mathbf B)-m\mathbf v_d/\tau$ with $v_{d,y}=0$. Its $y$ component gives $E_y=v_{d,x}B_z$. Consequently
$$
R_H=\frac1{nq},\qquad
R_H=-\frac1{ne}\quad\text{for electrons},\qquad
n=\frac1{e|R_H|},\qquad
\mu=\sigma|R_H|=\frac{e\tau}{m},\qquad
\tau=\frac{m\sigma}{ne^2}.
$$
Longitudinal <electrical conductivity> and the <Hall coefficient> therefore separate <number density> from <charge carrier mobility> in this single-species model. Inferring a <momentum relaxation time> additionally requires an appropriate <mass>. A constant-field longitudinal measurement alone cannot supply all three.

A <Hall coefficient> is not universally the reciprocal of signed <number density>. For example, adding independent <electron> and <electron hole> contributions with densities $n,p$ and positive <charge carrier mobilities> $\mu_e,\mu_h$ in the weak-<magnetic-field> limit gives
$$
\sigma=e(n\mu_e+p\mu_h),\qquad
R_H=\frac{p\mu_h^2-n\mu_e^2}{e(n\mu_e+p\mu_h)^2}.
$$
The <Hall coefficient> can vanish even while <electrical conductivity> is positive, and its sign weights <charge carrier mobility> as well as <number density>. The germanium handout explicitly asks students to track possible transverse sign changes with <temperature>; it does not establish a universal single-carrier interpretation. Real <electronic band structure> and unequal scattering also limit that interpretation.<The Hall Effect and Conductivity in Germanium by Rex Tayloe>[p. 1, Experiment, and p. 3, As a function of temperature: tracking carrier sign and Hall-voltage sign changes]{ref}

= High-frequency Drude plasma cutoff
{parent=Drude model}
{title2=$\omega_p^2=ne^2/(m\epsilon_0)$}

The <Drude model> relative permittivity is $1-\omega_p^2/[\omega(\omega+i/\tau)]$. At frequencies large compared with the collision rate but below $\omega_p$, its leading real part is negative and the physical spatial wave-number branch has positive imaginary part. The field therefore decays into the conductor; close to the threshold the full complex response must be retained.

= Joule heating
{c}
{parent=Ohm's law}
{wiki}
{title2=$P=VI=I^2R=V^2/R$}

= Ohmic heating
{c}
{synonym}

= Electrical dissipation
{synonym}

<Joule heating> is the dissipative conversion of electrical <work> into <internal energy> in an <electrical conductor>. In a metal, the <electric field> supplies <energy> to <charge carriers>, which transfer it to the material. This makes an immersed <resistor> a useful calibrated heater and makes <electrical resistance> a source of losses in wiring. Wind-driven <electric currents> are a proposed source of <heating of an inflated giant planet>.

For a stationary, purely resistive <Ohmic conductor>, use the <passive sign convention>: $V$ is the terminal <electric potential difference> from the entry to the exit of positive <electric current> $I$. The absorbed electrical <power> and <Ohm's law> give
$$
P=VI=I^2R=\frac{V^2}{R}\geq0,\qquad R>0.
$$
The last two expressions assume $V=IR$ at the relevant material <temperature>. For constant <electrical resistance> and <electric current> over a duration $\Delta t$, the dissipated <energy> is $E_{\rm diss}=I^2R\Delta t$; otherwise use $E_{\rm diss}=\int V(t)I(t)\,dt$. In <SI>, $\mathrm{V\,A}=\mathrm W=\mathrm{J/s}$.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others>[§9.5, Power Dissipated by a Resistor and Summary of Relationships: combining terminal power with Ohm's law]{ref}

Absorbed electrical <power> $VI$ is more general than <Joule heating>: a <capacitor> can store <energy>, a <battery> can change its chemical <energy>, and an <electrical circuit> can deliver mechanical <work> or emit <light>. Even a heated <resistor> need not show an indefinitely rising <temperature>: in a thermal <steady state>, its dissipated <power> leaves by <heat transfer>. With the heater inside a <calorimeter> boundary, electrical input crosses as <work>; the subsequent <heat transfer> to the room is a different term in the <first law of thermodynamics>.

= Resistor power under fixed drive
{parent=Joule heating}
{title2=$P_I=I_0^2R,\quad P_V=V_0^2/R$}

Whether increasing <electrical resistance> increases <Joule heating> depends on what the supply holds fixed. Consider separate ideal <resistor> experiments with $R=5\,\Omega$ and $R=10\,\Omega$, each maintained at its specified <temperature>. At fixed <electric current> $I_0=1\,\mathrm A$, doubling $R$ changes the required <electric potential difference> from $5$ to $10\,\mathrm V$ and doubles <power> from $5$ to $10\,\mathrm W$. At fixed <electric potential difference> $V_0=5\,\mathrm V$, doubling $R$ halves <electric current> from $1$ to $0.5\,\mathrm A$ and halves <power> from $5$ to $2.5\,\mathrm W$. These are stipulated model values, not measured data.

\Image[joule-heating-fixed-current-fixed-voltage.png]
{id=joule-heating-fixed-current-fixed-voltage}
{title=Increasing <electrical resistance> has opposite effects on <Joule heating> under fixed <electric current> and fixed <electric potential difference>}
{description=Original calculated comparisons, with $I_0=1\,\mathrm A$ on the left and $V_0=5\,\mathrm V$ on the right. The marked $5$ and $10\,\Omega$ cases are separate ideal experiments. Each lower panel shows the companion quantity that changes when the supply holds the other quantity fixed.}

The comparison also predicts feedback when a particular <resistor>'s <electrical resistance> rises with <temperature>: a supply maintaining <electric current> increases its heating <power>, whereas one maintaining terminal <electric potential difference> reduces it. Predicting the actual <temperature> still needs the material response $R(T)$ and its <heat transfer> to the surroundings; a nominal supply setting does not establish which drive is actually constant at the <resistor>.

= Local Joule heating rate
{parent=Joule heating}
{title2=$p_{\rm vol}=\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E$}

The <electric field> does <work> on matter at local <power> per <volume>
$$
p_{\rm vol}=\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E,\qquad
[p_{\rm vol}]=\frac{\mathrm A}{\mathrm{m^2}}\frac{\mathrm V}{\mathrm m}
=\mathrm{W\,m^{-3}}.
$$
Here $\mathbf J$ is <current density> and the <dot product> includes the relative direction. Positive $\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E$ means <energy> passes from the field to matter; negative means matter supplies <energy> to the field. In general this is a <work> rate, not automatically an irreversible heating rate.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[§27–2, equations 27.3–27.5 and the intervening charge-force argument: work on matter per unit volume and its sign in the field-energy balance]{ref}

For a stationary <isotropic> <Ohmic conductor> with real scalar <electrical conductivity> $\sigma>0$, local <Ohm's law> gives $\mathbf J=\sigma\mathbf E$. Thus the <local Joule heating rate> is
$$
p_{\rm vol}=\sigma|\mathbf E|^2
=\frac{|\mathbf J|^2}{\sigma}
=\rho_{\rm el}|\mathbf J|^2\geq0,\qquad
\rho_{\rm el}=\frac1\sigma.
$$
The <electrical resistivity> $\rho_{\rm el}$ has units $\Omega\,\mathrm m$, and <electrical conductivity> has units $(\Omega\,\mathrm m)^{-1}$. These formulas use the material's rest frame and dissipative conduction <current density>. Moving media, <complex conductivity>, and additional storage or transport processes require their appropriate <energy> balance rather than this scalar substitution.

For <uniform current in a straight homogeneous wire> of <length> $L$, <wire cross-sectional area> $A$ and constant <electrical conductivity>, $E=V/L$, $J=I/A$ and $R=L/(\sigma A)$. The <volume integral> recovers terminal <power>:
$$
P=\int_{\text{wire}}\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E\,d^3x
=\frac IA\frac VL(AL)=IV=I^2R.
$$
As a numerical check, the wire already used in <electrical resistance> has $L=1\,\mathrm m$, $A=10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m^2}$, $\sigma=5\times10^5\,(\Omega\,\mathrm m)^{-1}$ and $V=5\,\mathrm V$. It gives $J=2.5\times10^6\,\mathrm{A/m^2}$, $p_{\rm vol}=1.25\times10^7\,\mathrm{W/m^3}$ and $P=12.5\,\mathrm W$, agreeing with $I=2.5\,\mathrm A$ and $R=2\,\Omega$. Concentrating the same <electric current> in a smaller <wire cross-sectional area> therefore raises the local heating rate if <electrical resistivity> stays fixed.

= Electrical calorimetry of a resistor
{parent=Joule heating}
{title2=$E_{\rm el}=\int VI\,dt$}

An electrical <calorimetry> experiment asks whether a measured electrical <work> input accounts for a measured warming. The University of Iowa's demonstration uses an insulated flask containing <water>, an immersed heater, a <thermometer>, electrical meters and a timer. Its procedure compares the duration, electrical readings and <temperature> rise. The following low-<power> measurement design and synthetic readout develop that idea; they are not measurements reported by the demonstration. <5F15.10 - Electrical Equivalent of Heat by University of Iowa Department of Physics and Astronomy>[Equipment and Procedure: insulated flask, immersed heater, thermometry, electrical meters and timed warming]{ref}

Use a covered <calorimeter> with weighed <water>, an immersed <resistor>, a calibrated <thermometer> and gentle stirring. Put an <ammeter> in series with the heater branch and a high-input-<electrical-resistance> <voltmeter> across the immersed <resistor>'s terminals. Record $V(t)$, $I(t)$, <temperature> $T(t)$ and room <temperature> $T_a(t)$ before, during and after switching the heater on. Measure the <electric potential difference> at the heater, rather than at the supply: lead and contact <electrical resistance> outside the receiving boundary can consume <power> that never reaches the <water>. Account for <voltmeter loading> if the <ammeter> also includes the sensing branch's <electric current>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/10.4 Electrical Measuring Instruments>[Measuring Current with an Ammeter and Measuring Voltage with a Voltmeter, Figures 10.35–10.36: series current measurement, parallel voltage measurement and instrument resistance]{ref}

The electrical readout gives operating <electrical resistance> $R(t)=V(t)/I(t)$ when $I(t)\ne0$, and <power> $P(t)=V(t)I(t)$. To test <Ohm's law>, obtain several pairs at the same <temperature>; one ratio alone cannot establish a linear response. For small <electrical resistance>, use <four-terminal sensing> to exclude lead drops and reverse <electric current> to test stable offsets from the <thermoelectric effect>. Use brief low-<power> resistance measurements if the intended result is the unheated value: the test itself causes <Joule heating>.

For a covered assembly at approximately constant <pressure>, with negligible evaporation, negligible endpoint mechanical <energy> changes and no <phase transition>, take
$$
C_{\rm eff}=m_wc_w+C_{\rm cal},\qquad
E_{\rm el}=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}VI\,dt
=C_{\rm eff}(T_1-T_0)+Q_{\rm loss,out}-W_{\rm stir,on}.
$$
Here $c_w$ is the <water>'s <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>, $C_{\rm cal}$ is the participating vessel, heater and sensor <heat capacity>, $Q_{\rm loss,out}$ is signed outward <heat> leakage, and $W_{\rm stir,on}$ is stirring <work> entering the assembly. The storage term approximates its <enthalpy> change; for a rigid fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> receiver use the corresponding <heat capacity at constant volume>. Calibrate $C_{\rm eff}$ independently if the purpose is to test electrical input against thermal storage. Using the same heating run to define $C_{\rm eff}=E_{\rm el}/\Delta T$ and then claiming it verifies the equality would be circular.

\Image[joule-heating-electrical-calorimetry-readout.png]
{id=joule-heating-electrical-calorimetry-readout}
{title=An immersed <resistor> connects electrical readings to a corrected <calorimeter> warming}
{description=Original apparatus schematic and synthetic <temperature> trace, not Joule's original apparatus or a measured dataset. Separate sensing leads indicate the heater-terminal <electric potential difference>; the <ammeter> is in the heater branch. Shading marks $120\,\mathrm s$ of input at $5\,\mathrm W$. The dashed trace suppresses room <heat> leakage but keeps the same <heat capacity>.}

For the synthetic trace, stipulate $V=5\,\mathrm V$, $I=1\,\mathrm A$, $R=5\,\Omega$, $C_{\rm eff}=500\,\mathrm{J/K}$, room <temperature> $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and loss coefficient $G=0.5\,\mathrm{W/K}$, with negligible stirring input. The heater runs for $120\,\mathrm s$, beginning at room <temperature>. The <calorimeter heat-loss correction> model is
$$
C_{\rm eff}\frac{dT}{dt}=VI-G(T-T_a),\qquad
\Delta T=\frac{VI}{G}\left(1-e^{-G\Delta t/C_{\rm eff}}\right).
$$
It predicts $E_{\rm el}=600\,\mathrm J$, $\Delta T\approx1.13080\,\mathrm K$, storage $565.398\,\mathrm J$ and outward loss $34.602\,\mathrm J$. Neglecting leakage undercounts deposited <energy> by about $5.77\%$; assuming no loss predicts $1.20\,\mathrm K$. After switching off, the excess <temperature> undergoes <exponential decay> with <time constant> $C_{\rm eff}/G=1000\,\mathrm s$. A cooling slope can estimate $G$ only with known $C_{\rm eff}$ and a credible single-<temperature> model.

Controls separate causes of a <temperature> rise. Check the electrical meters' zeros and <thermometer calibration>; keep <water> <mass>, immersion, lid and stirring fixed between runs. Record a heater-off stirred baseline to estimate sensor drift and stirring input. Repeat cooling runs over the same <temperature> range to estimate environmental <heat transfer>; a baseline taken only at room <temperature> cannot measure warming-dependent leakage. Vary <electric current> while keeping $R$ and initial thermal conditions comparable: corrected deposited <energy> should scale as $I^2$ for equal durations. An independent <calorimeter calibration> or known reference <heat capacity> makes this a quantitative test rather than a qualitative observation.

Finite sensor response, incomplete <thermal equilibrium>, changing $R(T)$ or <heat capacity>, evaporation, and imperfect loss corrections limit the inference. A final <temperature> alone cannot distinguish smaller electrical input from larger <heat> leakage or a larger participating <heat capacity>. Agreement tests the stated assembly and its <energy> accounting; it does not show that all electrical <work> in arbitrary devices becomes <heat>.

Historically, the Royal Society's archive description of <James Prescott Joule>'s manuscript dated 13 October 1840 records a coil of wire in a jar of <water>, a <thermometer> and a <galvanometer>, and summarizes his inference that warming depends on <electrical resistance> and the square of <electric current> intensity. This electrical experiment precedes <Joule's paddle-wheel experiment>; the input here is electrical <work>, whereas the paddle supplies mechanical <work>. The catalogue, rather than the inaccessible manuscript images, supports this account. <Archive catalogue record AP-23-34: On the production of heat by voltaic electricity by The Royal Society Archives>[Royal Society Archives, AP/23/34, Description and Date: apparatus, resistance dependence and duplicate-ratio conclusion; manuscript dated 13 October 1840]{ref}

= Watt
{parent=Joule heating}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm W=\mathrm{J/s}$}

The <watt> is the <SI> unit of <power>, one <joule> per second. <Joule heating> at $12.5\,\mathrm W$ transfers $125\,\mathrm J$ of <energy> over a <time> of $10\,\mathrm s$ if that <power> stays constant. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/9.5 Electrical Energy and Power>[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, §9.5: power in watts]{ref}.

= Electric field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

The electric field is the <force> per unit charge on a stationary test particle.

= Electric force
{parent=Electric field}
{title2=$\mathbf F_E=q\mathbf E$}

The <electric force> on an <electric charge> $q$ is $\mathbf F_E=q\mathbf E$, using the <electric field> of the other sources and excluding the charge's own self-field. Positive <electric charge> gives <force> along the <electric field>; negative <electric charge> reverses it. A field $\mathbf E=400\,\mathrm{N/C}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ therefore gives $\mathbf F_E=-8\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm N\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ on $q=-2\,\mathrm{nC}$. This is the electric part of the <Lorentz force>; the magnetic contribution is $q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-4-electric-field[OpenStax, University Physics II, §5.4: electric field and force]{ref}.

= Electric flux
{parent=Electric field}
{title2=$\Phi_E=\int_S E\cdot n\,dS$}
{wiki}

<Electric flux> measures how much <electric field> crosses a chosen <oriented surface>, with a sign distinguishing the two crossing directions. A strong <electric field> running along a surface contributes nothing to its <electric flux>: only the <normal component> counts. This lets <Gauss's law> turn a potentially complicated <electric field> into one number recording the net enclosed <electric charge>.

For a surface $S$ with chosen <unit normal> $\mathbf n$, the <electric flux> is the <surface integral>
$$
\Phi_E(S)=\int_S\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n\,dA=\int_S\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf S,\qquad d\mathbf S=\mathbf n\,dA.
$$
Here $dA$ is positive <surface area>, while $d\mathbf S$ is the <vector area element>. The <dot product> makes <electric flux> a signed <scalar>, rather than a <vector> like the <electric field>. Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$: <electric field> measured using the <newton (unit)> per <coulomb>, multiplied by <surface area> in square metres. Reversing the <orientation of a surface> replaces $\mathbf n$ by $-\mathbf n$ and reverses the <electric flux>. Calling it <electric flux> does not imply that <electric charge> is moving through the surface. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_03.html#Ch3-S2[Feynman lectures II.3, §3–2: flux of a vector field]{ref}.

For an open patch in a <plane>, with <surface area> $A$ and a constant <electric field>, the <unit normal> is constant and the <surface integral> simplifies to
$$
\Phi_E=\mathbf E\cdot(A\mathbf n)=EA\cos\theta,\qquad E=|\mathbf E|.
$$
The <angle> $\theta$ is between the <electric field> and the chosen <unit normal>, not between the <electric field> and the <plane>. The <cosine> describes the signed projected <surface area> seen by the <electric field>. Thus <electric flux> is positive for $0\leq\theta<90^\circ$, zero at $90^\circ$, and negative for $90^\circ<\theta\leq180^\circ$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-1-electric-flux[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §6.1: electric flux]{ref}.

For example, take $E=100\,\mathrm{N/C}$ and $A=0.02\,\mathrm{m^2}$, so $EA=2\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$. Three choices of <angle> give
$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi_E(0^\circ)&=2\cos0^\circ=2\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C},\\
\Phi_E(60^\circ)&=2\cos60^\circ=1\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C},\\
\Phi_E(120^\circ)&=2\cos120^\circ=-1\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Keeping the $60^\circ$ patch fixed and reversing its <unit normal> gives $120^\circ$ and changes $+1$ to $-1\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$. The <electric field> and physical patch have not changed. If the <electric field> is instead tangent to the patch, its <dot product> with the <unit normal> vanishes: <electric flux> is zero even though $E=100\,\mathrm{N/C}$.

\Image[open-surface-electric-flux.png]
{title=Three open patches in a <plane> in a constant <electric field>: the <angle> to the chosen <unit normal> gives <electric fluxes> of 2, 1 and minus 1 newton square metres per <coulomb>}

For a <closed surface> enclosing a volume $V$, choose the outward <unit normal>. <Gauss's law> gives
$$
\Phi_E(\partial V)=\oint_{\partial V}\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n\,dA=\frac{Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}}{\varepsilon_0}.
$$
Here $\varepsilon_0$ is the <vacuum permittivity>, and $Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}$ is the signed total <electric charge> inside. The <electric field> in the <surface integral> includes all sources, including external <electric charges>. Assume no ideal <point charge> lies on the integration surface. Zero enclosed <electric charge> implies zero total <electric flux>; it does **not** imply zero <electric field> everywhere on the <closed surface> or inside it. Positive and negative contributions to the <surface integral> can cancel. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-2-explaining-gausss-law[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §6.2: outward flux and enclosed charge]{ref}.

To see the cancellation with sources inside, place <point charges> $+q$ at $(-a,0,0)$ and $-q$ at $(a,0,0)$, where $q>0$, $a=0.30\,\mathrm m$, and both lie inside the <sphere> of radius $R=1\,\mathrm m$ centered at the origin. This separated pair has the <electric dipole> geometry. Its enclosed <electric charge> is $q-q=0$, so its total outward <electric flux> is zero. At the point $P=(0,0,R)$ on the <sphere>, <Coulomb's law> and the <superposition principle> give the individual <electric fields>
$$
\mathbf E_+(P)=\frac{kq(a,0,R)}{(R^2+a^2)^{3/2}},\qquad
\mathbf E_-(P)=\frac{kq(a,0,-R)}{(R^2+a^2)^{3/2}},\qquad k=\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}.
$$
The $z$ components cancel and the positive $x$ components add:
$$
\mathbf E(P)=\frac{2kqa}{(R^2+a^2)^{3/2}}\widehat{\mathbf x}\approx0.527244\frac{kq}{R^2}\widehat{\mathbf x}\ne\mathbf0.
$$
For the stipulated approximation $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$ and $q=1\,\mathrm{nC}$, this is about $4.740\,\mathrm{N/C}$ along positive $x$. At $P$, the outward <unit normal> is $\widehat{\mathbf z}$, so this nonzero <electric field> is tangent to the <sphere>. At other points of the <sphere>, its <normal component> can be positive or negative. The source-field formula applies away from the two <point charges>, where the <electric field> has zero <divergence>; it is singular at the sources. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_04.html#Ch4-S2[Feynman lectures II.4, §§4–1 and 4–2: electrostatic sources and superposition]{ref}.

\Image[zero-net-flux-nonzero-field.png]
{title=Cross-section of the three-dimensional <electric field> of two opposite <point charges> inside a unit-radius <sphere>: zero total <electric flux> coexists with a nonzero tangential <electric field> at the top}

The diagram shows <streamlines> of the three-dimensional <electric field> in the section $y=0$. The circle is a cross-section of the <sphere>, not the integration surface: <Gauss's law> uses the entire three-dimensional <surface integral>. A two-dimensional <line integral> of outward field around that circle would be a different quantity, with units $\mathrm{N\,m/C}$ rather than the <electric flux> units $\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$.

= Electric potential
{parent=Electric field}
{title2=$\Phi$}
{wiki}

= Electrostatic potential
{synonym}

In <electrostatics>, the <electric potential> $\Phi$ tells us the interaction <potential energy> per unit test <electric charge> in the <electric field> of prescribed sources. Its most directly useful quantity is an <electric potential difference>: how much <work> per unit <electric charge> an external agent must supply to move that test <electric charge> slowly between two points. This replaces the bookkeeping of a <vector> <force> along a path with the difference of two <scalar> values. The test <electric charge> must be small enough not to rearrange the sources appreciably.

For motion from $A$ to $B$, write $\Delta\Phi=\Phi(B)-\Phi(A)$. With fixed sources and only the <electric force> doing <work>,
$$
\Delta\Phi=-\int_A^B\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l,\qquad
\Delta U=q\Delta\Phi,\qquad
W_{\mathrm{field}}=-q\Delta\Phi,\qquad
\Delta K=-\Delta U.
$$
Here $U$ is interaction <potential energy>, $K$ is <kinetic energy>, and the <line integral> uses the <electric field> produced by the other <electric charges>. For slow transport with unchanged <kinetic energy>, the external <work> is $W_{\mathrm{ext}}=\Delta U$, so $\Delta\Phi=W_{\mathrm{ext}}/q$ for nonzero $q$. This external <work> has the opposite sign to the <work> done by the <electric field>. The <SI> unit is the <volt>,
$$
1\,\mathrm V=1\,\mathrm{J/C}.
$$
A <volt> measures <energy> per unit <electric charge>, not <energy> itself. For example, crossing an <electric potential difference> of $+5\,\mathrm V$ changes the <potential energy> of $q=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$ by $+10\,\mathrm{nJ}$, but changes that of $q=-2\,\mathrm{nC}$ by $-10\,\mathrm{nJ}$. Both test <electric charges> encounter the same <electric potential difference>; their opposite signs reverse the <energy> change.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.2 Electric Potential and Potential Difference>[§7.2, equations 7.4–7.7: energy per charge, work and potential difference]{ref}

The <electrostatic potential> is a <scalar> <function> of position. The <electric field> is a <conservative vector field>, and all three components follow from the negative <gradient> of the <electric potential>:
$$
\mathbf E=-\nabla\Phi,\qquad E_x=-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x},\quad E_y=-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial y},\quad E_z=-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial z}.
$$
The <electric field> points toward decreasing <electric potential>. A positive test <electric charge> feels <electric force> in that direction; a negative test <electric charge> feels <electric force> in the opposite direction, since $\mathbf F=q\mathbf E$. A freely accelerated negative test <electric charge> therefore moves toward higher <electric potential> while lowering its <potential energy> $U=q\Phi$.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.4 Determining Field from Potential>[§7.4, equations 7.13–7.15: components and negative gradient]{ref}

Only differences fix physical <electrostatic potentials>: adding the same constant $C_0$ everywhere, $\Phi'=\Phi+C_0$, changes neither the <gradient> nor any <electric potential difference>. Choosing an <electrical reference potential> is this elementary <gauge transformation>. Setting zero at infinity is convenient for finitely many localized <point charges>; it is not available for an ideal uniform <electric field> extending to infinity. There we can choose a finite reference point. Assigning zero at ground is a convention, whereas physically grounding an <electrical conductor> also allows exchange of <electric charge> to impose a boundary value. Zero <electric potential> at a point never by itself implies zero <electric field>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.3 Calculations of Electric Potential>[§7.3, equation 7.8 and Figure 7.18: infinity and ground references]{ref}

For a fixed <point charge> $Q$ at the origin in <vacuum>, choose zero at infinity. <Coulomb's law> gives
$$
\Phi(r)=\frac{kQ}{r},\qquad
\mathbf E(r)=\frac{kQ}{r^2}\widehat{\mathbf r},\qquad
k=\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0},\qquad r>0,
$$
where $\varepsilon_0$ is the <vacuum permittivity>. Use the stipulated approximation $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$ and $Q=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$. At $r=0.20\,\mathrm m$,
$$
\Phi=89.9\,\mathrm V,\qquad E_r=449.5\,\mathrm{N/C}.
$$
A test <electric charge> $q=-3\,\mathrm{nC}$ has $U=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$ and radial <electric force> $F_r=-1.3485\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm N$, directed inward. Moving it inward from $r_A=0.20\,\mathrm m$ to $r_B=0.10\,\mathrm m$ raises the <electric potential> from $89.9$ to $179.8\,\mathrm V$ but lowers its <potential energy>:
$$
\Delta\Phi=+89.9\,\mathrm V,\qquad
\Delta U=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J,\qquad
W_{\mathrm{field}}=+2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J.
$$
This is the <Coulomb potential energy> calculation using <electric potentials>. The source's singular self-field at $r=0$ is excluded; when finding the <force> on a source <point charge>, use only the <electric field> of the other sources.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.3 Calculations of Electric Potential>[§7.3: point-charge potential and scalar superposition]{ref}

For a uniform <electric field> $\mathbf E=300\,\mathrm{V/m}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$, choose $\Phi(0)=12\,\mathrm V$. Then
$$
\Phi(x)=12\,\mathrm V-(300\,\mathrm{V/m})x,\qquad
\Delta\Phi=-\mathbf E\cdot(\mathbf r_B-\mathbf r_A).
$$
Moving from $x_A=0$ to $x_B=0.04\,\mathrm m$ gives $\Delta\Phi=-12\,\mathrm V$. For $q=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$ the change in <potential energy> is $-24\,\mathrm{nJ}$ and the <electric field> does $+24\,\mathrm{nJ}$ of <work>. For $q=-2\,\mathrm{nC}$, the same prescribed displacement gives $\Delta U=+24\,\mathrm{nJ}$: the external agent must supply that <work> for slow transport. This uniform <electric field> approximates the interior of a <parallel-plate capacitor> sufficiently far from the edges; the <fringing field of a capacitor> limits the approximation.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.4 Determining Field from Potential>[§7.4: voltage gradient in a uniform field]{ref}

\Image[electric-potential-work-and-equipotentials.png]
{id=electric-potential-work-and-equipotentials}
{title=Analytic <electric potential> models: <point charge> <equipotentials>, a uniform <electric field>, and the sign of the change in <potential energy>}

An <equipotential> is a <level set> $\Phi=\text{constant}$. On a smooth <equipotential> with nonzero <gradient>, every tangent displacement has $d\Phi=\nabla\Phi\cdot d\mathbf l=0$, so the <electric field> is normal to the <equipotential>. Thus moving a test <electric charge> along an <equipotential> requires no net electrical <work>. Around a <point charge>, the <equipotentials> are <spheres>; the circles in the diagram are their sections, not an assumption of two-dimensional <Coulomb's law>. A uniform <electric field> has parallel <plane> <equipotentials>. For contours separated by the same small <electric potential difference>, smaller normal separation means a stronger <electric field>, approximately $|\mathbf E|\approx|\Delta\Phi|/\Delta n$. At a point where $\nabla\Phi=0$, the <electric field> is zero and has no direction to call normal.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.5 Equipotential Surfaces and Conductors>[§7.5, Figures 7.30–7.32: equipotentials, perpendicular field and zero work]{ref}

The <superposition principle> adds <electric potentials> as signed <scalars>. For finitely many stationary <point charges> $q_j$ at $\mathbf r_j$ in <vacuum>,
$$
\Phi(\mathbf r)=k\sum_j\frac{q_j}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_j|},\qquad
\mathbf E(\mathbf r)=k\sum_jq_j\frac{\mathbf r-\mathbf r_j}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_j|^3}.
$$
For example, the <electric dipole> with $+q$ at $(a,0,0)$ and $-q$ at $(-a,0,0)$ has zero <electric potential> everywhere in the midplane $x=0$, yet its <electric field> at the midpoint is $\mathbf E=-(2kq/a^2)\widehat{\mathbf x}$. With $q=2\,\mathrm{nC}$ and $a=0.10\,\mathrm m$, this is $-3596\,\mathrm{N/C}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$. At $(2a,0,0)$, the same model gives $\Phi=2kq/(3a)\approx119.867\,\mathrm V$ and $E_x=8kq/(9a^2)\approx1598.222\,\mathrm{N/C}$. Cancellation of <electric potentials> is quite different from cancellation of <vector> <electric fields>.

\Image[electrostatic-dipole-potential.png]
{title=Computed dipole equipotentials and electric-field directions: zero potential on the vertical midline still permits a nonzero field}

A motivating measurement is <conductive-paper electric potential mapping>. A documented teaching experiment uses slightly conductive paper on an insulating board, two circular <electrodes> in highly conducting ink, a steady supply, and a digital <voltmeter>. Connect the supply to the two <electrodes>, keep the <voltmeter> common lead on the negative <electrode>, and use its other lead as a movable probe. Adjust the supply using the measured <electric potential difference> between <electrodes>; the published procedure uses $10\,\mathrm V$. The observable is the <voltmeter> reading $\Phi(P)-\Phi(\mathrm{COM})$ at the probe position $P$. Locate several points reading $2\,\mathrm V$, record their positions on a separate map, and join them into an <equipotential>; repeat at other readings. Lift the probe between contacts so it does not damage the paper. This procedure makes the <electrical reference potential> concrete: every reading compares two contacts.<Physics 2212 laboratory manual by Eric Murray/Physics 2212, Lab 5: Electric Potential and Field>{ref}

From neighboring readings infer an <electric field> component by a <finite difference>, $E_x\approx-[\Phi(x+h)-\Phi(x)]/h$. Reconstruct two in-plane components with displacements along two independent directions. Alternatively, draw the <electric field> normal to mapped <equipotentials>, toward lower <electric potential>. The second diagram illustrates the same measurement principle with bar <electrodes> spanning a uniform sheet, insulating top and bottom edges, and a synthetic model $\Phi(x)=(50\,\mathrm{V/m})x$. The model readings $4.0\,\mathrm V$ at $x=0.08\,\mathrm m$ and $5.0\,\mathrm V$ at $x=0.10\,\mathrm m$ imply $E_x=-50\,\mathrm{V/m}$. These values are calculated illustrations, not reported experimental data. The documented circular-<electrode> experiment similarly estimates the <electric field> from readings at successive centimetre marks.<Physics 2212 laboratory manual by Eric Murray/Physics 2212, Lab 5: Electric Potential and Field>{ref}

\Image[electric-potential-voltmeter-mapping.png]
{id=electric-potential-voltmeter-mapping}
{title=<Voltmeter> setup for <conductive-paper electric potential mapping> and a synthetic profile whose negative <derivative> gives the <electric field>}

Useful controls include checking the <voltmeter> zero, checking that the <electric potential difference> along each painted <electrode> is negligible, and repeating reference readings to detect supply drift. Reversing the supply should reverse the signs relative to the same reference contact. Contact resistance, leakage <electric currents>, and touching the sheet can distort the map. A <voltmeter> has finite input <electrical resistance> and draws a small <electric current>; <voltmeter loading> changes the quantity being measured if that <electrical resistance> is insufficiently large relative to the sheet's effective source <electrical resistance>.<Electricity and Magnetism laboratory manual/EC-2: Electric Fields>{ref}<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/10.4 Electrical Measuring Instruments>[§10.4, Measuring Voltage with a Voltmeter: parallel connection and large input resistance]{ref}

The paper carries a steady <electric current>, so this is a planar analogue rather than a direct measurement of isolated <point charges> in three-dimensional <vacuum>. For constant <electrical conductivity>, <Ohm's law> and steady <charge conservation> give $\mathbf j=-\sigma\nabla\Phi$ and $\nabla\cdot\mathbf j=0$, hence the two-dimensional <Laplace equation> away from <electrodes>. Insulating sheet edges impose zero normal <electric current>, while finite <electrodes> fix boundary values. This derivation explains both the analogy and its limits: inhomogeneous paper, finite boundaries, and finite <electrodes> can change the contours. One should not fit the three-dimensional <point charge> formula $kQ/r$ to a planar map without testing that model. Small probe spacing resolves spatial variation better, but amplifies uncertainty in the inferred <finite difference>. The published laboratory instructions explicitly ask students to assess whether their proposed field fit is appropriate.<Electricity and Magnetism laboratory manual/EC-2: Electric Fields>{ref}<Physics 2212 laboratory manual by Eric Murray/Physics 2212, Lab 5: Electric Potential and Field>{ref}

The scalar description has a crucial limit. <Faraday's law of induction> for a stationary closed path $\Gamma$ says
$$
\oint_\Gamma\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l=-\frac{d\Phi_B}{dt},\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\frac{\partial\mathbf B}{\partial t},
$$
where $\Phi_B$ is <magnetic flux>. Nonzero changing <magnetic flux> gives nonzero circulation, incompatible with a single-valued <electric potential> alone. More generally, a scalar <electric potential> and a <magnetic vector potential> together give
$$
\mathbf E=-\nabla\Phi-\frac{\partial\mathbf A}{\partial t},\qquad
\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A.
$$
Consequently, in the presence of induction, two <voltmeter> lead routes between the same contacts can produce different readings if the enclosed <magnetic flux> changes. This follows by subtracting the two path <line integrals>; it is a deduction from <Faraday's law of induction>, not a claimed outcome of the paper experiment. The steady setup and negligible induction justify interpreting its readings as <electric potential differences>. Merely introducing a scalar <electric potential> that depends on <time> cannot supply a nonzero <curl> of the <electric field>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 21: Solutions of Maxwell’s Equations with Currents and Charges>[§21–3]{ref}

A short mathematical justification connects the statements above. Along a smooth path, the <chain rule> gives $d\Phi=\nabla\Phi\cdot d\mathbf l$. Integrating and using $\mathbf E=-\nabla\Phi$ yields the endpoint formula by the <fundamental theorem for line integrals>, proving <path independence>. Conversely, vanishing circulation around every closed path lets us define $\Phi(P)=-\int_{P_0}^{P}\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l$ independently of the route, with $\Phi(P_0)=0$. Zero <curl> is a local test; for a continuously differentiable <curl-free vector field>, a <simply connected> open region suffices for a global potential. The explicit finite-source formula already gives a global <electric potential> away from its source points.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 4: Electrostatics>{ref}

= Voltmeter
{parent=Electric potential}
{wiki}

A <voltmeter> measures an <electric potential difference> between two contacts; its finite input <electrical resistance> can cause <voltmeter loading>.

= Voltmeter loading
{parent=Voltmeter}

<Voltmeter loading> is the change in an <electric potential difference> caused by the <electric current> drawn by the measuring <voltmeter>.

= Conductive-paper electric potential mapping
{parent=Electric potential}

<Conductive-paper electric potential mapping> uses a <voltmeter> to locate <equipotentials> on a resistive sheet, then infers the in-plane <electric field> from the measured <electric potential differences>.

= Electrical reference potential
{parent=Electric potential}
{title2=$\Phi_{\mathrm{ref}}=0$}

An <electrical reference potential> fixes the arbitrary additive constant of an <electric potential>. Assigning zero to ground makes other potentials differences from that baseline; it does not assert that the <electric field> vanishes there. If a source calculation gives $\Phi(a)=89.9\,\mathrm V$ and $\Phi(b)=179.8\,\mathrm V$ relative to infinity, choosing $a$ as the zero instead gives $\Phi'(a)=0$ and $\Phi'(b)=89.9\,\mathrm V$, with the same <electric potential difference>. Grounding an <electrical conductor> additionally connects it to a reservoir that can exchange <electric charge> to maintain the prescribed <electric potential>; that physical boundary condition differs from merely relabeling an unchanged solution.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.3 Calculations of Electric Potential>[§7.3: potential references, including ground in Figure 7.18]{ref}; https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_06.html#Ch6-S8[Feynman lectures II.6, §6–8: a grounded conducting plane]{ref}.

= Constrained electrostatic equilibrium
{parent=Electric potential}

A particle constrained to a surface can have a stable minimum of <electrostatic potential> restricted to that surface even when the unrestricted equilibrium is unstable. Away from source charges, <Laplace's equation> makes the full potential <Hessian matrix> trace zero, which rules out a positive-definite three-dimensional Hessian but does not rule out a positive-definite restriction to two dimensions. Equal positive charges at the corners of a square give a planar minimum at its centre and an unstable perpendicular direction.

= Electrostatic reciprocity identity
{parent=Electric potential}
{title2=$\int_V(\phi_1\rho_2-\phi_2\rho_1)\,dV=\varepsilon_0\int_{\partial V}(\phi_2\nabla\phi_1-\phi_1\nabla\phi_2)\cdot d\mathbf S$}

For two <electrostatic potentials> satisfying the <Poisson equation> $\Delta\phi_i=-\rho_i/\varepsilon_0$, <Green's second identity> yields the displayed reciprocity formula. The <product rule for divergence> applied to $\phi_1\nabla\phi_2-\phi_2\nabla\phi_1$ cancels the cross-gradient terms. The normal points outward from the integration region; for an exterior region this means inward toward the excluded object on its inner boundary. Boundary terms may be removed only under suitable decay or boundary conditions.

= Image charge
{parent=Electric potential}

An image charge is a fictitious point charge placed outside the physical solution region so that its potential, combined with the real sources, enforces the required conductor boundary values. The <uniqueness of the Dirichlet problem> then identifies the constructed potential with the physical one. The image need not describe the actual induced surface-charge distribution.

= Fall time of a charge toward a grounded plane
{parent=Image charge}
{title2=$t_{\rm hit}=\pi\sqrt{2\pi\varepsilon_0 m d^3}/|q|$}

The image of a charge $q$ at height $z$ is $-q$ at height $-z$. Their separation is $2z$, giving force $-q^2/(16\pi\varepsilon_0z^2)$. Starting at rest at height $d$, energy conservation gives $\dot z^2=q^2(1/z-1/d)/(8\pi\varepsilon_0m)$. Integrating the reciprocal speed to the plane yields the stated finite time in the electrostatic Newtonian model. The induced-conductor potential energy is $-q^2/(16\pi\varepsilon_0z)$; using the full image-pair energy would double the force incorrectly.

= Falling charge above a grounded conducting plane
{parent=Image charge}
{title2=$T=\pi\sqrt{2\pi\varepsilon_0md^3}/|q|$}

A real charge of height $z$ has a reflected <image charge> at $-z$. Its acceleration is $\ddot z=-\kappa/z^2$, with $\kappa=q^2/(16\pi\varepsilon_0m)$. Released from rest at height $d$, its ideal-model arrival time is
$$
T=\frac{\pi d^{3/2}}{2\sqrt{2\kappa}}.
$$
The image follows the instantaneous reflected position. The induced-charge energy carries one-half the full real-image pair interaction factor, consistent with this force.

= Image charge for a grounded conducting sphere
{parent=Image charge}
{title2=$q'=-aq/R,\quad\mathbf p'=a^2\mathbf p/R^2$}

For a charge $q$ at $\mathbf p$, $R=|\mathbf p|>a$, outside a grounded sphere of radius $a$, the displayed image gives zero potential on the sphere and decay at infinity. On $|\mathbf x|=a$, one has $|\mathbf x-\mathbf p'|=(a/R)|\mathbf x-\mathbf p|$, proving boundary cancellation. The induced force is inward, of magnitude $q^2aR/[4\pi\varepsilon_0(R^2-a^2)^2]$, from <Coulomb's law> applied to the image field. The self-field is excluded.

= Electric potential difference
{parent=Electric potential}
{title2=$V_{AB}=\phi(A)-\phi(B)$}

An <electric potential difference> compares <electric potentials> at two points. For a move from $a$ to $b$, define $\Delta\Phi=\Phi(b)-\Phi(a)$; the terminal convention $V_{AB}=\Phi(A)-\Phi(B)$ instead means the potential at $A$ relative to $B$, so the order must always be stated. In <electrostatics>, the <line integral> of the <electric field> gives
$$
\Delta\Phi=-\int_a^b\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l,\qquad
\Delta U=q\Delta\Phi,\qquad
W_{\mathrm{field}}=q\int_a^b\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l=q\bigl(\Phi(a)-\Phi(b)\bigr)=-\Delta U.
$$
Here $\Delta U$ is the change in interaction <potential energy> of the test <electric charge> $q$ with fixed sources, while $W_{\mathrm{field}}$ is <work> done by the <electric field>. For an external agent moving the charge slowly without a change in <kinetic energy>, its <work> is $\Delta U$. A positive <electric charge> gains <kinetic energy> when accelerated toward lower <electric potential>; a negative <electric charge> gains <kinetic energy> when accelerated toward higher <electric potential>. Both motions lower $q\Phi$.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.2 Electric Potential and Potential Difference>[§7.2: potential difference, work, and energy]{ref}

For example, fix $Q=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$ at the origin and compare distances $r_a=0.20\,\mathrm m$ and $r_b=0.10\,\mathrm m$. With the stipulated approximation $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$ and zero at infinity,
$$
\Phi(a)=\frac{kQ}{r_a}=89.9\,\mathrm V,\qquad
\Phi(b)=\frac{kQ}{r_b}=179.8\,\mathrm V,\qquad
\Delta\Phi=89.9\,\mathrm V.
$$
Moving $q=-3\,\mathrm{nC}$ inward therefore gives
$$
U_a=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J,\qquad
U_b=-5.394\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J,\qquad
\Delta U=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J,\qquad
W_{\mathrm{field}}=+2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J.
$$
If only the <electric force> does <work>, <conservation of energy> makes the gain in <kinetic energy> $2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$. Direct integration confirms the signs: $\int_{r_a}^{r_b}kQ\,dr/r^2=kQ(1/r_a-1/r_b)=-89.9\,\mathrm V$, which multiplied by the negative test <electric charge> gives positive <work>. The <electric potential difference> is unchanged by choosing a different <electrical reference potential>.

In an <electrical circuit>, an <electric potential difference> is the voltage across a component's terminals. In an <electrical network>, a vertex <voltage> is specified relative to a reference vertex; its differences across edges drive <current flow>. For a uniform <electric field>, the integral reduces to $\Delta\Phi=-\mathbf E\cdot(\mathbf r_b-\mathbf r_a)$. A field $\mathbf E=300\,\mathrm{V/m}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ across a displacement $0.04\,\mathrm m\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ therefore gives $\Delta\Phi=-12\,\mathrm V$, as in the interior approximation for a <parallel-plate capacitor>. The <volt>, measured in <joules> per <coulomb>, expresses <energy> transfer per unit <electric charge>, which makes it useful for comparing components without specifying the amount of charge transported.

= Point charge
{parent=Electric potential}
{wiki}

A point charge idealizes electric charge concentrated at one point. In vacuum, a charge $Q$ at the origin has potential $Q/(4\pi\epsilon_0r)$.

= Equipotential
{parent=Electric potential}
{wiki}

An equipotential is a level set of the electric potential. The electric field is normal to every smooth equipotential.

= Magnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$\mathbf B$}
{wiki}

= Magnetic flux density
{synonym}

A <magnetic field> is a <vector field> $\mathbf B(\mathbf r,t)$ whose local value determines the magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> on a body with <electric charge> $q$ and <velocity> $\mathbf v$. In <SI> units,
$$
\mathbf F_B=q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B,\qquad
|\mathbf F_B|=|q|vB\sin\alpha,
$$
where $v$ is the <speed>, $B=|\mathbf B|$, and $\alpha$ is the <angle> between the <velocity> and the <magnetic field>. The <cross product> makes a nonzero magnetic <force> perpendicular to both the <velocity> and the <magnetic field>. The <right-hand rule> gives its direction for positive <electric charge>; negative <electric charge> reverses it. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-2-magnetic-fields-and-lines[OpenStax, magnetic fields and forces]{ref}.

The <SI> unit of <magnetic field> is the <tesla (unit)>:
$$
1\,\mathrm T=\frac{1\,\mathrm N}{(1\,\mathrm C)(1\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}})}.
$$
For a stipulated example in <SI> units, take <electric charge> $q=2\,\mathrm C$, <velocity> $\mathbf v=(3,0,0)\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, and <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=(0,0,4)\,\mathrm T$. The <cross product> gives magnetic <force> $\mathbf F_B=(0,-24,0)\,\mathrm N$. With the same <speed> directed along $+z$, the magnetic <force> is zero: motion parallel to a <magnetic field> produces no magnetic <acceleration>.

<Magnetic field lines> visualize the direction of a <magnetic field> by being tangent to it at each point. They are not generally the path of a body with <electric charge>: in a uniform <magnetic field> along $+z$, the <magnetic field lines> are straight while transverse <velocity> produces <circular motion>, and adding parallel <velocity> produces <helical motion in a uniform magnetic field>. The worked <Lorentz force> example below illustrates this distinction.

A <magnetic field> need not be static. <Magnetostatics> is the restricted case with no dependence on <time>; an <electromagnetic wave> has changing <electric field> and <magnetic field> even in vacuum. A changing <magnetic field> is coupled to the <electric field> through <Faraday's law of induction>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_20.html[Feynman lectures, II.20: electromagnetic waves in free space]{ref}. The useful separation is between local bending, described by the <Lorentz force>, and the amount of <magnetic field> crossing a <surface>, described by <magnetic flux>.

= Tesla
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Magnetic field}
{title2=$\mathrm T$}
{wiki}

The <tesla (unit)> is the <SI> unit of <magnetic field>. From the magnetic <Lorentz force>, $1\,\mathrm T=1\,\mathrm{N\,s\,C^{-1}\,m^{-1}}$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-2-magnetic-fields-and-lines[OpenStax, magnetic-field units]{ref}.

= Solenoid
{parent=Magnetic field}
{title2=$B=\mu_0nI$}
{wiki}

A <solenoid> is a coil of <electrical conductor>, usually wire wound into a <helix> around a <circular cylinder>. The same <electric current> $I$ flows through its successive turns. Each turn contributes a <magnetic field>; the <superposition principle> makes their axial contributions reinforce inside the coil. Curl the fingers of your right hand in the direction of the <electric current> around the turns: the thumb gives the interior <magnetic field> direction by the <right-hand rule>. Throughout this branch, $N$ is the total number of turns, $\ell$ is the coil <length>, and $n=N/\ell$ is the <solenoid turn density>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_13.html#Ch13-S5[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, The Feynman Lectures on Physics, II.13–5: the field of a long coil]{ref}.

The attraction of a long <solenoid> is a controllable, nearly uniform <magnetic field> over an interior working region: change the <electric current> to tune its strength, or reverse the <electric current> to reverse its direction. This makes it a useful setting for studying the <Lorentz force>, measuring <magnetic flux>, and constructing an <inductor>. Its geometry turns the many contributions from individual turns into the simple <long-solenoid approximation>. A real finite coil has end fields and leakage; its exterior <magnetic field> is not zero. The <on-axis magnetic field of a finite solenoid> below quantifies that difference.

Historically, the coil helped connect <magnetic fields> to <electric currents>. In his memoir on the mathematical theory of electrodynamic phenomena, Ampère modeled the interactions of magnets using the ends of solenoids in place of microscopic magnetic constituents. This was a route toward explaining magnetism by <electric currents>, rather than a modern microscopic account of <magnetization>. https://www.e-rara.ch/zut/download/pdf/1038813[Ampère, Mémoire sur la théorie mathématique des phénomènes électro-dynamiques, concluding discussion on p. 289]{ref}. The historical setting is documented by the https://ampere-archives.fr/histoire-electricite/parcours-historique/lois-courants/ampere-loi[Ampère archives, investigations of current interactions in 1820–1826]{ref}.

= Solenoid turn density
{parent=Solenoid}
{title2=$n=N/\ell$}

The <solenoid turn density> $n$ counts turns per unit axial length in a uniformly wound <solenoid>. The total turn count $N$ is dimensionless, whereas $n$ has units $\mathrm{m^{-1}}$. A $1000$-turn coil of length $0.50\,\mathrm m$ has $n=2000\,\mathrm{m^{-1}}$; an axial interval of length $s=0.010\,\mathrm m$ then contains $ns=20$ turns. The effective circumferential <surface current density> in the closely spaced circular-turn model is $K=nI$, with units $\mathrm{A/m}$. https://web.mit.edu/8.02t/www/test/materials/StudyGuide/guide09_revised.pdf[MIT 8.02 course notes, §9.4, equations 9.4.3–9.4.4: turns per length and surface current]{ref}.

For a single-layer <helix>, the axial advance per turn is approximately $1/n$. The circular-turn model neglects this advance and the associated axial component of <electric current>, as well as the detailed <magnetic field> close to individual wires. The number of turns around the whole coil is always $N=n\ell$ here; it must not be substituted for $n$ in $B=\mu_0nI$.

= Long-solenoid approximation
{parent=Solenoid}
{title2=$\mathbf B_{\rm in}=\mu_0nI\,\widehat{\mathbf z},\quad\mathbf B_{\rm out}=0$}

In <magnetostatics>, an ideal infinitely long <solenoid> is a cylindrical sheet of circumferential <surface current density> $K=nI$. It has negligible winding advance, no magnetic core, and no imposed external <magnetic field>. Choose positive <electric current> to produce <magnetic field> along $+z$. In this ideal model the interior <magnetic field> is uniform and axial, $B=\mu_0nI$, and the exterior <magnetic field> is zero; $\mu_0$ is the <vacuum permeability>. For a finite, closely wound air-core coil, the same result is an approximation well inside the coil, several radii from either end. Air is treated as having the <magnetic permeability> of vacuum. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_13.html#Ch13-S5[Feynman lectures, II.13–5: long-solenoid field and the return field near the ends]{ref}.

For a short proof, the translational and rotational <symmetries> of the ideal cylindrical <current sheet>, together with the <Biot-Savart law>, give an axial <magnetic field>. <Ampère's law> on rectangles wholly outside makes that axial exterior value independent of radius; the condition of no imposed <magnetic field> makes it zero at infinity and hence everywhere outside. Rectangles wholly inside similarly show that the interior value is independent of radius. Now choose an <Amperian loop> with axial length $s$, one long side inside and the other outside. The transverse sides are perpendicular to the <magnetic field>, so their contributions to the <line integral> vanish. The loop crosses $ns$ turns of the winding, giving
$$
\oint_\Gamma\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf l
=Bs=\mu_0(ns)I,
\qquad B=\mu_0nI.
$$
Here $s$ is the integration-path length, not the whole coil <length> $\ell$. An <Amperian loop> surrounding both sides of the winding encloses equal and opposite <electric currents>; zero net circulation alone would not prove zero <magnetic field> at every exterior point.

\Image[solenoid-ampere-loop.png]
{title=<Ampère's law> for the ideal <long-solenoid approximation>}
{description=Axial cross-section of an infinite ideal winding: paired dots and crosses show <electric current> out of and into the page. The indicated <right-hand rule> produces interior <magnetic field> along $+z$. The counterclockwise <Amperian loop> crosses only the upper row, with $ns=6$ positive crossings; its interior side runs along $+z$, and its exterior return lies where the ideal <magnetic field> is zero.}

For the $1000$-turn, $0.50\,\mathrm m$ coil with <electric current> $I=0.50\,\mathrm A$, the <long-solenoid approximation> gives
$$
B\approx(1.256637\times10^{-6})(2000)(0.50)\,\mathrm T
=1.256637\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm T
\approx1.257\,\mathrm{mT}.
$$
This illustrative calculation uses <vacuum permeability> $\mu_0\approx1.256637\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{H/m}$, a rounded value, not an exact constant. Since the 2019 redefinition of <SI>, <vacuum permeability> is determined experimentally.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>[§2.3.1: the ampere and vacuum permeability]{ref} Doubling $I$ doubles the ideal <magnetic field>; doubling $N$ at fixed $\ell$ does the same. Increasing $\ell$ while keeping $N$ fixed decreases $n$ and hence the ideal <magnetic field>.

= On-axis magnetic field of a finite solenoid
{parent=Solenoid}
{title2=$B_z(z)$}

Model a finite <solenoid> as a continuum of coaxial turns shaped as <circles> of <radius> $a>0$, uniformly distributed over length $\ell$ with <solenoid turn density> $n=N/\ell$. Put its centre at $z=0$, so its ends are $z=\pm\ell/2$. With the same <electric current> orientation as in the <long-solenoid approximation>, its axial <magnetic field> is
$$
B_z(z)=\frac{\mu_0nI}{2}
\left[
\frac{z+\ell/2}{\sqrt{a^2+(z+\ell/2)^2}}
-\frac{z-\ell/2}{\sqrt{a^2+(z-\ell/2)^2}}
\right].
$$
This formula applies only on the axis, where <symmetry> cancels the transverse <magnetic field>. It does not describe points off the axis. It is exact for the continuum circular-turn model, which neglects winding thickness, discrete wire spacing, and helical advance. https://web.mit.edu/8.02t/www/test/materials/StudyGuide/guide09_revised.pdf[MIT 8.02 course notes, §9.4, equations 9.4.5–9.4.6: the finite-solenoid axial field]{ref}.

The derivation is an especially direct use of the <superposition principle>. The <on-axis magnetic field of a circular current loop> at source position $z'$ is $\mu_0Ia^2/[2(a^2+(z-z')^2)^{3/2}]$. There are $n\,dz'$ turns in an axial slice, so
$$
B_z(z)=\frac{\mu_0nIa^2}{2}
\int_{-\ell/2}^{\ell/2}
\frac{dz'}{[a^2+(z-z')^2]^{3/2}}.
$$
Substitute $u=z-z'$ and use the <antiderivative> $u/[a^2\sqrt{a^2+u^2}]$ to obtain the displayed expression. If the centre is instead at $z=z_0$, replace every $z$ in that expression by $z-z_0$.

At the centre and at either end, respectively,
$$
\frac{B_z(0)}{\mu_0nI}=\frac{\ell}{\sqrt{\ell^2+4a^2}},
\qquad
\frac{B_z(\ell/2)}{\mu_0nI}
=\frac{\ell}{2\sqrt{\ell^2+a^2}}.
$$
For aspect ratios $\ell/a=2,6,20$, the centre ratios are approximately $0.7071,0.9487,0.9950$. The end ratio tends to $1/2$ as $\ell/a$ becomes large, even though the centre ratio tends to $1$. Thus a long coil can have an excellent central <magnetic field> while the ends still differ strongly from that value. Convergence to the <long-solenoid approximation> is not uniform across the end region.

\Image[solenoid-axial-field-profile.png]
{title=Finite <solenoid> axial <magnetic fields> compared with the ideal interior value}
{description=Original profiles for $\ell/a=2,6,20$ on the axis, normalized by $\mu_0nI$. The dashed step is the approximation that assigns the ideal interior value within the coil and zero outside; an infinite <solenoid> itself has no ends. The finite profiles stay smooth through the end planes and remain nonzero beyond them. These curves describe no off-axis points.}

For a concrete geometry, give the earlier coil <radius> $a=0.025\,\mathrm m$, so $\ell/a=20$. Its central <magnetic field> is approximately $1.2504\,\mathrm{mT}$, its end value is $0.6275\,\mathrm{mT}$, and at $z=\ell$ its axial <magnetic field> is still $0.00277\,\mathrm{mT}$. The last point is outside the coil: finite length does not permit the zero-exterior conclusion of the infinite model. Off the axis, the <magnetic field lines> bend around to form the return field. A central axial value alone also does not determine the total <magnetic flux> through every turn of a short coil.

= Solenoid inductance
{parent=Solenoid}
{title2=$L\approx\mu_0N^2A/\ell$}

= Self-inductance of a solenoid
{synonym}

For a long vacuum-core or air-core <solenoid> with fixed geometry and linear magnetic response, neglecting end effects gives <self-inductance>
$$
L\approx\frac{\mu_0N^2A}{\ell}
=\mu_0n^2A\ell,
\qquad A=\pi a^2.
$$
Here $N$ is the total number of turns, $n=N/\ell$ is the <solenoid turn density>, and $A$ is the interior cross-sectional <area>. The reason for the square of $N$ is physical: more turns produce more <magnetic field>, and more turns link the resulting <magnetic flux>. Approximately equal <magnetic flux> $\Phi\approx BA$ through each turn gives <magnetic flux linkage> $\Lambda\approx N\Phi\approx\mu_0N^2AI/\ell$; division by the <electric current> yields $L$. https://s3.amazonaws.com/edx-textbooks/mit_802_textbook/Chapter11.pdf[MIT 8.02 course notes, Example 11.2: self-inductance of a solenoid]{ref}.

For the same $N=1000$, $\ell=0.50\,\mathrm m$, and $a=0.025\,\mathrm m$, the <area> is $A=\pi(0.025)^2\,\mathrm{m^2}\approx1.9635\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m^2}$. The long-coil estimate is
$$
L\approx\frac{(1.256637\times10^{-6})(1000)^2\pi(0.025)^2}{0.50}\,\mathrm H
\approx4.9348\,\mathrm{mH}.
$$
At $I=0.50\,\mathrm A$, each turn links approximately $2.4674\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{Wb}$ and the total <magnetic flux linkage> is approximately $2.4674\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{Wb}$. Their ratio to $I$ is the stated <self-inductance>. A real finite coil need not give precisely these values: the axial centre correction above is not by itself a correction formula for <inductance>, which depends on <magnetic flux> across all the turns.

At fixed $A$ and $\ell$, doubling $N$ quadruples $L$. At fixed <solenoid turn density> $n$ and <area> $A$, doubling $\ell$ doubles both $N$ and $L$. These are different changes to the geometry, so there is no contradiction between $L\propto1/\ell$ at fixed $N$ and $L\propto\ell$ at fixed $n$. A magnetic core requires its own <magnetic permeability> model; nonlinear <magnetization> can make a constant-$L$ description inadequate.

= Stored energy of a solenoid
{parent=Solenoid inductance}
{title2=$U=\tfrac12LI^2$}

The <magnetic energy> stored in a linear <solenoid> of constant <self-inductance> $L$ is
$$
U=\frac12LI^2,
\qquad
U\approx\frac{B^2}{2\mu_0}A\ell
$$
in the <long-solenoid approximation>. The first formula is an <inductor> relation; the second identifies its storage with <magnetic energy> in the interior <magnetic field>, under the vacuum-core approximation. This agreement joins the <electrical circuit> description to the spatial <magnetic field> description. The expression $B^2/(2\mu_0)$ is the vacuum magnetic <energy density>. https://s3.amazonaws.com/edx-textbooks/mit_802_textbook/Chapter11.pdf[MIT 8.02 course notes, §11.3: magnetic energy and its density]{ref}.

A short derivation starts from the terminal <electric potential difference> $v_L=L\,dI/dt$, with positive <electric current> entering the terminal at higher potential. The absorbed <power> is $v_LI$, and integrating from zero <electric current> gives
$$
U=\int_0^I Li\,di=\frac12LI^2.
$$
The result describes recoverable storage in the <inductor>; <Joule heating> in the wire is a separate expenditure of <energy>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_17.html#Ch17-S8[Feynman lectures, II.17–8: inductance and magnetic energy]{ref}.

For the long-coil example, $L\approx4.9348\,\mathrm{mH}$ and $I=0.50\,\mathrm A$ give $U\approx6.1685\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm J=0.61685\,\mathrm{mJ}$. Substituting the ideal $B\approx1.256637\,\mathrm{mT}$ and volume $A\ell\approx9.8175\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m^3}$ into $B^2A\ell/(2\mu_0)$ gives the same value. Doubling <electric current> quadruples stored <magnetic energy>. In a real coil, reducing <electric current> can return this <energy> to an <electrical circuit>, or release it as <Joule heating> in a closed <RL circuit>.

= Solenoidal magnetic-field constraint
{parent=Magnetic field}
{title2=$\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0$}

The <magnetic field> has zero divergence. The <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> preserves this constraint from initial data, since the divergence of its curl is zero.

= Magnetic field line
{parent=Magnetic field}

A magnetic field line is an integral curve tangent to the <magnetic field>. In coordinates $x^i$, it obeys $dx^i/B^i=dx^j/B^j$ wherever the relevant components are nonzero.

= Flux tube
{parent=Magnetic field line}
{wiki}

A flux tube is a bundle of <magnetic field lines> bounded by a surface everywhere tangent to the <magnetic field>. <Gauss's law for magnetism> makes the <magnetic flux> through any transverse cross-section constant along the tube. For a narrow tube with nearly uniform field magnitude $B$ and transverse area $A$, this gives $BA=\mathrm{constant}$.

= Flux-tube area
{parent=Flux tube}
{title2=$A$}

The cross-sectional area of a narrow <flux tube>, measured normal to its <magnetic field>. Constancy of the <magnetic flux> gives $A|\mathbf B|=\mathrm{constant}$ along the tube. This area controls the mass transport when flow follows the field.

= Magnetic-field-line equation
{parent=Magnetic field line}

The magnetic-field-line equation states that the tangent to a field line is parallel to $\mathbf B$. In spherical coordinates for an axisymmetric poloidal field, it can be written $dr/(r\,d\theta)=B_r/B_\theta$.

= Magnetic dipole field
{parent=Magnetic field line}

Outside a localized magnetic dipole $\boldsymbol\mu$, the vacuum field in Gaussian conventions is
$$
\mathbf B(\mathbf r)=\frac{3(\boldsymbol\mu\mathbin\cdot\mathbf r)\mathbf r}{r^5}-\frac{\boldsymbol\mu}{r^3}.
$$
For a dipole along the polar axis, $B_r=2\mu\cos\theta/r^3$ and $B_\theta=\mu\sin\theta/r^3$.

= Dipole magnetic-field line
{parent=Magnetic dipole field}

An axisymmetric dipole field line satisfies $r=C\sin^2\theta$, where $C$ is its equatorial crossing radius. This follows by integrating $dr/(r\,d\theta)=B_r/B_\theta=2\cot\theta$.

= Dipolar flux-tube area
{parent=Dipole magnetic-field line}

Near the polar axis of a <magnetic dipole field>, $B\propto r^{-3}$. Conservation of <magnetic flux> in a <flux tube> consequently gives $A(r)\propto r^3$. A <dipole magnetic-field line> intersecting a sphere of radius $R_\star$ at small polar angle $\theta_\star$ obeys
$$
\theta(r)^2\simeq\theta_\star^2\frac r{R_\star},
\qquad
A(r)\simeq\pi r^2\theta(r)^2
=\pi\theta_\star^2\frac{r^3}{R_\star}.
$$
This area is for one polar tube. The approximation requires $\theta_\star^2r/R_\star\ll1$; the full <magnetic dipole field> does not remain a narrow polar tube at arbitrarily large radius.

= Magnetostatic equilibrium
{parent=Magnetic field}
{wiki}

A magnetostatic equilibrium is a time-independent configuration in which magnetic, pressure, gravitational, and any other body-force densities balance.

= Spherical magnetostatic pressure balance
{parent=Magnetostatic equilibrium}
{title2=$\Delta_*\chi+\psi\psi'=-\mu_0r^2\sin^2\theta\,p'$}

For an axisymmetric <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=\psi\mathbf e_\phi/(r\sin\theta)+\nabla\times[\chi\mathbf e_\phi/(r\sin\theta)]$, the <poloidal magnetic flux function> gives $B_r=\chi_\theta/(r^2\sin\theta)$ and $B_\theta=-\chi_r/(r\sin\theta)$. Force balance implies locally $p=p(\chi)$ and $\psi=\psi(\chi)$ on regular connected flux surfaces. Direct calculation gives $(\nabla\times\mathbf B)\times\mathbf B=-(\Delta_*\chi+\psi\psi')\nabla\chi/(r^2\sin^2\theta)$, with $\Delta_*\chi=\chi_{rr}+\sin\theta\,\partial_\theta(\chi_\theta/\sin\theta)/r^2$. Equating this to $\mu_0\nabla p$ proves the displayed <magnetostatic equilibrium> equation, including its sign and dimensions.

= Confined linear-pressure spherical magnetic equilibrium
{parent=Spherical magnetostatic pressure balance}
{title2=$ (x^2-3)\sin x+3x\cos x=0$}

Set $\psi=\alpha\chi$, $p=p_0+P\chi$ and $\chi=F(r)\sin^2\theta$. A regular family is $F=A[\cos(\alpha r)-\sin(\alpha r)/(\alpha r)]+cr^2$, where $P=-\alpha^2c/\mu_0$. A zero <magnetic field> at radius a requires $F(a)=F'(a)=0$. For nonzero A this yields the displayed condition with $x=\alpha a$ and $c=-A[\cos x-\sin x/x]/a^2$. The boundary pressure is $p_0$, independent of angle. The trigonometric pair is tied by regularity and the radial differential equation; its coefficients are not independent.

= Magnetized isothermal atmosphere
{parent=Magnetostatic equilibrium}

A plane-parallel <magnetostatic equilibrium> with constant downward <gravitational acceleration> $g$, horizontal <magnetic field>, constant <isothermal sound speed> $c_s$, and constant <Alfvén speed> $v_a$ has
$$
H=\frac{c_s^2+v_a^2/2}{g},\qquad \rho=\rho_0e^{-z/H},\qquad p=c_s^2\rho,\qquad B=B_0e^{-z/(2H)}.
$$
Indeed the <magnetohydrodynamic total pressure> is $(c_s^2+v_a^2/2)\rho$, and its vertical derivative is $-g\rho$. The <magnetic pressure> therefore increases the <scale height> above that of an unmagnetized atmosphere.

= Cylindrical magnetostatic equilibrium
{parent=Magnetostatic equilibrium}

A cylindrical magnetostatic equilibrium is an <axisymmetric vector field> and <pressure> configuration independent of the axial coordinate. Its radial <Lorentz force density> balances the <pressure gradient>, as expressed by <cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance>.

= Z-pinch
{c}
{parent=Cylindrical magnetostatic equilibrium}
{wiki}

In a Z-pinch, an axial <electric current> produces a <toroidal magnetic field> whose inward <magnetic tension> confines a plasma. A cylindrical <magnetostatic equilibrium> balances the resulting <Lorentz force density> against a <pressure gradient>.

= Cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance
{parent=Cylindrical magnetostatic equilibrium}

For an <axisymmetric vector field> independent of the axial coordinate and regular on the axis, <Gauss's law for magnetism> gives $B_R=0$. The magnetostatic <Ampère-Maxwell equation> then gives $j_R=0$, $j_\phi=-cB_z'/(4\pi)$, and $j_z=c(RB_\phi)'/(4\pi R)$ in Gaussian units. Radial <magnetostatic equilibrium> is
$$
\frac d{dR}\left(p+\frac{B_z^2+B_\phi^2}{8\pi}\right)+\frac{B_\phi^2}{4\pi R}=0.
$$
For enclosed axial <electric current> $I(R)$, the magnetostatic <Ampère-Maxwell equation> gives $B_\phi=2I/(cR)$, hence
$$
\frac d{dR}\left(p+\frac{B_z^2}{8\pi}\right)
+\frac1{2\pi c^2R^2}\frac{dI^2}{dR}=0.
$$
The first term contains <pressure> and axial <magnetic pressure>; the second combines toroidal <magnetic pressure> with the hoop force from <magnetic tension>.

= Flux-tube axial field virial identity
{parent=Cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance}
{title2=$\langle B_z^2\rangle=B_p^2-2\mu_0\langle p\rangle$}

For a regular cylindrical <flux tube> with $\mathbf B=(0,B_\phi(s),B_z(s))$, vanishing field beyond radius $s_0$, and exterior <pressure> $p_e$, define $\langle h\rangle=2s_0^{-2}\int_0^{s_0}sh(s)\,ds$ and $B_p^2=2\mu_0p_e$. Integrating <cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance> against $s^2$ gives the displayed identity. The toroidal <magnetic pressure> and hoop <magnetic tension> cancel in this cross-sectional axial-field average. Nonnegative gas <pressure> gives $\langle B_z^2\rangle\leq B_p^2$, with strict inequality precisely when $\langle p\rangle>0$.

= Uniform-twist central axial field bound
{parent=Flux-tube axial field virial identity}
{title2=$B_z(0)^2\leq(1+\gamma^2)B_p^2$}

For a <flux tube> with $B_\phi=\gamma(s/s_0)B_z$, regularity at the axis and <cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance> give $B_z(0)^2=B_p^2-2\mu_0p(0)+\gamma^2\langle B_z^2\rangle$. The <flux-tube axial field virial identity> and $p\geq0$ give the bound. A twisted <toroidal magnetic field> can support a central axial field above the untwisted <magnetic pressure> limit even though its cross-sectional mean obeys that limit.

= Power-law pressure-supported axial current
{parent=Cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance}

For constant axial <magnetic field> and $p=p_0(1+R^2/a^2)^{-k}$ with $p_0>0$ and $k>0$, <cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance> gives, with $x=R^2/a^2$,
$$
I^2(R)=2\pi c^2p_0a^2
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle\frac{1-(1+kx)(1+x)^{-k}}{k-1},&k\ne1,\\
\log(1+x)+(1+x)^{-1}-1,&k=1.
\end{cases}
$$
The nontrivial <electric current> is finite at infinity exactly when $k>1$, in which case $I^2(\infty)=2\pi c^2p_0a^2/(k-1)$. Either sign of $I$ is possible because the <pressure> balance fixes its square.

= Magnetic pressure support with vanishing axial field
{parent=Cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance}

With no axial <electric current>, <cylindrical magnetostatic pressure balance> reduces to constant $p+B_z^2/(8\pi)$. For $p=p_0e^{-R^2/a^2}$ and $B_z(0)=0$, its radial solutions are
$$
B_z=\sigma\sqrt{8\pi p_0}\sqrt{1-e^{-R^2/a^2}},
\qquad
j_\phi=-\sigma\frac{c\sqrt{8\pi p_0}}{4\pi a^2}
\frac{R e^{-R^2/a^2}}{\sqrt{1-e^{-R^2/a^2}}},
\qquad \sigma\in\{-1,1\}.
$$
As $R\downarrow0$, $B_z\sim\sigma\sqrt{8\pi p_0}R/a$ and $j_\phi\to-\sigma c\sqrt{8\pi p_0}/(4\pi a)$. These finite scalar limits do not give a <smooth function> of Cartesian position: $R=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ has a cusp at the axis, and the azimuthal unit vector has no unique limit there. Thus these data describe radial equilibrium away from the axis, but cannot satisfy smooth vector-field regularity on the axis without modifying the data.

= Motional electric field
{parent=Magnetic field}

A conductor or plasma moving with nonrelativistic velocity $\mathbf u$ through a magnetic field experiences the motional electric field $\mathbf E=-\mathbf u\times\mathbf B/c$ in Gaussian units when the electric field vanishes in its rest frame.

= London equations
{c}
{parent=Magnetic field}
{wiki}

= London equation
{c}
{synonym}

The London equations relate the superconducting <electric current> to the <electromagnetic field>: $\partial_t\mathbf J=\mathbf E/(\mu_0\lambda_L^2)$ and $\nabla\times\mathbf J=-\mathbf B/(\mu_0\lambda_L^2)$. In a gauge with constant condensate phase the second follows from $\mathbf J=-\mathbf A/(\mu_0\lambda_L^2)$; it implies exponential <magnetic field> screening.

= London penetration depth
{c}
{parent=London equations}
{title2=$\lambda_L=\sqrt{m/(\mu_0n_sq^2)}$}
{wiki}

For uniform vortex-free <superconductivity>, the <London equation> and stationary Maxwell equations give $\nabla^2\mathbf B=\mathbf B/\lambda_L^2$. A field tangential to a planar superconducting half-space decays as $\mathbf B(z)=\mathbf B(0)e^{-z/\lambda_L}$. A nonzero normal boundary field is incompatible with this translation-invariant decaying Meissner solution, because divergence-free magnetic flux would require that component to remain constant.

= Meissner effect
{c}
{parent=London equations}
{wiki}

= Meissner-Ochsenfeld effect
{c}
{synonym}

The <Meissner effect> is the equilibrium expulsion of a sufficiently weak, static <magnetic field> from the bulk of a material exhibiting <superconductivity>. Expulsion includes a <magnetic field> that was already present before cooling: zero <electrical resistance> alone does not predict this. The <field-cooled Meissner experiment> tests that distinction. A finite <London penetration depth> means that the <magnetic field> and screening <electric current> occupy a surface layer, rather than disappearing at a mathematical boundary.<Lecture notes on the electrodynamics of metals and superconductors>[§2, pp. 12–13, Eqs. (64)–(74): acceleration, additional magnetic constraint and penetration length]{ref}

This makes <superconductivity> useful for shielding weak <magnetic fields>, and makes its magnetic response an independent test of the phase. The striking result is that the same screened equilibrium can be reached by two different preparation histories. The assumptions matter: <finite-thickness Meissner screening> can leave an appreciable <magnetic field> throughout a small sample, and the <limits of Meissner screening> distinguish <quantum vortex> penetration and retained <magnetic flux> from this equilibrium state.

= Meissner state
{c}
{parent=Meissner effect}

A <Meissner state> is an equilibrium state of <superconductivity> without <quantum vortex> penetration, with screening <electric currents> responding to the applied <magnetic field>. In a solid, <simply connected> sample much thicker than the <London penetration depth>, the deep interior <magnetic field> is approximately zero. This is a statement about a phase and a geometry, not a rule that every sample exhibiting <superconductivity> always has $\mathbf B=0$. In particular, the <Meissner state> exists for sufficiently weak <magnetic fields> in both <type-I superconductors> and <type-II superconductors>. <University Physics Volume 3 by Samuel J. Ling and others/9.8 Superconductivity>[§9.8, Properties of Superconductors, paragraphs distinguishing type I, type II and their two critical fields]{ref}

= Planar London screening
{c}
{parent=Meissner state}
{title2=$B(z)=B_0e^{-z/\lambda_L}$}

Take a homogeneous material exhibiting <superconductivity> in the <half-space> $z\geq0$, bounded by vacuum at $z=0$. Fix the <temperature> below the <superconducting transition temperature>, take a constant positive <London penetration depth> $\lambda_L$, and assume a local, linear <London equation> description in a <Meissner state>. The applied <magnetic field> is weak enough that it neither nucleates a <quantum vortex> nor appreciably suppresses the material's <superconductivity>. Assume translation invariance in $x,y$, no imposed transport <electric current>, and a tangential boundary <magnetic field> $\mathbf B(0)=B_0\widehat{\mathbf x}$. Here $B_0$ is the local surface <magnetic flux density>, equal to the imposed vacuum <magnetic field> in this infinite planar geometry; it is not automatically the far-away applied value for a finite object.

For a bounded, decaying interior solution, the <London equations>, <magnetostatic Ampère law> and <Gauss's law for magnetism> give
$$
\nabla^2\mathbf B=\frac{\mathbf B}{\lambda_L^2},\qquad
\frac{d^2B_x}{dz^2}=\frac{B_x}{\lambda_L^2},\qquad
B_x(0)=B_0,\quad B_x(\infty)=0.
$$
The solution is
$$
\mathbf B(z)=B_0e^{-z/\lambda_L}\widehat{\mathbf x}.
$$
At one <London penetration depth> the <magnetic field> is $e^{-1}\simeq0.368$ of its surface value; at five it is $e^{-5}\simeq0.00674$. There is no finite depth where this exponential is exactly zero. The <differential equation> excludes the growing exponential through the deep-interior boundary condition. A constant normal component cannot decay in this geometry: <Gauss's law for magnetism> would require $\partial_zB_z=0$. A perpendicular applied <magnetic field> on a finite plate therefore requires a different spatial problem.<Lecture notes on the electrodynamics of metals and superconductors>[§2, p. 13, Eqs. (68)–(74): London screening equation; the planar boundary-value solution here follows directly]{ref}

\Image[meissner-field-and-screening-current-profiles.png]
{title=<Magnetic field> and signed <Meissner screening current> profiles with finite <London penetration depth>}
{description=Original analytic profiles, not measured data. Left: the <planar London screening> solution, with vacuum at $z<0$ and the material at $z\geq0$. Right: <finite-thickness Meissner screening> in a slab of thickness $2\lambda_L$, with equal tangential surface <magnetic fields>. Blue is $B_x/B_0$; orange is the signed <current density> $\mu_0\lambda_LJ_y/B_0$. Different horizontal origins are labeled explicitly.}

= Meissner screening current
{c}
{parent=Planar London screening}
{title2=$J_y(z)=-B_0e^{-z/\lambda_L}/(\mu_0\lambda_L)$}

In <SI> units, the <magnetostatic Ampère law> gives the <current density> supporting <planar London screening>:
$$
\mathbf J=\frac{1}{\mu_0}\nabla\times\mathbf B
=-\frac{B_0}{\mu_0\lambda_L}e^{-z/\lambda_L}\widehat{\mathbf y}.
$$
Here $\mu_0$ is the <vacuum permeability>. The sign follows from $(\nabla\times\mathbf B)_y=\partial_zB_x$ in the chosen coordinates. Thus $B_0>0$ requires <electric current> along $-\widehat{\mathbf y}$. Its own <magnetic field> opposes the imposed <magnetic field> inside the material. The <electric current> is distributed over a finite layer; its depth <integral> is the effective <surface current density>
$$
\mathbf K=\int_0^\infty\mathbf J(z)\,dz
=-\frac{B_0}{\mu_0}\widehat{\mathbf y}.
$$
The units are $\mathrm{A\,m^{-2}}$ for $\mathbf J$ and $\mathrm{A\,m^{-1}}$ for $\mathbf K$. Shrinking $\lambda_L$ at fixed $B_0$ increases the surface <current density>, while this integrated <surface current density> stays fixed. The <current sheet> approximation is the large-length-scale limit of this layer, not a claim that an actual sample carries an infinitely thin <electric current>. It reproduces the <magnetic-field jump across a surface current> between the exterior $B_0\widehat{\mathbf x}$ and a field-free interior, with outward <unit normal> $-\widehat{\mathbf z}$.

= Finite-thickness Meissner screening
{c}
{parent=Meissner state}
{title2=$B_x(z)=B_0\cosh(z/\lambda_L)/\cosh(d/(2\lambda_L))$}

To isolate finite size from <quantum vortex> penetration, keep the same weak-field <London equations> but replace the <half-space> by an infinite slab $-d/2\leq z\leq d/2$. Put equal tangential <magnetic fields> $B_0\widehat{\mathbf x}$ at its two faces. With $z=0$ now at the slab center, the even solution and its signed <current density> are
$$
B_x(z)=B_0\frac{\cosh(z/\lambda_L)}{\cosh(d/(2\lambda_L))},\qquad
J_y(z)=\frac{B_0}{\mu_0\lambda_L}\frac{\sinh(z/\lambda_L)}{\cosh(d/(2\lambda_L))}.
$$
The <hyperbolic cosine> profile has equal boundary values, while the <electric currents> on the two sides have opposite signs. In particular,
$$
\frac{B_x(0)}{B_0}=\frac{1}{\cosh(d/(2\lambda_L))},\qquad
\frac{\overline B_x}{B_0}=\frac{1}{dB_0}\int_{-d/2}^{d/2}B_x(z)\,dz
=\frac{2\lambda_L}{d}\tanh\!\left(\frac{d}{2\lambda_L}\right).
$$
For the stipulated thickness $d=2\lambda_L$, the center retains about $0.648B_0$ and the mean is about $0.762B_0$, even though this is a <Meissner state>. For $d\gg\lambda_L$, the center <magnetic field> is exponentially small and the mean is about $2\lambda_LB_0/d$, reflecting the two surface layers. For $d\ll\lambda_L$, the mean tends to $B_0$: little screening is possible across such a short distance. These are analytic model examples, not material measurements. Real finite objects also need their <demagnetizing field> and edge geometry accounted for; the slab calculation only isolates the effect of thickness.

= Meissner effect and flux history
{c}
{parent=Meissner effect}
{title2=$\partial_t\mathbf B=0\text{ when }\mathbf E=0$}

Compare two ideal models for a stationary, solid, <simply connected> sample. In the <perfect conductor> flux-history model the interior <electric field> is constrained to zero from the moment perfect conduction starts. <Faraday's law of induction> then gives
$$
\partial_t\mathbf B=-\nabla\times\mathbf E=0.
$$
Consequently the interior <magnetic field> retains its value at that moment. Cooling in an already applied <magnetic field> freezes a nonzero interior value; starting perfect conduction in zero <magnetic field> and then applying a <magnetic field> preserves zero. The zero initial interior <magnetic field> often assumed in <perfect conductor> wave-reflection problems is one particular preparation, not a general prediction of expulsion. <University Physics Volume 3 by Samuel J. Ling and others/9.8 Superconductivity>[§9.8, Eq. (9.38) and the argument that zero resistivity conserves existing flux rather than expelling it]{ref}

\Image[meissner-cooling-and-field-history.png]
{title=Two preparation histories distinguish the <Meissner state> from <perfect conductor> flux memory}
{description=Original schematic end states for a solid sample much thicker than its <London penetration depth>, not measured data or transition kinetics. Both rows end at the same low <temperature> and applied weak <magnetic field>. Blue arrows indicate interior <magnetic field>; orange edges represent screening <electric currents>. The near-zero interior in the <Meissner state> omits its finite penetration layer. The <perfect conductor> panel uses the ideal zero-interior-<electric field> constraint.}

The <Meissner state> instead selects the screened equilibrium after either preparation, provided that equilibrium is accessible and the applied <magnetic field> lies in the <Meissner state> regime. The first <London equation> alone illustrates the missing information: at fixed $\lambda_L$, combining it with <Faraday's law of induction> gives
$$
\partial_t\!\left(\nabla\times\mathbf J+\frac{\mathbf B}{\mu_0\lambda_L^2}\right)=0.
$$
This permits an arbitrary time-independent integration field. The second <London equation> imposes the additional zero value of that bracket in the vortex-free local model.<Lecture notes on the electrodynamics of metals and superconductors>[§2, pp. 12–13, Eqs. (64), (66)–(68): time-independent freedom and the second London equation]{ref} Establishing <superconductivity> during cooling is not the fixed-parameter, zero-<electric field> model: transient <electric fields> accompany changing <magnetic flux>, and $\lambda_L$ itself depends on <temperature>. The equilibrium <London equations> predict final spatial screening, not the cooling rate or the time-resolved expulsion pulse.

= Meissner effect discovery
{c}
{parent=Meissner effect}

The 1933 report https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01504252[Ein neuer Effekt bei Eintritt der Supraleitfähigkeit by W. Meissner and R. Ochsenfeld] describes cooling tin and <lead> cylinders in a fixed transverse <magnetic field>. Two parallel cylinders were about $140\,\mathrm{mm}$ long and $3\,\mathrm{mm}$ thick, separated by $1.5\,\mathrm{mm}$. A roughly $10\,\mathrm{mm}$ rotating search coil between them drove a ballistic <galvanometer>. At about $5\,\mathrm{G}$, the reported below/above-transition induction ratios were $1.70$ for tin single crystals and $1.77$ for polycrystalline <lead>. The increased gap <magnetic field> indicates redistribution outside the cylinders, rather than an unchanged frozen field. Coil-winding geometry and noncircular tin specimens limited comparison with the ideal prediction. <Ein neuer Effekt bei Eintritt der Supraleitfähigkeit by Walther Meissner and others>[1933 report, first arrangement; English translation PDF pp. 1–2, printed p. 119, alongside the German text]{ref}

Their second arrangement used a <lead> tube, about $130\,\mathrm{mm}$ long with $3\,\mathrm{mm}$ outer and $2\,\mathrm{mm}$ inner diameters. A rotating search coil could sit inside or alongside it. The bore retained <magnetic flux> after removing the applied <magnetic field>; the coil nearly filled the bore, preventing a test of interior field uniformity. A hole containing <magnetic field> is distinct from <magnetic field> penetrating the surrounding material exhibiting <superconductivity>. <Ein neuer Effekt bei Eintritt der Supraleitfähigkeit by Walther Meissner and others>[1933 report, second arrangement; translation PDF p. 2, printed p. 119, bore retention and explicitly stated spatial-resolution limitation]{ref}

The modern <field-cooled Meissner experiment> provides a complementary readout. MIT's probe I places a sample in a brass holder, with an $810$-turn test coil of $7.1\,\mathrm{mm}$ inner diameter, $10.5\,\mathrm{mm}$ outer diameter and $12.3\,\mathrm{mm}$ length, inside a longer <solenoid>. A nearby silicon-diode <thermometer> tracks cooling by helium gas. With <lead> still normal, the guide applies about $200\,\mathrm{mA}$ to the <solenoid> and connects the test coil to a current-integrator box and <oscilloscope>. Cooling produces a transient; warming gives the opposite sign. Switching off the <solenoid> while the sample is normal checks the sign. <Superconductivity: The Meissner Effect, Persistent Currents and the Josephson Effects by MIT Department of Physics>[Probe I, §4.2 and Fig. 6, printed pp. 5–6; §4.3 temperature control; §5.2.2, printed p. 10, DC field cooling and sign control]{ref}

The inferred change is in linked <magnetic flux>, not a direct scan of $B(z)$. For a stationary test coil the pulse obeys $\int V\,dt=-\Delta\Psi$, where $\Psi$ is its <magnetic flux linkage>. An integrator or AC-coupled <oscilloscope> needs its transfer response accounted for before extracting this pulse area. Keep the source <electric current> fixed during cooling, monitor <temperature>, and compare warming and cooling slowly enough to reduce sample–<thermometer> lag. An AC <inductance> change alone can reflect changed normal-state <electrical conductivity>; the fixed-field expulsion test distinguishes that alternative. <Superconductivity: The Meissner Effect, Persistent Currents and the Josephson Effects by MIT Department of Physics>[§5.1, printed pp. 8–9, thermometer lag; §5.2.2, printed p. 10, limitation of AC mutual-inductance evidence]{ref} Neither this coil readout nor the historical gap-field measurement resolves the microscopic <London penetration depth> by itself.

= Limits of Meissner screening
{c}
{parent=Meissner effect}

For a bulk <type-II superconductor> with negligible <demagnetizing field>, raising the applied <magnetic field intensity> past the lower critical value $H_{c1}$ favors a <superconducting mixed state> containing <quantum vortex> lines; <superconductivity> survives until the upper critical value $H_{c2}$. The <magnetic field> extends around each vortex over a penetration scale, not solely inside its core. Thus a nonzero average <magnetic flux density> can coexist with <superconductivity>. Below $H_{c1}$, a <type-II superconductor> also supports a <Meissner state>; a vortex-free state above the equilibrium entry field may instead be <metastable>. <University Physics Volume 3 by Samuel J. Ling and others/9.8 Superconductivity>[§9.8, type-II critical-field and mixed-state paragraphs; the source uses B_c1 and B_c2 for applied-field scales]{ref}

Real preparation histories may leave <quantum vortex> lines held in place by material inhomogeneities, retaining <magnetic flux> and producing <hysteresis>. This <flux pinning> restricts access to equilibrium: it is different from the arbitrary frozen interior <magnetic field> of the ideal <perfect conductor> model. Harvard's vortex-imaging work illustrates strong pinning to bulk disorder in a <type-II superconductor>. <Scanning Tunneling Microscopy Research by Hoffman Laboratory>[Vortex Imaging in Iron Pnictide Superconductor, Fig. 2 and the discussion of disordered vortices and bulk pinning]{ref} Separately, <flux conservation in a superconducting loop> and <fluxoid and flux quantization in a superconductor> explain why a hole can retain <magnetic flux>. Neither bore retention, vortex penetration nor weak screening in a thin sample should be silently identified with the equilibrium <planar London screening> calculation.

= Magnetic flux
{parent=Magnetic field}
{title2=$\Phi_B$}
{wiki}

<Magnetic flux> measures the signed amount of <magnetic field> crossing an <oriented surface> $S$. Choose a <unit normal> $\widehat{\mathbf n}$ and use the <oriented surface element> $d\mathbf S=\widehat{\mathbf n}\,dA$. The defining <surface integral> is
$$
\Phi_B=\int_S\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S
=\int_S\mathbf B\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}\,dA.
$$
For a uniform <magnetic field> across a flat <surface> of <area> $A$, the <dot product> reduces this to
$$
\Phi_B=BA\cos\theta,
$$
where $\theta$ is the <angle> between the <magnetic field> and the chosen <unit normal>, rather than the <plane> of the <surface>. The <SI> unit is the <weber (unit)>, $1\,\mathrm{Wb}=1\,\mathrm{T\,m^2}$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-1-faradays-law[OpenStax, magnetic flux and its orientation]{ref}.

For a stipulated <magnetic field> $B=2\,\mathrm T$ and <area> $A=3\,\mathrm{m^2}$, the <magnetic flux> is $6\,\mathrm{Wb}$ at $\theta=0$, $3\,\mathrm{Wb}$ at $\theta=\pi/3$, and zero at $\theta=\pi/2$. In the last case the <magnetic field> lies in the <plane> of the <surface>, so it does not cross it. Reversing the chosen <unit normal> reverses the sign of <magnetic flux> without changing the physical <magnetic field>; at $\theta=\pi$ the same example gives $-6\,\mathrm{Wb}$.

\Image[magnetic-flux-orientation.png]
{title=<Magnetic flux> as a flat <surface> rotates in a fixed <magnetic field>}
{description=Left: an edge-on <surface> and its <unit normal> at three <angles> to the fixed <magnetic field>. Right: the signed <magnetic flux> $\Phi_B=6\cos\theta\,\mathrm{Wb}$, including reversal of the <unit normal>. The stipulated <magnetic field> is $2\,\mathrm T$ and the <area> is $3\,\mathrm{m^2}$.}

The geometric idea is projected <area>: tilting a flat <surface> reduces the <area> seen perpendicular to the <magnetic field> by the factor $\cos\theta$. This explains how rotation alone can change <magnetic flux> even when the <magnetic field> is constant; <Faraday's law of induction> turns such changes into an <electromotive force>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-1-faradays-law[OpenStax, induction from changing magnetic flux]{ref}.

= Weber
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Magnetic flux}
{title2=$\mathrm{Wb}$}
{wiki}

The <weber (unit)> is the <SI> unit of <magnetic flux>, with $1\,\mathrm{Wb}=1\,\mathrm{T\,m^2}$. A uniform <magnetic field> of $1\,\mathrm T$ crossing a flat <surface> of <area> $1\,\mathrm{m^2}$ along its chosen <unit normal> gives $1\,\mathrm{Wb}$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-1-faradays-law[OpenStax, magnetic-flux units]{ref}.

= Capacitance
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$C$}
{wiki}

The <capacitance> of a linear two-terminal <capacitor> is the constant relating its separated <electric charge> to its terminal <electric potential difference>:
$$
Q=CV,\qquad C=\frac QV\quad(V\ne0).
$$
Here $Q$ is the <electric charge> on one <electrical conductor>, the other carries $-Q$, and $V$ is their <electric potential difference> with the same ordering. Reversing the terminal ordering reverses both $Q$ and $V$ and leaves <capacitance> unchanged. An ideal passive <capacitor> has $C\ge0$; an ordinary pair of separated <electrical conductors> with positive <permittivity> has $C>0$. At $V=0$, use $Q=CV$ rather than the undefined ratio $0/0$.

For example, <electric charge> magnitude $Q=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}$ at terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$ gives <capacitance> $C=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$. At $20\,\mathrm V$, the same linear <capacitor> has $Q=1.7708\,\mathrm{nC}$ and the same <capacitance>. The plots in <capacitor energy> show that <capacitance> is the <slope> of the <electric charge> versus terminal <electric potential difference> graph.

For fixed geometry and linear material response, <capacitance> depends on dimensions and <permittivity>, rather than the source's terminal <electric potential difference>. This is why <capacitance> is a useful property of a component: once it is known, $Q=CV$ predicts the separated <electric charge> for each operating terminal <electric potential difference>. The <SI> unit is the <farad>, $1\,\mathrm F=1\,\mathrm{C/V}$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: capacitance and its unit]{ref}.

For combinations of <capacitors>, the same terminal definition gives an equivalent <capacitance>. Two identical <capacitors> of <capacitance> $C$ connected in parallel share the same terminal <electric potential difference>, and their <electric charges> add, giving $C_{\rm eq}=2C$. Connected in series with an initially neutral isolated junction, they carry equal plate <electric charge> magnitudes and their terminal <electric potential differences> add, giving $C_{\rm eq}=C/2$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-2-capacitors-in-series-and-in-parallel[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.2: capacitors in series and in parallel]{ref}.

= Farad
{parent=Capacitance}
{title2=$\mathrm F$}
{wiki}

The <farad>, symbol $\mathrm F$, is the <SI> unit of <capacitance>:
$$
1\,\mathrm F=1\,\frac{\mathrm C}{\mathrm V}.
$$
A <capacitor> of <capacitance> $1\,\mathrm F$ carries separated <electric charge> magnitude $1\,\mathrm C$ at terminal <electric potential difference> $1\,\mathrm V$. A picofarad is $10^{-12}\,\mathrm F$, so the worked <parallel-plate capacitor>'s <capacitance> $88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$ is $8.854\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm F$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: the farad]{ref}.

= Capacitance of an isolated conducting sphere
{parent=Capacitance}
{title2=$C=4\pi\epsilon R$}

An isolated conducting sphere of radius $R$ in a medium of <permittivity> $\epsilon$ has surface <electrostatic potential> $V=Q/(4\pi\epsilon R)$ relative to infinity. Thus its <capacitance> is $C=Q/V=4\pi\epsilon R$.

= Sphere-plane capacitance
{parent=Capacitance}
{title2=$C(R,h)$}

For a conducting sphere of radius $R$ at center height $h>R$ above a grounded infinite plane,
$$
C(R,h)=4\pi\epsilon R\sinh\alpha\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac1{\sinh(n\alpha)},
\qquad\cosh\alpha=\frac hR.
$$
The distance $h$ is measured to the center, so the surface gap is $h-R$. Relative to the <capacitance of an isolated conducting sphere>, the enhancement is $c(R/h)=C(R,h)/(4\pi\epsilon R)$.

The <method of images> gives a constructive proof. At fixed sphere potential $V_s$, start with $q_0=4\pi\epsilon RV_s$ at height $z_0=h$ and place its opposite image below the plane. Cancel that image's nonconstant sphere potential by the successive charges
$$
q_{n+1}=\frac R{h+z_n}q_n,
\qquad z_{n+1}=h-\frac{R^2}{h+z_n},
$$
with opposite mirror charges below the plane at every step. The resulting sphere potential is $V_s$ and the plane potential is zero. Since $q_n=4\pi\epsilon RV_s\sinh\alpha/\sinh((n+1)\alpha)$, summing the sphere charges gives the displayed <capacitance>. In particular $c(y)=1+y/2+O(y^2)$ for small $y$.

The exact sphere-plane boundary-value problem is also treated in https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6034[Behunin and colleagues' analysis of sphere-plane electrostatics].

= Capacitance per unit length
{parent=Capacitance}

Capacitance per unit length is the charge per unit length divided by the potential difference. A vacuum coaxial cable with radii $a<b$ has
$$
C'=\frac{2\pi\varepsilon_0}{\log(b/a)}.
$$

= Inductance
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$L$}
{wiki}

<Inductance> relates <electric currents> to the <magnetic flux linkage> they produce. For a single linear coil with fixed geometry and magnetic response, its <self-inductance> $L$ is the constant in
$$
\Lambda=LI.
$$
Here $\Lambda$ is <magnetic flux linkage> from the coil's own <electric current> $I$. The <SI> unit of <inductance> is the <henry (unit)>. Knowing <inductance> lets us predict the induced <electromotive force> when <electric current> changes, and the <magnetic energy> stored by an <inductor>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: self-inductance and inductors]{ref}.

For example, a coil of <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$ carrying <electric current> $3\,\mathrm A$ has <magnetic flux linkage> $1.5\,\mathrm{Wb}$. If its $100$ turns link the same <magnetic flux>, each turn has <magnetic flux> $0.015\,\mathrm{Wb}$. Keeping the geometry and magnetic response fixed while doubling <electric current> doubles <magnetic flux linkage> and quadruples the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy>.

The comparison with <capacitance> is useful: a linear <capacitor> has $Q=CV$ and stores <capacitor energy> $CV^2/2$ in its <electric field>, whereas a linear <inductor> has $\Lambda=LI$ and stores <magnetic energy> $LI^2/2$ in its <magnetic field>. The <RLC circuit> brings both forms of <energy> storage together.

= Magnetic flux linkage
{parent=Inductance}
{title2=$\Lambda=\sum_{k=1}^N\Phi_k$}

= Flux linkage
{synonym}

<Magnetic flux linkage> adds the signed <magnetic fluxes> through the turns of a coil:
$$
\Lambda=\sum_{k=1}^N\Phi_k,\qquad
\Phi_k=\int_{S_k}\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S.
$$
Choose each <oriented surface> $S_k$ consistently with positive <electric current> around its turn using the <right-hand rule>. If all $N$ turns link approximately the same <magnetic flux> $\Phi$, then $\Lambda\approx N\Phi$; equality holds when every turn links exactly the same <magnetic flux>. Uniform <magnetic field> perpendicular to identical flat turns of <area> $A$ gives $\Phi=BA$. For a stipulated $100$-turn coil with <magnetic flux> $0.015\,\mathrm{Wb}$ per turn, <magnetic flux linkage> is $1.5\,\mathrm{Wb}$, not $0.015\,\mathrm{Wb}$. The turn count is dimensionless. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: flux through the turns of a coil]{ref}.

= Henry
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Inductance}
{title2=$\mathrm H$}
{wiki}

The <henry (unit)> is the <SI> unit of <inductance>:
$$
1\,\mathrm H=1\,\frac{\mathrm{Wb}}{\mathrm A}
=1\,\frac{\mathrm{V\,s}}{\mathrm A}.
$$
A coil with <self-inductance> $1\,\mathrm H$ has <magnetic flux linkage> $1\,\mathrm{Wb}$ at <electric current> $1\,\mathrm A$. A rate of change of <electric current> $1\,\mathrm{A/s}$ produces induced <electromotive force> of magnitude $1\,\mathrm V$. The <weber (unit)> measures <magnetic flux> and <magnetic flux linkage>; the <henry (unit)> measures their ratio to <electric current>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: the unit of self-inductance]{ref}.

= Self-inductance
{parent=Inductance}
{title2=$\Lambda=LI,\quad\mathcal E_{\rm ind}=-L\dot I$}

<Self-inductance> is <inductance> associated with a coil's own <electric current>, rather than <magnetic flux> supplied by another coil. In the linear model, fixed geometry and magnetic response give constant $L$ in $\Lambda=LI$. With the turn orientations defined in <magnetic flux linkage>, <Faraday's law of induction> gives the self-induced <electromotive force>
$$
\mathcal E_{\rm ind}=-\frac{d\Lambda}{dt}=-L\frac{dI}{dt}.
$$
The final equality requires constant $L$; if the geometry changes, use $-d\Lambda/dt$. The minus sign expresses <Lenz's law>: the induced <electromotive force> opposes the change in <electric current>. It does not always oppose the <electric current> itself. When positive <electric current> decreases, the induced <electromotive force> acts in its direction. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: self-induced emf and its direction]{ref}.

For terminal sign bookkeeping, label the ideal <inductor>'s terminals $a,b$, choose positive <electric current> entering $a$ and leaving $b$, and define $v_L$ as the terminal <electric potential difference> drop from $a$ to $b$ in the lumped <electrical circuit> model. This passive convention gives
$$
v_L=+L\frac{dI}{dt}=-\mathcal E_{\rm ind}.
$$
Thus a coil with <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$ and increasing <electric current> $dI/dt=12\,\mathrm{A/s}$ has terminal drop $v_L=6\,\mathrm V$ and induced <electromotive force> $\mathcal E_{\rm ind}=-6\,\mathrm V$. These signs describe the same process with different reference conventions. The <RL circuit> uses the terminal-drop convention throughout.

A finite terminal <electric potential difference> cannot make the <electric current> of an ideal <inductor> with constant $L>0$ jump. Integrating its <differential equation> gives
$$
I(t_2)-I(t_1)=\frac1L\int_{t_1}^{t_2}v_L(t)\,dt.
$$
If $v_L$ stays bounded, the <integral> tends to zero as the interval shrinks, ensuring <continuity> of <electric current>. A step in terminal <electric potential difference> changes $dI/dt$ immediately, while <electric current> remains continuous. At constant <electric current>, the self-induced <electromotive force> is zero, although the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy> can remain nonzero.

= Inductor
{parent=Self-inductance}
{wiki}

An <inductor> is an <electrical circuit> component designed to provide <self-inductance>, often a coil such as a <solenoid>. An ideal linear <inductor> has constant $L>0$ and zero <electrical resistance>. Its stored <magnetic energy>, with zero chosen at zero <electric current>, is
$$
U=\frac12LI^2.
$$
For <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$ and <electric current> $3\,\mathrm A$, this gives $U=2.25\,\mathrm J$. Unlike <Joule heating>, ideal storage is recoverable: the <inductor> can return <energy> to an <electrical circuit> when its <electric current> decreases. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-3-energy-in-a-magnetic-field[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.3: energy stored by an inductor]{ref}.

The short derivation uses the terminal convention in <self-inductance>. The <power> absorbed by an ideal <inductor> is
$$
P_L=v_L I=LI\frac{dI}{dt}=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac12LI^2\right).
$$
Starting from zero <electric current>, the <integral> of this <power> gives
$$
U=\int_0^t P_L(t')\,dt'=\int_0^I Li\,di=\frac12LI^2.
$$
Positive $P_L$ increases stored <magnetic energy>; negative $P_L$ returns <energy>. At constant <electric current> $3\,\mathrm A$ in the example, $P_L=0$ and induced <electromotive force> is zero, but $U=2.25\,\mathrm J$ still resides in the <magnetic field>. The <battery> in the steady <RL circuit> supplies <Joule heating> in the <electrical resistance>, rather than further increasing the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy>.

\Image[inductor-energy-storage-and-release.png]
{title=Stored <magnetic energy> and its release as <Joule heating> in an <RL circuit>}
{description=Left: the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy> during buildup and a separate decay experiment starting at $3\,\mathrm A$. Right: the <power> of <Joule heating> during decay; the shaded <integral> over all time is $2.25\,\mathrm J$. The decay path stays closed through <electrical resistance> $2\,\Omega$ and <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$.}

During the closed-loop release in <RL circuit>, stored <magnetic energy> plus accumulated <Joule heating> stays $2.25\,\mathrm J$. A real coil also has <electrical resistance>, so its <Joule heating> must be included separately from ideal <inductor> storage. The constant-$L$ formulas assume a fixed linear magnetic response.

= Bound charge and bound current
{parent=Electromagnetism}

Polarization and magnetization produce the effective sources
$$
\rho_{\rm b}=-\nabla\cdot P,
\qquad
J_{\rm b}=\partial_tP+\nabla\times M.
$$
Defining $D=\epsilon_0E+P$ and $H=B/\mu_0-M$ moves these sources into the macroscopic fields.

= Bound current
{parent=Bound charge and bound current}
{title2=$\mathbf J_b=\partial_t\mathbf P+\nabla\times\mathbf M$}

The current associated with <electric polarization> and <magnetization> in matter. If the magnetic response is neglected, it is $\mathbf J_b=\partial_t\mathbf P$. It is distinguished from a free conduction current in the <Maxwell equations in matter>.

= Polarization density
{title2=$\mathbf P$}
{parent=Bound charge and bound current}
{wiki}

= Electric polarization
{synonym}

Electric polarization is electric dipole moment per unit volume. It produces bound volume and surface charge
$$
\rho_{\rm b}=-\nabla\cdot\mathbf P,
\qquad
\sigma_{\rm b}=\mathbf P\cdot\mathbf n.
$$
Across an interface, the net bound sheet charge is $(\mathbf P_1-\mathbf P_2)\cdot\mathbf n$, where $\mathbf n$ points from medium one to medium two.

= Electric displacement field
{title2=$\mathbf D$}
{parent=Polarization density}
{wiki}

The electric displacement is $\mathbf D=\epsilon_0\mathbf E+\mathbf P$ and obeys $\nabla\cdot\mathbf D=\rho_{\rm free}$. In a linear isotropic dielectric, $\mathbf D=\epsilon\mathbf E$.

= Magnetization
{title2=$\mathbf M$}
{parent=Bound charge and bound current}
{wiki}

Magnetization is magnetic dipole moment per unit volume. It produces bound volume and surface currents
$$
\mathbf J_{\rm b}=\nabla\times\mathbf M,
\qquad
\mathbf K_{\rm b}=\mathbf M\times\mathbf n.
$$
Across an interface, the net bound sheet current is $(\mathbf M_1-\mathbf M_2)\times\mathbf n$.

= Paramagnetism
{parent=Magnetization}
{wiki}
{title2=$\chi>0$}

In a linear isotropic regime, a paramagnetic material has positive <magnetic susceptibility>: its induced <magnetization> is aligned with the applied <magnetic field intensity>. <Molecular oxygen> supplies an example discussed through <molecular orbital theory> in the <covalent bond> article.

= Curie temperature
{c}
{parent=Magnetization}
{title2=$T_C$}
{wiki}

The Curie temperature is the transition temperature above which a ferromagnetic material loses spontaneous <magnetization>. A hot conducting planetary core can generate a <magnetic field> through <dynamo action> without behaving as a permanent magnet. The blocking temperature at which a mineral records crustal remanence also depends on its relaxation timescale and need not equal its Curie temperature.

= Remanent magnetization
{parent=Magnetization}

= Remanence
{synonym}

Remanent magnetization is <magnetization> retained after the applied <magnetic field> has been removed. Magnetic minerals cooling in an ambient field can preserve information about that field in a planetary crust. Such a record may outlive the <planetary dynamo> which originally produced it. Crustal remanence therefore differs from a present field maintained by conducting-fluid motion.

= Magnetization current
{title2=$J_{\mathrm{mag}}^\mu$}
{parent=Magnetization}

A relativistic magnetization current has the form $J_{\mathrm{mag}}^\mu=\partial_\nu M^{\nu\mu}$ for an antisymmetric polarization-magnetization tensor. It is identically conserved because commuting derivatives contract an antisymmetric tensor.

= Magnetic field intensity
{title2=$\mathbf H$}
{parent=Magnetization}

The magnetic field intensity is $\mathbf H=\mathbf B/\mu_0-\mathbf M$ and obeys $\nabla\times\mathbf H=\mathbf J_{\rm free}$ in magnetostatics. In a linear isotropic medium, $\mathbf B=\mu\mathbf H$.

= Magnetic spherical-shell matching
{parent=Electromagnetism}

Across an interface with no free surface current, the normal component of $B$ and tangential component of $H$ are continuous. For an axisymmetric spherical shell, harmonic scalar potentials have dipole form $(ar+b/r^2)\cos\theta$ in each radial region.

= Maxwell stress tensor
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

With electromagnetic momentum density $\mathbf g=\epsilon_0\mathbf E\times\mathbf B$, the momentum-flux convention
$$
\sigma_{ij}=-\epsilon_0E_iE_j+\frac{\epsilon_0}{2}E^2\delta_{ij}
-\frac1{\mu_0}B_iB_j+\frac1{2\mu_0}B^2\delta_{ij}
$$
satisfies
$$
\partial_tg_j+\partial_i\sigma_{ij}=-(\rho\mathbf E+\mathbf J\times\mathbf B)_j.
$$

= Electromagnetic momentum density
{title2=$\mathbf g=\epsilon_0\mathbf E\times\mathbf B=\mathbf S/c^2$}
{parent=Maxwell stress tensor}

The electromagnetic momentum density in vacuum is $\mathbf g=\epsilon_0\mathbf E\times\mathbf B$. It equals the <Poynting vector> divided by $c^2$, expressing the relativistic relation between energy flux and momentum density.

= Local conservation of electromagnetic momentum
{parent=Maxwell stress tensor}

With the momentum-flux sign convention used for the <Maxwell stress tensor>, Maxwell's equations imply
$$
\nabla\mathbin{\cdot}\boldsymbol\sigma
+\partial_t\mathbf g=-\mathbf f_{\rm L},
$$
where $\mathbf g$ is the <electromagnetic momentum density> and $\mathbf f_{\rm L}$ is the <Lorentz force density> exerted by the field on matter. Thus total field-plus-matter momentum is locally conserved.

= Radiation pressure on a perfectly absorbing surface
{parent=Maxwell stress tensor}

A normally incident vacuum wave of time-averaged intensity $I$ transfers momentum $I/c$ per unit area and time to a perfectly absorbing surface, so its radiation pressure is
$$
p=\frac Ic.
$$
A perfect reflector reverses the normal momentum and gives $2I/c$.

= Proca action
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

The Proca action adds $m^2A_\mu A^\mu/2$ to the Maxwell Lagrangian. It describes a massive spin-one field and explicitly breaks the Abelian gauge symmetry of the massless theory.

= Proca equation
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}

A massive vector field coupled to a conserved current obeys
$$
\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}-m^2A^\nu=-\mu_0J^\nu.
$$
The mass term breaks electromagnetic gauge invariance.

= Proca field
{c}
{parent=Proca equation}

A Proca field is a massive spin-one vector field. Its field equation enforces transversality and leaves three physical polarizations.

= Proca propagator
{c}
{parent=Proca field}

For metric signature $(+---)$, the momentum-space Feynman propagator of a Proca field is
$$
\widetilde\Delta_{\mu\nu}(p)=\frac{-i}{p^2-m^2+i\epsilon}\left(\eta_{\mu\nu}-\frac{p_\mu p_\nu}{m^2}\right).
$$
The longitudinal numerator term inverts the massive vector kinetic operator without gauge fixing.

= High-energy obstruction for a hard vector mass
{parent=Proca propagator}
{title2=$D_{\mu\nu}(k)=-i(g_{\mu\nu}-k_\mu k_\nu/m^2)/(k^2-m^2+i0)$}

A free <Proca field> is consistent and has three physical <particle polarizations>. The difficulty for generic interactions is the ultraviolet behavior of its <Proca propagator>: the longitudinal numerator makes part of it approach order $1/m^2$, rather than decay as $1/k^2$. Longitudinal external <polarization vectors> also behave as $\varepsilon_L^\mu=k^\mu/m+O(m/E)$. Without suitable identities and cancellations, amplitudes grow with energy and perturbative counterterms of increasing dimension are needed. Conserved-current Abelian models and the <Stueckelberg mechanism> are important exceptions; a massive interacting vector is not intrinsically inconsistent as an <effective field theory>.

= Proca time-ordering contact term
{c}
{parent=Proca propagator}
{title2=$\Delta^{\rm cov}_{ab}-\Delta^{T}_{ab}=i\delta_a^0\delta_b^0\delta^{(4)}/m^2$}

For a free massive <Proca field> in the mostly-plus <Minkowski metric>, the covariant inverse of the quadratic <action> and the canonical <time-ordered product> of vector potentials differ locally. With <Fourier transform> $e^{iq\cdot(x-y)}$, the covariant <Proca propagator> is $-i(\eta_{ab}+q_aq_b/m^2)/(q^2+m^2-i0)$, while the literal canonical <two-point correlation function> subtracts $i\delta_a^0\delta_b^0/m^2$ in momentum space. The time component is nondynamical; its physical-mode numerator is $|\boldsymbol q|^2/m^2$, rather than $-1+(q^0)^2/m^2$. The difference is an instantaneous <Dirac delta function>, not another propagating state. Both expressions agree away from coincidence. This distinction matters when identifying a covariant <Green function> with a canonical correlator in a constrained field theory.

= Transverse vector field
{parent=Proca field}

A vector field is transverse when $\partial_\mu V^\mu=0$ in spacetime, or $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf V=0$ for a spatial Fourier mode.

= Lorenz constraint in Proca theory
{parent=Proca equation}
{c}

Taking a divergence and using antisymmetry of $F^{\mu\nu}$ and current conservation gives $m^2\partial_\mu A^\mu=0$. For nonzero mass, the Lorenz condition follows from the field equation rather than from a gauge choice.

= Yukawa potential
{parent=Proca equation}
{c}
{wiki}

The static Green function of $\nabla^2-m^2$ in three dimensions is proportional to $e^{-mr}/r$. It describes an interaction screened beyond range $m^{-1}$ and tends to the Coulomb potential as $m\to0$.

= Relativistic Lorentz force
{parent=Electromagnetism}

$m\,du^\mu/d\tau=qF^\mu{}_\nu u^\nu$ gives $d\mathbf p/dt=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$ and $d(\gamma mc^2)/dt=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$.

= Constant-field relativistic Lorentz-force solution
{parent=Relativistic Lorentz force}

For a constant <electromagnetic field tensor>, the <relativistic Lorentz force> is a constant-coefficient linear system on <four-velocity>, with solution
$$
u(\tau)=\exp\!\left(\frac qmF\tau\right)u(0).
$$
The electric-type blocks of $F$ generate hyperbolic rotations, while magnetic-type blocks generate ordinary rotations.

= Relativistic motion in parallel electric and magnetic fields
{parent=Constant-field relativistic Lorentz-force solution}

When uniform $\mathbf E$ and $\mathbf B$ are parallel, the <relativistic Lorentz force> splits into independent hyperbolic motion along the fields and circular motion in the perpendicular plane. The circle is periodic in <proper time>, while electric acceleration makes the helix's longitudinal pitch grow exponentially from one proper-time cyclotron period to the next.

= Relativistic hyperbolic motion in a uniform electric field
{parent=Relativistic Lorentz force}

A particle released from rest in a constant electric field parallel to its motion follows a spacetime hyperbola and has rapidity proportional to proper time.

This force law realizes <hyperbolic motion> with constant <proper acceleration>.

= Null crossed electromagnetic field
{parent=Relativistic Lorentz force}

Perpendicular fields with $\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf B=0$ and $E^2-c^2B^2=0$ form a null electromagnetic field. The constant-field Lorentz-force generator is nilpotent, so its exponential terminates as a polynomial.

Both invariants of the <electromagnetic field tensor> vanish for this field class.

= Relativistic trajectory in a constant null crossed field
{parent=Null crossed electromagnetic field}

Choose
$$
\mathbf B=B\,\mathbf e_z,\qquad
\mathbf E=cB\,\mathbf e_x,
\qquad
\Omega=\frac{qB}{m}.
$$
A particle released from rest at the origin has the proper-time trajectory
$$
t(\tau)=\tau+\frac{\Omega^2\tau^3}{6},
\qquad
x(\tau)=\frac{c\Omega\tau^2}{2},
\qquad
y(\tau)=-\frac{c\Omega^2\tau^3}{6},
\qquad z(\tau)=0.
$$
Eliminating <proper time> gives the semicubical parabola
$$
\boxed{y^2=\frac{2qB}{9mc}x^3.}
$$
This signed coefficient assumes that the positive $x$ direction is chosen along $\mathbf E$; reversing that axis changes the signs of both $x^3$ and its coefficient.

= Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A velocity cross magnetic-field force bends perpendicular motion into a circle while doing no work.

= Magnetic angular-momentum invariant
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}
{title2=$\mathbf L\cdot\mathbf B+\frac q2|\mathbf r\times\mathbf B|^2$}

For a particle obeying $m\dot{\mathbf v}=q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$ in a constant uniform <magnetic field>, the displayed combination is conserved. The derivative of mechanical <angular momentum> projected onto the field is canceled by the quadratic position term. This is the rotational analogue of the distinction between mechanical and <canonical momentum> in an electromagnetic field.

= Helical motion in a uniform magnetic field
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}

In a uniform <magnetic field>, the <Lorentz force> leaves the component of <velocity> parallel to the field constant and rotates the perpendicular component at the nonrelativistic <cyclotron frequency> $|q|B/m$. The resulting path is a <helix> around a field line.

= Guiding center
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}
{wiki}

= Guiding centre
{synonym}

The guiding center is the center of a charged particle's circular cyclotron orbit. In a nonuniform field it drifts slowly compared with the rapid <cyclotron frequency>.

= Damped cyclotron motion
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}
{wiki}

Adding linear drag to cyclotron motion produces an exponentially contracting spiral.

= Electrostatics
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

<Electrostatics> studies time-independent <electric charge> distributions and their <electric fields>. In <vacuum>, its local equations in <SI> units are
$$
\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=\frac{\rho}{\epsilon_0},\qquad \nabla\times\mathbf E=\mathbf0,\qquad \mathbf E=-\nabla\Phi,\qquad \nabla^2\Phi=-\frac{\rho}{\epsilon_0}.
$$
Here $\rho$ is <charge density>, $\epsilon_0$ is <vacuum permittivity>, and $\Phi$ is <electric potential>. <Gauss's law> supplies the <divergence> equation; the static form of <Faraday's law of induction> supplies the zero <curl>. The <Poisson equation> becomes <Laplace's equation> where there is no <electric charge>. Prescribed <electric charges> give <electric fields> through <Coulomb's law> and the <superposition principle>; mobile <electric charges> on an <electrical conductor> instead require solving for their distribution together with the <electric potential>. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node40.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Electrostatics and magnetostatics: field equations and boundary data]{ref}.

The useful distinction is between prescribed sources and prescribed <boundary conditions>. On a grounded <electrical conductor>, an external connection fixes its <electric potential> relative to an <electrical reference potential> and allows <electric charge> to enter or leave. On an isolated <electrical conductor>, total <electric charge> is fixed and its constant <electric potential> is an unknown. A neutral isolated <electrical conductor> can therefore have a nonzero local <surface charge density> through <electrostatic induction>. Equal <electric potentials> also do not imply equal <electric charges>: <charge sharing between distant connected spheres> gives a simple geometric example. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_06.html#Ch6-S6[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.6: charged conductors and connected-sphere estimates]{ref}.

<Electrostatics> is useful because a spatial <boundary value problem> replaces the need to follow every moving <charge carrier> during <charge relaxation>. It describes the settled state of a <capacitor> and explains <electrostatic shielding> of sensitive equipment. Its assumptions must match the apparatus: stationary boundaries and sources, negligible time variation of the <magnetic field>, and enough time for <charge relaxation>. A metal carrying a maintained <electric current> through finite <electrical resistance> generally needs a nonzero internal <electric field> by <Ohm's law>; it is not a <conductor in electrostatic equilibrium>. Static macroscopic <electric charge> also does not mean that every <electron> is motionless. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_07.html#Ch7-S1[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.7.1: conductor potentials as boundary-value problems]{ref}.

= Faraday's ice pail experiment
{c}
{parent=Electrostatics}

<Faraday's ice pail experiment> distinguishes **redistribution** of <electric charge> by <electrostatic induction> from **transfer** of <electric charge> by contact. Lower a charged body into an initially neutral isolated metal pail: an externally connected <electrometer> responds even though the body never touches the metal. Withdraw the body without contact and the response disappears. Repeat with conductive contact before withdrawal and a residual response can remain. The striking point is that an exterior response does not establish that the pail has gained net <electric charge>.

**Apparatus and readout.** A modern implementation uses two concentric wire-mesh cylinders: a 10 cm diameter, 15 cm high inner pail on insulating rods and a grounded outer shield. Connect the <electrometer>'s signal lead to the inner pail and its reference lead to the shield. Momentarily ground and zero the signal assembly, then isolate it; the shield remains grounded. A charged disk on an insulating handle goes into the lower half without touching. The <electrometer> measures <electric potential difference> in <volts>, with sign determined by the lead connections. It does not directly measure <electric charge> in <coulombs>.<Basic Electrostatics System (ES-9080B), Instruction Manual 012-07227G by Cecilia Hernandez>[Basic Electrostatics System, instruction manual 012-07227G, printed pp. 9–10, 13–15, Figure 1.1 and Procedure 1A]{ref}

\Image[faraday-ice-pail-apparatus-and-enclosed-charge.png]
{title=<Faraday's ice pail experiment>: apparatus and isolated versus grounded <electric charge> accounting}
{description=Original qualitative schematics. The open pail approximates enclosure; the two closed <cavity in a conductor> panels stipulate exact enclosure, <electrostatic equilibrium>, no exterior sources and zero <electric potential> at infinity. Signs denote total <electric charges>, not a measured or uniform <surface charge density>.}

**A charge ledger before contact.** To separate exact reasoning from the open apparatus, first idealize the pail as a closed <cavity in a conductor> with one internal body of total <electric charge> $q$. Let the isolated metal have total <electric charge> $Q_0$. The metal's <electric field> is zero at <electrostatic equilibrium>.<Intermediate College Physics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Ideal Conductors>[Richard Fitzpatrick, Ideal Conductors, equations 2.124–2.126: zero field and constant potential in metal]{ref} A <Gaussian surface> enclosing the <cavity in a conductor> but lying wholly in the metal therefore has zero <electric flux>. <Gauss's law> and <charge conservation> give
$$
q+Q_{\rm inner}=0,\qquad Q_{\rm inner}+Q_{\rm outer}=Q_0,\qquad Q_{\rm outer}=Q_0+q.
$$
For $Q_0=0$ and $q>0$, the inner-wall total is $-q$ and the outer-wall total is $+q$: the metal remains neutral. The exterior <electric field> can therefore be nonzero without any transfer from the body. The connected <electrometer> and signal cable belong to the same isolated conducting assembly and share its exterior <electric charge>. The zero-<electric field> assertion concerns metal, not the occupied <cavity in a conductor>.

This ledger fixes totals, not the local <surface charge density>. Moving an off-center body changes the inner-wall distribution while its <integral> remains $-q$. With fixed outer geometry, fixed $Q_0+q$ and unchanged exterior sources, <uniqueness of the Dirichlet problem> together with the fixed total outer <electric charge> determines the same exterior solution: the outside cannot reveal the body's position or detailed internal distribution. For example, internal bodies with stipulated <electric charges> $+2q$ and $-q$ give the same total exterior response as a single body with $+q$. Internal bodies with $+q$ and $-q$ can give a null exterior response while retaining a nonzero internal <electric field>. This is why a null reading cannot establish an empty <cavity in a conductor>.

**Withdrawal is the control.** Record a baseline, insert without contact, withdraw, and check return to baseline. After a fresh zero, repeat with the charged disk touching the inner wall, then withdraw and record the residual response. Finally zero the pail and reinsert the disk without contact to test remaining <electric charge>. Keep the operator grounded, discharge unwanted handle <electric charge>, and keep the unused charged disk distant. Dirty supports and humidity promote leakage. These controls separate transfer from <electrostatic induction> and stray coupling.<Basic Electrostatics System (ES-9080B), Instruction Manual 012-07227G by Cecilia Hernandez>[Basic Electrostatics System, instruction manual 012-07227G, printed pp. 4–5 and 14–15, Procedure 1A steps 3–8: handle charge, withdrawal, contact and disk retest]{ref}

\Image[faraday-ice-pail-withdrawal-contact-readout.png]
{title=<Faraday's ice pail experiment>: qualitative withdrawal, contact and grounding sequences}
{description=Stipulated ideal-model outcomes for a body initially carrying positive <electric charge> $q$, with a separate neutral initial state for each row. No measured <electric potential differences> or time scale are depicted. The grounding sequence disconnects ground before withdrawal; reversing that order removes its residual <electric charge>.}

In the ideal contact test, the internal body is itself an <electrical conductor> and makes a conducting connection to the wall. After <charge relaxation>, body and enclosure become a single <equipotential>; the remaining charge-free void has zero <electric field>. The body then carries no excess <electric charge> on surfaces facing that void. Withdrawal without subsequent charging leaves total metal <electric charge> $Q_0+q$. A coated paddle can retain <electric charge> on insulating patches, so merely touching it does not guarantee complete transfer. A residual reading establishes some transfer under the controls; the disk retest checks the stronger claim of complete transfer. The ideal argument assumes no leakage and no relevant contact-generated charging.

**Grounding changes the ledger.** With no exterior sources and zero <electric potential> at infinity, a closed grounded enclosure has zero exterior <electric field>, inner-wall <electric charge> $-q$ and zero outer-wall <electric charge>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[II.5, §5–10, final shielding argument: a closed grounded conducting shell separates the two electrostatic problems]{ref} Consequently, disconnecting ground before withdrawal retains total metal <electric charge> $-q$, which moves to the exterior after withdrawal. Keeping ground connected during withdrawal instead restores zero <electric charge>. A lasting opposite-sign response can therefore arise through grounding without body–wall contact.

For the real open pail, no <Gaussian surface> lying wholly in metal closes over the mouth. Increasing insertion depth and seeking a stable response tests whether enclosure is a good approximation; mesh openings, rim proximity and nearby objects limit it. In a fixed-geometry approximation, the positive insertion response obeys $\Delta V\simeq q/C_{\rm eff}$, where $C_{\rm eff}$ is the effective <capacitance> of the pail, shield, cable and <electrometer> input. Changed geometry changes that conversion. Wait for <charge relaxation> and instrument settling at each step, while keeping the observation short compared with leakage. The experiment probes settled <electrostatics>; it does not establish shielding against arbitrary changing <electromagnetic fields>.

= Electrostatic interface boundary conditions
{parent=Electrostatics}
{title2=$[D_n]=\sigma_{\rm fixed}$}

Across a charged dielectric interface the tangential <electric field> is continuous and the normal displacement jump is the fixed <surface charge density>. Without a dipole sheet the <electrostatic potential> is continuous. For a charge sheet with equal <permittivity> on both sides, its normal potential derivative has jump $-\sigma_{\rm fixed}/\varepsilon$. Setting the field to zero on one side is an additional physical boundary assumption, as in a conductor or a negligible-exterior-admittance idealization.

= Debye–Hückel approximation
{c}
{parent=Electrostatics}
{title2=$(\nabla^2-\kappa_D^2)\phi=-\rho_{\rm fixed}/\varepsilon$}

The Debye–Hückel approximation linearizes mobile-ion Boltzmann densities about a neutral bulk electrolyte. It is valid for ionic potential energies small compared with $k_BT$. The inverse <Debye–Hückel screening length> satisfies $\kappa_D^2=\sum_i n_i z_i^2e^2/(\varepsilon k_BT)$. The quadratic screened charging free energy includes both <electric field> energy and the linearized ionic-response term $\varepsilon\kappa_D^2\phi^2/2$.

= Screened sinusoidal surface-charge mode
{parent=Debye–Hückel approximation}
{title2=$p=\sqrt{k^2+\kappa_D^2}$}

A lateral sinusoidal <surface charge density> with wave number $k$ in the <Debye–Hückel approximation> generates vertical exponential or hyperbolic functions with inverse length $p=\sqrt{k^2+\kappa_D^2}$. In a homogeneous electrolyte filling all space, an isolated sheet at $z_s$ produces $\phi=\sigma_k(x)e^{-p|z-z_s|}/(2\varepsilon p)$. Boundary conditions determine the normalization when the electrolyte occupies only a gap.

= Phase registration of screened charged sheets
{parent=Screened sinusoidal surface-charge mode}
{title2=$\theta_{\min}=\pi\pmod{2\pi}$}

Two equally modulated charge sheets in identical full-space electrolyte have charging energy per area $\alpha^2[1+e^{-pd}\cos\theta]/(4\varepsilon p)$, where $p=\sqrt{k^2+\kappa_D^2}$. Its phase-dependent interaction favors a half-wavelength offset, with opposite charge patches facing each other. For an electrolyte gap with negligible exterior displacement response, the energy is instead $\alpha^2[\coth(pd)+\cos\theta/\sinh(pd)]/(2\varepsilon p)$, with the same minimizing phase. Absolute energies require specified exterior <electrostatic interface boundary conditions>.

= Bjerrum length
{parent=Electrostatics}
{c}
{title2=$\ell_B$}
{wiki}

For a chosen reference charge $q$, the separation at which its Coulomb interaction energy equals $k_BT$. The charge-$q$ convention is $\ell_B=q^2/(4\pi\epsilon_{\rm abs}k_BT)$ in SI, or $\ell_B=\beta q^2/\epsilon$ in <Gaussian units>. The commonly tabulated value uses the elementary charge; changing <counterion> valence requires tracking that reference convention. It sets the strength of <counterion condensation>.

= Poisson-Boltzmann equation
{parent=Electrostatics}
{c}
{wiki=Poisson–Boltzmann_equation}

A mean-field coupling of the <Poisson equation> to an ideal <Boltzmann distribution> of mobile <ions>. In <Gaussian units> with uniform dielectric constant $\epsilon$, $\nabla^2\phi=-(4\pi/\epsilon)[\rho_f+\sum_i q_i n_{i,0}e^{-\beta q_i\phi}]$. The potential reference and chemical potentials determine the normalization factors. It neglects discrete-ion correlations and assumes local <thermal equilibrium>. A counterion-only model is not the same equation as a symmetric salt reservoir.

= Neutral cylindrical cell model
{parent=Poisson-Boltzmann equation}
{title2=$E(R)=0$}

Confine the <counterions> of a uniformly charged infinite cylinder within a coaxial cylinder of radius $R$, with total mobile line charge $\lambda$. The <electric field> vanishes at $R$, while its surface value at $a$ is fixed by the bare line charge. This gives $a\phi'(a)=2\lambda/\epsilon$ and $R\phi'(R)=0$ in <Gaussian units>. Sending $R/a\to\infty$ defines the <infinite-dilution limit of cylindrical counterions>.

= Infinite-dilution limit of cylindrical counterions
{parent=Neutral cylindrical cell model}

In a neutral counterion-only cylindrical cell, send the outer radius to infinity at fixed cylinder charge. Below the <Manning parameter> threshold, number density tends to zero at each fixed radius. Above threshold, the <cylindrical counterion-only Poisson-Boltzmann profile> retains line charge $\lambda-q/\ell_B$ locally, while the rest recedes to infinity. Therefore the limit of the full finite-cell charge integral is not the charge integral of the pointwise limiting density.

= Cylindrical counterion-only Poisson-Boltzmann profile
{parent=Poisson-Boltzmann equation}
{title2=$\Phi=2\log[1+(\eta-1)u]$}

For $u=\log(r/a)$ and $\Phi=\beta q\phi-2u$, a counterion-only <Poisson-Boltzmann equation> reduces to $\Phi''=-Ce^{-\Phi}$. Conditions $\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi\to\infty$ and $\Phi'\to0$ give $\Phi=2\log(1+bu)$, $b=\sqrt{C/2}>0$. Matching surface <Gauss's law> gives $b=\eta-1$, so this branch requires the <Manning parameter> $\eta>1$. Its number density is $n(r)=(\eta-1)^2(a/r)^2/[2\pi\ell_Ba^2(1+(\eta-1)\log(r/a))^2]$. It leaves residual line-charge magnitude $q/\ell_B$.

= Counterion condensation
{parent=Poisson-Boltzmann equation}
{wiki}

= Manning condensation
{c}
{synonym}

Localization of a finite fraction of oppositely charged <ions> near a charged object even as the confining volume grows without bound. In the salt-free infinite-cylinder mean-field model, the <Manning parameter> must exceed one for a nonzero limiting local density. The condensed charge does not include <ions> that escape to infinity; finite-cell charge neutrality and the local infinite-volume limit must not be interchanged.

= Manning condensed fraction
{parent=Counterion condensation}
{c}
{title2=$f_c=\max(1-1/\eta,0)$}

In the mean-field infinite-dilution cylinder model, the fraction of neutralizing charge that remains localized is $f_c=\max(1-1/\eta,0)$, where $\eta$ is the <Manning parameter>. For $\eta>1$, integrating the <cylindrical counterion-only Poisson-Boltzmann profile> gives $Q_c=\lambda-q/\ell_B$. Below threshold, the local limiting density vanishes even though every finite neutral cell contains the full <counterion> charge.

= Cylindrical screening charge
{parent=Counterion condensation}
{title2=$Q(r)=2\pi\int_a^r s\rho(s)\,ds$}

The positive mobile charge per unit axial length accumulated from cylinder radius $a$ to $r$ is $Q(r)=2\pi\int_a^r s\rho(s)\,ds$. For fixed negative line charge $-\lambda$, <Gauss's law> gives signed radial <electric field> $E_r=2[-\lambda+Q(r)]/(\epsilon r)$ in <Gaussian units>. Its absolute value is the field magnitude. An integral over a local infinite-dilution profile measures condensed charge, rather than every <ion> in a finite neutral cell.

= Manning parameter
{parent=Counterion condensation}
{c}
{title2=$\eta=\lambda\ell_B/q$}

The dimensionless line-charge coupling $\eta=\lambda\ell_B/q$, with negative cylinder line charge $-\lambda$ and <counterions> of charge $q>0$. Here the <Bjerrum length> uses the same reference charge $q$. The mean-field infinite-dilution threshold is $\eta=1$. If $\ell_B$ is instead tabulated for elementary charge $e$ and <counterions> have charge $ze$, the corresponding coupling is $z\lambda\ell_B/e$.

= Coulomb's law
{parent=Electrostatics}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathbf F_{2\leftarrow1}=kq_1q_2\mathbf r/|\mathbf r|^3$}

<Coulomb's law> gives the mutual <electric force> between stationary <point charges> in <vacuum>. Put <electric charges> $q_1$ and $q_2$ at $\mathbf r_1$ and $\mathbf r_2$, and define the <displacement> **from charge 1 to charge 2** by $\mathbf r=\mathbf r_2-\mathbf r_1$, with separation $r=|\mathbf r|>0$ and <unit vector> $\widehat{\mathbf r}=\mathbf r/r$. In <SI> units, the <force> on charge 2 due to charge 1 is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf F_{2\leftarrow1}=kq_1q_2\frac{\mathbf r}{|\mathbf r|^3}=\frac{kq_1q_2}{r^2}\widehat{\mathbf r},\qquad k=\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}.}
$$
Here $\varepsilon_0$ is the <vacuum permittivity>. The magnitude is $k|q_1q_2|/r^2$. If $q_1q_2>0$, the <force> on charge 2 points away from charge 1; if $q_1q_2<0$, it points toward charge 1. Interchanging the charges gives $\mathbf F_{1\leftarrow2}=-\mathbf F_{2\leftarrow1}$, consistent with <Newton's third law> in this electrostatic setting. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-3-coulombs-law[OpenStax, University Physics II, §5.3: Coulomb's law]{ref}.

For a fully computed example, stipulate $q_1=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$, $q_2=-3\,\mathrm{nC}$, $\mathbf r_1=\mathbf0$, $\mathbf r_2=0.20\,\mathrm m\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ and the approximate value $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$. Then
$$
\mathbf F_{2\leftarrow1}=\frac{(8.99\times10^9)(2\times10^{-9})(-3\times10^{-9})}{(0.20)^2}\,\mathrm N\,\widehat{\mathbf x}=-1.3485\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm N\,\widehat{\mathbf x}.
$$
The <force> is attractive, of magnitude $1.3485\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm N$. The source <electric field> at charge 2 is $\mathbf E_1(\mathbf r_2)=449.5\,\mathrm{N/C}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$, pointing away from the positive source. Multiplication by the negative $q_2$ reverses the direction to give the attractive <force>. This field excludes charge 2's own self-field.

Holding the <electric charges> fixed while changing the separation from $R$ to $2R$ or $3R$ gives the normalized <inverse-square law> values
$$
\frac{|\mathbf F(R)|}{|\mathbf F(R)|}=1,\qquad \frac{|\mathbf F(2R)|}{|\mathbf F(R)|}=\frac14,\qquad \frac{|\mathbf F(3R)|}{|\mathbf F(R)|}=\frac19.
$$
For the stipulated example, separations $0.20$, $0.40$ and $0.60\,\mathrm m$ give magnitudes $1.3485\times10^{-6}$, $3.37125\times10^{-7}$ and $1.498333\ldots\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm N$. The diagram computes these ratios and uses one common arrow-length scale for both sign choices.

\Image[coulomb-force-distance.png]
{title=Equal and opposite Coulomb force vectors: like charges repel, unlike charges attract, and doubling or tripling separation gives one quarter or one ninth of the force}

The <inverse-square law> has a geometric explanation through <Gauss's law>: a source <point charge> gives fixed total <electric flux> through a concentric <sphere>, whose <surface area> is $4\pi r^2$. Dividing that flux by the area gives a radial <electric field> proportional to $1/r^2$. This connects a pairwise <force> calculation to a global conservation-like accounting of field sources. For several stationary <point charges>, the <superposition principle> adds the individual <electric fields> as <vectors>, and $\mathbf F_i=q_i\mathbf E_{\mathrm{other}}(\mathbf r_i)$ uses all sources except charge $i$ itself. The <Coulomb potential energy> gives an alternative route when the desired quantity is <work> rather than instantaneous <force>.

= Coulomb potential energy
{parent=Coulomb's law}
{c}
{title2=$U(r)=kq_1q_2/r$}

= Coulomb potential
{synonym}
{c}

The <Coulomb potential energy> is the interaction <potential energy> of two <point charges> in <vacuum>. With separation $r>0$, <electric charges> $q_1,q_2$, $k=1/(4\pi\varepsilon_0)$ and zero <potential energy> at infinite separation,
$$
\boxed{U(r)=\frac{kq_1q_2}{r}.}
$$
This is mutual interaction <energy>; it does not include the divergent self-energies of ideal <point charges>. Unlike <electric potential>, which has units of <volts>, $U$ has <SI> units of joules. With charge 1 fixed and positive radial direction away from it, the <conservative force> in <Coulomb's law> gives
$$
U(r)-U(\infty)=-\int_\infty^r\frac{kq_1q_2}{s^2}\,ds=\frac{kq_1q_2}{r},\qquad
F_r=-\frac{dU}{dr}=\frac{kq_1q_2}{r^2}.
$$
Thus the $1/r$ <potential energy> and the <inverse-square law> for <force> are two descriptions of the same interaction. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-1-electric-potential-energy[OpenStax, University Physics II, §7.1: electric potential energy]{ref}.

For $q_1=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$, $q_2=-3\,\mathrm{nC}$ and $r=0.20\,\mathrm m$, using the stipulated approximation $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$,
$$
U=\frac{(8.99\times10^9)(2\times10^{-9})(-3\times10^{-9})}{0.20}\,\mathrm J=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J.
$$
At $r=0.40\,\mathrm m$, $U=-1.3485\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$. Separating the pair from $0.20$ to $0.40\,\mathrm m$ therefore requires external <work> $\Delta U=+1.3485\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$ if the initial and final <kinetic energies> agree; the <electric force> does $-1.3485\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$ of <work>. Separating them all the way to infinity requires $+2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$. The negative initial <potential energy> means that the pair lies below the chosen separated reference; it does not mean a negative <kinetic energy>.

Like-signed <electric charges> have positive $U$, which decreases as they separate, so their <electric force> is repulsive. Opposite-signed <electric charges> have negative $U$, which decreases as they approach, so their <electric force> is attractive. This attractive interaction supplies the <Coulomb potential> used in the <hydrogen atom>; determining its stationary quantum states also requires <quantum mechanics>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/III_19.html#Ch19-S1[Feynman lectures III.19, §19–1: the Schrödinger equation for hydrogen]{ref}. Dividing $U$ by a nonzero test <electric charge> $q_2$ gives the source's <electric potential> $\Phi_1=kq_1/r$. More generally, the <electrostatic potential energy of point charges> sums each unordered charge pair once. This <energy> description is useful because <work> between two separations follows from endpoint values, without integrating a complicated trajectory.

= Electrostatic equilibrium
{parent=Electrostatics}

A state with a stationary <electric charge> distribution and no net drift of mobile charge driven by the <electric field>. A <conductor in electrostatic equilibrium> has zero interior <electric field>, while bound charges in an insulating material need not move to eliminate its interior field.

= Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium
{parent=Electrostatics}

A <conductor in electrostatic equilibrium> is an <electrical conductor> at rest whose mobile <charge carriers> have finished redistributing, with no maintained bulk <electric current>. In the ideal macroscopic model,
$$
\mathbf E_{\rm metal}=\mathbf0,\qquad \Phi=V_c\ \text{on each connected conductor},\qquad \rho_{\rm metal}=0.
$$
A residual bulk <electric field> would drive further <electric charge> redistribution. The <electric potential difference> between any two points joined through the metal is $-\int\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l=0$, so each connected piece is an <equipotential>. <Gauss's law> then gives zero net bulk <charge density>. This is a statement about net macroscopic <electric charge>, not the absence of positive <atomic nuclei> and negative <electrons>. Excess <electric charge> is represented by <surface charge density>; atomic surface structure is outside this continuum model. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-4-conductors-in-electrostatic-equilibrium[Samuel Ling, William Moebs and Jeff Sanny, Conductors in electrostatic equilibrium]{ref}.

The word **inside** must distinguish metal from a <cavity in a conductor>. A completely enclosed, charge-free <cavity in a conductor> has constant boundary <electric potential> $V_c$. The constant solution of <Laplace's equation> and <uniqueness of the Dirichlet problem> give $\Phi=V_c$ and $\mathbf E=\mathbf0$ throughout the <cavity in a conductor>, regardless of its shape or external <electric charges>. Grounding is unnecessary for this inward <electrostatic shielding>; an isolated enclosure can change its overall <electric potential> without producing an internal <electric field>. Zero enclosed net <electric charge> alone would only give zero <electric flux>. A positive and negative pair inside a <cavity in a conductor> can have zero total <electric charge> and a nonzero <electric field>. <Intermediate College Physics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Ideal Conductors>[Richard Fitzpatrick, Ideal Conductors: charge-free cavities and uniqueness]{ref}.

If fixed <electric charges> inside a <cavity in a conductor> sum to $q$, a <Gaussian surface> lying entirely in the metal and enclosing that <cavity in a conductor> has zero <electric flux>. Thus the total induced inner-wall <electric charge> is $-q$. For one such <cavity in a conductor> and an isolated enclosure with total metal <electric charge> $Q_c$, the outer-wall <electric charge> is $Q_c+q$. In particular, an initially neutral isolated enclosure generally has an exterior <electric field> when $q\ne0$. For fixed internal sources, external changes only shift the constant <electric potential> inside an isolated enclosure, leaving the internal <electric field> unchanged. https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.04248[Aritro Pathak, On the electrostatic equilibrium of charges and cavities in a conductor]{ref}.

A grounded enclosure fixes $V_c=0$ and can acquire metal <electric charge> $-q$; with no exterior sources and $\Phi\to0$ at infinity, <uniqueness of the Dirichlet problem> gives zero exterior <electric field>. With external sources present, the exterior <electric field> need not vanish, but it is independent of the internal sources at fixed grounded boundary <electric potential>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_05.html#Ch5-S10[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.5.10: shielding by a closed grounded enclosure]{ref}.

<Faraday's ice pail experiment> makes the distinction between redistribution and transfer visible. In his letter of 4 February 1843, Faraday described an insulated pewter pail connected by wire to a gold-leaf <electrometer>, and a brass ball suspended on a long dry silk thread to reduce the hand's influence. He discharged the pail and charged the ball away from it. Lowering the ball without contact produced leaf divergence, which grew during entry and became approximately independent of further depth; withdrawing it collapsed the leaves. Contact with the bottom transferred <electric charge>, and the withdrawn ball was discharged. The readout was leaf divergence, not a direct map of the internal <electric field>. These observations distinguish <electrostatic induction> from contact transfer of <electric charge>. https://physics.kenyon.edu/EarlyApparatus/Static_Electricity/Faradays_Ice_Pail/Faraday_Ice_Pail.html[Thomas B. Greenslade Jr., Faraday Ice Pail, reproducing Faraday's 1843 letter]{ref}.

For a modern version of <Faraday's ice pail experiment>, connect an insulated inner metal pail to the signal input of a high-input-impedance <electrometer>, with its reference lead and an outer shield grounded. Zero and momentarily ground the pail, then isolate the signal side. Lower a charged paddle deeply without touching and record the <electric potential difference>; withdraw it and check return to zero. Repeat with contact, then withdrawal: a residual reading indicates transferred <electric charge>. Reverse the paddle's sign and repeat. The <electrometer> reads <volts>; inferring <electric charge> requires the effective <capacitance> of pail, cable and input, so a stable reading at fixed geometry is a relative measure, not automatically a value in <coulombs>. Keep supports clean, nearby charged objects distant, and the operator grounded; humidity can cause leakage. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Basic-Electrostatics-Sys-Manual-ES-9080B1.pdf#page=13[PASCO, Basic Electrostatics System manual, equipment description and demonstration 1A]{ref}.

\Image[electrostatic-conductor-equilibrium-ice-pail.png]
{title=<Faraday's ice pail experiment>: insertion without contact versus transfer by contact}
{description=Original procedural schematic, not measured data or a reproduction of historical apparatus. The deep-insertion charge accounting idealizes a nearly enclosing pail; positive exterior <electric charge> is shared over the pail and connected signal assembly. The grounded outer shield and reference connection are shown in the insertion panel. Exact closed-enclosure predictions apply to a fully enclosed <cavity in a conductor>, not automatically to an open pail near its rim.}

A complementary <electrostatic shielding> demonstration uses a charged hollow metal enclosure, a small metal sampling ball on an insulating handle, and an <electrometer>. Discharge the sampling ball between trials; touch it to the exterior, withdraw it, and transfer its collected <electric charge> to the <electrometer>. Repeat by touching the inner wall without touching the exterior during withdrawal. The exterior sample produces a response, whereas the inner sample gives a null within instrument sensitivity. This compares accessible surface responses rather than assuming a perfectly spherical enclosure. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_05.html#Ch5-S8[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.5.8: inner and outer sampling of a charged conductor]{ref}. Useful additional controls are an uncharged enclosure baseline, a repeat exterior sample to verify sensitivity, and repeated trials at different depths. An access hole, charged handle, leakage or disturbed <surface charge density> limits the inference; a null bounds detectable transfer, rather than proving an exactly zero <electric field>. An open pail's depth plateau tests good enclosure empirically. Neither demonstration establishes shielding of arbitrary time-dependent <electromagnetic fields>.

= Cavity in a conductor
{parent=Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium}

A <cavity in a conductor> is a void surrounded by an <electrical conductor>; **completely enclosed** is the essential qualification in exact <electrostatic shielding> arguments. At <electrostatic equilibrium>, the surrounding connected metal is an <equipotential>. An empty closed <cavity in a conductor> has constant <electric potential> and zero <electric field>, even if the metal is isolated and charged. An occupied closed <cavity in a conductor> generally has a nonzero <electric field>; its inner-wall total <electric charge> cancels the total <electric charge> of the internal sources. These are distinct statements, illustrated by <Faraday's ice pail experiment>.<Intermediate College Physics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Ideal Conductors>[Richard Fitzpatrick, Ideal Conductors, equations 2.127 and the following uniqueness argument: a charge-free enclosed cavity]{ref}

= Electrostatic shielding
{parent=Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium}

<Electrostatic shielding> decouples interior and exterior <electric fields> using a <conductor in electrostatic equilibrium>. Exterior sources cannot produce an <electric field> in an empty closed <cavity in a conductor>, whether grounded or isolated. Outward independence requires grounding: internal sources can affect an isolated enclosure's exterior, as in <Faraday's ice pail experiment>. Grounding makes the exterior solution independent of internal sources, while allowing exterior sources' own response. Openings and changing <electromagnetic fields> need additional analysis.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[II.5, §5–10, concluding paragraphs on inward shielding and closed grounded enclosures]{ref}

= Electrostatic boundary conditions at a conductor
{parent=Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium}

= Electrostatic boundary condition
{synonym}

The <electrostatic boundary conditions at a conductor> relate the one-sided total <electric fields> at a smooth metal–<vacuum> interface. Choose a <unit normal> $\mathbf n$ **from metal into the adjacent vacuum**, let $\mathbf E_{\rm metal}$ and $\mathbf E_{\rm vac}$ be the two limiting <electric fields>, and let $\sigma$ be the signed <surface charge density>. A thin <Gaussian pillbox> with cap <area> $A$ has outward cap normals $+\mathbf n$ on the <vacuum> side and $-\mathbf n$ on the metal side. As its height tends to zero, its side <electric flux> tends to zero for bounded local <electric fields>, leaving
$$
A\mathbf E_{\rm vac}\cdot\mathbf n-A\mathbf E_{\rm metal}\cdot\mathbf n=\frac{\sigma A}{\epsilon_0},\qquad \mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf E_{\rm vac}-\mathbf E_{\rm metal})=\frac{\sigma}{\epsilon_0}.
$$
The minus sign comes from the second outward cap normal, not from the sign of the <electric charge>. A shrinking rectangular loop across the interface and the zero closed <line integral> in <electrostatics> give continuity of the <tangential component>:
$$
\mathbf n\times(\mathbf E_{\rm vac}-\mathbf E_{\rm metal})=\mathbf0.
$$
For a <conductor in electrostatic equilibrium>, $\mathbf E_{\rm metal}=\mathbf0$, hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbf E_{\rm vac}=\frac{\sigma}{\epsilon_0}\mathbf n,\qquad \Phi|_S=V_c,\qquad \sigma=-\epsilon_0\left.\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial n}\right|_{\rm vac}.}
$$
Thus positive <surface charge density> gives an <electric field> away from metal and negative <surface charge density> gives an <electric field> toward metal. The derivative uses the same chosen <unit normal>, from the <vacuum> side. The ideal surface layer has no unique <electric field> value on the layer itself; these are limiting values. Ordinary surface <electric charge> preserves continuity of <electric potential>; an ideal <electric dipole> sheet would require a different model. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node70.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Boundary conditions for E and D: pillbox and loop conditions]{ref}.

Here “exterior” means just outside the **metal**, including the <vacuum> within a <cavity in a conductor>. On a spherical inner wall of radius $a$, its <unit normal> from metal into the <cavity in a conductor> is $-\widehat{\mathbf r}$. A central positive <point charge> $q$ gives $\mathbf E_{\rm vac}=q\widehat{\mathbf r}/(4\pi\epsilon_0a^2)$ there, so
$$
\sigma_{\rm inner}=-\frac{q}{4\pi a^2},\qquad \int_{S_{\rm inner}}\sigma_{\rm inner}\,dA=-q.
$$
The outward normal of the <cavity in a conductor> as a volume is instead $+\widehat{\mathbf r}$; confusing these two <unit normals> reverses the inferred sign. For a stipulated spherical enclosure with inner radius $a$, outer radius $b$, no external sources and total isolated metal <electric charge> $Q_c$, <spherical symmetry> and <Gauss's law> give the analytical model
$$
E_r(r)=\begin{cases}
q/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2),&0<r<a,\\
0,&a<r<b,\\
(Q_c+q)/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2),&r>b.
\end{cases}
$$
Its outer <surface charge density> is $(Q_c+q)/(4\pi b^2)$. Grounding to $V_c=0$ with zero <electric potential> at infinity instead gives $Q_c=-q$ and zero exterior <electric field>, while preserving the same nonzero <electric field> inside the charged <cavity in a conductor>.

\Image[electrostatic-conductor-equilibrium-pillbox-cavity.png]
{title=Signed <Gaussian pillbox> accounting and an analytical charged <cavity in a conductor>}
{description=Original schematic and analytical model, not measured data. Left: the two cap normals give the normal <electric field> jump at a positive metal–<vacuum> surface. Right: a central positive <point charge> in a spherical enclosure with $b=2a$; solid blue is an isolated initially neutral enclosure, and dashed orange is a grounded enclosure. Both have zero <electric field> in the metal and the same nonzero <electric field> in the <cavity in a conductor>. The jumps show one-sided limits, with the point-source singularity excluded.}

As a numerical sign check, stipulate $q=1\,\mathrm{nC}$, $a=0.10\,\mathrm m$, $b=0.20\,\mathrm m$ and $1/(4\pi\epsilon_0)\simeq8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$. The inner <electric field> is approximately $899\,\mathrm{N/C}$ along $+\widehat{\mathbf r}$, but $\mathbf E_{\rm vac}\cdot\mathbf n=-899\,\mathrm{N/C}$ and $\sigma_{\rm inner}\simeq-7.96\,\mathrm{nC/m^2}$. An isolated neutral enclosure has $+1\,\mathrm{nC}$ on the outer wall, approximately $+1.99\,\mathrm{nC/m^2}$ and $225\,\mathrm{N/C}$ just outside. Multiplying each <surface charge density> by its spherical <surface area> recovers the signed wall <electric charges>.

The local result uses the total <electric field>, including all induced and imposed sources. The factor $\sigma/\epsilon_0$ follows from the zero metal-side <electric field>; a symmetric isolated sheet in <vacuum> instead has magnitude $|\sigma|/(2\epsilon_0)$ on each side. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_05.html#Ch5-S9[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.5.9: why a conductor differs from an isolated charge sheet]{ref}. At sharp corners, limiting <electric fields> can be singular and the smooth local pillbox argument needs care. In material outside the metal, use the normal free-charge jump of the <electric displacement field> and the material's <permittivity>, as in <electrostatic interface boundary conditions>. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node70.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Boundary conditions for E and D: free and bound surface charge]{ref}.

= Surface charge density
{title2=$\sigma$}
{parent=Electrostatic boundary conditions at a conductor}
{wiki}

Surface charge density is electric charge per unit area. At a conductor in electrostatic equilibrium it equals $\epsilon_0\mathbf E_{\rm out}\mathbin{\cdot}\mathbf n$ for the normal pointing out of the conductor.

= Charge sharing between distant connected spheres
{parent=Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium}

Two widely separated conducting spheres of radii $R_1,R_2$ connected by a thin wire have equal potentials, so a total charge $Q$ divides as
$$
Q_i=\frac{R_i}{R_1+R_2}Q.
$$

= Charge on connected concentric spherical shells
{parent=Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium}

Two connected concentric spherical shells form one equipotential conductor. In the absence of charge in the inner cavity, all net charge lies on the exterior surface of the outer shell.

= Induced charge on a conducting sphere in a uniform electric field
{parent=Conductor in electrostatic equilibrium}

A neutral conducting sphere of radius $R$ in the field $E\widehat z$ has exterior potential and induced surface charge
$$
\Phi=-E\left(r-\frac{R^3}{r^2}\right)\cos\theta,
\qquad
\sigma=3\epsilon_0E\cos\theta.
$$
The surface integral of $\sigma$ is zero.

= Electrostatic energy
{parent=Electrostatics}
{wiki}

Electrostatic energy can be written as one half the charge-potential integral or epsilon-zero over two times the electric-field energy integral.

= Electrostatic energy of a three-plate capacitor
{parent=Electrostatic energy}
{title2=$W=\frac{\epsilon_0AV^2H}{H^2-a^2}$}

In the parallel-plate approximation, a central plate at potential $V$ between grounded plates at $z=\pm H$ forms two parallel capacitors of gaps $H+a$ and $H-a$. Summing their field energies gives the displayed formula. At fixed voltage it is minimized at $a=0$. Finite circular plates have fringe fields; the uniform-gap expression assumes those edge corrections are neglected.

= Electrostatic energy of a uniformly charged solid sphere
{parent=Electrostatic energy}
{title2=$U=\frac{3Q^2}{20\pi\epsilon_0R}$}

A solid sphere of radius $R$ and uniform <charge density> has total <electric charge> $Q$. <Gauss law> gives the radial <electric field> $E_r=Qr/(4\pi\epsilon_0R^3)$ inside and $E_r=Q/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2)$ outside. The <electric potential> vanishing at infinity is $Q(3-r^2/R^2)/(8\pi\epsilon_0R)$ inside and $Q/(4\pi\epsilon_0r)$ outside. Integrating the <electrostatic energy> density $\epsilon_0E^2/2$ over both regions gives $U=3Q^2/(20\pi\epsilon_0R)$. In a fixed-density family with $Q\propto Z$ and $R^3\propto Z$, the <electrostatic energy> scales as $Z^{5/3}$.

= Electrostatic energy of alternating-charge concentric shells
{parent=Electrostatic energy}
{title2=$W=\frac{q^2}{8\pi\epsilon_0r_1}$}

A first spherical shell with charge $q$ followed by shells of charges $2(-1)^{n+1}q$ leaves enclosed charge $(-1)^{n+1}q$ after each shell. <Gauss's law> therefore gives the same squared <electric field> $q^2/(16\pi^2\epsilon_0^2r^4)$ in every gap outside the first radius. Integrating the <electrostatic energy> density over all space gives $W=q^2/(8\pi\epsilon_0r_1)$, independent of the other shell radii.

= Electrostatic energy of a uniformly charged spherical shell
{parent=Electrostatic energy}

A thin spherical shell of radius $a$ carrying total charge $Q$ has surface potential
$$
\Phi(a)=\frac{Q}{4\pi\epsilon_0a}
$$
and electrostatic self-energy
$$
U=\frac12Q\Phi(a)=\frac{Q^2}{8\pi\epsilon_0a}.
$$

= Electrostatic potential energy of point charges
{parent=Electrostatic energy}

For distinct <point charges> $q_i$ at positions $r_i$, the electrostatic <potential energy> is
$$
U=\frac1{4\pi\epsilon_0}\sum_{i<j}\frac{q_iq_j}{|r_i-r_j|}.
$$
When some charges are fixed, the terms depending on a movable charge form its potential energy in their <electric potential>.

= Coaxial cylindrical capacitor
{parent=Electrostatic energy}

For coaxial cylinders of radii $a<b$ and length $L\gg b$, carrying charges per unit length $\pm\lambda$,
$$
E(r)=\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\epsilon_0r}\widehat r,
\qquad
V=\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\epsilon_0}\log\frac ba,
\qquad
C=\frac{2\pi\epsilon_0L}{\log(b/a)}.
$$
The field energy is $U=\lambda^2L\log(b/a)/(4\pi\epsilon_0)=QV/2$.

= Coaxial capacitor with grounded inner and outer cylinders
{parent=Coaxial cylindrical capacitor}
{title2=$C'=2\pi\epsilon_0(1/\log\lambda+1/\log(2/\lambda))$}

A driven cylindrical conductor between two grounded coaxial conductors forms two <coaxial cylindrical capacitors> in parallel. For radii $a,\lambda a,2a$, the <capacitance per unit length> is the sum $C'=2\pi\epsilon_0[1/\log\lambda+1/\log(2/\lambda)]$. Writing $u=\log\lambda$ and $L=\log2$, the function $1/u+1/(L-u)$ is strictly <convex> on $(0,L)$ and symmetric about $L/2$. Hence $C'$ has its unique minimum at $\lambda=\sqrt2$, with value $8\pi\epsilon_0/\log2$. As $\lambda=1+\delta$ approaches the inner conductor, $C'\sim2\pi\epsilon_0/\delta$, agreeing with the local <parallel-plate capacitor> approximation.

= Electric dipole moment
{parent=Electrostatics}
{wiki}

The <electric dipole moment> measures the first spatial moment of signed <electric charge>. For localized <point charges> $q_i$ at positions $\mathbf r_i$, or an integrable <charge density> $\rho(\mathbf r)$ with a finite first moment, define
$$
\mathbf p=\sum_i q_i\mathbf r_i,
\qquad
\mathbf p=\int\mathbf r\rho(\mathbf r)\,d^3r,
\qquad
Q=\sum_iq_i=\int\rho(\mathbf r)\,d^3r.
$$
https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node20.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Electric Dipoles: continuous definition]{ref}.

The <electric dipole moment> is a <vector> with <SI> units $\mathrm{C\,m}$: <coulombs> times <length> in metres. Opposite <electric charges> can cancel in $Q$ while contributing in the same direction to $\mathbf p$. For a neutral pair with positive <electric charge> $q>0$ at $\mathbf r_+$ and negative <electric charge> $-q$ at $\mathbf r_-$,
$$
\mathbf d=\mathbf r_+-\mathbf r_-,\qquad \mathbf p=q\mathbf d.
$$
Thus the <electric dipole moment> points from negative to positive <electric charge>, not along the attraction between the two <point charges>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-7-electric-dipoles[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics 2, 5.7: charge-pair geometry and dipole moment]{ref}.

Changing the coordinate origin changes the position vectors, not the physical <electric charge> distribution. If the new origin is at $\mathbf a$ relative to the old origin, $\mathbf r_i'=\mathbf r_i-\mathbf a$, so direct substitution gives
$$
\mathbf p'=\sum_iq_i(\mathbf r_i-\mathbf a)=\mathbf p-Q\mathbf a.
$$
Consequently <charge neutrality> makes the <electric dipole moment> independent of the origin. For a system with nonzero total <electric charge>, always specify the origin: a single <point charge> at the old origin has $\mathbf p=0$ but $\mathbf p'=-Q\mathbf a$. This is coordinate bookkeeping, not a newly created <electric dipole>. The full <electric potential> stays the same at the same physical point; the terms of its <electric multipole expansion> and the observation coordinates change together.

As a stipulated numerical example, put $+2\,\mathrm{nC}$ at $(1,0,0)\,\mathrm{mm}$ and $-2\,\mathrm{nC}$ at $(-1,0,0)\,\mathrm{mm}$. Their total <electric charge> is zero and $\mathbf p=(4\times10^{-12},0,0)\,\mathrm{C\,m}$. Translating the origin by $(3,-2,0)\,\mathrm{mm}$ leaves this <electric dipole moment> unchanged. Adding $+1\,\mathrm{nC}$ at the old origin instead gives $Q=1\,\mathrm{nC}$ and $\mathbf p'=(1,2,0)\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{C\,m}$. Distinguishing these two cases prevents the mistaken claim that every <electric dipole moment> is independent of the origin.

The usefulness of the <electric dipole moment> is compression: many internal <electric charges> determine one <vector> governing the leading distant <electric potential> of a neutral localized object when $\mathbf p\ne0$. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_06.html#Ch6-S2[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.6.2: distant dipole fields]{ref}. A <polar molecule> need not literally contain two <point charges>; its <atomic nuclei> and <electron density> collectively give an <electric dipole moment>. For example, the <chemical polarity> of <water> reflects its <bent molecular geometry>, whereas the symmetric linear <molecular geometry> of <carbon dioxide> makes its permanent <electric dipole moment> vanish. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity[Flowers, Theopold, Langley and Robinson, Chemistry 2e, 7.6: molecular geometry and polarity]{ref}. A collection of such <electric dipole moments> per unit volume gives <electric polarization>. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/Electromagnetism/node40.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Polarization: dipole moment per unit volume]{ref}.

= Time derivative of the electric dipole moment
{parent=Electric dipole moment}

For a localized charge and current distribution satisfying <charge conservation from Maxwell equations>,
$$
\dot{\mathbf p}=\int\mathbf J(x,t)\,d^3x.
$$

= Electric dipole
{parent=Electric dipole moment}
{wiki}

A finite <electric dipole> consists of equal and opposite <electric charges> separated by a nonzero <displacement>. It has <charge neutrality> but a nonzero <electric dipole moment> $\mathbf p=q\mathbf d$. An ideal point <electric dipole> is the mathematical limit $d=|\mathbf d|\to0$ with $\mathbf p$ fixed, so $q$ must increase as $1/d$. Shrinking the separation while holding $q$ fixed instead makes the <electric dipole moment> vanish. The ideal <electric dipole> describes an exterior <electric field>; it does not reproduce the two near-charge singularities or the internal structure of a <molecule>. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node20.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Electric Dipoles: the fixed-moment point limit]{ref}.

Place the pair's midpoint at the origin and write $k_e=1/(4\pi\epsilon_0)$, where $\epsilon_0$ is <vacuum permittivity>. By <superposition principle> and <Coulomb's law>, its exact <electric potential> away from the two <point charges> is
$$
\Phi_{\rm pair}(\mathbf r)=k_eq\left(\frac1{|\mathbf r-\mathbf d/2|}-\frac1{|\mathbf r+\mathbf d/2|}\right).
$$
For $r=|\mathbf r|\gg d$, retain the first nonzero term of a <Taylor series>. The <electric potential> and its negative <gradient> become
$$
\Phi_{\rm dip}(\mathbf r)=k_e\frac{\mathbf p\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r}}{r^2},
\qquad
\mathbf E_{\rm dip}(\mathbf r)=\frac{k_e}{r^3}\left[3(\mathbf p\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r})\widehat{\mathbf r}-\mathbf p\right],\qquad r>0.
$$
Here $\widehat{\mathbf r}=\mathbf r/r$ and the products in parentheses are <dot products>. These are exact exterior formulas for the ideal <electric dipole> and distant approximations for a finite pair. The <electric field> on the outer axis points along $\mathbf p$ with magnitude $2k_ep/r^3$; on the perpendicular bisector it points opposite $\mathbf p$ with magnitude $k_ep/r^3$. This directional structure is what a scalar total <electric charge> misses. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_06.html#Ch6-S2[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.6.2: exact pair potential and dipole expansion]{ref}.

In a prescribed uniform external <electric field> $\mathbf E_{\rm ext}$, a rigid pair has opposite <forces> $\mathbf F_\pm=\pm q\mathbf E_{\rm ext}$. Its net <force> is zero, but summing their moments about the midpoint gives the <torque>
$$
\boldsymbol\tau=\frac{\mathbf d}{2}\times q\mathbf E_{\rm ext}+\left(-\frac{\mathbf d}{2}\right)\times\left(-q\mathbf E_{\rm ext}\right)=\mathbf p\times\mathbf E_{\rm ext}.
$$
The <cross product> makes the <torque> perpendicular to both the <electric dipole moment> and the external <electric field>. If $\theta$ is the angle from $\mathbf E_{\rm ext}$ to $\mathbf p$ in their plane, the signed turning <torque> is $\tau_\theta=-pE_{\rm ext}\sin\theta$. It tends to reduce a small positive $\theta$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-7-electric-dipoles[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics 2, 5.7: forces and torque on a dipole]{ref}.

The external interaction <potential energy>, with zero chosen at $\theta=\pi/2$, is
$$
U=-\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf E_{\rm ext}=-pE_{\rm ext}\cos\theta,\qquad \tau_\theta=-\frac{dU}{d\theta}.
$$
Parallel alignment minimizes this <potential energy>; antiparallel alignment maximizes it even though the <torque> is zero in both positions. This omits the pair's constant internal binding <potential energy> and assumes a fixed permanent <electric dipole moment>. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/Electromagnetism.pdf[Richard Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism, exercise 2.10: dipole torque and energy]{ref}.

\Image[electric-dipoles-pair-torque.png]
{title=Neutral charge pair and orientation energy in a uniform electric field}
{description=Original analytical illustration for a rigid <electric dipole>. Equal opposite <forces> cancel in translation but give a clockwise <torque> at the depicted positive angle. The <potential energy> curve is the dimensionless model $U/(pE_{\rm ext})=-\cos\theta$, not measured data.}

A nonuniform external <electric field> can exert a net <force> on a neutral <electric dipole>. Before taking the small-pair approximation, the exact external <force> is $\mathbf F=q[\mathbf E_{\rm ext}(\mathbf R+\mathbf d/2)-\mathbf E_{\rm ext}(\mathbf R-\mathbf d/2)]$, where $\mathbf R$ is the midpoint. A first-order <Taylor series> gives, for a fixed <electric dipole moment> and an external <electric field> varying slowly across $d$,
$$
\mathbf F\simeq(\mathbf p\cdot\nabla)\mathbf E_{\rm ext}=\nabla(\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf E_{\rm ext})
$$
in <electrostatics>. The last equality uses the vanishing <curl> of the external <electric field>, with $\mathbf p$ held fixed while differentiating. Thus uniform external <electric fields> turn a misaligned <electric dipole>, whereas gradients can also move its midpoint. For example, the local source-free <electric field> $\mathbf E_{\rm ext}=Gx\widehat{\mathbf x}+(E_0-Gy)\widehat{\mathbf y}$ gives $\mathbf F=(Gp_x,-Gp_y,0)$, so reversing the <electric dipole moment> reverses the net <force>. This stipulated local model has zero <divergence> and zero <curl>.

A motivating experiment is Filsinger and colleagues' study of electrostatic deflection of <polar molecules>. Benzonitrile ($\mathrm{C_7H_5N}$) in <helium> expanded from a pulsed valve into <vacuum>; two 1-mm skimmers narrowed the beam before a 15-cm deflector. A grounded trough and a high-voltage rod produced a vertical <electric field> gradient. Scanning a focused <laser> vertically downstream and recording benzonitrile ions from <photoionization> measured the beam's relative density. With voltage off, the profile spanned about 2 mm; applying voltage shifted and broadened it upwards. At 10 kV, many <molecules> lay outside the original profile. https://pure.mpg.de/pubman/item/item_736019_2/component/file_736018/433451%28filsinger%29.pdf[Filsinger et al., Quantum state selection, alignment, and orientation of large molecules using static electric and laser fields, sections II and III A, figure 3]{ref}.

Interpretation uses a <quantum state>'s <Stark effect> <energy> $W(E)$: $\mu_{\rm eff}=-dW/dE$ gives $F_y=\mu_{\rm eff}\,\partial_yE$. States whose <energy> falls with increasing <electric field> seek stronger fields. The voltage-off profile controls the mechanical aperture. Inferring state populations needs calculated trajectories, <speed> distributions and a rotational <temperature> model; profile displacement alone does not measure a permanent <electric dipole moment> uniquely. This is an electric <Stark effect> experiment, with <photoionization> occurring after deflection. https://pure.mpg.de/pubman/item/item_736019_2/component/file_736018/433451%28filsinger%29.pdf[Filsinger et al., section III A: Stark slopes, trajectory modelling and state selection]{ref}.

For the symmetric finite pair, the first omitted <electric multipole expansion> term after the <electric dipole> is third order, so typical relative errors scale as $(d/r)^2$. This stronger error estimate need not hold for an arbitrary neutral <molecule> with a nonzero <electric quadrupole moment>. A concrete check for a pair on the $z$ axis compares the exact and ideal <electric fields> at $r>d/2$ on the positive axis and at $r>0$ on the perpendicular bisector:
$$
\frac{E_{z,\rm pair}(0,0,r)}{E_{z,\rm dip}(0,0,r)}=\left(1-\frac{d^2}{4r^2}\right)^{-2},
\qquad
\frac{E_{z,\rm pair}(r,0,0)}{E_{z,\rm dip}(r,0,0)}=\left(1+\frac{d^2}{4r^2}\right)^{-3/2}.
$$
Both perpendicular-bisector components are negative. At $r=5d$, the ratios are approximately $1.0203$ and $0.9852$: errors of $+2.03\%$ and $-1.48\%$ relative to the ideal <electric field>. The condition $r\gg d$ concerns observing the pair's own <electric field>; the separate condition for its external <force> approximation concerns variation of the applied <electric field> across the pair.

\Image[electric-dipoles-finite-field.png]
{title=Finite charge pair approaching its ideal electric dipole field}
{description=Original geometry and analytical field-ratio curves for two <point charges> $\pm q$ separated by $d$. The outer axial <electric field> points along the <electric dipole moment>; the perpendicular-bisector <electric field> points against it. The curves compare exact <Coulomb's law> sums with the ideal <electric dipole> prediction and are models, not experimental measurements.}

= Electric dipole-dipole interaction
{parent=Electric dipole}

Two point dipoles separated by $\mathbf r$ have interaction proportional to
$$
\frac{\mathbf p_1\cdot\mathbf p_2}{r^3}
-\frac{3(\mathbf p_1\cdot\mathbf r)(\mathbf p_2\cdot\mathbf r)}{r^5}.
$$

The formula combines the source <electric dipole> field with the second dipole's coupling to that field.

= Electric multipole expansion
{parent=Electrostatics}

= Multipole expansion
{synonym}

= Electrostatic multipole expansion
{synonym}

For a localized charge distribution, the far <electric potential> is an inverse-radius expansion whose first terms are the total charge monopole, the electric dipole moment, and the electric quadrupole moment.

= Electric quadrupole moment
{parent=Electric multipole expansion}
{title2=$Q_{ij}=\sum_a q_a(3r_{a,i}r_{a,j}-|r_a|^2\delta_{ij})$}

The trace-free <electric quadrupole moment> is a symmetric tensor measuring the second-order term in an <electric multipole expansion>. For a localized charge distribution its contribution to the far-field <electrostatic potential> is $Q_{ij}\widehat r_i\widehat r_j/(8\pi\epsilon_0r^3)$, with summation over indices. The moment may be nonzero even when the total <electric charge> or <electric dipole moment> is nonzero; it should not be confused with an <electric quadrupole> whose lower moments vanish. Changing the origin generally changes the tensor when lower moments are nonzero.

= Electric quadrupole
{parent=Electric multipole expansion}
{wiki}

An electric quadrupole is a charge distribution whose total charge and <electric dipole moment> vanish while its second charge moment does not. Its potential decays as $r^{-3}$.

= Multipole expansion of three collinear charges
{parent=Electric multipole expansion}

For charges $-Q,NQ,-MQ$ at $z=d,0,-d$, respectively,
$$
\Phi(r,\theta)=\frac{Q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\left[
\frac{N-M-1}{r}+\frac{(M-1)d\cos\theta}{r^2}
-\frac{(M+1)d^2P_2(\cos\theta)}{r^3}+O(r^{-4})\right].
$$
The monopole vanishes when $N=M+1$, the dipole vanishes when $M=1$, and both vanish when $(N,M)=(2,1)$.

= Dipole and quadrupole scaling limits of three collinear charges
{parent=Multipole expansion of three collinear charges}

Subject to $N=M+1$, keeping $Qd$ fixed as $d\to0$ gives a finite point-dipole limit when $M\ne1$. Keeping $Qd^2$ fixed gives a finite pure-quadrupole limit only when $M=1$; otherwise the dipole diverges.

= Transverse force from a collinear electric quadrupole
{parent=Multipole expansion of three collinear charges}

For charges $-Q,2Q,-Q$ at $z=d,0,-d$, the exact force on a charge $-Q$ at $(x,0,0)$ is
$$
F=-\frac{Q^2x}{2\pi\epsilon_0}
\left(\frac1{|x|^3}-\frac1{(x^2+d^2)^{3/2}}\right)\widehat x.
$$
It points toward the origin for every nonzero $x$.

= Magnetostatics
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

Magnetostatics studies time-independent magnetic fields and steady currents. In vacuum its field equations are $\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B=0$ and $\nabla\mathbin\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J$.

= Magnetic scalar potential
{parent=Magnetostatics}
{title2=$\mathbf B=-\nabla\psi$}
{wiki}

In a simply connected current-free region of constant <magnetic permeability>, <Ampère's circuital law> makes the <magnetic field> curl-free, so it admits a scalar potential. The absence of magnetic monopoles then gives <Laplace's equation> for that potential. Some conventions define the potential for $\mathbf H$ instead of $\mathbf B$, differing by the constant permeability. Boundary conditions, including imposed fields at infinity, determine the potential up to an additive constant.

= Gravity-equivalent magnetic surface density
{parent=Magnetic scalar potential}
{title2=$\Sigma_M=-B_{z0}/\sqrt{\pi G\mu_0}$}

For an isolated razor-thin disk, rescale its exterior <magnetic scalar potential> to satisfy $\sqrt{4\pi G/\mu_0}\mathbf B=-\nabla\Phi_M$. Comparing the normal magnetic boundary derivative with $\partial_z\Phi=2\pi G\Sigma$ identifies the displayed equivalent signed <surface density>. Both exterior potentials satisfy <Laplace's equation>. The equivalent <surface density> is a representation of the magnetic boundary data, rather than physical negative mass.

= Maxwell equations
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

= Maxwell's equations
{c}
{synonym}

The <Maxwell equations> are the field equations of classical <electromagnetism>. In <SI> units, in vacuum with prescribed <charge density> $\rho$ and <current density> $\mathbf J$, they are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\nabla\cdot\mathbf E&=\rho/\epsilon_0,
&\nabla\cdot\mathbf B&=0,\\
\nabla\times\mathbf E&=-\frac{\partial\mathbf B}{\partial t},
&\nabla\times\mathbf B&=\mu_0\mathbf J+\mu_0\epsilon_0\frac{\partial\mathbf E}{\partial t}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here $\mathbf E$ is the <electric field> in volts per metre, $\mathbf B$ the <magnetic field> in teslas, $\rho$ in coulombs per cubic metre, and $\mathbf J$ in amperes per square metre. The constants are <vacuum permittivity> $\epsilon_0$ and the vacuum value $\mu_0$ of <magnetic permeability>. “Vacuum” specifies the field laws without a material response; “source-free” additionally means $\rho=0$ and $\mathbf J=0$. Matter described by averaged fields instead requires <Maxwell equations in matter> and <constitutive relations>.

The first equation, <Gauss's law>, makes <electric charge> a source of <electric flux>. <Gauss's law for magnetism> sets the net <magnetic flux> through a closed surface to zero. The two <curl> equations, <Faraday's law> and the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>, couple changes of the <electric field> and <magnetic field>. Appropriate <initial conditions> and <boundary conditions> select a physical solution; the two <divergence> equations constrain the initial fields. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-02-physics-ii-electricity-and-magnetism-spring-2007/2d2f227e6fd26ee25f366759e02f03dc_chapte13em_waves.pdf[MIT 8.02 notes, §13.3: Maxwell's equations]{ref}.

= Electromagnetic duality
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{wiki}

Vacuum <Maxwell equations> without sources are invariant under $E'=E\cos\alpha+cB\sin\alpha$, $cB'=cB\cos\alpha-E\sin\alpha$. At $\alpha=\pi/2$ this is $E'=cB$, $B'=-E/c$. Electric-conductor boundary conditions are not invariant under arbitrary duality rotations; the transformed fields must be tested separately against the wall conditions.

= Derivative-order ambiguity in covariant Maxwell potential equations
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{title2=$\nabla_b\nabla^a A^b-\nabla^a\nabla_bA^b=-C^a{}_bA^b$}

Use the negative curvature convention defined in <curvature-sign convention in Killing derivative identities>, and $C_{ab}=C^c{}_{acb}$. Covariant derivatives of a vector obey $[\nabla_b,\nabla^a]A^b=-C^a{}_bA^b$. Hence the flat-space equivalent expressions $\Box A^a-\partial_b\partial^aA^b$ and $\Box A^a-\partial^a\partial_bA^b$ acquire a difference $+C^a{}_bA^b$ under the <comma-to-semicolon prescription>. Covariantizing $F_{ab}=2\nabla_{[a}A_{b]}$ and its <Maxwell equations> gives $\Box A^a-\nabla^a(\nabla_bA^b)+C^a{}_bA^b=\mu_0J^a$. This form respects <gauge invariance>. Dropping the Ricci term is generally not gauge invariant. The two expressions coincide in a Ricci-flat <vacuum spacetime>; vanishing matter alone with nonzero <cosmological constant> does not imply Ricci flatness.

= Magnetic Galilean limit of the pre-Maxwell equations
{parent=Maxwell equations}

The magnetic nonrelativistic limit neglects displacement current and uses $\partial_t\mathbf B=-\nabla\times\mathbf E$, $\nabla\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J$, $\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0$. Under a <Galilean transformation> $\mathbf x'=\mathbf x-\mathbf vt$, $t'=t$, its consistent field transformations are $\mathbf B'=\mathbf B$, $\mathbf J'=\mathbf J$ and $\mathbf E'=\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$. Indeed $\partial_{t'}=\partial_t+\mathbf v\cdot\nabla$ and $\nabla\times(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)=-(\mathbf v\cdot\nabla)\mathbf B$, giving covariance of the induction equation. The full charge-current transformation has $\mathbf J'=\mathbf J-\rho_e\mathbf v$; neglecting its convective correction belongs to the magnetic quasineutral approximation, not exact relativistic electrodynamics. With $\mathbf u'=\mathbf u-\mathbf v$, the combination $\mathbf E+\mathbf u\times\mathbf B$ is invariant. Vanishing electric field in the fluid rest frame therefore gives the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation>.

= Gauss's law for magnetism
{c}
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{wiki}

Gauss's law for magnetism is $\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B=0$. Its integral form says that the net magnetic flux through every closed surface vanishes, so the normal magnetic field is continuous across an ordinary interface.

= Vacuum Maxwell equations
{c}
{parent=Maxwell equations}

For the electromagnetic two-form $F$, the source-free Maxwell equations are $dF=0$ and $d(*F)=0$. The first is the Bianchi identity and the second is the dynamical equation.

= Anti-self-dual Maxwell equations
{parent=Vacuum Maxwell equations}
{title2=$F_{AA'BB'}=\epsilon_{A'B'}\varphi_{AB},\quad \varphi_{AB}=\varphi_{BA}$}

= ASD Maxwell equations
{synonym}
{c}

In the <two-component spinor> convention with primed symmetric <gauge curvature> self-dual, an <Abelian> <Maxwell field> is anti-self-dual when its primed symmetric <gauge curvature> <two-component spinor> vanishes. For a locally exact field $F=dA$, the <Bianchi identity> gives $dF=0$; the duality relation then also gives $d{*}F=0$. Thus it obeys the source-free <Maxwell equations>. In complexified Lorentzian signature the <Hodge star> <eigenvalues> are $\pm i$; the <two-component spinor> condition specifies which component is meant without imposing a Euclidean eigenvalue on that signature.

// Destination: general-relativity.bigb

= Maxwell equations in matter
{c}
{parent=Maxwell equations}

In macroscopic matter with free charge density $\rho$ and free current density $J$,
$$
\nabla\cdot D=\rho,
\quad
\nabla\cdot B=0,
\quad
\nabla\times E=-\partial_tB,
\quad
\nabla\times H=J+\partial_tD.
$$
The electric displacement $D$ and magnetic field strength $H$ encode the material response.

These are the <Maxwell equations> after separating free and bound material sources.

= Constitutive relations
{parent=Maxwell equations in matter}

Constitutive relations specify how matter responds to fields. A homogeneous isotropic linear electromagnetic medium obeys $\mathbf D=\varepsilon\mathbf E$ and $\mathbf B=\mu\mathbf H$.

= Permeability
{title2=$\mu$}
{disambiguate=electromagnetism}
{parent=Constitutive relations}
{wiki}

= Magnetic permeability
{synonym}

Electromagnetic permeability relates magnetic flux density to magnetic field strength through $\mathbf B=\mu\mathbf H$ in an isotropic linear medium.

= Vacuum permeability
{parent=Permeability (electromagnetism)}
{title2=$\mu_0$}

Vacuum permeability is the <magnetic permeability> appearing in the vacuum <Maxwell equations>. In <SI>, it relates vacuum magnetic field strength to <magnetic flux density> through $\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf H$ and obeys $\mu_0\varepsilon_0c^2=1$, where $c$ is the <speed of light> and $\varepsilon_0$ the <vacuum permittivity>.

= Linear anisotropic dielectric
{parent=Maxwell equations in matter}

A linear anisotropic medium has constitutive relations $D_i=\varepsilon_{ij}E_j$ and $B_i=\mu_{ij}H_j$. Symmetric, time-independent tensors make the field-energy density a quadratic form.

Here <permittivity> is a tensor, so the electric displacement need not be parallel to the electric field.

= Birefringence
{parent=Linear anisotropic dielectric}
{title2=$\Delta n=n_e-n_o$}
{wiki}

<Birefringence> is the existence of different optical propagation constants for different eigenpolarizations in an anisotropic material. It provides the propagation-mode separation needed for <adiabatic optical following in a twisted nematic>.

Two different <refractive indices> can produce two displaced images, as in the <calcite double-image experiment>, or a <phase retardation> between overlapping components, as in a <waveplate>. Spatial separation concerns where <energy flux> travels; <phase retardation> concerns the relative <wave phase>. A specimen need not show two visibly separated images to have <birefringence>.

= Calcite double-image experiment
{parent=Birefringence}

Place a clear https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calcite[calcite] specimen on a transparency bearing printed words on an overhead projector. The projected readout is two copies of the lettering. Put a rotatable linear <polarizer> after the specimen: suitable orientations suppress one copy or the other. These are the actual apparatus and observations in the University of Iowa demonstration.<Polarization - Calcite & Iceland Spar by University of Iowa, Department of Physics and Astronomy>[Demonstration 6H35.15, Equipment and Procedure: crystals, polarizers, printed overhead transparency, two sets of words and selective extinction; final note on natural defects]{ref}

For a useful control, remove the specimen while keeping the transparency, projector and screen fixed: the doubling should disappear. Replace it, hold it stationary and rotate only the <polarizer>; complementary brightness changes distinguish two <linear polarizations> from an accidental duplicate transparency. These are suggested controls, rather than additional reported measurements. Ideally the two copies obey complementary <Malus's law> curves, $I_o(\alpha)=I_{o,\max}\cos^2\alpha$ and $I_e(\alpha)=I_{e,\max}\sin^2\alpha$, with <angle> $\alpha$ measured from the exiting <ordinary wave>'s <linear polarization>. Unequal maxima do not invalidate the test. Finite <polarizer> extinction, poor focus and specimen defects limit visibility; image separation alone does not determine either <refractive index> without the specimen geometry.

\Image[birefringent-polarization-ray-splitting.png]
{title=Separated <ordinary wave> and <extraordinary wave> paths carry different <linear polarizations>}
{description=An ideal parallel slab with a tilted <optic axis> illustrates <birefringent walk-off> at normal incidence. Both internal <wavevectors> are normal to the faces, but the <extraordinary wave>'s <Poynting vector> is tilted. The rays emerge parallel with a lateral displacement. The paired letters symbolize two image channels, not an optical prescription for the projector. The <polarizer> inset shows how an exiting channel is selected.}

= Uniaxial optical medium
{parent=Birefringence}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\varepsilon=\varepsilon_0\operatorname{diag}(n_o^2,n_o^2,n_e^2)$}

A transparent, nonmagnetic <linear anisotropic dielectric> is uniaxial when two principal <permittivities> agree. With the <optic axis> along $z$, its <permittivity> <tensor> has the displayed <diagonal matrix> form. Here $n_o$ and $n_e$ are principal <refractive indices> at a specified <frequency>; $n_e$ is not the <extraordinary wave>'s index in every direction.<Fundamentals of Photonics and Quantum Electronics lecture notes by Franz X. Kärtner>[§2.8.1, printed pp. 154–156, equations 2.406–2.413: uniaxial tensor and direction-dependent extraordinary index]{ref}

= Optic axis
{parent=Uniaxial optical medium}

The symmetry direction of a <uniaxial optical medium>. For a <wavevector> along it, the two <refractive indices> coincide: there is no <birefringence> for that propagation direction, even though $n_e\ne n_o$.

= Ordinary wave
{parent=Uniaxial optical medium}
{title2=$n_o$}

The <ordinary wave>'s <electric field> is perpendicular to the plane containing the <wavevector> and <optic axis>. Its <refractive index> $n_o$ is direction independent at fixed <frequency>.

= Extraordinary wave
{parent=Uniaxial optical medium}
{title2=$n_{\mathrm{ex}}(\theta)$}

The <extraordinary wave>'s <electric displacement field> lies in that plane and is perpendicular to the <wavevector>; its <electric field> need not be. For <angle> $\theta$ between the <wavevector> and <optic axis>,
$$
\frac{1}{n_{\mathrm{ex}}(\theta)^2}=\frac{\cos^2\theta}{n_o^2}+\frac{\sin^2\theta}{n_e^2}.
$$
Thus $n_{\mathrm{ex}}(0)=n_o$ and $n_{\mathrm{ex}}(\pi/2)=n_e$.<Fundamentals of Photonics and Quantum Electronics lecture notes by Franz X. Kärtner>[§§2.8.2–2.8.3, printed p. 156, equations 2.410–2.413 and longitudinal electric-field discussion]{ref}

A short derivation starts from the existing <plane wave in an anisotropic dielectric> equation. Put $\mathbf k=(k_x,0,k_z)$ and $k_0=\omega/c$, with <angular frequency> $\omega$ and vacuum <speed of light> $c$. The $y$ component gives the <ordinary wave> relation $k_x^2+k_z^2=n_o^2k_0^2$. The <determinant> of the $x,z$ system gives
$$
\frac{k_x^2}{n_e^2}+\frac{k_z^2}{n_o^2}=k_0^2.
$$
Substituting $k_x=n_{\mathrm{ex}}k_0\sin\theta$ and $k_z=n_{\mathrm{ex}}k_0\cos\theta$ yields the displayed directional <refractive index>.

= Birefringent walk-off
{parent=Extraordinary wave}
{title2=$\rho=\phi-\theta$}

The <extraordinary wave>'s <Poynting vector> can differ in direction from its <wavevector>. The latter specifies <wave phase> propagation, while the former specifies <energy flux>. This angular separation is walk-off.<Fundamentals of Photonics and Quantum Electronics lecture notes by Franz X. Kärtner>[§2.8.3, printed pp. 157–158, equations 2.414–2.419: Poynting direction and walk-off]{ref}

In the lossless, nondispersive model above, take the <gradient> of $\omega=c\sqrt{k_x^2/n_e^2+k_z^2/n_o^2}$ to obtain the <group velocity>, parallel to the <Poynting vector>. If its <angle> to the <optic axis> is $\phi$, this gives $\tan\phi=(n_o^2/n_e^2)\tan\theta$. Consequently the <extraordinary wave> generally carries <energy> away from its <wavevector> direction. An internal <wavevector> normal to a slab face therefore does not guarantee a normal energy path. <Birefringent walk-off> vanishes at $\theta=0$ and $\theta=\pi/2$; the latter direction still has unequal <refractive indices>. This is a useful <waveplate> geometry: overlapping components can acquire <phase retardation> without spatial splitting. A thin specimen, insufficient imaging resolution, or excitation of only one <linear polarization> can also hide a double image, for different reasons.

= Waveplate
{parent=Birefringence}
{wiki}

= Wave plate
{synonym}

A <waveplate> uses <birefringence> to change <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> through <phase retardation>. Its fast axis has the smaller <refractive index>, hence the larger <phase velocity>; its slow axis has the larger index. Consider normal incidence with the <optic axis> in the plate face, so both components follow the same straight path. A <linear polarization> at $45^\circ$ to these axes excites equal <wave amplitudes>.

A complementary experiment uses two sheet <polarizers> with a cellophane sheet between them. First cross the <polarizers> and remove the sheet: the field is dark. Insert and rotate the sheet about the beam: light returns at intermediate orientations, but extinction recurs at two perpendicular orientations. The sheet has changed the <polarization of an electromagnetic wave>, without requiring a resolved double image. With white <light>, different <wavelengths> can produce different transmitted colors.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others>[I §33–3, Fig. 33–3 and the paragraphs describing removal, rotation and white-light colors of cellophane]{ref}. Brightness alone does not establish <circular polarization>: an ideal rotating linear <polarizer> also gives orientation-independent <light intensity> for <unpolarized light>.

= Phase retardation
{parent=Waveplate}
{title2=$\delta=2\pi\Delta n\,d/\lambda_0$}

For the overlapping <monochromatic wave> components, let $d$ be the thickness and $\lambda_0$ the vacuum <wavelength>. Define $\Delta n=n_s-n_f>0$ using the slow and fast <refractive indices>. With <complex amplitude> convention $e^{i(kz-\omega t)}$, the accumulated slow-minus-fast <phase difference> is
$$
\delta=(k_s-k_f)d=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda_0}(n_s-n_f)d.
$$
This is the <optical path difference> $\Delta n\,d$ expressed as a <wave phase>. It is a signed accumulation convention; with the opposite time convention the sign reverses. The outgoing <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> depends on $\delta$ modulo $2\pi$. Here the positive slow-minus-fast $\Delta n$ differs from the signed principal-index $n_e-n_o$ used in the parent <birefringence> entry when $n_e<n_o$.

For an original worked example, stipulate $\Delta n=0.0100$ at $\lambda_0=600\,\mathrm{nm}$ and a uniform thickness $d=15.0\,\mathrm{\mu m}$. These are design assumptions, not measured material data. The <optical path difference> is $150\,\mathrm{nm}$, so
$$
\delta=2\pi\frac{150\,\mathrm{nm}}{600\,\mathrm{nm}}=\frac{\pi}{2}.
$$
It is a <quarter-wave plate>. At the same <wavelength> and <refractive indices>, doubling $d$ to $30.0\,\mathrm{\mu m}$ gives $\delta=\pi$, a <half-wave plate>. A $1.0\,\mathrm{\mu m}$ thickness increase changes $\delta$ by $\pi/30$, or $6^\circ$; retaining the same <birefringence> at $500\,\mathrm{nm}$ would instead give $\delta=3\pi/5$ for the $15.0\,\mathrm{\mu m}$ plate. Real <refractive indices> also vary with <wavelength>, so this last calculation explicitly neglects <optical dispersion>.

\Image[birefringent-waveplate-phase-thickness.png]
{title=<Phase retardation> grows with thickness while overlapping components change <polarization of an electromagnetic wave>}
{description=The stipulated $\Delta n=0.0100$, $\lambda_0=600\,\mathrm{nm}$ model gives quarter-wave and half-wave thicknesses of $15$ and $30\,\mathrm{\mu m}$. Insets trace the normalized <electric field> at a fixed point for equal fast/slow <wave amplitudes>, with $E_f=\cos\tau$ and $E_s=\cos(\tau+\delta)$. The intermediate <ellipse>, circle and rotated line require no spatial beam separation.}

For these equal transmitted <wave amplitudes>, <orthogonal projection> onto a <polarizer> crossed with the initial $45^\circ$ <linear polarization> gives a simple independent readout. After removing a common <wave phase>, the component <complex amplitudes> are proportional to $(1,e^{i\delta})/\sqrt2$; <orthogonal projection> onto $(1,-1)/\sqrt2$ gives $(1-e^{i\delta})/2$. Squaring its <modulus> yields $I_\perp/I_{\mathrm{in}}=\sin^2(\delta/2)$: zero at $d=0$, one half at $15\,\mathrm{\mu m}$, and one at $30\,\mathrm{\mu m}$. This assumes ideal <polarizers>, negligible <specular reflection> and <wave absorption>, overlapping components and stable relative <wave phase>. It measures a periodic function of <phase retardation>, so one brightness measurement cannot determine the thickness uniquely.

= Quarter-wave plate
{parent=Waveplate}
{title2=$\delta=\pm\pi/2\pmod{2\pi}$}

A <quarter-wave plate> gives a quarter-cycle <phase retardation>. Equal transmitted <wave amplitudes> initially in <linear polarization> at $45^\circ$ become <circular polarization>; unequal <wave amplitudes> generally give <elliptical polarization>. Reversing the sign reverses the traversal of the same circle.

= Half-wave plate
{parent=Waveplate}
{title2=$\delta=\pi\pmod{2\pi}$}

A <half-wave plate> reverses the relative sign of the two components. For initial <linear polarization> at <angle> $\alpha$ to the fast axis, the outgoing line is at $-\alpha$ modulo $\pi$. The $45^\circ$ input therefore rotates by $90^\circ$.

= Ampère's circuital law
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{c}
{wiki}

= Ampère-Maxwell equation
{c}
{synonym}

= Ampere-Maxwell law
{c}
{synonym}

= Ampère's law
{c}
{synonym}

The Ampère-Maxwell equation
$$
\nabla\times B=\mu_0J+\mu_0\epsilon_0\frac{\partial E}{\partial t}
$$
relates magnetic circulation to conduction and displacement current.

= Amperian loop
{parent=Ampère's circuital law}
{c}
{wiki}

An Amperian loop is a closed curve used in the integral form of Ampère's circuital law or the Ampère-Maxwell equation.

= Magnetostatic Ampère law
{parent=Ampère's circuital law}
{title2=$\oint_C\mathbf B\cdot d\boldsymbol\ell=\mu_0I_{\mathrm{enc}}$}

In vacuum <magnetostatics>, the <Ampère-Maxwell equation> reduces to $\nabla\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J$. Applying <Stokes' theorem> gives $\oint_C\mathbf B\cdot d\boldsymbol\ell=\mu_0I_{\mathrm{enc}}$, where $I_{\mathrm{enc}}$ is the <electric current> crossing an oriented spanning surface. Time-dependent <electric fields> require the additional <displacement current> term in the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>.

= Displacement current
{title2=$\mathbf J_D=\epsilon_0\partial_t\mathbf E$}
{parent=Ampère's circuital law}
{wiki}

In vacuum, <displacement current> is the term $\mathbf J_D=\epsilon_0\partial_t\mathbf E$ in the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>. It has the units of <current density>, but need not describe <electric charge> crossing the region. In <Maxwell equations in matter>, the corresponding term is $\partial_t\mathbf D$, with $\mathbf D$ the <electric displacement field>.

Consider a slowly charging <parallel-plate capacitor> with vacuum gap, plate area $A$, and charge $Q(t)$ on its positive plate. Neglect fringing and assume the charging timescale is long compared with the light-travel time across the apparatus. The nearly uniform gap <electric field> has magnitude $E=Q/(\epsilon_0A)$, so the <displacement current> through the gap is
$$
I_D=\epsilon_0 A\frac{dE}{dt}=\frac{dQ}{dt}=I.
$$
For $A=0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and charging <electric current> $I=1.0\,\mathrm{mA}$, this gives $dE/dt\simeq1.13\times10^{10}\,\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$ and $J_D=0.10\,\mathrm{A/m^2}$. No conducting <electric current> crosses the gap. A spanning surface cutting the wire and another passing between the plates nevertheless give the same magnetic circulation when the full <Ampère-Maxwell equation> is used. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_18.html#Ch18-S2[Feynman lectures, II.18.2: charging a capacitor]{ref}.

The local consistency check is short. Without <displacement current>, taking the <divergence> of $\nabla\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J$ would force $\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0$, allowing no local accumulation of <electric charge>. With <displacement current>, the <divergence of a curl is zero> and <Gauss's law> give
$$
0=\mu_0\left(\nabla\cdot\mathbf J+\epsilon_0\partial_t\nabla\cdot\mathbf E\right)
=\mu_0\left(\nabla\cdot\mathbf J+\partial_t\rho\right).
$$
This is precisely the <charge continuity equation>. <Electric charge conservation> fixes the required <divergence> of the added term; it does not by itself exclude other divergence-free additions. The specific <Maxwell equations> also yield the <electromagnetic wave equation>, linking this consistency correction to propagation at the <speed of light>.

= Faraday's law of induction
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\partial_t\mathbf B$}

= Faraday's law
{c}
{synonym}

= Faraday law
{c}
{synonym}

<Faraday's law of induction> relates a changing <magnetic field> to circulation of the <electric field>. For a fixed <oriented surface> $S$ with boundary $C=\partial S$, choose the positive direction around $C$ by the <right-hand rule>: the fingers follow $C$ while the thumb follows the chosen <unit normal>. The fixed-loop electromagnetic <electromotive force> is
$$
\mathcal E=\oint_C\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l
=-\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int_S\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S
=-\frac{d\Phi_B}{dt},
\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\frac{\partial\mathbf B}{\partial t}.
$$
The final <derivative> of <magnetic flux> is an ordinary time derivative, with the <surface> held fixed. Reversing both the <unit normal> and the boundary direction reverses both signs. The loop may be an imagined path: the field law does not require a wire or an <electric current>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_17.html#Ch17-S1[Feynman lectures, II.17.1: electric-field circulation and induction]{ref}.

For a computed example, a fixed single-turn loop has <area> $A=0.020\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and out-of-page <unit normal>. Prescribe an external uniform <magnetic field> in that direction with $B(t)=0.10\,\mathrm T+(0.30\,\mathrm{T/s})t$ for $0\le t\le1.0\,\mathrm s$. Then
$$
\Phi_{\mathrm{ext}}(t)=AB(t)
=0.0020\,\mathrm{Wb}+(0.0060\,\mathrm{Wb/s})t,
\qquad
\mathcal E_{\mathrm{CCW}}=-0.0060\,\mathrm V.
$$
In a quasistatic resistive model with <electrical resistance> $R=2.0\,\Omega$, neglect the loop's <self-inductance> and its own contribution to changing <magnetic flux>. <Ohm's law> gives
$$
I_{\mathrm{CCW}}=\frac{\mathcal E_{\mathrm{CCW}}}{R}=-0.0030\,\mathrm A.
$$
The <magnetic flux> rises from $0.0020\,\mathrm{Wb}$ to $0.0080\,\mathrm{Wb}$ while the induced <electromotive force> stays constant: it measures the rate of change, rather than the amount of <magnetic flux>. The negative sign means clockwise <electric current>; its <magnetic field> through the loop points into the page, as explained by <Lenz's law>. The toy model describes the interval of steady ramping, excluding switching transients at its ends.

\Image[faraday-changing-flux-and-lenz-current.png]
{title=<Faraday's law of induction> and <Lenz's law> for increasing out-of-page <magnetic flux>}
{description=Counterclockwise is the positive orientation. Dots denote the external out-of-page <magnetic field>, and crosses denote the in-page <magnetic field> produced by the clockwise induced <electric current>. The plots show $\Phi_{\mathrm{ext}}$ increasing from $2$ to $8\,\mathrm{mWb}$, with constant $\mathcal E=-6\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $I=-3\,\mathrm{mA}$ in the resistive approximation.}

For a moving conducting loop $C(t)$, the electromagnetic <electromotive force> instead includes the <Lorentz force> contribution from the local <velocity> $\mathbf v$ of each conductor segment:
$$
\mathcal E(t)=\oint_{C(t)}(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf l
=-\frac{d}{dt}\int_{S(t)}\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S.
$$
This form assumes a thin-wire model with a well-defined closed contour and spanning <oriented surface> that move compatibly, a <magnetic field> satisfying <Gauss's law for magnetism>, and conductor speeds small compared with the <speed of light>. Ideal sliding contacts such as those in <motional electromotive force> can be treated piecewise. Arbitrary changes of electrical connections require checking the actual contour and conductor velocities; differentiating the flux through an arbitrary chosen moving path is insufficient. The <velocity> here describes conductor motion, not the charge carriers' drift along the wire. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_17.html#Ch17-S2[Feynman lectures, II.17.2: limits of the flux rule]{ref}.

A short derivation of the fixed-loop integral statement applies <Stokes theorem> to the local <curl> equation and differentiates under the fixed <surface integral>. For the moving loop, the <magnetic flux transport for a moving circuit> adds the swept-area term $-\oint_C(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot d\mathbf l$ to $d\Phi_B/dt$. Combining these two identities gives the moving-loop statement. This derives the integral forms from the local field law; it is not a proof of that empirical law from mathematics alone.

The useful unity is geometric: changing the <magnetic field>, the enclosed <area>, or the orientation can change <magnetic flux> and hence produce an <electromotive force>. A coil's multiple turns are accounted for by <magnetic flux linkage>, rather than by multiplying a single-turn flux without checking which flux each turn receives. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-1-faradays-law[OpenStax University Physics 2, §13.1: flux changes and coils]{ref}.

Faraday's report read on 24 November 1831 describes opposite momentary responses when a neighboring circuit is connected and disconnected, with no continuing response while its steady <electric current> is maintained. These are concrete experimental roots of the changing-flux idea. The report predates the modern <Maxwell equations> notation used above. https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14986/14986-h/14986-h.htm[Faraday, Experimental Researches in Electricity, First Series (1831), §§10–11, reprinted in volume I]{ref}.

= Lenz's law
{parent=Faraday's law of induction}
{c}
{wiki}

<Lenz's law> expresses the minus sign in <Faraday's law of induction>: the induced <electromotive force> drives a passive resistive loop's <electric current> in the direction whose own <magnetic field> opposes the imposed change in <magnetic flux>. **It opposes the change of flux, not invariably the external magnetic field.** Choose an <oriented surface> and its positive boundary direction first, find the sign of $d\Phi_{\mathrm{ext}}/dt$, and then use the <right-hand rule> to translate the signed <electric current> into a physical direction. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-2-lenzs-law[OpenStax University Physics 2, §13.2: current direction and changing flux]{ref}.

In the fixed-loop example in <Faraday's law of induction>, out-of-page <magnetic flux> increases at $0.0060\,\mathrm{Wb/s}$. With counterclockwise positive, $I=-0.0030\,\mathrm A$ is clockwise, producing an in-page <magnetic field> through the loop. Reverse the ramp to $B(t)=0.40\,\mathrm T-(0.30\,\mathrm{T/s})t$ on the same one-second interval: now $\mathcal E=+0.0060\,\mathrm V$ and $I=+0.0030\,\mathrm A$. The counterclockwise <electric current> produces an out-of-page <magnetic field>, supporting the decreasing external <magnetic flux>. A steady out-of-page <magnetic field> with fixed loop geometry gives no induced <electromotive force> in either direction.

For the increasing-area example in <motional electromotive force>, clockwise <electric current> runs down the right-hand rod. Its <magnetic force on a current-carrying wire> points left, opposing the imposed motion to the right. Writing $I_{\mathrm{mag}}=B\ell v/R$ for the current magnitude, the <power> required to maintain constant <speed> is
$$
P_{\mathrm{drive}}=(I_{\mathrm{mag}}\ell B)v
=\frac{(B\ell v)^2}{R}=I_{\mathrm{mag}}^2R=P_{\mathrm{Joule}}.
$$
Thus the sign agrees with <conservation of energy>: the external drive supplies the <Joule heating>. The <magnetic forces do no work> on individual charges; mechanical <work> by the drive supplies the electrical <energy>. This also explains the usefulness of <electromagnetic braking of a sliding rod> and the <terminal speed of a falling conducting loop>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-3-motional-emf[OpenStax University Physics 2, §13.3: mechanical power and resistive heating]{ref}.

The sign of induced <electromotive force> alone does not determine an arbitrary circuit's instantaneous <electric current>. Retaining constant <self-inductance> $L$ gives $L\,dI/dt+RI=-d\Phi_{\mathrm{ext}}/dt$, so a pre-existing <electric current> can persist while the induced drive opposes its change. The distinction matters in the <lossless limit of magnetic loop braking>. The resistive toy examples use $L=0$ as an approximation, not a general consequence of <Lenz's law>.

= Terminal speed of a falling conducting loop
{parent=Lenz's law}

If a loop of resistance $R$ has magnetic flux $\Phi_B=-kz$, then its induced current is $I=k\dot z/R$. When its magnetic force is $-kI$ in the vertical direction, its equation under gravity is
$$
m\ddot z=-mg-\frac{k^2}{R}\dot z,
$$
so its downward terminal speed is $Rmg/k^2$.

= Lossless limit of magnetic loop braking
{parent=Terminal speed of a falling conducting loop}
{title2=$\mathcal L I+Kz=\text{constant}$}

For an external loop flux $Kz$, positive current orientation and upward <Lorentz force> $KI$, including <self-inductance> gives $\mathcal L\dot I+RI=-K\dot z$ and $m\ddot z=-mg+KI$. At zero resistance total linked flux is conserved. If initially $I=0$ at $z=z_0$, then $I=-K(z-z_0)/\mathcal L$ and the mechanical motion is an undamped oscillator about $z_0-mg\mathcal L/K^2$. The finite-resistance terminal speed tends to zero as $R\to0$, but neglecting <self-inductance> is a singular approximation in that limit. A lossless loop does not dispose of <energy> through <Joule heating>.

= Gauss's law
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$\oint_{\partial V}\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n\,dS=Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}/\varepsilon_0$}

= Gauss law
{c}
{synonym}

<Gauss's law> relates the <electric field> on a <closed surface> to the net <electric charge> inside it. In <SI> units, with outward <unit normal> $\mathbf n$,
$$
\boxed{\oint_{\partial V}\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n\,dS=\frac{Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}}{\varepsilon_0},\qquad Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}=\int_V\rho\,dV.}
$$
Here $\rho$ describes a volume <charge density>, and $\varepsilon_0$ is the <vacuum permittivity>; for ideal point or surface charges, use the enclosed <electric charge> directly rather than an ordinary volume-density integral. The left side is <electric flux>: the <surface integral> of the outward normal component of the <electric field>. Field directed outward contributes positively; field directed inward contributes negatively. The equality uses net signed <electric charge>, so positive and negative enclosed charges can cancel. The <Gaussian surface> is an imagined accounting boundary, not necessarily a material object. See https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-2-explaining-gausss-law[OpenStax, explaining Gauss's law].

Where the <electric field> and <charge density> are smooth, the local statement is $\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=\rho/\varepsilon_0$. The <divergence theorem> connects this <divergence> to the <surface integral>. This is the satisfying connection between local sources and global accounting: a surface can register how much net <electric charge> it encloses without revealing where that charge is located within it. A <point charge> requires care with the singular field at its position; the integral formulation remains useful with a boundary that avoids the charge.

The law fixes total <electric flux>, rather than every value of the <electric field>. In an uncharged region, a uniform <electric field> $\mathbf E=(E_0,0,0)$ crosses a cube of side $\ell$. Its left and right faces contribute $-E_0\ell^2$ and $+E_0\ell^2$; the other four faces contribute zero. Thus **the net electric flux is zero while the electric field is nonzero everywhere**. Charges outside a <Gaussian surface> can change the field on it without changing its net <electric flux>. Conversely, fields cannot generally be reconstructed from enclosed <electric charge> alone. <Electrostatics> also requires the <electric field> to be the negative <gradient> of an <electric potential>, with appropriate boundary information. This distinction is emphasized in https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_05.html[Feynman lectures II.5, application of Gauss' law].

\Image[gaussian-flux-and-field.png]
{title=Zero net flux can coexist with a nonzero uniform field, and equal enclosed charge can give different field magnitudes on different spheres}

<Spherical symmetry> supplies the extra information needed for a particularly simple calculation. An isolated <spherically symmetric> charge distribution in vacuum produces a radial <electric field>, constant in magnitude on each concentric <sphere>. Its <electric flux> is therefore $4\pi r^2E_r(r)$, giving
$$
E_r(r)=\frac{Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}(r)}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r^2}.
$$
Outside the distribution, doubling $r$ quadruples the <surface area> and divides the radial <electric field> by four, while the <electric flux> stays $Q/\varepsilon_0$. The inverse-square dependence is an area-dilution result. The <electric field of a uniformly charged ball> and <electric field of a uniformly charged spherical shell> show that the same total charge can give different interior fields. These symmetry arguments and their limits are developed in https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-3-applying-gausss-law[OpenStax, applying Gauss's law].

= Electric field of a uniformly charged ball
{parent=Gauss's law}
{title2=$E_r(r)=Qr/(4\pi\varepsilon_0R^3)\quad(0\leq r\leq R)$}

= Electric field of a uniformly charged solid sphere
{synonym}

Distribute a positive <electric charge> $Q$ uniformly through a <closed ball> of radius $R$ in vacuum, with no external field. Its volume <charge density> is $3Q/(4\pi R^3)$. For $0<r<R$, the enclosed fraction is $(r/R)^3$. Applying <Gauss's law> on a concentric <Gaussian surface> gives
$$
E_r(r)=\begin{cases}\dfrac{Qr}{4\pi\varepsilon_0R^3},&0\leq r\leq R,\\[4pt]\dfrac{Q}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r^2},&r\geq R.\end{cases}
$$
The <electric field> grows linearly inside and decays as an inverse square outside. With $E_R=Q/(4\pi\varepsilon_0R^2)$, its values at $r/R=1/2,1,2$ are $E_R/2,E_R,E_R/4$. The field vanishes at the centre by <spherical symmetry> and is continuous at the surface because this ideal volume distribution has no singular surface layer. Its interior behavior differs from a <conductor in electrostatic equilibrium>, where mobile charge redistributes.

\Image[gauss-charged-ball-and-shell.png]
{title=A uniform volume charge and a uniform thin-shell charge have the same exterior field but different interior fields}

= Electric field of a uniformly charged spherical shell
{parent=Gauss's law}
{title2=$E_r(r)=0\ (r<R),\quad E_r(r)=Q/(4\pi\varepsilon_0r^2)\ (r>R)$}

Place the same positive <electric charge> $Q$ uniformly on the <sphere> of radius $R$, with no external field. Every concentric interior <Gaussian surface> encloses zero charge; <spherical symmetry> then makes the interior <electric field> zero. Outside, it is again $Q/(4\pi\varepsilon_0r^2)$. At $R$, the inner and outer limits differ by $Q/(4\pi\varepsilon_0R^2)=\sigma/\varepsilon_0$, with <surface charge density> $\sigma=Q/(4\pi R^2)$. The ideal surface charge makes the field discontinuous, so the plot displays one-sided limits rather than assigning a single field value on the charged sheet. An isolated charged conducting sphere in <electrostatic equilibrium> realizes this exterior/interior pattern.

= Hyperbolic-space Coulomb field
{parent=Gauss's law}
{title2=$E(\xi)=Q/[4\pi\epsilon_0R^2\sinh^2(\xi/R)]$}

In static time times round three-dimensional <hyperbolic space> of curvature radius $R$, a geodesic sphere has area $4\pi R^2\sinh^2(\xi/R)$. The <Gauss law> therefore gives the displayed radial field. It approaches the flat-space inverse-square field near the charge and falls exponentially at large geodesic distance. The curvature changes the area of a sphere, not the conserved flux.

= Force between hemispheres of a uniformly charged ball
{parent=Gauss's law}
{title2=$F=3Q^2/(64\pi\varepsilon_0R^2)$}

For uniform <charge density> $\rho_e=3Q/(4\pi R^3)$, <Gauss's law> gives the interior <electric field> $E=\rho_e r/(3\varepsilon_0)$. Integrate its vertical force density $\rho_e^2z/(3\varepsilon_0)$ over the upper hemisphere: $\int z\,dV=\pi R^4/4$. Internal pair forces in that hemisphere cancel, so its net force is exactly the force from the lower hemisphere. The result is upward and has magnitude $3Q^2/(64\pi\varepsilon_0R^2)$.

= Gaussian surface
{c}
{parent=Gauss's law}
{wiki}

A <Gaussian surface> is a <closed surface> used with the integral form of <Gauss's law> to relate <electric flux> to enclosed <electric charge>. It need not coincide with the surface of any charged body. Choosing it to match a <symmetry> can make the normal component of the <electric field> constant on a portion of it, allowing that component to be taken outside a <surface integral>. A concentric <sphere> works for an isolated <spherically symmetric> charge distribution; an arbitrarily placed <sphere> generally does not.

Zero enclosed <electric charge> implies zero total <electric flux>. Inferring zero <electric field> additionally needs information such as the <spherical symmetry> used in the <electric field of a uniformly charged spherical shell>. The imagined boundary does not itself block or redirect the <electric field>.

= Gaussian pillbox
{c}
{parent=Gauss's law}

A Gaussian pillbox is a thin cylindrical <Gaussian surface> crossing an interface. Shrinking its height converts a divergence law into a jump condition for the field's normal component.

= Charge conservation from Maxwell equations
{parent=Maxwell equations}

Taking the divergence of the Ampère-Maxwell equation and using Gauss's law gives
$$
\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot J=0.
$$
Hence the charge in a fixed volume changes only through current crossing its boundary.

= Charge continuity equation
{parent=Charge conservation from Maxwell equations}
{title2=$\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0$}

= Continuity equation for electric charge
{synonym}

The local form of <conservation of electric charge> relates <charge density> to <current density>. Taking the <divergence> of the <Ampère-Maxwell equation> and substituting <Gauss's law> proves the equation, using that the <divergence of a curl is zero>.

= Covariant Maxwell equation with the minus-plus-plus-plus metric
{parent=Maxwell equations}
{c}

With metric signature $(-,+,+,+)$, $A_0=-\Phi/c$, and $j^\mu=(c\rho,J)$, the field tensor satisfies
$$
\partial_\nu F^{\mu\nu}=\mu_0j^\mu,
\qquad
\partial_{[\lambda}F_{\mu\nu]}=0.
$$
Its components are $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, so these tensor equations reproduce all four Maxwell equations.

= Source-free Maxwell equations in a linear medium
{parent=Maxwell equations}

For $D=\epsilon E$ and $B=\mu H$ with no free charge or current,
$$
\nabla\cdot D=0,\qquad
\nabla\cdot B=0,\qquad
\nabla\times E=-\partial_tB,\qquad
\nabla\times H=\partial_tD.
$$

= Plane electromagnetic wave in a linear medium
{parent=Source-free Maxwell equations in a linear medium}

A plane wave in a homogeneous linear medium satisfies
$$
\omega^2=\frac{|k|^2}{\epsilon\mu},
\qquad
v=\frac1{\sqrt{\epsilon\mu}},
\qquad
B_0=\frac1\omega k\times E_0.
$$

= Transversality of an electromagnetic plane wave
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave in a linear medium}

The source-free divergence equations imply $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf E_0=\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf B_0=0$, so both fields are transverse to the wavevector.

= Magnetic vector potential
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A magnetic vector potential satisfies B equal to its curl and is defined up to a gradient.

= Magnetic helicity
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{title2=$H=\int_V\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B\,dV,\quad\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A$}
{wiki}

The <magnetic helicity> integrates the <dot product> of a <magnetic vector potential> and its <magnetic field>. It measures linkage and twisting of magnetic flux. For a <solenoidal vector field> $\mathbf B$ tangent to the boundary, it is unchanged by the gauge transformation $\mathbf A\mapsto\mathbf A+\nabla\chi$, since the change is $\int_V\nabla\cdot(\chi\mathbf B)\,dV=\int_{\partial V}\chi\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S=0$. If its density satisfies $\partial_t h=\nabla\cdot(f\mathbf B-h\mathbf u)$ with both vector fields tangent to the boundary of a fixed volume, the <divergence theorem> gives conservation of its integral. This is an instance of <helicity conservation by tangent boundary conditions>.

= Magnetic-energy lower bound from helicity
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$E_B\geq |H_M|/(2\mu_0 C_D)$}

In a bounded regular domain, fix the global extension or circulation convention for the <magnetic vector potential>. A bounded inverse of <curl> on tangent <solenoidal vector fields> gives $\|\mathbf A\|_2\leq C_D\|\mathbf B\|_2$. The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> then gives $|H_M|\leq C_D\|\mathbf B\|_2^2=2\mu_0 C_DE_B$. One construction extends the tangent field by zero outside the domain and uses the whole-space <Biot-Savart law>; its kernel of size $|\mathbf x-\mathbf y|^{-2}$ is integrable over a bounded region, giving the required $L^2$ bound. Nonzero conserved <magnetic helicity> therefore prevents complete decay of <magnetic energy>.

= Current helicity
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$H_c=\langle\mathbf b\cdot\nabla\times\mathbf b\rangle$}

Current helicity correlates a <magnetic field> with its <curl>, which is proportional to <electric current density>. It differs from <magnetic helicity>, which uses a <magnetic vector potential>. For a solenoidal monochromatic field of wavenumber $k$ in a periodic domain, choose $\mathbf a_b=\nabla\times\mathbf b/k^2$. Then $\nabla\times\mathbf a_b=\mathbf b$ and $H_c=k^2\langle\mathbf a_b\cdot\mathbf b\rangle$.

= Gauge dependence of helicity in an open magnetic flux tube
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$H_m/L=\pi bCR_0^2$}

An open <flux tube> carries <magnetic flux> through its end caps. Its ordinary <magnetic helicity> can therefore change under a single-valued <gauge transformation>. For $\mathbf B=aR\mathbf e_\phi+b\mathbf e_z$ inside $R<R_0$ and zero outside, a regular family of <magnetic vector potentials> is $A_\phi=bR/2$, $A_z=C-aR^2/2$ inside and $A_\phi=bR_0^2/(2R)$, $A_z=C-aR_0^2/2$ outside. Their tangential components agree at $R_0$. Inside, $\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B=bC$, so the helicity per unit length is $\pi bCR_0^2$. Changing $C$ is the gauge transformation $\Lambda=\Delta C\,z$, and changes this result whenever $b\ne0$. The convention $A_z=0$ outside gives $C=aR_0^2/2$ and helicity per unit length $\pi abR_0^4/2$, while $C=0$ gives zero. Without an end-cap or gauge convention the ordinary helicity is not unique.

= Material transport of magnetic helicity density
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$D_t(\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B/\rho)$}

For smooth <ideal magnetohydrodynamics>, write $\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A$ and $\partial_t\mathbf A=\mathbf u\times\mathbf B-\nabla\varphi$. The <continuity equation> and induction equation give $D_t(\mathbf B/\rho)=(\mathbf B/\rho)\cdot\nabla\mathbf u$. The vector-potential equation becomes $D_t\mathbf A=\nabla(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf A-\varphi)-(\nabla\mathbf u)^T\mathbf A$. Contracting these two equations cancels their velocity-gradient terms and proves
$$
D_t\left(\frac{\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B}{\rho}\right)=\frac{\mathbf B}{\rho}\cdot\nabla(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf A-\varphi).
$$
Consequently the <magnetic helicity> in a material volume satisfies $dH_m/dt=\int_{\partial V}(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf A-\varphi)\mathbf B\cdot\mathbf n\,dS$. It is conserved when the <magnetic field> is tangent to the boundary. A single-valued <gauge transformation> changes $H_m$ by $\int_{\partial V}\Lambda\mathbf B\cdot\mathbf n\,dS$, which also vanishes under that condition.

= Radial-gauge potential for a divergence-free magnetic field
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{title2=$\mathbf A(\mathbf x)=\int_0^1s\,\mathbf B(s\mathbf x)\times\mathbf x\,ds$}

For a smooth <divergence>-free field on $\mathbb R^3$, the <differential form> $F=\iota_{\mathbf B}(dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3)$ is closed. The radial homotopy formula gives a global <magnetic vector potential> $\mathbf A(\mathbf x)=\int_0^1s\,\mathbf B(s\mathbf x)\times\mathbf x\,ds$ with <curl> $\mathbf B$. For constant $\mathbf B$, this reduces to $\mathbf A=\tfrac12\mathbf B\times\mathbf x$.

= Neumann's mutual-inductance formula
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{c}

For thin closed wire loops $C_1,C_2$, the mutual inductance is
$$
L_{12}=L_{21}
=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}
\oint_{C_1}\oint_{C_2}
\frac{dx_1\cdot dx_2}{|x_1-x_2|}.
$$

= Coulomb gauge
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{c}
{wiki}

The Coulomb gauge imposes
$$
\nabla\cdot A=0
$$
on the <magnetic vector potential>. A <gauge transformation> $A\mapsto A+\nabla\chi$ reaches this gauge when $\chi$ solves
$$
\nabla^2\chi=-\nabla\cdot A.
$$

= Radiation gauge
{parent=Coulomb gauge}
{title2=$A_0=0,\quad\partial_iA_i=0$}

For a free <electromagnetic field> with suitable boundary conditions, combine <Coulomb gauge> $\partial_iA_i=0$ with $A_0=0$. <Gauss's law> makes the second condition consistent in the source-free problem. The remaining field operator is spatially transverse and describes the two radiative <photon> polarizations. A constrained scalar potential must be restored when charges are present.

= Gauge transformation
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{wiki}

The transformation $A\mapsto A+\nabla\chi$ leaves $B=\nabla\times A$ unchanged because the <curl> of a <gradient> vanishes.

= Gauge-covariant time translation
{parent=Gauge transformation}
{title2=$\delta A_\nu=\alpha F_{0\nu}$}

For the <Maxwell field>, the variation $\delta A_\nu=\alpha F_{0\nu}=\alpha(\partial_0A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_0)$ is time translation combined with a <gauge transformation>. With constant $\alpha$, $\delta F_{\mu\nu}=\alpha\partial_0F_{\mu\nu}$, and the Lagrangian changes by $\alpha\partial_0\mathcal L$. Its <Noether current> is directly the time component of the <gauge-invariant Maxwell stress-energy tensor>, providing the physical energy density without a potential-dependent improvement step.

= Magnetic dipole moment
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathbf m$}

= Magnetic moment
{synonym}

A <magnetic dipole moment> is a <vector> that summarizes the strength and orientation of a localized magnetic source. For a planar <current loop>, it is the <electric current> times the <vector area>: $\mathbf m=IA\widehat{\mathbf n}$. The <right-hand rule> fixes the unit <normal vector> $\widehat{\mathbf n}$: curl the fingers of the right hand along the <conventional current>, and the thumb points along $\mathbf m$. This single <vector> predicts both the distant <magnetic dipole field> and the turning effect of an imposed <magnetic field>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/11.5 Force and Torque on a Current Loop>[§11.5, equations 11.20–11.22: loop moment, winding count and torque]{ref}

The <SI> unit is <ampere> times square metre, $\mathrm{A\,m^2}$, equivalently <joule> per <tesla (unit)>. A <magnetic dipole moment> is neither a <magnetic field> nor a <magnetic monopole>: a closed <current loop> can have a nonzero <magnetic dipole moment> while its net outward <magnetic flux> through any closed surface remains zero, as required by <Gauss's law for magnetism>.

For a localized, steady <electric current density> $\mathbf J(\mathbf r)$, the more general definition is
$$
\mathbf m=\frac12\int_{\mathbb R^3}\mathbf r\times\mathbf J(\mathbf r)\,d^3r.
$$
Here the <cross product> is integrated over the complete source, including return paths. Steadiness requires $\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0$, and localization requires sufficiently rapid decay for the boundary terms to vanish. Under these assumptions the <localized steady-current moment identities> give $\int\mathbf J\,d^3r=0$: shifting the origin by $\mathbf a$ changes the proposed <magnetic dipole moment> by $-\tfrac12\mathbf a\times\int\mathbf J\,d^3r=0$. An isolated open wire segment does not satisfy these assumptions. <Classical Electromagnetism by Richard Fitzpatrick/Localized Current Distribution>{ref}.

= Current loop
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{title2=$\mathbf m=I\mathbf A$}

A <current loop> is a closed path carrying <electric current>. For a thin wire following a closed <curve> $C$, the <volume integral> defining its <magnetic dipole moment> becomes a <line integral>:
$$
\mathbf m=\frac I2\oint_C\mathbf r\times d\mathbf r=I\mathbf A,
\qquad
\mathbf A=\frac12\oint_C\mathbf r\times d\mathbf r.
$$
The <vector area> $\mathbf A$ is $A\widehat{\mathbf n}$ for a planar <current loop>. For a nonplanar <current loop>, use the <vector area>, rather than the scalar <area> of an arbitrarily chosen spanning surface. Reversing the <electric current> reverses $\mathbf m$ without changing the geometry. <Classical Electromagnetism by Richard Fitzpatrick/Localized Current Distribution>{ref}.

\Image[current-loop-moment-right-hand-rule.png]
{title=Current direction and the right-hand rule for a planar magnetic dipole moment}
{description=Viewed face-on, counterclockwise <conventional current> gives a <magnetic dipole moment> out of the page, shown by a dot; reversing the <electric current> gives a <magnetic dipole moment> into the page, shown by a cross. The curled fingers follow the <electric current> and the thumb follows the unit <normal vector>.}

For $N$ closely spaced identical turns carrying the same <electric current>, the <magnetic dipole moments> add: $\mathbf m=NIA\widehat{\mathbf n}$. Each turn contributes its own <vector area>; unequal areas or differently oriented turns require a <vector> sum. As a calculated example, twelve circular turns of <radius> $R=3.0\,\mathrm{cm}$ carrying $I=0.40\,\mathrm A$ give $m=NI\pi R^2=1.36\times10^{-2}\,\mathrm{A\,m^2}$. This is an idealized example, not an experimental measurement.

= Helmholtz coil
{c}
{parent=Current loop}
{title2=$B_0=(4/5)^{3/2}\mu_0N_HI_H/R_H$}
{wiki}

A <Helmholtz coil> arrangement uses two identical coaxial circular windings separated by their <radius>, carrying equal <electric currents> in the same circulation sense. Their combined <magnetic field> is especially uniform near the midpoint, making it useful for a <current-loop torque experiment>. The cancellation of the axial second <derivative> there follows from the <on-axis magnetic field of a circular current loop>.

= Magnetic dipole torque
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\tau=\mathbf m\times\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}$}

An externally imposed <magnetic field> that is nearly uniform over a rigid <current loop> produces the <torque>
$$
\boldsymbol\tau=\mathbf m\times\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}},
\qquad
U=-\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}=-mB\cos\theta.
$$
The <angle> $\theta$ is measured between the <magnetic dipole moment> and the external <magnetic field>, not between the plane of the <current loop> and the <magnetic field>. The <magnetic dipole potential energy> uses a <dot product>, whereas the <torque> uses a <cross product>. The <torque> is largest when the <magnetic dipole moment> is perpendicular to the <magnetic field>; parallel alignment minimizes the <potential energy>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/11.5 Force and Torque on a Current Loop>[§11.5, equations 11.22–11.23: vector torque and orientation energy]{ref}

For the sign convention $\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}=B\widehat{\mathbf x}$ and $\mathbf m=m(\cos\theta\widehat{\mathbf x}+\sin\theta\widehat{\mathbf y})$, positive rotation increases $\theta$ and
$$
\tau_z=-mB\sin\theta=-\frac{dU}{d\theta}.
$$
Thus at $0<\theta<\pi$ the <torque> turns the <magnetic dipole moment> toward the external <magnetic field>. Both parallel and antiparallel alignment have zero <torque>, but they are respectively a <stable equilibrium> and an <unstable equilibrium>. With no <damping>, a freely released <current loop> can undergo <oscillation> around the <stable equilibrium>; settling requires <damping>.

\Image[magnetic-dipole-angular-energy-torque.png]
{title=Angular potential energy and signed torque of a magnetic dipole in an imposed field}
{description=The ideal model has $U/(mB)=-\cos\theta$ and $\tau_z/(mB)=-\sin\theta$. The diagram fixes the external <magnetic field> along positive $x$, so the <torque> is clockwise for the illustrated positive <angle>. These curves are calculated, not measured data.}

In the twelve-turn example, take $B=0.020\,\mathrm T$ and $\theta=60\degree$. The predicted <torque> is $\tau_z=-2.35\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{N\,m}$ and the <magnetic dipole potential energy> is $U=-1.36\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm J$. The unit check is $(\mathrm{A\,m^2})\mathrm T=\mathrm{N\,m}=\mathrm J$; <torque> and <energy> have the same <physical dimensions> but different physical meanings.

A short derivation starts with the <magnetic force on a current-carrying wire>, $d\mathbf F=I\,d\mathbf r\times\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}$. In an exactly uniform <magnetic field>, the net <force> is $I(\oint_Cd\mathbf r)\times\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}=0$, but its <torque> need not vanish: opposite sides produce a turning <couple>. Integrating $\mathbf r\times d\mathbf F$ gives $\mathbf m\times\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}$. Integrating $dU=-\tau_z\,d\theta$ at fixed $m$ and $B$ gives the orientation <potential energy>. This is the mechanical orientation <potential energy> for a maintained <electric current>, with the external source held fixed; the <energy> accounting for field and power supply is a separate problem. A varying external <magnetic field> requires corrections across a finite <current loop>, and can also give a net <force>.

= Current-loop torque experiment
{parent=Magnetic dipole torque}

The PHYWE guide <Magnetic moment in the magnetic field> makes the <magnetic dipole moment> measurable. A circular test <current loop> sits between a pair of <Helmholtz coils>, with separate supplies and meters for the test <electric current> $I$ and field-producing <electric current> $I_H$. The test <current loop> is mounted on a <torsion dynamometer>. The guide varies $I_H$, the number of turns, the test <electric current>, diameter, and the <angle> between the <magnetic dipole moment> and <magnetic field>. Its angular settings are spaced by $15\degree$. The leads hang loosely and are twisted together to suppress extra <torque>; the instrument zero is checked frequently because moving leads can shift it. Its reported results include approximately linear dependence on each <electric current> and on winding count, and approximately quadratic dependence on diameter.<Magnetic moment in the magnetic field>[pp. 4–9: theory, equipment, setup, angular settings, Table 1 and Figures 2–6]{ref}

The readout is a balance of <torques>. According to the manufacturer's <Torsion dynamometer 0.01 N operating instructions>, a metallic band supplies the restoring <torque>. First set the indication to zero with the applied <force> absent and center the lever using the zero knob. Apply the load, then turn the indicating knob until the lever returns to its original zero position. The scale then reads a force equivalent, not a <torque> directly. The quoted scale division is $0.1\,\mathrm{mN}$. For a <force> applied perpendicular to the calibration <lever arm> at distance $\ell$, the conversion is $\tau=F\ell$; a <current loop> applies a <couple>, so its <torque> must be compared with the calibrated restoring <torque>.<Torsion dynamometer 0.01 N operating instructions>[§§1–4: metallic-band compensation, zero procedure, force indication and scale specification]{ref}

For a quantitative repeat, independently check that conversion with known <forces> at a measured perpendicular <lever arm>, and check the imposed <magnetic field> at the test <current loop>. These are proposed calibration checks, not additional measurements reported here. The ideal <Helmholtz coil> model predicts
$$
B_0=\left(\frac45\right)^{3/2}\frac{\mu_0N_HI_H}{R_H},
\qquad
|\tau|=NI\pi R^2 B_0|\sin\theta|,
$$
where $N_H$ and $R_H$ are the winding count and radius of each field-producing <Helmholtz coil>, distinct from $N$ and $R$ of the test <current loop>. Derive $B_0$ by adding the <on-axis magnetic field of a circular current loop> at distances $R_H/2$ from both coils. Independently calibrating $B_0$ and the restoring <torque> lets a fitted <torque> amplitude determine $m$ as amplitude divided by $B_0$, to compare with $NI\pi R^2$.

Useful additional controls are to reverse only $I$, reverse only $I_H$, and reverse both: the signed <torque> should reverse in either single reversal and be unchanged in the double reversal. Record baselines with each <electric current> separately zero, and subtract a nearby zero reading to reduce <torsion balance zero drift>. Repeat angular settings in both directions to reveal lead-dependent offsets. These are suggested controls. Limits include finite <magnetic field> uniformity, uncertainty in effective winding area and angular zero, meter calibration, <Joule heating>, and residual lead <torque>. An apparently sinusoidal trace alone does not establish the absolute <magnetic dipole moment>. No raw laboratory data or new measured values are claimed here.

= Torsion dynamometer
{parent=Current-loop torque experiment}
{tag=Torsion balance}

A <torsion dynamometer> compares an applied <torque> with the restoring <torque> of a twisted elastic element. Returning the lever to its zero position allows a <current-loop torque experiment> to hold the test orientation fixed while reading the compensating <torque>.

= Far-field approximation for a current loop
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{title2=$\mathbf B\simeq\mu_0[3(\mathbf m\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r})\widehat{\mathbf r}-\mathbf m]/(4\pi r^3)$}

Far outside a localized steady source of size $a$, at distance $r\gg a$, its <multipole expansion> starts with the <magnetic dipole moment> if that moment is nonzero. In <vacuum>, using <SI> units,
$$
\mathbf A_{\mathrm{dip}}(\mathbf r)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\frac{\mathbf m\times\mathbf r}{r^3},
\qquad
\mathbf B_{\mathrm{dip}}(\mathbf r)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi r^3}\left[3(\mathbf m\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r})\widehat{\mathbf r}-\mathbf m\right],
$$
where $\mu_0$ is the <vacuum permeability>, $\widehat{\mathbf r}=\mathbf r/r$, and the <magnetic field> is the <curl> of the <magnetic vector potential>. The <magnetic dipole field> falls as $r^{-3}$, and the <magnetic vector potential> as $r^{-2}$. The <localized steady-current moment identities> remove the putative $r^{-1}$ term in the <magnetic vector potential>. No <magnetic monopole> is needed. If different <current loops> cancel the total <magnetic dipole moment>, a higher term of the <multipole expansion> supplies the leading distant <magnetic field>. <Classical Electromagnetism by Richard Fitzpatrick/Localized Current Distribution>{ref}.

The approximation is not valid at the center of a finite <current loop>. For a circular <current loop> of <radius> $R$ and <electric current> $I$, the exact <on-axis magnetic field of a circular current loop> and the distant approximation are
$$
B_z(z)=\frac{\mu_0IR^2}{2(R^2+z^2)^{3/2}},
\qquad
B_{z,\mathrm{dip}}(z)=\frac{\mu_0IR^2}{2|z|^3}.
$$
Their ratio is $B_z/B_{z,\mathrm{dip}}=(1+R^2/z^2)^{-3/2}$: at $|z|=10R$, the approximate <magnetic field> exceeds the exact <magnetic field> by about $1.50\%$ relative to the exact value. Near the source, retain its geometry rather than replacing it with a point source specified only by its <magnetic dipole moment>.

= Localized steady-current moment identities
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{title2=$\int j_i=0,\quad\int x_i j_k=-\int x_k j_i$}

A localized steady <electric current density> satisfies $\nabla\cdot\mathbf j=0$. Integrate the divergences of $x_i\mathbf j$ and $x_ix_k\mathbf j$ over a region enclosing its support; all boundary fluxes vanish. The first identity removes the monopole term from the magnetic vector potential expansion. The antisymmetric first moment is equivalent to the <magnetic dipole moment> $\frac12\int\mathbf x\times\mathbf j\,dV$.

= Magnetic dipole potential energy
{title2=$U=-\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf B$}
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}

In an external field that varies slowly across a localized magnetic dipole,
$$
U=-\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf B,
\qquad
\mathbf F=\nabla(\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf B).
$$

The <magnetic dipole moment> determines the coupling to the external magnetic field.

= Bohr magneton
{title2=$\mu_B$}
{c}
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{wiki}

The Bohr magneton,
$$
\mu_B=\frac{e\hbar}{2m_e},
$$
is the natural unit of an electron's orbital and spin magnetic moment.

= Coulomb-gauge vector potential of a localized steady current
{c}
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}

If $\nabla\cdot J=0$ and the current decays enough for boundary terms to vanish, then
$$
A(x)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int\frac{J(x')}{|x-x'|}\,d^3x'
$$
satisfies $\nabla\cdot A=0$. Its far field is
$$
A(x)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\frac{m\times x}{|x|^3}+\cdots.
$$

= Magnetic dipole moment of a rigidly rotating charge distribution
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}

A circular charge distribution rotating with angular speed $\omega$ can be decomposed into current loops. A hoop of line density $\eta$ and radius $R$ has $m=\pi\eta\omega R^3\hat n$; a disc of surface density $\sigma$ has $m=\pi\sigma\omega R^4\hat n/4$.

= Biot-Savart law
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

For a steady <current density> $J$, the <magnetic field> is
$$
B(r)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int
\frac{J(r')\times(r-r')}{|r-r'|^3}\,d^3r'.
$$

= On-axis magnetic field of a circular current loop
{parent=Biot-Savart law}

A circular loop of radius $R$ carrying <electric current> $I$ has, on its axis,
$$
B(z)=\frac{\mu_0IR^2}{2(R^2+z^2)^{3/2}}\,\hat z,
$$
where the sign of $\hat z$ is fixed by the <right-hand rule>. At its centre, $B(0)=\mu_0I/(2R)$.

= Magnetic-field cancellation between oppositely driven coaxial loops
{parent=Biot-Savart law}

If coaxial loops of radii $R$ and $2R$, separated by $D$, carry currents $I$ and $2I$ in opposite senses, their on-axis fields cancel between the loops at distance $D/3$ from the smaller loop.

= Beltrami field
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A Beltrami field is a vector field parallel to its curl:
$$
\nabla\times J=\lambda J.
$$
If it is divergence-free and used as a magnetostatic current with constant nonzero $\lambda$, then $B=\mu_0J/\lambda$ solves $\nabla\times B=\mu_0J$ and $\nabla\cdot B=0$.

= Relativistic electromagnetism
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

Relativistic electromagnetism combines electric and magnetic fields into Lorentz tensors.

= Relativistic charged-particle action
{parent=Relativistic electromagnetism}

For metric signature $(-,+,+,+)$, a particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ coupled to a four-potential has the reparametrization-invariant action
$$
S=-mc\int\sqrt{-\dot x^2}\,d\lambda
+q\int A_\mu(x)\dot x^\mu\,d\lambda.
$$

= Electromagnetic minimal coupling
{parent=Relativistic charged-particle action}

Electromagnetic minimal coupling replaces the free kinetic momentum in an action or Hamiltonian by a combination involving the electromagnetic potential. For $L=-m\sqrt{\dot x^a\dot x_a}-qA_a\dot x^a$, the canonical covector is $P_a=-m\dot x_a/\sqrt{\dot x^2}-qA_a$, so $P_a+qA_a$ recovers the potential-independent covector. Under $A_a\mapsto A_a+\partial_a\chi$, the action changes by an endpoint term. The fixed-endpoint equations are the <relativistic Lorentz force> equation and are gauge invariant.

= Gauge invariance of the charged-particle worldline action
{parent=Relativistic charged-particle action}

Under $A_\mu\mapsto A_\mu+\partial_\mu\chi$, the interaction changes by
$$
q\int\frac{d\chi(x(\lambda))}{d\lambda}\,d\lambda
=q\,[\chi]_{\rm endpoints}.
$$
Fixed-endpoint equations of motion are therefore gauge invariant.

= Lorentz-force equation from the worldline action
{parent=Relativistic charged-particle action}
{c}

Varying the <relativistic charged-particle action> with fixed endpoints gives
$$
m\frac{du_\mu}{d\tau}=qF_{\mu\nu}u^\nu,
$$
or the equivalent raised-index equation.

= Electromagnetic four-potential
{parent=Relativistic electromagnetism}

= Four-potential
{synonym}

The four-potential combines the <electric potential> and <magnetic vector potential> as $A^\mu=(\phi/c,\mathbf A)$. The electromagnetic four-potential $A_\mu$ transforms under
$$
A_\mu\mapsto A_\mu+\partial_\mu\chi,
$$
while the antisymmetric field tensor $F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu$ is unchanged.

= Electromagnetic multipole expansion
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}

An electromagnetic multipole expansion expresses the potentials or fields of a localized source as successively smaller monopole, dipole, quadrupole, and higher moments in the ratio of source size to observation distance.

= Retarded potential
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}
{wiki}

= Retarded electromagnetic potential
{synonym}

A retarded potential evaluates each source at the event on the source's past light cone. This accounts for propagation at the speed of light and enforces <causality>.

= Retarded time
{title2=$t_{\rm ret}$}
{parent=Retarded potential}
{wiki}

For a source point $y(s)$ and field event $(x,t)$, the retarded time solves
$$
t_{\rm ret}+\frac{|x-y(t_{\rm ret})|}{c}=t.
$$

= Uniqueness of retarded time for a subluminal source
{parent=Retarded time}

For $R(s)=|x-y(s)|$ and $|v|<c$, the function $F(s)=s+R(s)/c$ has
$$
F'(s)=1-\widehat R\cdot v/c>0.
$$
It is strictly increasing, so the retarded-time equation has at most one solution.

= Liénard–Wiechert potential
{c}
{parent=Retarded potential}
{wiki}

The potentials of a point charge $q$ are
$$
\phi=\frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}
\frac1{R-\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf R/c},
\qquad
\mathbf A=\frac{\mathbf v}{c^2}\phi,
$$
with every source quantity evaluated at the <retarded time>.

= Lorenz gauge condition
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}
{wiki}

= Lorenz gauge
{c}
{synonym}

The Lorenz gauge condition is
$$
\frac1{c^2}\partial_t\phi+\nabla\cdot\mathbf A=0,
$$
or $\partial_\mu A^\mu=0$ in covariant notation.

= Residual Lorenz gauge symmetry
{parent=Lorenz gauge condition}
{title2=$\Box\lambda=0$}

An Abelian <gauge transformation> $A_a\mapsto A_a+\partial_a\lambda$ changes $\partial\cdot A$ by $\Box\lambda$. Consequently <gauge fixing> by a quadratic function of $\partial\cdot A$ retains transformations satisfying $\Box\lambda=0$, subject to the same <boundary conditions>. Eliminating those residual transformations is part of specifying an inverse with a particular <Green function> prescription.

= Electromagnetic field tensor
{title2=$F^{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}
{wiki}

The electromagnetic field tensor is
$$
F^{\mu\nu}=\partial^\mu A^\nu-\partial^\nu A^\mu.
$$
It packages the electric and magnetic fields into an antisymmetric rank-two Lorentz tensor and transforms as
$$
F'^{\mu\nu}(x')
=\Lambda^\mu{}_\rho\Lambda^\nu{}_\sigma F^{\rho\sigma}(x).
$$

= Dual electromagnetic field tensor
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{title2=$\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}$}

The dual electromagnetic field tensor is obtained by applying the <Hodge star operator> to the <electromagnetic field tensor>. For metric signature $(+---)$ and $\epsilon^{0123}=+1$, define $\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}=\tfrac12\epsilon^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}F_{\rho\sigma}$. With $F^{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F^{ij}=-\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, it has components $\widetilde F^{0i}=-B_i$ and $\widetilde F^{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}E_k/c$. The homogeneous <Maxwell equations> become $\partial_\mu\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}=0$.

= Maxwell Lagrangian
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{title2=$\mathcal L=-\frac14F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}$}

The source-free electromagnetic <Lagrangian density> is quadratic in the <electromagnetic field tensor>. Its <Euler-Lagrange field equation> is $\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}=0$. Antisymmetry of $F$ makes the density invariant under <gauge transformations>, and contraction of its indices makes it a <Lorentz scalar>.

= Maxwell action
{c}
{parent=Maxwell Lagrangian}
{title2=$S_E=\tfrac14\int F_{\mu\nu}F_{\mu\nu}\,d^4x$}

The source-free <Maxwell Lagrangian> gives $S=-\tfrac14\int F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}\,d^4x$ in mostly-minus Lorentzian signature. Its <Euclidean action> is the displayed positive quadratic functional of the <electromagnetic field tensor>. Variation, with boundary terms vanishing, yields $\partial_\mu F_{\mu\nu}=0$.

= First-order Maxwell action
{parent=Maxwell action}
{title2=$S=\frac1{16\pi}\int\sqrt{-g}\,[F^{ab}F_{ab}-2F^{ab}(\nabla_aA_b-\nabla_bA_a)]$}

Vary the antisymmetric auxiliary <electromagnetic field tensor> and the <electromagnetic four-potential> independently. The first variation sets $F_{ab}=\nabla_aA_b-\nabla_bA_a$ algebraically, and variation of the potential gives $\nabla_bF^{ab}=0$. Substitution of the algebraic equation reduces the first-order action to the usual negative quadratic <Maxwell action>. Metric variation gives the same stress tensor after eliminating the auxiliary field, because the chain-rule term multiplying its variational equation then vanishes.

= Electromagnetic Bianchi identity
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}

Because the <electromagnetic field tensor> is the <exterior derivative> $F=dA$ of a potential, $dF=d^2A=0$. For a torsion-free connection this is
$$
\nabla_aF_{bc}+\nabla_bF_{ca}+\nabla_cF_{ab}=0,
$$
which is the covariant form of the two homogeneous <Maxwell equations>.

= Lorentz force
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf F=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$}
{wiki}

= Lorentz force law
{c}
{synonym}

The <Lorentz force> is the <force> exerted by an <electric field> $\mathbf E$ and a <magnetic field> $\mathbf B$ on a body with <electric charge> $q$ and <velocity> $\mathbf v$. In <SI> units its form in nonrelativistic <classical mechanics>, using <mass> $m$ and <Newton's second law>, is
$$
\mathbf F=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B),\qquad
m\frac{d\mathbf v}{dt}=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B).
$$
Both fields are evaluated at the body's <position> at the current <time>. The <electric field> contributes $q\mathbf E$ even at rest, whereas the magnetic <force> depends on <velocity> through a <cross product>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-4-electric-field[OpenStax, electric force and field]{ref}; https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-2-magnetic-fields-and-lines[OpenStax, magnetic force on moving charge]{ref}.

For a concrete <charged particle in a uniform magnetic field>, use <nondimensionalization> to stipulate $m=q=B=1$, <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=(0,0,1)$, zero <electric field>, initial <position> $(0,0,0)$, and initial <velocity> $(1,0,0)$. These are rescaled quantities, not an assertion that the <mass>, <electric charge>, and <magnetic field> have the same physical units. At the start, $(1,0,0)\times(0,0,1)=(0,-1,0)$, so the magnetic <acceleration> points toward $-y$. The resulting <circular motion> is
$$
\mathbf r(t)=(\sin t,\cos t-1,0),\qquad
\mathbf v(t)=(\cos t,-\sin t,0),\qquad
\mathbf a(t)=(-\sin t,-\cos t,0).
$$
At $t=\pi/2$ the <position> is $(1,-1,0)$ and the <velocity> is $(0,-1,0)$; at $t=\pi$ the <position> is $(0,-2,0)$ and the <velocity> is $(-1,0,0)$. Thus the <circle> has center $(0,-1,0)$ and <radius> one. Viewed from $+z$ toward the $xy$ <plane>, the motion is clockwise. Changing only the <electric charge> to $q=-1$ gives <position> $(\sin t,1-\cos t,0)$ and reverses the bending to counterclockwise.

\Image[lorentz-force-orbits.png]
{title=<Lorentz force>: <circular motion> and a <helix> in the same uniform <magnetic field>}
{description=Stipulated rescaled $m=q=B=1$ and zero <electric field>. Left: positive <electric charge> follows the clockwise <circle> from initial <velocity> $(1,0,0)$; the <velocity> is tangent and the magnetic <force> points inward. Dots denote a <magnetic field> out of the <plane>. Right: adding parallel <velocity> $v_z=0.5$ gives <helical motion in a uniform magnetic field>, with <position> $z=0.5t$ and the same transverse <radius> one. Straight arrows show <magnetic field lines>, distinct from the curved path.}

Adding initial <velocity> $v_z=0.5$ leaves the transverse <circular motion> unchanged and gives
$$
\mathbf r(t)=(\sin t,\cos t-1,0.5t),\qquad
\mathbf v(t)=(\cos t,-\sin t,0.5).
$$
This is a <helix> of transverse <radius> one, <cyclotron frequency> $\omega_c=1$, and transverse <period> $T=2\pi$; the advance along $z$ in one turn is $\pi$. The parallel component of <velocity> stays constant because the magnetic <force> has no parallel component: there is no magnetic <acceleration> along the <magnetic field>. If the initial <velocity> were entirely parallel to the <magnetic field>, the magnetic <force> would vanish and the path would be straight. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-3-motion-of-a-charged-particle-in-a-magnetic-field[OpenStax, circular and helical motion]{ref}.

More generally, with zero <electric field>, no other <forces>, and a uniform constant <magnetic field> of magnitude $B>0$, decompose <velocity> into perpendicular and parallel components. For nonzero <electric charge> $q$, the perpendicular <speed> $v_\perp$ determines the <gyroradius>, while the <cyclotron frequency> is an <angular frequency>:
$$
r=\frac{mv_\perp}{|q|B},\qquad
\omega_c=\frac{|q|B}{m},\qquad
T=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_c}=\frac{2\pi m}{|q|B}.
$$
These are nonrelativistic formulas; see the <relativistic cyclotron orbit> for the correction. The beauty is that increasing perpendicular <speed> increases the <radius> in exactly the proportion needed to leave the <period> unchanged. Conversely, known <magnetic field>, perpendicular <speed>, and measured <radius> determine the magnitude of the <electric charge> divided by <mass>. This makes the same geometry useful for comparing bodies with different <electric charge> and <mass>.

Two short checks explain the result. First, the <cross product> gives $\mathbf v\times\mathbf B=(-\sin t,-\cos t,0)$ for both stipulated positive-<electric charge> paths, exactly their <acceleration>. Second, the transverse magnetic <force> supplies the <centripetal acceleration>, so $mv_\perp^2/r=|q|v_\perp B$; for $v_\perp>0$ this gives the <gyroradius>, and $\omega_c=v_\perp/r$ gives the <cyclotron frequency>.

<Magnetic forces do no work>: the instantaneous <power> of the magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> vanishes because a <cross product> is perpendicular to its first argument. The <work-energy theorem> therefore gives
$$
\frac{dK}{dt}=\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf v
=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v
+q\mathbf v\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)
=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v,
\qquad K=\tfrac12m|\mathbf v|^2.
$$
The <dot product> $\mathbf v\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$ is zero at every instant, rather than only after a full turn. With zero <electric field> and no other <forces>, the <kinetic energy> and <speed> remain constant; the <electric field> can increase or decrease <kinetic energy> according to the sign of $q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$. In the two stipulated examples the constant <kinetic energy> is respectively $K=1/2$ and $K=5/8$ in rescaled units. The extra parallel <velocity> raises <kinetic energy> without changing the <gyroradius> or the <cyclotron frequency>.

For an electric example in rescaled units, stipulate $m=q=1$, zero <magnetic field>, constant <electric field> $\mathbf E=(2,0,0)$, no other <forces>, and rest at $t=0$. The <Lorentz force> gives <acceleration> $(2,0,0)$ and <velocity> $(2t,0,0)$, hence <kinetic energy> $K=2t^2$ and <power> $dK/dt=4t=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$. At $t=1$, the <kinetic energy> has risen from zero to two: the <electric field> has done positive <work>.

The existing <electromagnetic field tensor> formulation expresses the same interaction covariantly. For <four-momentum> $p^\mu$ and <four-velocity> $u^\mu$, using <proper time> $\tau$, the <Lorentz force law> is
$$
\frac{dp^\mu}{d\tau}=qF^{\mu\nu}u_\nu.
$$
Its spatial and temporal components are
$$
\frac{d\mathbf p}{dt}=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B),
\qquad
\frac{d\mathcal E}{dt}=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v.
$$

= Hall effect
{c}
{parent=Lorentz force}
{wiki}

The <Hall effect> is a transverse <electric potential difference> produced when an <electric current> flows through a material in a transverse <magnetic field>. The contact orientation fixes its measured sign. The <Drude conductivity and Hall experiment> gives an explicit convention and the limitations of inferring a <number density> from it.

= Hall coefficient
{c}
{parent=Hall effect}
{title2=$R_H=E_y/(J_xB_z)$}

The <Hall coefficient> relates a transverse <electric field> to longitudinal <current density> and transverse <magnetic field>, with oriented axes as in the displayed formula. Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{m^3/C}$. For one isotropic <Drude model> species it is $1/(nq)$; multiple <charge carriers> need not follow that formula.

= Gyroradius
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$r=mv_\perp/(|q|B)$}

= Larmor radius
{c}
{synonym}

The <gyroradius> is the <radius> of the transverse <circular motion> of a <charged particle in a uniform magnetic field>. In nonrelativistic <classical mechanics>, with zero <electric field> and no other <forces>, it is $mv_\perp/(|q|B)$, where $v_\perp$ is the <speed> perpendicular to the <magnetic field>. The parallel component of <velocity> changes the advance of the <helix>, rather than its transverse <radius>.

= Larmor theorem
{c}
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\omega=-e\mathbf B/(2m)$}

For particles with the same charge-to-mass ratio, rotationally invariant mutual <central forces>, and a weak uniform <magnetic field>, a frame with the displayed <angular velocity> cancels the velocity-dependent magnetic <Lorentz force> against the <Coriolis force>. The remaining rotational and magnetic terms are quadratic in $\mathbf B$. Thus, to first order in the field, the rotating-frame equations coincide with the zero-field equations. This is the classical dynamical basis of <Larmor precession>; equality is an approximation rather than an exact removal of the magnetic field.

= Radial escape in an inverse-radius axial magnetic field
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\dot r^2=v_\perp^2-(\ell/r-c)^2,\quad c=qB_0/m$}

For an axial <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=(B_0/r)\mathbf e_z$, let $L=mr^2\dot\theta$. The <Lorentz force> gives $\dot L=-qB_0\dot r$, so $\ell=(L+qB_0r)/m$ is constant. Since <magnetic forces do no work> and $\dot z$ is constant, the transverse speed $v_\perp$ is constant and $\dot r^2=v_\perp^2-(\ell/r-c)^2$. If $c\ell>0$ and $0<v_\perp<|c|$, two finite radial <turning points> confine the motion. If $v_\perp>|c|$, an outward branch escapes to infinite radius. At equality a marginal escaping branch exists when $c\ell>0$, but $\ell=0$ gives a circular orbit instead. The transverse <escape velocity> threshold is $|qB_0|/m$; motion parallel to the <magnetic field> cannot provide transverse escape energy.

= Velocity rotation in a time-dependent axial magnetic field
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\dot x+i\dot y=V\exp\left(i\phi-\frac{iq}{m}\int_0^t B_z(s)\,ds\right)$}

Under the magnetic-only <Lorentz force>, planar velocity has constant magnitude and an accumulated phase determined by the time integral of the axial field. For $B_z(t)=\beta t$, the planar position involves <Fresnel integrals>. With initial planar velocity $(1,0)$ and zero initial position, its limit is $(\sqrt{\pi m/(4q\beta)},-\sqrt{\pi m/(4q\beta)})$. The model prescribes a magnetic-only force; a physical time-dependent field may also produce an induced electric field.

// Target: number-theory.bigb

= Magnetic forces do no work
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$(q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot\mathbf v=0$}

The magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> is perpendicular to the particle <velocity>. Its power is therefore zero, so with no other forces the <kinetic energy> remains constant. This remains true relativistically because $dE/dt=\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf v$; a magnetic force can change momentum direction without changing total energy.

= Geometrization of the Lorentz force by an affine connection
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\Gamma^i{}_{tt}=-E^i,\quad\Gamma^i{}_{tj}=\epsilon^i{}_{jk}B^k$}

For smooth stationary fields on $\mathbb R^3$, set $\Gamma^i{}_{tt}=-E^i$ and $\Gamma^i{}_{tj}=\Gamma^i{}_{jt}=\epsilon^i{}_{jk}B^k$, with all other <Christoffel symbols> zero. This <torsion-free connection> makes the equation $\ddot{\mathbf x}=\mathbf E+2\mathbf B\times\dot{\mathbf x}$ a <geodesic equation> with time as <affine parameter>. Arbitrary smooth fields suffice; a metric realization imposes additional potential conditions.

= Electric drift in crossed uniform fields
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\mathbf v_D=\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/B^2$}

= Electric drift
{synonym}

In uniform perpendicular electric and <magnetic fields>, the guiding centre of a charged particle drifts with $\mathbf v_D=\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/|\mathbf B|^2$. This <velocity> is independent of the charge and mass; cyclotron motion is superposed on it.

= Lorentz four-force
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$f^\mu=qF^\mu{}_{\nu}u^\nu$}
{c}

The covariant force on a charged particle is its charge times the electromagnetic field-strength tensor contracted with its <four-velocity>. Antisymmetry keeps the four-force orthogonal to that velocity and preserves the rest mass.

= Magnetic force on a current-carrying wire
{parent=Lorentz force}

A wire carrying current $I$ in a magnetic field experiences
$$
d\mathbf F=I\,d\boldsymbol\ell\times\mathbf B.
$$
This follows by summing the magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> over the moving charge carriers.

= Relativistic cyclotron orbit
{title2=$\omega_\tau=qB/m$}
{parent=Lorentz force}

In a constant <magnetic field> $B\widehat z$ with no <electric field>, a charged relativistic particle moves in a circle in the perpendicular plane. Its angular frequency with respect to <proper time> is
$$
\omega_\tau=\frac{qB}{m},
$$
whereas its angular frequency in an inertial coordinate time is $\omega_t=qB/(\gamma m)$. The orbit radius at speed $v$ is
$$
R=\frac{\gamma mv}{|q|B}.
$$

= Charged-particle action over one relativistic cyclotron orbit
{parent=Relativistic cyclotron orbit}

For $qB>0$, choose $A_y=Bx$ and parametrize one clockwise orbit by $x=R\cos(\omega_\tau\tau)$ and $y=-R\sin(\omega_\tau\tau)$. Over $\Delta\tau=2\pi/\omega_\tau$, the <relativistic charged-particle action> is
$$
S=-mc^2\Delta\tau+q\oint A\mathbin\cdot dx
=-\frac{\pi mc^2}{\omega_\tau}(1+\gamma^2).
$$
The same formula with $|\omega_\tau|$ applies for either orbit orientation.

= Lorentz force density
{title2=$\mathbf f_{\rm L}$}
{parent=Lorentz force}

The Lorentz force per unit volume is $\mathbf f_{\rm L}=\rho_e\mathbf E+\mathbf j\times\mathbf B/c$ in Gaussian units.

= E-cross-B drift
{title2=$\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/B^2$}
{c}
{parent=Lorentz force}
{wiki}

In uniform perpendicular electric and magnetic fields, a charged particle's cyclotron orbit has guiding-centre velocity
$$
\mathbf v_D=\frac{\mathbf E\times\mathbf B}{B^2},
$$
independent of its charge and mass.

= Relativistic electromagnetic work-energy theorem
{parent=Lorentz force}

For $\mathcal E=\gamma mc^2$, the temporal component of the covariant Lorentz-force equation gives
$$
\frac{d\mathcal E}{dt}=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v.
$$
The magnetic field does no work because $\mathbf v\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)=0$.

= Coordinate acceleration of a relativistic charged particle
{parent=Lorentz force}

Combining $d(\gamma m\mathbf v)/dt=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$ with $mc^2\,d\gamma/dt=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$ gives
$$
\frac{d\mathbf v}{dt}
=\frac q{m\gamma}
\left[
\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B
-\frac{\mathbf v(\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf E)}{c^2}
\right].
$$

= Electromagnetic field invariants
{title2=$F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu},\,F_{\mu\nu}\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{wiki}

For signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and a consistent orientation,
$$
F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}
=2\left(B^2-\frac{E^2}{c^2}\right),
\qquad
F_{\mu\nu}\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}
=-\frac4c\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf B.
$$
Overall signs depend on tensor and orientation conventions, while their vanishing does not.

= Null electromagnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetic field invariants}
{wiki}

A null field has both electromagnetic invariants zero:
$$
\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf B=0,
\qquad
E^2=c^2B^2.
$$
For a nonzero constant null field, the mixed tensor is nilpotent of index three,
$$
F^\mu{}_\rho F^\rho{}_\sigma F^\sigma{}_\nu=0.
$$

= Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{wiki=Electromagnetic_stress–energy_tensor}

For metric signature $(-,+,+,+)$,
$$
T^{\mu\nu}=\frac1{\mu_0}
\left(F^{\mu\rho}F^\nu{}_{\rho}
-\frac14\eta^{\mu\nu}F^{\rho\sigma}F_{\rho\sigma}\right).
$$
In particular, $T^{00}=\tfrac12(\epsilon_0E^2+B^2/\mu_0)$.

= Electromagnetic stress-energy divergence identity
{parent=Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$\nabla_bT^{ab}=F^a{}_c\nabla_bF^{cb}/(4\pi)$}

For signature $(+---)$ in Gaussian electromagnetic units, $T^{ab}=(4\pi)^{-1}[-F^a{}_cF^{bc}+g^{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd}/4]$. The <electromagnetic Bianchi identity> gives $F^{bc}\nabla_bF^a{}_c=\nabla^a(F_{cd}F^{cd})/4$, so differentiating the stress tensor proves the displayed identity. Stress conservation implies the source-free <Maxwell equations> if the matrix $F^a{}_c$ is invertible; otherwise it only forces the field divergence into that matrix's kernel. Conversely source-free Maxwell equations always imply stress conservation.

= Gauge-compensated time translation in electromagnetism
{parent=Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$\delta A_\nu=\alpha F_{0\nu}$}

With signature $(+,-,-,-)$ and $\mathcal L=-F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}/4$, combine a time translation $\alpha\partial_0A_\nu$ with the <gauge transformation> of parameter $-\alpha A_0$. Its <Noether current> is $J^\mu=-F^{\mu\nu}F_{0\nu}-\delta^\mu_0\mathcal L$, the gauge-invariant energy current. The density is $(|\mathbf E|^2+|\mathbf B|^2)/2$. On the source-free field equations it differs from the canonical translation current by $\partial_\nu(A_0F^{\mu\nu})$, an antisymmetric superpotential divergence, so total energies agree when the boundary term vanishes.

= Null energy condition for the electromagnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor}

For every null vector $k$, put $q^\rho=k_\mu F^{\mu\rho}$. Antisymmetry gives $q\cdot k=0$, so $q$ has nonnegative norm and
$$
T^{\mu\nu}k_\mu k_\nu=\frac1{\mu_0}q^\rho q_\rho\geq0.
$$

= Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields
{parent=Relativistic electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

Lorentz boosts mix transverse electric and magnetic field components.

= Relativistic charge density of counterstreaming beams
{parent=Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields}
{title2=$\lambda'=-\gamma vI/c^2$}

Two equal opposite line charge densities moving in opposite directions have zero net line charge but a nonzero total current $I$. Under a longitudinal <Lorentz boost>, the charge-current four-vector gives $\lambda'=\gamma(\lambda-vI/c^2)=-\gamma vI/c^2$. Equivalently, for lab densities $n$ and speeds $\pm u$, their separate boosted number densities are $\gamma n(1\mp vu/c^2)$. Their imbalance creates the boosted radial <electric field>, consistently with <Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields> and <Gauss's law>.

= Electromagnetic jump conditions across a normally moving plane
{parent=Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields}

If a plane is stationary in one frame and an observer moves normally to it with velocity $\mathbf v$, then
$$
[B'_n]=0,
\qquad
[\mathbf E'_t]=\mathbf v\mathbin\times[\mathbf B'_t].
$$
The second relation accounts for the magnetic-field discontinuity moving through the observer's frame.

= Electromagnetic wave
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

An <electromagnetic wave> is a propagating disturbance of the <electric field> and <magnetic field> obeying the <Maxwell equations>. In source-free vacuum its speed is
$$
c=\frac1{\sqrt{\mu_0\epsilon_0}}\simeq2.998\times10^8\,\mathrm{m/s},
$$
the <speed of light>. An <electromagnetic wave> can travel through vacuum: a conducting medium is unnecessary. A single traveling <plane electromagnetic wave> has mutually perpendicular <electric field>, <magnetic field>, and propagation direction. This statement applies to that wave; arbitrary <near and far field> configurations need not share these directions or the relation $|\mathbf B|=|\mathbf E|/c$.

The visualization compares perpendicular components on a common scale: $c\mathbf B$ has the same units as $\mathbf E$. The curves are field values along space, rather than paths traced by particles. Their common phase moves toward $+z$, while the <Poynting vector> records the associated <energy flux>.

\Image[electromagnetic-plane-wave.png]
{title=Vacuum plane wave with electric field along x, magnetic field along y, and energy flux along positive z}

The <electromagnetic wave equation> gives a propagation law, while the full <Maxwell equations> additionally enforce the field directions and relative amplitudes. This is why solving an independent scalar <wave equation> for each component is insufficient to construct an <electromagnetic wave>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_20.html[Feynman lectures, II.20: solutions in free space]{ref}.

= Electromagnetic waveguide
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}

An electromagnetic waveguide confines an <electromagnetic wave> transversely while allowing propagation along its axis. Conducting or dielectric boundaries select transverse modes. In a uniform guide a mode with transverse eigenvalue $k_\perp^2$ has longitudinal wavenumber $k_x^2=k^2-k_\perp^2$; below cutoff it is evanescent. At an open <aperture>, the confined field radiates into an angular spectrum rather than retaining a single guided mode.

= Scalar-potential conducting waveguide modes
{parent=Electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\gamma^2=\omega^2/c^2-k^2$}

For dependence $e^{i(kz-\omega t)}$, a <Helmholtz equation> $(\Delta_\perp+\gamma^2)\psi=0$ generates transverse-magnetic fields $e_\perp=\nabla_\perp\psi$, $e_z=-i\gamma^2\psi/k$, $b_\perp=\omega\,\widehat z\times\nabla_\perp\psi/(kc^2)$, $b_z=0$, for $k\ne0$. The conducting wall requires a <Dirichlet boundary condition> on $\psi$. A transverse-electric representation is $e_\perp=\widehat z\times\nabla_\perp\psi$, $e_z=0$, $b_\perp=-k\nabla_\perp\psi/\omega$, $b_z=i\gamma^2\psi/\omega$, with a <Neumann boundary condition>. These are families of modes, not a completeness assertion for multiply connected guides and their possible TEM fields.

= Electromagnetic aperture angular spectrum
{parent=Electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\mathbf q=(\kappa,\nu,\eta),\quad\kappa=\sqrt{k^2-\nu^2-\eta^2}$}

For an aperture normal to $\mathbf e_x$, let $F_y,F_z$ be its tangential <electric field> traces extended by zero outside the aperture. With the outgoing branch of $\kappa$, the <Weyl plane-wave representation> and the curl aperture integral give a spectrum proportional to $(-(\nu\widehat F_y+\eta\widehat F_z)/\kappa,\widehat F_y,\widehat F_z)$. Each wave is transverse to $\mathbf q$. The longitudinal electric component is regenerated by oblique diffraction even when it vanishes at the input. For $G=e^{ikr}/(4\pi r)$ and a unit prefactor on the stated curl integral, the spectrum has an overall factor $1/2$; a doubled half-space Green kernel gives factor one. The normalization must follow the chosen Green kernel and equivalent-current convention.

= Rectangular electromagnetic waveguide
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}

A rectangular electromagnetic waveguide has a vacuum interior bounded by four <perfect conductor> walls parallel to its axis. The harmonic fields obey zero tangential <electric field> and zero normal <magnetic field> at the walls. <Maxwell equations> determine transverse fields from longitudinal components; a purely longitudinal oscillating <magnetic field> by itself cannot propagate, since its divergence would be nonzero.

= Waveguide cutoff frequency
{parent=Rectangular electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\omega_{mn}=c\pi\sqrt{m^2/a^2+n^2/b^2}$}

For a <transverse electric mode of a rectangular waveguide>, the dispersion relation is $\omega^2=c^2(k^2+\kappa_{mn}^2)$, with $\kappa_{mn}^2=(m\pi/a)^2+(n\pi/b)^2$. Below $\omega_{mn}=c\kappa_{mn}$, the longitudinal wave number is imaginary and the mode is evanescent. If $a\ge b$, the lowest propagating cutoff is $\omega_{10}=\pi c/a$, or ordinary frequency $c/(2a)$.

= Waveguide phase and group velocities
{parent=Waveguide cutoff frequency}
{title2=$v_{\mathrm{ph}}v_g=c^2$}

Above a mode's <waveguide cutoff frequency> $\omega_c$, its <phase velocity> is $c/[1-(\omega_c/\omega)^2]^{1/2}$ and its <group velocity> is $c[1-(\omega_c/\omega)^2]^{1/2}$. Differentiate $\omega^2=c^2k^2+\omega_c^2$ to obtain the latter. Phase travels faster than $c$ while the wave packet travels slower, so the phase speed does not imply superluminal information transport.

= Transverse electric mode of a rectangular waveguide
{parent=Rectangular electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\mathrm{TE}_{mn}$}

For a transverse electric mode, $E_z=0$ and $B_z=B_0(x,y)e^{i(kz-\omega t)}$. Put $\Delta=(\omega/c)^2-k^2$. <Maxwell equations> give $B_x=ik\partial_xB_z/\Delta$, $B_y=ik\partial_yB_z/\Delta$, $E_x=i\omega\partial_yB_z/\Delta$, and $E_y=-i\omega\partial_xB_z/\Delta$. The wall conditions become <Neumann boundary conditions> for $B_0$, whose separated solutions are $\cos(m\pi x/a)\cos(n\pi y/b)$. The constant $(0,0)$ candidate is excluded by Faraday's equation because its corresponding electric field vanishes.

= Transverse magnetic polarization
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\mathbf H=H_y\widehat{\mathbf y}$}

= TM wave
{c}
{synonym}

= TM polarization
{c}
{synonym}

Relative to a propagation plane, transverse magnetic polarization has its magnetic field perpendicular to that plane. At a perfectly conducting corrugation its scalar magnetic component obeys a Neumann condition. It reduces to a scalar two-dimensional problem, but not to the Dirichlet problem for the transverse electric component.

= Transverse electric polarization
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\mathbf E=E_y\widehat{\mathbf y}$}

= TE wave
{c}
{synonym}

= TE polarization
{c}
{synonym}

Relative to a propagation plane, transverse electric polarization has its electric field perpendicular to that plane. For a conducting corrugation independent of the perpendicular coordinate, the scalar electric component obeys the Helmholtz equation with Dirichlet data at the metal surface.

= TE wave equation in a stratified magnetodielectric
{c}
{parent=Transverse electric polarization}
{title2=$E_{y,xx}+E_{y,zz}-\frac{\mu'}\mu E_{y,z}+\omega^2\mu\varepsilon E_y=0$}

With permittivity and permeability depending only on $z$ and time dependence $e^{i\omega t}$, a <transverse electric polarization> $\mathbf E=\widehat{\mathbf y}E_y(x,z)$ obeys the displayed equation. <Maxwell's equations> give $H_x=E_{y,z}/(i\omega\mu)$ and $H_z=-E_{y,x}/(i\omega\mu)$. The latter satisfies $H_{z,xx}+H_{z,zz}+(\mu'/\mu)H_{z,z}+[\omega^2\mu\varepsilon+(\log\mu)'']H_z=0$. At normal incidence $H_z=0$; the nonzero magnetic component is $H_x$.

= Refractive index
{title2=$n$}
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

The refractive index is the ratio of the vacuum speed of light to a medium's phase speed. In a homogeneous isotropic medium, $n=c\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}$ in compatible units.

= Inverse-square refractive-index profile
{parent=Refractive index}
{title2=$k^2(z)=k_0^2[a^2-b^2/(z-z_0)^2]$}

A <transverse electric polarization> with constant permeability and conserved transverse wavenumber $k_0\sin\theta_0$ satisfies $e''+[\beta^2-(\nu^2-1/4)/(z-z_0)^2]e=0$, with $\beta^2=k_0^2(a^2-\sin^2\theta_0)$ and $\nu^2=1/4+k_0^2b^2$. Substitution $e=\sqrt{|z-z_0|}Z_\nu(\beta|z-z_0|)$ reduces it to the <Bessel equation>. The profile is singular at $z_0$, so each side requires its own boundary conditions.

= Electromagnetic wave equation
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\nabla^2\mathbf E-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\mathbf E=0$}
{wiki}

In source-free vacuum the <electric field> and <magnetic field> obey
$$
\nabla^2\mathbf E-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\mathbf E=0,\qquad
\nabla^2\mathbf B-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\mathbf B=0.
$$
A brief derivation takes the <curl> of <Faraday's law> and substitutes the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>. The <vector calculus> identity $\nabla\times(\nabla\times\mathbf E)=\nabla(\nabla\cdot\mathbf E)-\nabla^2\mathbf E$, together with source-free <Gauss's law>, leaves $\nabla^2\mathbf E=\mu_0\epsilon_0\partial_t^2\mathbf E$. The same argument gives the <magnetic field> equation. The operator $\nabla^2$ is the <Laplacian>, applied componentwise.

= Plane electromagnetic wave
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\mathbf B=\widehat{\mathbf k}\times\mathbf E/c$}
{wiki}

A monochromatic vacuum <plane electromagnetic wave> has phase $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t$, where $\mathbf k$ is the <wavevector>, $\omega>0$ the <angular frequency>, and $\omega=c|\mathbf k|$. Its field amplitudes obey $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf E_0=\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf B_0=0$ and $\mathbf B_0=\mathbf k\times\mathbf E_0/\omega$.

For the <linear polarization> and orientation in the visualization, choose a right-handed <Cartesian coordinate system> and put
$$
\mathbf E(z,t)=E_0\cos(kz-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf x},\qquad
\mathbf B(z,t)=\frac{E_0}{c}\cos(kz-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf y},\qquad
k>0,\quad\omega=ck.
$$
Constant phase means $z=ct+\text{constant}$, so propagation is along $+z$. The <cross product> $\widehat{\mathbf x}\times\widehat{\mathbf y}=\widehat{\mathbf z}$ fixes the positive <Poynting vector>. Both fields reverse together every half-period and are in phase. Reversing the propagation direction with the same electric orientation reverses the magnetic orientation.

These fields are spatially uniform across every transverse plane. An infinite <plane electromagnetic wave> is an idealization with infinite total <electromagnetic energy>; it is useful as a local approximation and as a building block for <superposition>. For <frequency> $f=100\,\mathrm{MHz}$, $\omega=2\pi f$ and <wavelength> $\lambda=c/f\simeq3.00\,\mathrm m$, independently of $E_0$.

= Polarization of an electromagnetic wave
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}

= Electromagnetic polarization
{synonym}

The polarization of an electromagnetic wave describes the path traced by its transverse electric-field vector at a fixed point. A monochromatic wave may be linearly, circularly, or elliptically polarized; in vacuum it has two independent transverse polarization states.

This specializes <polarization of waves> to electric-field oscillations.

= Faraday effect
{c}
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

= Faraday rotation
{c}
{synonym}

The Faraday effect rotates the orientation of linear <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> during propagation through a magnetized medium. The two opposite <circular polarizations> accumulate different phases; their recombination rotates the linear polarization. In a sufficiently high-frequency cold <plasma>, this angle varies as the squared <wavelength> times a <rotation measure>.

= Rotation measure
{parent=Faraday effect}
{title2=$\mathrm{RM}=a_0\int n_e B_\parallel\,dz$}

For the high-frequency <Faraday effect> in a cold <plasma>, the rotation measure is the coefficient in $\Delta\phi=\lambda^2\mathrm{RM}$. It is a density-weighted line integral of the line-of-sight <magnetic field>; the coefficient $a_0$ depends on the units. A sign convention for the line of sight fixes its sign. Correlations across an extended source can constrain a foreground <magnetic energy spectrum>, subject to statistical assumptions.

= Long-path projection of a magnetic correlation
{parent=Rotation measure}
{title2=$C_{\mathrm{RM}}(s)\simeq a_0^2n_e^2L\int H(k_\perp)e^{i\mathbf k_\perp\cdot\mathbf s}d^2k_\perp/(2\pi)^2$}

For constant <electron> density, common path length $L$, and a <solenoidal isotropic spectral tensor>, the rotation-measure <correlation> contains the window $|\int_0^Le^{ik_zz}dz|^2$. As $L$ becomes large compared with the <correlation length>, this window acts as $2\pi L\delta(k_z)$ within the <integral>. The transverse projector then has $zz$ component one, yielding the displayed two-dimensional <Fourier transform>. For finite $L$, the windowed integral retains longitudinal modes and endpoint corrections. The method of using such <rotation measure> correlations to infer magnetic spectra is developed by https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0302426[Enßlin and Vogt].

= Hankel inversion of a rotation-measure correlation
{c}
{parent=Long-path projection of a magnetic correlation}
{title2=$H(k)=\frac{2\pi}{a_0^2n_e^2L}\int_0^\infty rJ_0(kr)C_{\mathrm{RM}}(r)\,dr$}

Invert the two-dimensional <Fourier transform> in the <long-path projection of a magnetic correlation>, then perform the angular integration in <polar coordinates>. The angular integral is $2\pi J_0(kr)$, a <Bessel function of the first kind>, giving the displayed <Hankel transform> with the stated <Fourier-Hankel normalization for an isotropic spectrum>. The result recovers the scalar tensor coefficient $H$, whose shell-integrated <magnetic energy spectrum> has an additional factor $k^2$.

= Polarizer
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

= Polariser
{synonym}

A linear <polarizer> transmits one <linear polarization> and suppresses the orthogonal one. Two ideal crossed <polarizers> block light unless an intervening medium changes its <polarization of an electromagnetic wave>. A <twisted nematic field effect> uses this selection to convert field-driven reorientation into brightness modulation.

= Stokes parameters
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{c}
{title2=$(I,Q,U,V)$}

The Stokes parameters $I,Q,U,V$ describe intensity, two linear-polarization components and circular polarization. $Q$ and $U$ depend on the chosen transverse basis. On the celestial sphere, $Q\pm iU$ transform as spin-two fields rather than as ordinary scalars.

= Linear polarization
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}

A wave is linearly polarized when its oscillating <electric field> always lies along one fixed line. For a transverse <plane wave>, that line is perpendicular to the <wavevector>. Unlike <circular polarization>, the field oscillates along the line rather than rotating through directions.

= Circular polarization
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

An electromagnetic wave has circular polarization when its transverse <electric field> has constant magnitude and rotates once during each period. In complex-amplitude notation, two orthogonal components have equal magnitude and a relative phase of one quarter-cycle.

= Perfect conductor
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

At the surface of a perfect conductor, the tangential <electric field> and normal <magnetic field> vanish. An incident <plane electromagnetic wave> is therefore accompanied by a reflected wave whose tangential electric field cancels the incident field at the surface.

= Scalar polarization reduction at a conducting corrugation
{parent=Perfect conductor}
{title2=$E_y|_\Gamma=0,\quad\partial_nH_y|_\Gamma=0$}

If a metal surface is $z=h(x)$ and the incident field is independent of $y$, Maxwell's equations separate into <TE polarization> and <TM polarization>. The metal condition that the tangential electric field vanish sets $E_y=0$ in the TE sector. In the TM sector $\mathbf E=i\nabla\times(H_y\widehat{\mathbf y})/(\omega\epsilon)$, so the same condition gives $(\partial_z-h'\partial_x)H_y=0$. This explains why the TE problem agrees with scalar Dirichlet acoustics while the TM problem has different rough-surface boundary forcing.

= Normal reflection of an electromagnetic wave from a perfect conductor
{parent=Perfect conductor}

At normal incidence, the reflected electric amplitude is the negative of the incident amplitude at the surface, while the reflected magnetic amplitude has the same sign. Their superposition forms a <standing wave> with an electric node and a magnetic antinode at the conductor.

= Interface conditions for electromagnetic fields
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

Across an interface with unit normal $\mathbf n$ from medium one to medium two, <Maxwell equations> imply $\mathbf n\times(\mathbf E_2-\mathbf E_1)=0$, $\mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf B_2-\mathbf B_1)=0$, $\mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf D_2-\mathbf D_1)=\sigma_f$, and $\mathbf n\times(\mathbf H_2-\mathbf H_1)=\mathbf K_f$. Here $\sigma_f$ and $\mathbf K_f$ are the free surface <electric charge> and <electric current> densities.

= Dielectric-interface boundary conditions
{parent=Interface conditions for electromagnetic fields}

Without free surface charge or current, the tangential components of $E$ and $H$ and the normal components of $D$ and $B$ are continuous across an interface.

= Phase matching at a planar wave interface
{parent=Dielectric-interface boundary conditions}

Equality of the boundary fields for every tangential position and time forces equal frequency and equal tangential wavevector for incident, reflected, and transmitted plane waves.

= Snell law for electromagnetic waves
{parent=Phase matching at a planar wave interface}
{c}

For refractive indices $n_-$ and $n_+$,
$$
\theta_R=\theta_I,
\qquad
n_-\sin\theta_I=n_+\sin\theta_T.
$$

= Total internal reflection
{parent=Snell law for electromagnetic waves}
{wiki}

Total internal reflection occurs when a wave travels from higher to lower <refractive index> above the critical incidence angle, leaving no propagating transmitted plane wave normal to the interface.

= Critical angle for total internal reflection
{title2=$\theta_c$}
{parent=Total internal reflection}

For refractive indices $n_->n_+$, the critical angle satisfies $\sin\theta_c=n_+/n_-$. Above it, the transmitted normal wavenumber is imaginary and the transmitted field is an <evanescent wave>.

= Fresnel equations
{c}
{parent=Dielectric-interface boundary conditions}
{wiki}

The <Fresnel equations> give reflection and transmission amplitudes for both transverse-electric and transverse-magnetic <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> at a planar interface. They follow by applying <interface conditions for electromagnetic fields> to incident, reflected and refracted <plane electromagnetic waves>.

= Transverse-electric Fresnel reflection coefficient
{parent=Fresnel equations}

When the permeabilities agree, the signed reflected-to-incident electric-field ratio for polarization normal to the plane of incidence is
$$
r_{\rm TE}
=\frac{n_-\cos\theta_I-n_+\cos\theta_T}
{n_-\cos\theta_I+n_+\cos\theta_T}.
$$
For incidence from lower to higher refractive index it never vanishes.

= Reflected-to-transmitted transverse-electric amplitude ratio
{parent=Transverse-electric Fresnel reflection coefficient}

For equal permeabilities on the two sides of an interface, tangential-field continuity and <Snell law for electromagnetic waves> give
$$
\frac{E_R}{E_T}
=\frac12\left(1-\frac{\tan\theta_R}{\tan\theta_T}\right)
$$
for consistently signed electric amplitudes normal to the plane of incidence.

= Plane wave in an anisotropic dielectric
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}

For $D_i=\varepsilon_{ij}E_j$, $B=\mu H$, and a plane wave with electric polarization $e$, the source-free Maxwell equations imply
$$
k\times(k\times e)+\omega^2\mu\varepsilon e=0.
$$
If $e$ is an eigenvector of $\varepsilon$, then $e\perp k$, the dispersion relation is $|k|^2=\omega^2\mu\varepsilon_e$, and the <Poynting vector> is parallel to $k$.

= Poynting vector
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf S=\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/\mu_0$}
{wiki}

In vacuum the <Poynting vector>
$$
\mathbf S=\frac1{\mu_0}\mathbf E\times\mathbf B
$$
is the local <energy flux>, measured in watts per square metre. Its <surface integral> $P=\int_A\mathbf S\cdot\mathbf n\,dA$ gives signed <power> crossing a surface with unit <normal vector> $\mathbf n$. Positive flux follows the chosen normal; for a closed surface the usual convention is outward. <Power> depends on the normal component and area, rather than just the field magnitude.

The <Poynting vector> applies to steady fields as well as <electromagnetic waves>. In a driven resistive <electrical circuit>, surrounding fields can carry <energy> into the conductor, where electrical <work> becomes <Joule heating>. This turns a circuit-level power balance into a spatial description of energy delivery. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-6-001-electromagnetic-fields-and-energy-spring-2008/0f814aee1c72517df7d7e7e96b7365e1_11.pdf[Haus and Melcher, Electromagnetic Fields and Energy, chapter 11, §§11.0–11.3]{ref}.

= Poynting theorem
{parent=Poynting vector}
{c}
{title2=$\partial_tu+\nabla\cdot\mathbf S=-\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E$}

= Poynting's theorem
{c}
{synonym}

The <Poynting theorem> is the local <energy> balance of classical <electromagnetism>. For vacuum field laws, with <current density> $\mathbf J$, the <electromagnetic energy> per unit volume is
$$
u=\frac{\epsilon_0|\mathbf E|^2}{2}+\frac{|\mathbf B|^2}{2\mu_0},
$$
and
$$
\partial_tu+\nabla\cdot\mathbf S=-\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E.
$$
Thus a decrease in local <electromagnetic energy> can supply outward <energy flux> or <work> on <electric charge>. The term $\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E$ is <power> transferred to matter per unit volume. Its sign matters: positive values remove field <energy>, while negative values describe matter supplying the field. The magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> supplies no work term.

For a fixed volume $V$, the <divergence theorem> gives the directly measurable balance
$$
\frac{d}{dt}\int_Vu\,dV
=-\int_{\partial V}\mathbf S\cdot\mathbf n\,dA
-\int_V\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E\,dV.
$$
For example, $2.0\,\mathrm W$ entering a region with $0.50\,\mathrm W$ leaving and $1.20\,\mathrm W$ transferred to matter means stored <electromagnetic energy> increases at $0.30\,\mathrm W$. In source-free vacuum only the boundary flux changes the stored <energy>; a nonzero local <Poynting vector> can still have zero net closed-surface flux.

A brief proof takes the <dot product> of the <Ampère-Maxwell equation> with $\mathbf E/\mu_0$ and uses <Faraday's law> with $\mathbf B/\mu_0$. The identity $\nabla\cdot(\mathbf E\times\mathbf B)=\mathbf B\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf E)-\mathbf E\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf B)$ turns the remaining terms into the <divergence> of the <Poynting vector>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_27.html#Ch27-S3[Feynman lectures, II.27.3: local field-energy conservation]{ref}.

= Closed-surface Poynting flux of a plane wave
{parent=Poynting theorem}

For a real linearly polarized monochromatic vacuum <plane wave>, the local <Poynting vector> is proportional to $\cos^2(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf k}$. Its flux through a closed surface generally oscillates at $2\omega$, with zero cycle average, although particular volume geometries can cancel it identically. For a circularly polarized plane wave of constant amplitude the <Poynting vector> is spatially and temporally constant, so the net flux through every closed surface is exactly zero. A nonzero constant local energy flux must not be confused with the net closed-surface flux.

= Electromagnetic energy
{title2=$U_{\rm EM}$}
{parent=Poynting theorem}

In vacuum the electromagnetic energy density is
$$
u=\frac{\varepsilon_0E^2}{2}+\frac{B^2}{2\mu_0},
$$
and the field energy in a region is $U_{\rm EM}=\int u\,dV$.

= Magnetic energy
{parent=Electromagnetic energy}
{title2=$E_B$}

In vacuum-permeability <magnetohydrodynamics>, the <magnetic energy> in a volume is $E_B=\int B^2/(2\mu_0)\,dV$. Its volume density equals the <magnetic pressure>.

= Magnetic energy spectrum
{parent=Magnetic energy}
{title2=$E_B(k)$}

The shell-integrated magnetic energy spectrum distributes <magnetic energy> over <wavenumbers>. With a <solenoidal isotropic spectral tensor> normalized to $\widehat C_{ii}=2H(k)$ and the inverse <Fourier transform> measure $d^3k/(2\pi)^3$, one has $\mathbb E|\mathbf B|^2=\pi^{-2}\int k^2H(k)dk$. In <Gaussian units>, $E_B(k)=k^2H(k)/(8\pi^3)$ integrates to the energy density $\mathbb E|\mathbf B|^2/(8\pi)$. A spectrum per unit mass or normalized to $B^2/2$ has different constant factors. Scale-local fluctuation estimates give $\delta B_l^2$ proportional to $kE_B(k)$ at $k\sim l^{-1}$ when the spectrum is normalized consistently.

= Energy density and flux of a plane electromagnetic wave
{parent=Poynting theorem}

For a single traveling vacuum <plane electromagnetic wave>, $\mathbf B=\widehat{\mathbf k}\times\mathbf E/c$ gives equal electric and magnetic contributions to <electromagnetic energy>:
$$
u_E=u_B=\frac12\epsilon_0|\mathbf E|^2,\qquad
u=\epsilon_0|\mathbf E|^2,\qquad
\mathbf S=cu\widehat{\mathbf k}.
$$
For the fields with <linear polarization> along $x$ and magnetic orientation along $y$ specified above,
$$
\mathbf S(z,t)=\epsilon_0cE_0^2\cos^2(kz-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf z},\qquad
\langle\mathbf S\rangle=\frac{\epsilon_0cE_0^2}{2}\widehat{\mathbf z}.
$$
Angle brackets mean an average over one period at fixed position. The factor $1/2$ comes from averaging squared <cosine>; $E_0$ is the peak amplitude, whereas the <root mean square> field is $E_{\mathrm{rms}}=E_0/\sqrt2$. The equal energy contributions oscillate together, rather than trading energy as in an <RLC circuit>. https://live.ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-013-electromagnetics-and-applications-spring-2009/d3be4ea78b036a6362230fb41780cf54_MIT6_013S09_notes.pdf[MIT 6.013 course notes, §2.7.3: power and energy in uniform plane waves]{ref}.

For $E_0=100\,\mathrm{V/m}$, using $c=299792458\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and $\epsilon_0\simeq8.854\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{F/m}$,
$$
B_0=\frac{100}{c}\,\mathrm{V/m}\simeq3.34\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm T,
\qquad
\langle S_z\rangle\simeq13.3\,\mathrm{W/m^2},
\qquad
\langle u\rangle\simeq4.43\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{J/m^3}.
$$
A $0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ surface normal to $+z$ therefore has average crossing <power> $0.133\,\mathrm W$. This is incident <power>; the amount absorbed depends on the surface. Doubling the field amplitude quadruples the average flux.

= Poynting theorem in a linear anisotropic medium
{c}
{parent=Poynting theorem}

For symmetric time-independent constitutive tensors,
$$
u=\frac12(\varepsilon_{ij}E_iE_j+\mu_{ij}H_iH_j),
\qquad
S=E\times H,
$$
and the macroscopic Maxwell equations give $\partial_tu+\nabla\cdot S=-E\cdot J$.

= Near and far field
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

The <near and far field> distinguish source-dependent electromagnetic field regions. Close to a localized source, reactive fields and induction terms contribute; sufficiently far away, the leading transverse fields decrease as the inverse distance and carry outgoing power. The <radiation zone> describes this far-field limit.

= Radiation zone
{parent=Near and far field}

In the radiation zone of a localized source, the leading electromagnetic fields decay as $1/R$, are transverse to the observation direction, and satisfy $B=\widehat R\times E/c$. Terms decaying faster than $1/R$ do not contribute to the limiting radiated power.

= Intensity of an isotropic radiator
{parent=Radiation zone}

An isotropic source of average power $P$ has intensity
$$
\langle S\rangle=\frac{P}{4\pi r^2}
$$
at distance $r$. For monochromatic vacuum radiation, its electric-field amplitude is $E_0=\sqrt{2\langle S\rangle/(\epsilon_0c)}$.

= Multipole radiation
{parent=Radiation zone}
{wiki}

<Multipole radiation> expands radiation from localized time-dependent sources into electric and magnetic multipoles. <Electric dipole radiation> is the leading electric term, while <magnetic dipole radiation> and higher electric multipoles contribute when the leading term vanishes or higher precision is needed.

= Electric dipole radiation
{parent=Multipole radiation}

For a localized source whose size is small compared with its radiation wavelength, the radiation fields of its electric dipole moment $p$ are
$$
E_{\rm rad}=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi R}\widehat R\times(\widehat R\times\ddot p(t-R/c)),
\qquad
B_{\rm rad}=\frac1c\widehat R\times E_{\rm rad}.
$$
Their total instantaneous power is
$$
\mathcal P=\frac{\mu_0}{6\pi c}|\ddot p(t-R/c)|^2.
$$

= Electric-dipole approximation for radiation
{parent=Electric dipole radiation}

= Dipole approximation for radiation
{synonym}

For a source of characteristic size $a$ and <angular frequency> $\omega$, the electric-dipole approximation requires
$$
\frac{\omega a}{c}\ll1,
$$
so the source is much smaller than its radiation wavelength. Observing the pure $1/R$ radiation field additionally requires the <radiation zone>, $R\gg c/\omega$ and $R\gg a$.

= Angular distribution of electric dipole radiation
{parent=Electric dipole radiation}

If $\theta$ is the angle between the observation direction and $\ddot p$, then
$$
\frac{dP}{d\Omega}
=\frac{\mu_0}{16\pi^2c}|\ddot p|^2\sin^2\theta.
$$
Integrating over <solid angle> uses $\int\sin^2\theta\,d\Omega=8\pi/3$ and gives the total <electric dipole radiation> power.

= Radiation damping of a harmonically oscillating charge
{parent=Electric dipole radiation}

A charge $q$ undergoing <simple harmonic motion> of <angular frequency> $\omega$ and slowly varying amplitude $A$ radiates the averaged power
$$
\langle P\rangle=\frac{\mu_0q^2A^2\omega^4}{12\pi c}.
$$
Its mechanical energy $E=mA^2\omega^2/2$ therefore has <exponential decay> rate $\gamma=\mu_0q^2\omega^2/(6\pi mc)$ and <half-life>
$$
t_{1/2}=\frac{6\pi mc\log2}{\mu_0q^2\omega^2}.
$$

= Magnetic dipole radiation
{parent=Multipole radiation}

Electromagnetic duality replaces the electric dipole $p$ by $m/c$ for a magnetic dipole moment $m$. Therefore
$$
\mathcal P=\frac{\mu_0}{6\pi c^3}|\ddot m|^2.
$$

This is the magnetic-dipole term of the <multipole radiation> expansion.

= Magnetic-dipole spin-down
{parent=Magnetic dipole radiation}

If a rigid body's inclined magnetic dipole rotates at angular speed $\Omega$, its radiated power is $C\Omega^4$. Conservation of rotational energy $I\Omega^2/2$ then gives
$$
\dot\Omega=-\frac CI\Omega^3,
\qquad
\Omega(t)^{-2}=\Omega_0^{-2}+\frac{2C}{I}t.
$$
